window.CISI_DATA.securities = {"key": "securities", "title": "Securities", "subtitle": "CISI Level 3 - Capital Markets Programme", "examFormat": "CBT (multiple choice) - modules organised thematically for revision", "chapters": [{"id": "sec-m1", "number": 1, "title": "The Financial Services Industry", "examWeight": "Foundational module", "summaryHtml": "<p>The financial services industry channels capital between organisations that need it (eg, growing companies) and those with capital to invest (eg, individuals saving via pension funds), providing execution, payment, advisory and management services along the way. Major long-standing financial centres include New York, London and Tokyo, with growing importance from Shanghai, Shenzhen, Hong Kong, Frankfurt, Paris, Mumbai and Jakarta. The industry is constantly reshaped by external events: <strong>Brexit</strong> shifted some EU-facing activity from London to Paris, Frankfurt and Amsterdam while freeing the UK to diverge from EU rules (eg, listing rules); <strong>COVID-19</strong> changed how and where people work; the <strong>Russia-Ukraine war</strong> (from 2022) triggered sanctions, energy price shocks and disrupted Ukrainian grain/fertiliser exports, feeding global inflation; and <strong>Trump-era tariffs</strong> have increased trade uncertainty. Central bank monetary policy, government fiscal policy and periodic crises (eg, the 2007-2008 global financial crisis) also drive structural and regulatory change.</p><p><strong>Wholesale markets</strong> comprise equity, bond and FX markets. The <strong>bond market</strong> is OTC-dominated (few trades occur on exchange), involves large investors (asset managers, insurers) dealing with bank dealers, and reached <strong>US$145.1 trillion</strong> outstanding at the end of 2024; the US has the largest domestic bond market, while international bond trading is concentrated in Europe. The <strong>FX market</strong> is the largest financial market of all, with average daily turnover of <strong>US$9.6 trillion</strong> (April 2025 BIS triennial survey), up 28% from US$7.5 trillion in April 2022. It trades 24/7, is OTC (no central exchange), and the USD is on one side of <strong>89.2%</strong> of trades, followed by the EUR (28.9%) and JPY (16.8%); London is the most active FX centre.</p><p>The <strong>retail sector</strong> includes retail banks (deposits, lending, payments, and increasingly mortgages/investments/pensions/insurance), pension funds (long-term investors in shares, bonds, cash and alternatives), insurance companies (life and general insurance, major stock market investors via premium income), investment services (advice/dealing via banks, brokers and robo-advisors), and financial planning/wealth management (holistic advice covering budgeting, retirement, risk, tax and estate planning, delivered by <strong>fiduciaries</strong> who must legally put clients first).</p><p>The <strong>wholesale sector</strong> comprises investment banks (corporate finance, underwriting, treasury, investment management, securities trading, structured products), fund managers (institutional vs private client, plus alternative managers like hedge funds; the US is the largest global centre, the UK the largest in Europe), brokers (institutional and execution-only, earning commission and/or spread), and custodian banks (safekeeping, settlement, corporate actions, cash and FX management, reporting - now a consolidated, competitive global industry).</p>", "sections": [{"heading": "1.1 The Role of Financial Services and the Global Context", "html": "<p>Financial services provide the essential <strong>link between organisations needing capital and those with capital available for investment</strong>. For example, a growing company needing capital may receive it from individuals saving for retirement through a pension fund. The financial services sector channels invested money to the organisations that need it, and provides execution, payment, advisory and management services.</p><h4>Major Financial Centres</h4><ul><li>Long-standing centres: <strong>New York, London, Tokyo</strong></li><li>Increasingly significant Chinese cities: <strong>Shanghai, Shenzhen, Hong Kong</strong></li><li>European hubs: <strong>Frankfurt, Paris</strong></li><li>Emerging market hubs: <strong>Mumbai, Jakarta</strong></li></ul><p>Financial services provide considerable employment in these centres and supply vital functions enabling businesses to raise funds, expand and prosper.</p><h4>Recent Developments Reshaping the Industry</h4><table><tr><th>Event</th><th>Key Impact</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Brexit</strong> (UK departure from EU)</td><td>Some European activities centred in London moved to Paris, Frankfurt and Amsterdam. Also enabled the UK to move away from EU requirements, eg, changes to listing rules.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>COVID-19 pandemic</strong></td><td>Significantly re-shaped how companies do business and how/where employees work.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Russia-Ukraine war</strong> (began 2022)</td><td>Sanctions imposed on Russia (a major energy/gas provider) impacted energy prices; curtailed Ukraine's grain and fertiliser exports, impacting food prices globally; contributed to global inflation at levels not seen for decades.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Trump tariffs</strong> (2nd term)</td><td>Reshaping international trade, increasing global uncertainty; concerns about reliability of economic data could affect company valuations and cause hesitancy in M&amp;A activity.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2007-2008 Global Financial Crisis</strong></td><td>Example of a market crisis causing shifts and permanent (eg, regulatory) changes in how the industry operates.</td></tr></table><p>In addition to these events, <strong>central bank monetary policy</strong> and <strong>government fiscal policy</strong> dramatically impact inflation, economic growth and financial markets. Markets also go through natural cycles, occasionally producing crises that cause lasting structural change.</p>"}, {"heading": "1.2 Wholesale Markets: Equity and Bond Markets", "html": "<p>Wholesale (professional) markets cover dealing between financial institutions, in contrast to retail markets which serve individual clients directly. The three key wholesale markets are <strong>equity markets, bond markets and foreign exchange (FX) markets</strong>.</p><h4>Equity Markets</h4><p>Equity markets enable companies to raise capital by issuing shares to investors, and allow investors to trade those shares on public exchanges. Equity market participants include retail and institutional investors, brokers and market makers, with dealing concentrated on organised stock exchanges (in contrast to the OTC-dominated bond market).</p><h4>Bond Markets</h4><p>Bond markets, though less visible than equities, are critical to global finance.</p><ul><li>Major participants: large institutional investors (funds run by asset managers and insurers) generally dealing with dealers/traders at large banks.</li><li>Unlike equities, <strong>little dealing is done via stock exchange systems</strong> - the majority of trades take place away from exchanges, <strong>OTC (over-the-counter)</strong>.</li><li>Trading volume is lower than equities: a smaller number of much larger trades.</li><li>The amount outstanding on the <strong>global bond market reached US$145.1 trillion</strong> at the end of 2024.</li><li>Instruments range from <strong>domestic bonds</strong> (issued by companies and governments) to <strong>international bonds</strong> (issued by companies, governments and supranational agencies such as the World Bank or other development banks).</li><li>The <strong>US has the largest bond market</strong>, but <strong>international bond trading is concentrated in Europe</strong>, reflecting the region's role as a hub for cross-border finance.</li></ul>"}, {"heading": "1.3 Wholesale Markets: Foreign Exchange (FX) Markets", "html": "<p>Foreign exchange (FX) markets are the <strong>largest of all financial markets</strong>.</p><table><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Figure</th></tr><tr><td>Average daily turnover (April 2025, BIS triennial survey)</td><td><strong>US$9.6 trillion</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Average daily turnover (April 2022)</td><td>US$7.5 trillion</td></tr><tr><td>Growth 2022 to 2025</td><td><strong>28% increase</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Trading hours</td><td>24 hours a day, 7 days a week</td></tr><tr><td>Most active financial centre</td><td><strong>London</strong></td></tr></table><p>The rate at which one currency is exchanged for another is driven by <strong>supply and demand</strong>. For example, strong demand from Japanese investors for US assets (property, bonds, shares) will tend to push the US dollar up in value relative to the Japanese yen. Governments, companies and individuals use the FX market to deal with cash inflows and outflows denominated in overseas currencies.</p><p>FX is an <strong>OTC market</strong> - brokers/dealers negotiate directly with one another, and there is <strong>no central exchange or clearing house</strong>. Instead, FX trading is distributed among major financial centres. One minor exception: certain FX futures and options contracts are traded on a few exchanges.</p><h4>Currency Composition (BIS Triennial Survey, April 2025)</h4><p>The BIS releases figures on FX market composition every three years. As currency trades involve two currencies, percentages sum to 200%.</p><table><tr><th>Currency</th><th>% of All Trades</th></tr><tr><td><strong>US dollar (USD)</strong></td><td><strong>89.2%</strong> (most traded currency)</td></tr><tr><td>Euro (EUR)</td><td>28.9%</td></tr><tr><td>Japanese yen (JPY)</td><td>16.8%</td></tr></table><p>The Chinese renminbi and the Swiss franc have both been gaining importance in FX trading.</p>"}, {"heading": "1.4 Retail Sector: Banks, Pension Funds and Insurance Companies", "html": "<h4>3.1 Retail Banks</h4><p>Retail banks take deposits from, and lend funds to, retail customers, and provide payment and money transmission services. They may provide similar services to business customers. Historically operated through physical branches, then telephone banking; <strong>today, most retail banking is conducted online and through mobile applications</strong>. Larger retail banks also offer mortgage lending, investments, pensions and insurance.</p><h4>3.2 Pension Funds</h4><p>Pension funds are a key method for individuals to provide for retirement, ranging from employer-provided schemes to self-directed schemes. They are <strong>large, long-term investors</strong> in shares, bonds and cash; some also invest in physical assets (eg, property) or alternative assets (eg, venture capital, hedge funds). Substantial sums are invested to meet the aim of providing a pension on retirement.</p><h4>3.3 Insurance Companies</h4><p>Insurance companies effectively manage risks by providing:</p><ul><li><strong>Life products</strong> - pay a named beneficiary when the insured person dies.</li><li><strong>General insurance</strong> - covers risks arising from incidents such as accidents and theft (eg, motor insurance, household insurance).</li></ul><p>Insurance companies are also important providers of <strong>pension products</strong>. Protection planning products range from payment protection policies (covering loan/mortgage repayments) to fleet insurance against risks such as an airline's planes crashing. Insurance companies collect <strong>premiums</strong> in exchange for cover; this premium income is invested in shares and bonds, making insurers <strong>major players in the stock market</strong>. Investments are subsequently realised to pay claims.</p>"}, {"heading": "1.5 Retail Sector: Investment Services, Financial Planning and Wealth Management", "html": "<h4>3.4 Investment Services</h4><p>Specialists help retail customers select, purchase, sell, and keep under review their investments, collect income, and report regularly - generally for a fee.</p><ul><li>Providers include <strong>retail banks</strong> and <strong>stockbrokers</strong>.</li><li><strong>Institutional brokers</strong> operate in the wholesale/professional market, using discretion and skill to execute large trades for clients (investment institutions, private clients).</li><li><strong>Execution-only brokers</strong> give no advice, simply offering trading services to retail clients.</li><li>Both types of brokers earn profits by charging commissions and/or profiting from the <strong>bid-ask spread</strong> on trades executed.</li><li><strong>Robo-advisors</strong>: use algorithms to provide automated investment advice at relatively low fees. Customers complete an online assessment; the robo-advisor makes recommendations and rebalances the portfolio as necessary. Often subsidiaries of traditional financial institutions or large technology firms.</li></ul><h4>3.5 Financial Planning and Wealth Management</h4><p><strong>Wealth managers</strong> (many are former traditional stockbrokers) do more than provide investment advice - they consider the client's <strong>total financial situation</strong>, charging a fee for advice and commission on transactions, and may charge custody and portfolio management fees for looking after client assets. Wealth management firms can be independent, or divisions of larger entities such as banks or insurance companies.</p><p><strong>Financial planning</strong> is available to individuals, families and businesses needing objective assistance organising their financial affairs. It extends beyond investment management to include:</p><ul><li>Financial management and budgeting</li><li>Retirement planning</li><li>Risk management/insurance planning</li><li>Tax planning</li><li>Estate planning</li></ul><p>Financial planning is about meeting the <strong>client's</strong> financial and lifestyle objectives, not the adviser's. Planners may charge a flat fee for developing a plan, or (if also acting as an adviser) may not charge for the plan and instead earn commission on recommended securities sold.</p><p>Financial planners and wealth managers are typically considered <strong>fiduciaries</strong>: a fiduciary acts on behalf of others and puts clients' interests ahead of their own, maintaining good faith and trust. This differs from less formal advisers/consultants, as a fiduciary is <strong>both ethically and legally bound</strong> to put the client first.</p>"}, {"heading": "1.6 Wholesale Participants: Investment Banks and Fund Managers", "html": "<h4>4.1 Investment Banks</h4><p>Investment banks provide advice and arrange finance for companies that want to float/list on a stock market, raise capital by issuing shares or bonds, or carry out mergers and acquisitions. They also serve investors such as pension funds and asset managers. A full-service investment banking group may offer:</p><table><tr><th>Activity</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td>Corporate finance and advisory</td><td>New issues of securities to raise capital; M&amp;A advice; underwriting or bookbuilding/bookrunning of public listings or private placements.</td></tr><tr><td>Banking</td><td>For governments, institutions and companies.</td></tr><tr><td>Treasury dealing</td><td>Foreign currency dealing for corporate clients; financial engineering to manage interest rate and exchange rate risk.</td></tr><tr><td>Investment management</td><td>For large investors (corporate pension funds, charities, private clients) - direct investment for HNWIs, or pooled investment via collective investment funds.</td></tr><tr><td>Securities trading</td><td>Equities, bonds and derivatives; broking and distribution facilities.</td></tr><tr><td>Structured products</td><td>Complex instruments combining several types of securities or currencies to meet specific hedging or investment needs.</td></tr></table><p>Only a few investment banks provide services across all these areas - most specialise. Some have diversified into proprietary trading, servicing hedge funds, or private equity investments.</p><h4>4.2 Fund Managers</h4><p>Fund management is the professional management of investment portfolios for institutions and private investors. <strong>The US is the largest global centre for fund management; the UK is the largest in Europe.</strong></p><p>Fund managers (investment/asset managers) invest money held by institutions (pension funds, insurance companies), collective investment schemes (CISs, eg, US mutual funds, Europe's investment companies with variable capital (ICVCs)), and wealthier individuals. Some firms focus solely on fund management; others are divisions of insurers or banks.</p><table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Clients</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Institutional fund managers</strong></td><td>Work for institutions in the wholesale/professional sector, eg, a company pension fund or insurance company fund.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Private client fund managers</strong></td><td>Invest money of relatively wealthy individuals in the retail sector.</td></tr></table><p>Institutional portfolios are usually larger than private client portfolios. There is overlap: <strong>family offices</strong> (single or multi-family) manage a large pool of assets for a wealthy family/group of families and sit between private client and institutional in size. Fund managers charge a management fee (percentage of fund value) and may charge an incentive fee based on performance above a benchmark. Fund management also extends to companies, charities and local government authorities. <strong>Alternative fund managers</strong> invest in non-traditional assets (eg, private equity) or trade complex long/short strategies (eg, hedge funds).</p>"}, {"heading": "1.7 Wholesale Participants: Brokers and Custodian Banks", "html": "<h4>4.3 Brokers</h4><p>Brokers arrange trades in financial instruments on behalf of clients. <strong>Institutional brokers</strong> in the wholesale market earn revenue by executing large trades with discretion and skill. Many broking activities are undertaken by divisions of large investment banks rather than independent entities. Like retail brokers, they earn revenue by charging commissions and/or profiting from the bid-ask spread on trades executed.</p><h4>4.4 Custodian Banks</h4><p>Custodians are banks that specialise in <strong>safe custody services</strong>, looking after shares, bonds and cash on behalf of others (eg, fund managers, pension funds, insurance companies), often in multiple currencies. Core activities include:</p><ul><li>Holding assets in safekeeping (equities and bonds)</li><li>Arranging settlement of purchases and sales of securities</li><li>Processing corporate actions, including collecting income from assets - dividends (equities) and interest (bonds)</li><li>Providing information on underlying companies and their corporate actions</li><li>Managing cash transactions</li><li>Performing FX transactions when required</li><li>Providing access to foreign markets via an international network of country branches or affiliated local custody firms (larger custodians)</li><li>Providing regular reporting on all activities to clients</li></ul><p>Competition has driven down custody charges, resulting in <strong>consolidation</strong> within the profession. The custody business is now dominated by a small number of <strong>global custodians</strong>, some of which are divisions of large banks.</p>"}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What is the fundamental role of the financial services industry, as described in relation to capital?", "options": ["To regulate the money supply on behalf of central banks", "To provide the link between organisations needing capital and those with capital available for investment", "To set interest rates for retail savers", "To act solely as a tax collection intermediary for governments"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Financial services fundamentally channel money from those with capital available for investment (eg, pension fund savers) to organisations needing capital (eg, growing companies), while providing execution, payment, advisory and management services."}, {"question": "Which of the following is NOT listed among the long-standing, major global financial centres?", "options": ["New York", "London", "Tokyo", "Sydney"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "The long-standing financial centres cited are New York, London and Tokyo, with Shanghai, Shenzhen, Hong Kong, Frankfurt, Paris, Mumbai and Jakarta noted as growing centres. Sydney is not mentioned."}, {"question": "As a result of Brexit, which change occurred in the UK financial services industry?", "options": ["All London-based financial activity relocated permanently to New York", "The UK was required to adopt stricter EU financial regulations", "Some European activities centred in London moved to cities such as Paris, Frankfurt and Amsterdam, and the UK gained the ability to diverge from EU requirements", "The Bank of England lost its independence"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Brexit caused some EU-facing financial activity to move from London to other European cities, and also enabled the UK to move away from EU requirements, such as amending listing rules."}, {"question": "The Russia-Ukraine war, which began in 2022, primarily contributed to global inflation through its impact on which two commodity areas?", "options": ["Precious metals and technology components", "Energy (especially gas) and agricultural commodities (grain and fertiliser)", "Automobiles and semiconductors", "Property and construction materials"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Sanctions on Russia (a major energy/gas provider) affected energy prices, while the war curtailed Ukraine's ability to export grain and fertiliser, together contributing to global inflation."}, {"question": "What was the amount outstanding on the global bond market at the end of 2024?", "options": ["US$9.6 trillion", "US$7.5 trillion", "US$145.1 trillion", "US$45.1 billion"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The amount outstanding on the global bond market reached US$145.1 trillion at the end of 2024."}, {"question": "In contrast to equity markets, how is the majority of bond market dealing conducted?", "options": ["Via centralised stock exchange order books", "Predominantly OTC (over-the-counter), away from exchanges", "Exclusively through government auction houses", "Via retail investment platforms"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Little bond dealing is done via stock exchange systems; the majority of trades take place OTC, with a smaller number of much larger trades than in equity markets."}, {"question": "Which country has the largest domestic bond market, while international bond trading is concentrated in a different region?", "options": ["The US has the largest bond market; international bond trading is concentrated in Europe", "China has the largest bond market; international bond trading is concentrated in Asia", "The UK has the largest bond market; international bond trading is concentrated in the US", "Japan has the largest bond market; international bond trading is concentrated in the Middle East"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Although the US has the largest bond market, international bond trading is concentrated in Europe, reflecting the region's role as a hub for cross-border finance."}, {"question": "According to the BIS triennial survey, what was the average daily turnover of the FX market in April 2025?", "options": ["US$7.5 trillion", "US$9.6 trillion", "US$145.1 trillion", "US$28 billion"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Average daily FX turnover was around US$9.6 trillion in April 2025 according to the BIS triennial survey, a 28% increase from US$7.5 trillion in April 2022."}, {"question": "By what percentage did FX market daily turnover grow between April 2022 and April 2025?", "options": ["9.6%", "89.2%", "28%", "16.8%"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Daily FX turnover rose from US$7.5 trillion in April 2022 to US$9.6 trillion in April 2025, a 28% increase."}, {"question": "According to the April 2025 BIS survey, what percentage of all FX trades involved the US dollar?", "options": ["28.9%", "16.8%", "50.0%", "89.2%"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "The US dollar remained the most traded currency, accounting for 89.2% of all currency trades as of April 2025."}, {"question": "Which financial centre is described as the most active in the world for FX trading?", "options": ["New York", "Tokyo", "London", "Singapore"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "London is the most active financial centre in the world for FX trading."}, {"question": "The FX market is best described as which type of market structure?", "options": ["A centrally cleared exchange-traded market with a single global exchange", "An OTC market where brokers/dealers negotiate directly with one another, with no central exchange or clearing house", "A government-run auction market", "A market open only during London and New York business hours"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "FX is an OTC market with no central exchange or clearing house; trading is distributed among major financial centres, with only a minor exception for certain FX futures and options traded on exchanges. The market operates 24 hours a day, 7 days a week."}, {"question": "A retail bank customer notices their bank now also offers mortgages, pensions and insurance products alongside traditional deposit and lending services. This best illustrates which trend described in the study material?", "options": ["Retail banks are legally required to offer only deposit and lending services", "Larger retail banks increasingly offer other financial products such as mortgage lending, investments, pensions and insurance", "This is illegal cross-selling under FCA rules", "Retail banks have stopped offering payment and money transmission services"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "As well as traditional deposit-taking, lending and payment services, larger retail banks also offer other financial products such as mortgage lending, investments, pensions and insurance."}, {"question": "What is a defining characteristic of pension funds as investors?", "options": ["They are short-term speculative traders focused solely on cash", "They are large, long-term investors in assets such as shares, bonds and cash, and sometimes property or alternative assets", "They only invest in government bonds", "They are prohibited from investing in alternative assets"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Pension funds are large, long-term investors in shares, bonds and cash, with some also investing in physical assets such as property or alternative assets such as venture capital or hedge funds."}, {"question": "An insurance company pays a named beneficiary a sum of money when the insured person dies. Which type of insurance product is this?", "options": ["General insurance", "Life products", "Fleet insurance", "Payment protection insurance"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Life products pay a named beneficiary when the insured dies, whereas general insurance covers risks arising from incidents such as accidents and theft."}, {"question": "Why are insurance companies considered major players in the stock market?", "options": ["They are required by law to hold a majority stake in listed companies", "Premium income collected from policyholders is used to buy investments such as shares and bonds", "They only trade FX derivatives", "They act exclusively as custodian banks"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Insurance companies collect premiums in exchange for cover; this premium income is used to buy investments such as shares and bonds, making the insurance industry a major stock market player. Investments are later realised to pay claims."}, {"question": "An investment services provider gives no advice to clients but simply offers trading services to retail clients, earning revenue through commissions and the bid-ask spread. What type of broker is this?", "options": ["An institutional broker", "A wealth manager", "An execution-only broker", "A custodian bank"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Execution-only brokers give no advice, simply offering trading services to retail clients, earning profits from commissions and/or the bid-ask spread. Institutional brokers, by contrast, operate in the wholesale market using discretion and skill to execute large trades."}, {"question": "A customer completes an online risk assessment and receives automated, algorithm-generated investment recommendations at a relatively low fee, with the portfolio rebalanced automatically over time. What is this service called?", "options": ["A robo-advisor", "A custodian", "A fiduciary planner", "An institutional broker"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Robo-advisors use algorithms to provide automated investment advice at a relatively low fee, based on an online assessment, and rebalance the portfolio as necessary. They are often subsidiaries of traditional financial institutions or large technology firms."}, {"question": "How does financial planning differ from pure investment management, according to the study material?", "options": ["Financial planning only concerns tax filing", "Financial planning integrates all components of a client's financial life, including budgeting, retirement planning, risk/insurance planning, tax planning and estate planning, not just investments", "Financial planning is only available to institutional clients", "Financial planning excludes retirement planning"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Financial planning extends beyond investment management to address financial management and budgeting, retirement planning, risk management/insurance planning, tax planning and estate planning - integrating all components of the client's financial life."}, {"question": "Which statement correctly defines a 'fiduciary' as used in relation to financial planners and wealth managers?", "options": ["A person or organisation that acts on behalf of others and puts clients' interests ahead of their own, ethically and legally bound to do so", "Any individual who sells financial products for commission", "A regulator that supervises financial advisers", "A term used only for pension fund trustees"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "A fiduciary acts on behalf of others and puts clients' interests ahead of their own, maintaining good faith and trust. Unlike less formal advisers/consultants, a fiduciary is both ethically and legally bound to put the client first."}, {"question": "Which of the following is NOT typically among the core services offered by a full-service investment banking group?", "options": ["Corporate finance and advisory work, including underwriting and bookbuilding", "Treasury dealing and financial engineering for FX and interest rate risk", "Taking retail customer deposits and providing everyday current account banking", "Creation of and dealing in structured products"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Investment banks typically offer corporate finance/advisory, banking for governments/institutions/companies, treasury dealing, investment management, securities trading and structured products - not everyday retail deposit-taking, which is the domain of retail banks."}, {"question": "Which country is described as the largest global centre for fund management, and which is the largest in Europe?", "options": ["The US is the largest globally; the UK is the largest in Europe", "The UK is the largest globally; Germany is the largest in Europe", "China is the largest globally; France is the largest in Europe", "Japan is the largest globally; Switzerland is the largest in Europe"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The US is the largest centre globally for fund management, followed by the UK, which is the biggest in Europe."}, {"question": "What is the key distinction between institutional and private client fund managers?", "options": ["Institutional fund managers work on behalf of institutions in the wholesale/professional sector (eg, pension funds, insurance company funds), while private client fund managers invest money of relatively wealthy individuals in the retail sector", "Institutional fund managers only manage government bonds", "Private client fund managers manage larger portfolios than institutional fund managers", "There is no meaningful distinction between the two"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Institutional fund managers work for institutions such as pension funds or insurance company funds in the wholesale/professional sector; private client fund managers invest money for relatively wealthy individuals in the retail sector. Institutional portfolios are usually larger."}, {"question": "An organisation manages a large pool of assets controlled by a single wealthy family or a group of families, sitting in size somewhere between a private client and an institution. What is this called?", "options": ["A custodian bank", "A family office", "A robo-advisor", "An execution-only broker"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Family offices (single or multi-family) are organisations tasked with managing a large pool of assets controlled by a wealthy family or group of families, generally sized between private clients and institutions."}, {"question": "Which of the following is a core activity performed by custodian banks?", "options": ["Underwriting new issues of shares for public listing", "Holding assets in safekeeping, arranging settlement, processing corporate actions, managing cash and FX transactions, and providing regular reporting", "Setting monetary policy interest rates", "Providing execution-only retail trading platforms"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Custodians look after shares, bonds and cash on behalf of others, including holding assets safely, arranging settlement, processing corporate actions (eg, collecting dividends/interest), managing cash and FX transactions, and providing regular reporting to clients - not underwriting, which is an investment banking activity."}, {"question": "What has been the overall structural trend in the custodian banking industry due to competition on fees?", "options": ["Fragmentation into thousands of small local custodians", "Consolidation, with the custody business now dominated by a small number of global custodians", "Complete elimination of custody fees industry-wide", "A shift of custody activity entirely to retail banks"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Competition has driven down charges for traditional custody services, resulting in consolidation within the profession; the custody business is now dominated by a small number of global custodians, some of which are divisions of large banks."}], "flashcards": [{"front": "What is the fundamental role of financial services?", "back": "To provide the link between organisations needing capital and those with capital available for investment, providing execution, payment, advisory and management services."}, {"front": "Name the long-standing major global financial centres.", "back": "New York, London and Tokyo, with growing importance for Shanghai, Shenzhen, Hong Kong, Frankfurt, Paris, Mumbai and Jakarta."}, {"front": "What was the main effect of Brexit on financial services?", "back": "Some EU-facing activity centred in London moved to Paris, Frankfurt and Amsterdam; the UK also gained the ability to diverge from EU requirements, eg, listing rules."}, {"front": "How did the Russia-Ukraine war (from 2022) affect the global economy?", "back": "Sanctions on Russia (a major energy/gas provider) raised energy prices; disrupted Ukrainian grain and fertiliser exports raised food prices, contributing to global inflation not seen for decades."}, {"front": "What was the global bond market amount outstanding at end of 2024?", "back": "US$145.1 trillion."}, {"front": "How is the majority of bond market trading conducted?", "back": "OTC (over-the-counter), away from exchanges - little dealing occurs via stock exchange systems."}, {"front": "Which country has the largest bond market, and where is international bond trading concentrated?", "back": "The US has the largest bond market; international bond trading is concentrated in Europe."}, {"front": "What are the two categories of bond instruments described?", "back": "Domestic bonds (issued by companies and governments) and international bonds (issued by companies, governments and supranational agencies such as the World Bank)."}, {"front": "What was the average daily FX market turnover in April 2025 (BIS triennial survey)?", "back": "US$9.6 trillion."}, {"front": "By how much did FX daily turnover grow from April 2022 to April 2025, and from what figure?", "back": "A 28% increase, from US$7.5 trillion (2022) to US$9.6 trillion (2025)."}, {"front": "What are the FX market's trading hours?", "back": "24 hours a day, 7 days a week."}, {"front": "Is the FX market exchange-traded or OTC?", "back": "OTC - brokers/dealers negotiate directly, with no central exchange or clearing house (minor exception: some FX futures/options trade on exchanges)."}, {"front": "How often does the BIS release FX market composition data, and via what survey?", "back": "Every three years, via the BIS triennial survey."}, {"front": "What % of all FX trades involved the US dollar in April 2025?", "back": "89.2% (most traded currency)."}, {"front": "What % of FX trades involved the euro and the Japanese yen in April 2025?", "back": "Euro: 28.9%; Japanese yen: 16.8%."}, {"front": "Which two currencies were noted as gaining importance in FX trading?", "back": "The Chinese renminbi and the Swiss franc."}, {"front": "Which financial centre is the most active in the world for FX trading?", "back": "London."}, {"front": "What core services do retail banks provide?", "back": "Taking deposits, lending funds, and providing payment/money transmission services to retail (and business) customers; larger banks also offer mortgages, investments, pensions and insurance."}, {"front": "What kind of investor is a pension fund?", "back": "A large, long-term investor in shares, bonds and cash, sometimes also property or alternative assets (eg, venture capital, hedge funds)."}, {"front": "What is the difference between life products and general insurance?", "back": "Life products pay a named beneficiary when the insured dies; general insurance covers risks from incidents such as accidents and theft (eg, motor, household)."}, {"front": "How does insurance company premium income relate to the stock market?", "back": "Premiums collected are used to buy shares and bonds, making insurers major stock market players; investments are later realised to pay claims."}, {"front": "What do typical retail investment services include?", "back": "Selecting investments, arranging purchase/sale, keeping investments under review, collecting income, and regular reporting to customers, generally for a fee."}, {"front": "Distinguish institutional brokers from execution-only brokers.", "back": "Institutional brokers operate in the wholesale market, using discretion and skill to execute large trades; execution-only brokers give no advice, simply offering trading services to retail clients."}, {"front": "What is a robo-advisor?", "back": "A service using algorithms to provide automated investment advice at low fees, based on an online customer assessment, with automatic portfolio rebalancing; often a subsidiary of a traditional institution or tech firm."}, {"front": "What distinguishes a wealth manager from a basic investment adviser?", "back": "Wealth managers consider the client's total financial situation, charge a fee for advice plus commission on transactions, and may charge custody/portfolio management fees for looking after client assets."}, {"front": "What topics does financial planning cover beyond investment management?", "back": "Financial management and budgeting, retirement planning, risk management/insurance planning, tax planning and estate planning."}, {"front": "Define 'fiduciary'.", "back": "A person or organisation that acts on behalf of others, putting clients' interests ahead of their own, and is both ethically and legally bound to do so."}, {"front": "List the core services a full-service investment bank may offer.", "back": "Corporate finance/advisory (underwriting, bookbuilding), banking, treasury dealing, investment management, securities trading, and structured products."}, {"front": "Which country is the largest global centre for fund management, and which the largest in Europe?", "back": "The US is largest globally; the UK is the largest in Europe."}, {"front": "Distinguish institutional fund managers from private client fund managers.", "back": "Institutional fund managers invest for institutions (eg, pension funds, insurance companies) in the wholesale/professional sector; private client fund managers invest for relatively wealthy individuals in the retail sector. Institutional portfolios are usually larger."}, {"front": "What is a family office?", "back": "An organisation managing a large pool of assets for a wealthy family or group of families, sized between a private client and an institution."}, {"front": "How do fund managers typically charge clients?", "back": "A management fee based on a small percentage of fund value, plus potentially an incentive fee based on performance above a benchmark."}, {"front": "What are the core activities of a custodian bank?", "back": "Safekeeping of assets, arranging settlement, processing corporate actions (collecting dividends/interest), providing company information, managing cash and FX transactions, enabling access to foreign markets, and regular client reporting."}, {"front": "What has driven consolidation among custodian banks?", "back": "Competition has driven down custody charges, resulting in consolidation - the industry is now dominated by a small number of global custodians, some of which are bank divisions."}]}, {"id": "sec-m2", "number": 2, "title": "Equities & Bonds Fundamentals", "examWeight": "Core module — heavily examined, calculation-intensive", "summaryHtml": "\n<p><strong>Module 2 covers the building blocks of the securities markets: ordinary and preference shares, the core features of debt instruments, and the calculations that dominate this exam — yields, conversion premiums, accrued interest and present value.</strong> Expect several numeric questions.</p>\n<h4>Shares</h4>\n<p>Ordinary shareholders carry the greatest risk (maximum loss = 100% of purchase price + commission) but unlimited upside; they are paid last on liquidation and vote at AGMs/EGMs (directly or via a proxy). Preference shares rank above ordinary shares for dividends and capital but usually carry no vote. Variants: <strong>cumulative</strong> (unpaid dividends roll forward), <strong>participating</strong> (extra upside beyond the fixed dividend), <strong>redeemable</strong> (company can buy back — debt-like), <strong>convertible</strong> (right to convert into ordinary shares) and <strong>zero coupon preference shares</strong> (no dividend, redeem above issue price).</p>\n<h4>Equities vs bonds</h4>\n<p>Bondholders lend money, rank ahead of shareholders on insolvency, and have legal recourse if coupons/principal are unpaid. Equity holders own a stake, are repaid last, and have voting rights. Securities are held in <strong>registered</strong> form (register of ownership) or <strong>bearer</strong> form (possession = title), though most bearer securities are now immobilised in CSDs (Euroclear/Clearstream).</p>\n<h4>Debt instrument features</h4>\n<p><strong>Nominal (par) value</strong> = amount repaid at redemption. <strong>Coupon</strong> = interest rate, which may be <strong>fixed</strong>, <strong>floating-rate</strong> (reset against a reference such as SOFR — an FRN), or <strong>index-linked</strong> (coupon and principal adjusted for inflation, e.g. CPI/RPI).</p>\n<h4>Key formulas</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Flat (running/interest) yield</strong> = (Annual coupon / Market price) x 100. Ignores capital gain/loss to redemption and the time value of money.</li>\n<li><strong>Gross redemption yield (GRY/YTM)</strong> = the IRR of the bond's cash flows — the discount rate that equates the present value of coupons + redemption proceeds to the current price. Ignores tax.</li>\n<li><strong>Net redemption yield (NRY)</strong> = GRY using after-tax cash flows.</li>\n<li><strong>Modified duration</strong> = approximate % price change for a 1% change in interest rates. Price change = (Modified duration / 100) x price x (change in rates as a whole number). Lower coupon and longer maturity = higher (more volatile) modified duration.</li>\n<li><strong>Conversion premium (%)</strong> = ((Convertible bond price − Conversion value) / Conversion value) x 100, where Conversion value = shares receivable x share price.</li>\n<li><strong>Conversion ratio</strong> = Nominal value / Conversion price of shares.</li>\n<li><strong>Accrued interest</strong> = Coupon payment x (Days since last payment / Days between payments). Dirty (purchase) price = Clean (flat/quoted) price + accrued interest.</li>\n<li><strong>Present value (PV)</strong> of a sum receivable in n years at rate r = Sum / (1+r)^n. The price of a bond = the sum of the PVs of all its future cash flows; the GRY is the discount rate that makes that sum equal the market price.</li>\n<li><strong>Real interest rate</strong> (exact) = ((1 + nominal rate)/(1 + inflation rate)) − 1; rule-of-thumb approximation = nominal rate − inflation rate.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Spreads, the yield curve and inflation-linked instruments</h4>\n<p>A <strong>spread</strong> (in basis points, 1bp = 0.01%) measures the extra yield a bond offers over a benchmark — a comparable government bond, the swap rate, or SOFR (successor to LIBOR). The <strong>yield curve</strong> plots GRYs against maturity: normally upward-sloping (liquidity preference — investors demand more yield for less liquidity/longer maturity); occasionally <strong>inverted</strong> (short yields exceed long yields, signalling expected rate cuts/recession); occasionally <strong>negative</strong> (investors pay to preserve capital). <strong>Index-linked gilts</strong> and <strong>US TIPS</strong> adjust coupon and principal by an index ratio tied to RPI/CPI, protecting real returns; in deflation, some (US, France) have a deflation floor — UK gilts do not. <strong>STRIPS</strong> (Separate Trading of Registered Interest and Principal of Securities) split a bond into individual zero-coupon instruments (each coupon plus the principal), removing reinvestment risk — a 10-year US T-note strips into 21 securities (20 coupon strips + 1 principal strip).</p>\n", "sections": [{"heading": "2.1 Ordinary Shares and Preference Shares", "html": "\n<p>Ordinary (common) shareholders carry the greatest risk of any capital provider: on liquidation they are paid only after creditors, bondholders and preference shareholders. Their maximum loss is 100% of the purchase price plus commission; their maximum gain is theoretically unlimited. Dividends are proposed by the directors and ratified by shareholders at the AGM — there is no guarantee of payment. Shares usually carry one vote each (exercisable directly or via a <strong>proxy</strong>), though some companies issue separate classes (e.g. A/B/C shares) to separate ownership from control.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example — Alphabet Inc voting control (from the source text):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Class A shares: 5.817bn shares x 1 vote = 5.817bn votes (ticker GOOGL)</li>\n<li>Class B shares: 0.847bn shares x 10 votes = 8.47bn votes (not traded, held mainly by founders)</li>\n<li>Class C shares: 5.430bn shares x 0 votes = 0 votes (ticker GOOG)</li>\n<li>Total votes = 14.287bn</li>\n<li>Proportion of votes held by Class B (founders) = 8.47bn / 14.287bn x 100 = <strong>59.28%</strong> — control remains with the founders despite them holding a minority of the shares in issue.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Extra practice:</em> A company has Class A shares (10m shares, 1 vote each) and Class B shares (2m shares, 20 votes each). Total votes = 10m + 40m = 50m. Class B's proportion of votes = 40m / 50m x 100 = <strong>80%</strong>, despite Class B representing only 2m of the 12m shares in issue.</p>\n<p>Shares may be <strong>partly paid</strong> (issuer has not yet called the full nominal value) or <strong>fully paid</strong>. Most ordinary shares are <strong>registered</strong> (issuer maintains a register); the alternative is <strong>bearer</strong> form, where possession proves title.</p>\n<h4>Types of preference shares</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cumulative</strong> — unpaid dividends from prior years roll forward and must be paid before any ordinary dividend. Non-cumulative shares forfeit any unpaid dividend.</li>\n<li><strong>Participating</strong> — beyond the fixed dividend, holders can share in additional distributions (including on liquidation) alongside ordinary shareholders.</li>\n<li><strong>Redeemable</strong> — the company can buy the shares back at an agreed price/date; economically similar to debt.</li>\n<li><strong>Convertible</strong> — the holder has the right (not obligation) to convert into a predetermined number of ordinary shares.</li>\n<li><strong>Zero coupon preference shares</strong> — pay no dividend but redeem at a price above the issue price.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A single preference share can combine more than one of these features (e.g. cumulative and convertible).</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Ordinary shares</th><th>Preference shares</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Dividend</td><td>Variable, discretionary, can be nil</td><td>Fixed rate, paid before ordinary dividends</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Voting rights</td><td>Usually yes</td><td>Usually no</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ranking on liquidation</td><td>Last (residual claim)</td><td>Above ordinary, below debt</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Capital growth potential</td><td>Unlimited</td><td>Limited (unless convertible/participating)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Maximum loss</td><td>100% of price paid + commission</td><td>100% of price paid + commission</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Liquidity</td><td>Generally higher</td><td>Often lower, less actively traded</td></tr>\n</table>\n"}, {"heading": "2.2 Equities and Bonds — Key Differences", "html": "\n<p>Bonds are debt: the issuer borrows a sum of money in exchange for a bond, a contractual promise to pay interest (the coupon) and to repay the principal (nominal value) at a set maturity date.</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Bond investors</th><th>Equity investors</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Lend money to the issuer for an agreed rate of interest</td><td>Purchase a small ownership stake (a share) of a company</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Have an agreed date for repayment of principal</td><td>Cannot be certain the value of the company will increase</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Are paid ahead of shareholders on insolvency</td><td>Are repaid after bondholders/creditors on insolvency</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Have legal recourse if interest/principal is not paid</td><td>Cannot be certain of receiving any dividend</td></tr>\n<tr><td>No voting rights (generally)</td><td>Have voting rights to influence company decisions</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Can suffer loss but return is contractually defined</td><td>Can suffer a total loss of investment if the company collapses</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Registered vs bearer securities</h4>\n<p><strong>Registered</strong> securities: the issuer/registrar records the holder's ownership; transfer requires a change of entry in the register. <strong>Bearer</strong> securities: physical possession of the certificate is proof of ownership and they are transferred simply by handing them over. Bearer form makes ownership harder for authorities to monitor (money-laundering risk) and creates physical security costs, but most bearer instruments (e.g. eurobonds, ADRs) are now <strong>immobilised</strong> in central securities depositories (CSDs) such as Euroclear and Clearstream, removing the physical security concern and easing income payment.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "2.3 Features and Characteristics of Debt Instruments", "html": "\n<p>A bond is an IOU issued by a borrower (issuer) in return for money lent. Key terms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Nominal (par) value</strong> — the amount the issuer repays the holder at maturity.</li>\n<li><strong>Redemption/maturity date</strong> — the date the nominal value is repaid.</li>\n<li><strong>Coupon</strong> — the interest rate paid, usually a fixed % of nominal value (<strong>fixed interest</strong>).</li>\n<li><strong>Floating-rate notes (FRNs)</strong> — coupon is reset periodically against a reference rate (historically LIBOR, now commonly the Secured Overnight Financing Rate, SOFR).</li>\n<li><strong>Index-linked bonds</strong> — both coupon and principal repayment are adjusted for a published inflation index (e.g. CPI in the US, RPI for UK gilts).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The riskier the issuer (more likely to default on coupons or principal), the higher the coupon it must offer to attract investors. Major government issuers include US Treasuries, Japanese Government Bonds (JGBs), German bunds and UK gilts.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "2.4 Bond Yields — Flat Yield, GRY/YTM, NRY and Modified Duration", "html": "\n<h4>Flat (running/interest) yield</h4>\n<p>Formula: <strong>Flat yield = (Annual coupon / Price) x 100</strong>. It only reflects the coupon and ignores any capital gain/loss to redemption and the timing of cash flows.</p>\n<p><em>Worked example:</em> A 5% gilt redeeming in six years, priced at £104.40: (5 / 104.40) x 100 = <strong>4.79%</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Practice (from source):</em> (a) 4% gilt, redeeming in eight years, priced £98.90 → (4/98.90) x 100 = <strong>4.04%</strong>. (b) 7% gilt, redeeming in three years, priced £108.60 → (7/108.60) x 100 = <strong>6.45%</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Extra practice:</em> A 6% gilt priced at £120 → (6/120) x 100 = <strong>5.00%</strong>.</p>\n<p>Because coupons are fixed, when market interest rates rise, bond prices must fall for yields to rise to match — the classic inverse price/yield relationship.</p>\n<h4>Gross redemption yield (GRY) / yield to maturity (YTM)</h4>\n<p>The GRY is the bond's internal rate of return (IRR) — the single discount rate that equates the present value of all future cash flows (coupons + redemption proceeds) to the current price. It captures both income and capital gain/loss, but ignores tax ('gross'), making it especially relevant for non-taxpaying investors (pension funds, charities).</p>\n<p><em>Worked example:</em> A 5% US Treasury note, 5 years to maturity, priced at $115. $10,000 nominal costs $11,500. Annual coupon = $500, flat yield = (500/11,500) x 100 = 4.35%. At redemption only $10,000 is received back — a $1,500 loss over 5 years (~$300 p.a.). Approximating: net annual return ≈ $500 − $300 = $200, giving an approximate yield of (200/11,500) x 100 ≈ 1.74% — the true GRY (properly discounted) is closer to <strong>1.9%</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Comparison table (illustrates why flat yield alone is misleading):</em></p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Bond</th><th>Term</th><th>Price</th><th>Flat yield</th><th>GRY</th></tr>\n<tr><td>7.625% Treasury</td><td>5 years</td><td>114.56</td><td>6.66%</td><td>4.40%</td></tr>\n<tr><td>6.875% Treasury</td><td>5 years</td><td>110.72</td><td>6.21%</td><td>4.51%</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>The first bond looks better on flat yield alone, but the second offers the higher GRY and is the better overall return if held to maturity.</p>\n<h4>Net redemption yield (NRY)</h4>\n<p>Same concept as GRY but using after-tax cash flows — relevant for taxpaying investors holding to maturity.</p>\n<h4>Modified duration</h4>\n<p>Modified duration measures the approximate % change in a bond's price for a 1% change in interest rates. Higher modified duration = more price-volatile. All else equal: lower coupon = higher modified duration; longer maturity = higher modified duration.</p>\n<p><em>Worked example:</em> A government bond priced at $95.84 has modified duration 1.02. A 1 percentage point rise in rates → price falls by 1.02/100 x $95.84 = <strong>$0.98</strong>. A 0.5 percentage point rise → 1.02/100 x $95.84 x 0.5 = <strong>$0.49</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Extra practice:</em> A bond priced at $110 has modified duration 2.5. A 1% rise in rates → price falls by 2.5/100 x $110 = <strong>$2.75</strong>.</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Yield/measure</th><th>What it captures</th><th>Best used by</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Flat yield</td><td>Coupon only, ignores redemption gain/loss & timing</td><td>Short-term holders</td></tr>\n<tr><td>GRY / YTM</td><td>Coupon + capital gain/loss (IRR), gross of tax</td><td>Non-taxpayers holding to maturity (pension funds, charities)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>NRY</td><td>Coupon + capital gain/loss, after tax</td><td>Taxpaying investors holding to maturity</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Modified duration</td><td>Price sensitivity to a 1% rate change</td><td>Assessing interest rate risk/volatility</td></tr>\n</table>\n"}, {"heading": "2.5 Interest and Conversion Premium Calculations", "html": "\n<h4>Simple interest income on corporate debt</h4>\n<p>Interest income = Nominal value held x coupon rate (the current market price is irrelevant to this calculation).</p>\n<p><em>Worked example:</em> XYZ Inc bonds pay an 8% annual coupon, priced at 156 (investors pay $156 per $100 nominal). An investor buying $5,000 nominal pays $5,000 x 156/100 = $7,800. Annual interest income = $5,000 x 8% = <strong>$400</strong> (paid as $200 every six months if semi-annual). Flat yield = (8/156) x 100 = <strong>5.13%</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Extra practice:</em> An investor buys $8,000 nominal of a 6% bond priced at 120. Cost = $8,000 x 120/100 = $9,600. Annual interest = $8,000 x 6% = <strong>$480</strong>. Flat yield = (6/120) x 100 = <strong>5.00%</strong>.</p>\n<h4>Convertible bonds — conversion value and conversion premium</h4>\n<p>A convertible bond gives the holder the right (not obligation) to convert into a predetermined number of ordinary shares. Because it combines equity upside with bond downside protection, convertibles trade at a <strong>premium</strong> to their conversion (share) value.</p>\n<p><strong>Conversion premium (%) = ((Bond price − Conversion value) / Conversion value) x 100</strong>, where Conversion value = number of shares receivable x current share price.</p>\n<p><em>Worked example:</em> XYZ Inc convertible trades at $142.5 (i.e. $1,000 nominal costs $1,425). $1,000 nominal converts into 3 shares; shares trade at $380. Conversion value = 3 x $380 = $1,140. Premium (absolute) = $1,425 − $1,140 = $285 per $1,000 nominal. Premium (%) = (285/1,140) x 100 = <strong>25%</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Practice (from source):</em> ABC plc convertible trades at £110; £100 nominal converts into 15 ABC shares; shares trade at £6.40. Conversion value = 15 x £6.40 = £96. Premium = £110 − £96 = £14. Premium (%) = (14/96) x 100 = <strong>14.58%</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Extra practice:</em> A convertible bond trades at $130 per $100 nominal; $100 nominal converts into 20 shares; shares trade at $5.50. Conversion value = 20 x $5.50 = $110. Premium = $130 − $110 = $20. Premium (%) = (20/110) x 100 = <strong>18.18%</strong>.</p>\n<h4>Conversion ratio</h4>\n<p><strong>Conversion ratio = Nominal value / Conversion price of shares.</strong></p>\n<p><em>Worked example:</em> £100 nominal converts at £4.46 per share → conversion ratio = 100/4.46 = <strong>22.42 shares</strong>. A 1-for-1 bonus issue would halve the conversion price (to £2.23) and double the conversion ratio (to 44.84 shares).</p>\n<p><em>Extra practice:</em> £1,000 nominal, conversion price £5.00 → ratio = 1,000/5 = <strong>200 shares</strong>. Following a 1-for-1 bonus issue, the conversion price halves to £2.50, so the new ratio = <strong>400 shares</strong>.</p>\n<h4>Limitations of flat yield</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Ignores redemption gain/loss — misleading for bonds priced well above or below par.</li>\n<li>Ignores the timing of cash flows / time value of money (no discounting).</li>\n<li>For FRNs, the coupon varies, so choosing which coupon to use for the calculation is arbitrary.</li>\n</ul>\n"}, {"heading": "2.6 Accrued Interest and Day Count Conventions", "html": "\n<p>Quoted (listed) bond prices are <strong>clean/flat prices</strong> and exclude accrued interest. Between coupon dates, the price actually paid (the <strong>dirty price</strong>) is the clean price plus accrued interest.</p>\n<p><strong>Accrued interest = Coupon payment x (Days since last payment / Days between payments)</strong>. Dirty price = Clean price + Accrued interest.</p>\n<p><em>Worked example (from source):</em> Settlement 15 September; face value $1,000; nominal yield 8% paid semi-annually on 15 February and 15 August; listed (clean) price 100.25. Semi-annual coupon = $40. Days since last payment (15 Aug → 15 Sep) = 31. Days between coupons (15 Feb → 15 Aug) = 184. Accrued interest = $40 x (31/184) = <strong>$6.74</strong>. Clean price value = 100.25 x $1,000/100 = $1,002.50. Dirty (purchase) price = $1,002.50 + $6.74 = <strong>$1,009.24</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Extra practice:</em> Face value $1,000, 10% coupon paid semi-annually on 1 March and 1 September (184 days apart), clean price 101, settlement 1 June (92 days after 1 March). Semi-annual coupon = $50. Accrued interest = $50 x (92/184) = <strong>$25.00</strong>. Dirty price = $1,010.00 + $25.00 = <strong>$1,035.00</strong>.</p>\n<h4>Day count conventions</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Convention</th><th>Month treatment</th><th>Year length</th><th>Typical use</th></tr>\n<tr><td>ACT/360</td><td>Actual days</td><td>360 (always)</td><td>US Treasury bills, money market instruments</td></tr>\n<tr><td>30/360</td><td>Each month = 30 days</td><td>360 (always)</td><td>US agency/corporate issuers, eurobonds</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ACT/365</td><td>Actual days</td><td>365 (always, ignores leap years)</td><td>Various money markets</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ACT/ACT</td><td>Actual days</td><td>Actual (365 or 366)</td><td>US Treasuries, UK gilts</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Example: 1 February 2024 (leap year) to 1 April 2024 = 59 days under ACT/360 or ACT/365 (actual days but conventions differ on year base), 60 days under 30/360 and ACT/ACT (actual days including the extra leap day within the period).</p>\n<h4>Ex-dividend and cum-dividend</h4>\n<p>Shortly before a coupon date there is a short <strong>ex-dividend period</strong> (e.g. seven working days for most gilts) during which the bond trades without entitlement to the imminent coupon. At all other times the bond trades <strong>cum-dividend</strong>.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "2.7 Spreads and Pricing Benchmarks", "html": "\n<p>A <strong>spread</strong> is the difference between two yields, usually expressed in <strong>basis points (bps)</strong> — 1bp = 1/100 of 1% (0.01%). Spreads measure the relative risk of a bond versus a benchmark; the riskier the issuer, the wider the spread.</p>\n<p>Common benchmarks:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Government bond benchmark</strong> — typically the most recently issued government bond closest in maturity to the corporate bond (gilts in the UK, bunds in Europe, Treasuries in the US).</li>\n<li><strong>Swap rate benchmark</strong> — rates from the active fixed-for-floating swaps market.</li>\n<li><strong>Interbank offered rate benchmark</strong> — historically LIBOR, now largely replaced by rates such as SOFR.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Worked example:</em> UBX Inc bonds (≈10 years) yield 3.40%. Comparable 10-year government bonds yield 3.15%; the 10-year swap rate is 3.20%; SOFR is 2.28%.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Spread over government bonds = 3.40% − 3.15% = <strong>25bps</strong></li>\n<li>Spread over SOFR = 3.40% − 2.28% = <strong>112bps</strong></li>\n<li>Spread over swap rates = 3.40% − 3.20% = <strong>20bps</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Extra practice:</em> Corporate Bond X yields 5.75%. The 10-year government benchmark yields 4.95%; the swap rate is 5.10%. Spread over government bonds = 5.75% − 4.95% = <strong>80bps</strong>. Spread over swap rate = 5.75% − 5.10% = <strong>65bps</strong>.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "2.8 The Yield Curve", "html": "\n<p>Plotting the GRYs of government bonds of the same issuer against their time to maturity produces the <strong>yield curve</strong> — a visual representation of the <strong>term structure of interest rates</strong>. It is a key comparison tool: e.g. if ten-year gilts yield 4%, a ten-year corporate bond should yield more, to compensate for default risk.</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Shape</th><th>Description</th><th>Explanation / theory</th><th>Typical signal</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Normal (upward-sloping)</td><td>Longer maturities yield more than shorter ones</td><td>Liquidity preference theory — investors prefer liquidity and demand a higher yield to compensate for holding less liquid, longer-dated bonds</td><td>Normal/stable economic conditions</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Inverted (downward-sloping)</td><td>Short-term yields exceed long-term yields</td><td>Market expects a significant future fall in interest rates; investors accept lower yields on long bonds that will benefit from that fall</td><td>Often signals slowing growth/recession expectations</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Negative yields</td><td>Investors accept a yield below zero</td><td>Extreme preference for capital preservation/safety outweighs the desire for a positive return (seen in parts of Europe/Japan post-2008 financial crisis)</td><td>Very weak growth/deflation fears, aggressive monetary easing</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Normal yield curve</h4>\n<p>The typical, upward-sloping shape reflects investors' <strong>liquidity preference</strong> — they accept a lower yield for holding more liquid, shorter-dated bonds and demand a higher yield for less liquid, longer-dated bonds. Given the same coupon, a shorter-dated bond will therefore have a higher price (and lower yield) than a longer-dated one.</p>\n<h4>Inverted yield curve</h4>\n<p>Occasionally short-term yields exceed long-term yields. This happens when the market expects a significant fall in interest rates in future: investors are willing to accept lower yields on longer bonds (which will benefit from the anticipated rate fall) while demanding higher yields on short bonds. An inverted curve is often read as a signal of an expected economic slowdown or recession.</p>\n<h4>Negative yields</h4>\n<p>After the 2007–08 financial crisis, several central banks (Denmark, Sweden, Switzerland, Japan, the ECB) experimented with negative rates to encourage lending; some government bonds were priced to produce negative yields, with investors effectively paying governments to look after their capital. Example: in September 2021 the 1.70% coupon German bund maturing 2032 yielded −0.370%; by late summer 2025, with eurozone inflation near 2.1%, the same bond yielded a positive 2.72%. Negative rates raise concerns: they can push savers into cash, squeeze bank profitability, harm pension funds/insurers seeking positive returns to match liabilities, and encourage risky \"yield chasing\".</p>\n<h4>Inflation and the yield curve</h4>\n<p><strong>Nominal yields</strong> (from conventional bonds) embed investors' inflation expectations. <strong>Real yields</strong> (from index-linked bonds such as UK ILGs or US TIPS) already strip out an inflation uplift. The gap between nominal and real yields reveals the market's implied <strong>term structure of inflation</strong> expectations. Generally, rising inflation expectations push yields up; but pre-emptive central bank rate hikes can lower medium/long-dated yields if investors gain confidence inflation will be brought under control.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "2.9 Present Value (PV) of a Bond", "html": "\n<p>Money has a time value. <strong>PV of a sum receivable in n years at rate r = Sum / (1+r)^n.</strong> This process is called <strong>discounting</strong>, and r is the discount rate.</p>\n<p><em>Worked examples:</em> $100 receivable in 1 year at 5% → PV = 100/1.05 = <strong>$95.24</strong>. $100 receivable in 2 years at 5% → PV = 100/1.05² = 100/1.1025 = <strong>$90.70</strong>.</p>\n<h4>Price of a bond = sum of the PVs of its cash flows</h4>\n<p><em>Worked example:</em> $1,000 nominal, 2-year bond, 10% annual coupon, market interest rate 5%.</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Timing</th><th>Cash flow</th><th>PV calculation</th><th>PV</th></tr>\n<tr><td>End of year 1</td><td>$100 (coupon)</td><td>100/1.05</td><td>$95.24</td></tr>\n<tr><td>End of year 2</td><td>$1,100 (coupon + principal)</td><td>1,100/1.05²</td><td>$997.73</td></tr>\n<tr><td colspan=\"3\"><strong>Sum of PVs = price of the bond</strong></td><td><strong>$1,092.97</strong></td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>The 5% discount rate that produces this price given the known cash flows and market price of $1,092.97 is, by definition, the bond's <strong>gross redemption yield</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Practice (from source) — same $1,000 nominal, 2-year, 10% coupon bond at different rates:</em></p>\n<p>(a) At 6%: Year 1 PV = 100/1.06 = $94.34; Year 2 PV = 1,100/1.06² = 1,100/1.1236 = $979.07. Price = 94.34 + 979.07 = <strong>$1,073.41</strong>.</p>\n<p>(b) At 4%: Year 1 PV = 100/1.04 = $96.15; Year 2 PV = 1,100/1.04² = 1,100/1.0816 = $1,016.85. Price = 96.15 + 1,016.85 = <strong>$1,113.00</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note the pattern: as the discount rate (market interest rate) rises, the bond's price falls, and vice versa — again illustrating the inverse price/yield relationship.</p>\n<p><em>Extra practice:</em> $1,000 nominal, 2-year, 8% coupon bond, market rate 6%. Year 1 CF = $80, PV = 80/1.06 = $75.47. Year 2 CF = $1,080, PV = 1,080/1.06² = 1,080/1.1236 = $961.23. Price = 75.47 + 961.23 = <strong>$1,036.70</strong>.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "2.10 Government Bonds, Inflation-Protected Securities and STRIPS", "html": "\n<h4>Government bond interest and accrued interest</h4>\n<p>Government bonds specify a nominal (redemption) value and a coupon, typically paid in two semi-annual instalments. Example: $1,000 nominal of the US 3% Treasury Bond 2047 pays $30 p.a., split into two $15 payments (15 May and 15 November) until the $1,000 principal is repaid on 15 May 2047. Coupons go to the <strong>registered</strong> holder; shortly before each payment date the bond trades <strong>ex-dividend</strong> (no entitlement to the imminent coupon — seven working days for most gilts); otherwise it trades <strong>cum-dividend</strong>.</p>\n<p>US Treasuries are considered the <strong>risk-free rate</strong> for USD bonds (the government controls the currency and is credit risk-free); other bonds are priced at a margin above the risk-free rate to compensate for additional credit risk. There is an <strong>inverse relationship between interest rates and bond prices</strong>: as market interest rates fall, gilt/bond prices rise (and vice versa), because investors will pay more for a fixed coupon that now looks relatively attractive.</p>\n<h4>Index-linked bonds (UK ILGs, US TIPS)</h4>\n<p>Coupon and principal are adjusted for a published inflation index (RPI for UK gilts; CPI for US TIPS). An <strong>index ratio</strong> = reference index at the relevant date / reference index at issue. UK ILGs issued after 2005 use a three-month indexation lag; older issues used an eight-month lag.</p>\n<p><em>Worked example:</em> A 2% Index-Linked Gilt 2035 pays semi-annual coupons on 26 January and 26 June. An investor holding £20,000 nominal receives 2% x 6/12 x £20,000 x index ratio each half-year (i.e. £200 x index ratio).</p>\n<p><em>Extra practice:</em> If the reference RPI at issue was 260 and the reference RPI at a coupon date is 280, the index ratio = 280/260 = 1.0769. Coupon = £20,000 x 2% x 6/12 x 1.0769 = £200 x 1.0769 = <strong>£215.38</strong>.</p>\n<p>For US TIPS, the CPI adjusts the principal directly (up in inflation, down in deflation); the coupon = fixed rate x adjusted principal. At maturity, TIPS pay the greater of the adjusted principal and the original principal. Some sovereign index-linked bonds (US, France) have this <strong>deflation floor</strong> on redemption; UK index-linked gilts do <strong>not</strong> — they could redeem below nominal value in a sustained deflation.</p>\n<h4>Inflation measures</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>CPI (Consumer Price Index)</strong> — UK base year 2015 = 100; BoE MPC inflation target is 2%. Example: if the CPI basket costs £150 in 2025 (base 100 in 2015), prices have risen 50% over 10 years.</li>\n<li><strong>RPI (Retail Prices Index)</strong> — an older UK measure, still used for index-linked gilts (legacy prospectus terms); once published, RPI figures are never revised.</li>\n<li><strong>PPI (Producer Price Index)</strong> — measures prices earlier in the production process (input/\"wholesale\" prices and output/\"factory gate\" prices).</li>\n<li><strong>GDP deflator</strong> — a broader macroeconomic inflation measure used mainly by economists/organisations such as the IMF.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Real interest rates</h4>\n<p><strong>Real rate (exact) = ((1 + nominal rate)/(1 + inflation rate)) − 1.</strong> Approximation (rule of thumb): <strong>Real rate ≈ Nominal rate − Inflation rate.</strong></p>\n<p><em>Worked/extra practice:</em> Nominal rate 6%, inflation 4%: exact real rate = (1.06/1.04) − 1 = <strong>1.92%</strong>; approximate = 6% − 4% = <strong>2%</strong>. If inflation exceeds the nominal rate (e.g. nominal 7%, inflation 9%), the real rate turns negative: exact = (1.07/1.09) − 1 = <strong>−1.83%</strong>; approximate = 7% − 9% = <strong>−2%</strong> — savers are not being rewarded for saving.</p>\n<h4>STRIPS (Separate Trading of Registered Interest and Principal of Securities)</h4>\n<p>Stripping separates a bond's individual coupons and principal into separate tradeable zero-coupon securities. Each strip trades at a discount to its face value, with the discount size driven by prevailing rates and time to the strip's payment date. A 10-year US T-note (semi-annual coupons) strips into <strong>21 securities</strong>: 20 coupon strips + 1 principal strip. A 2-year semi-annual UK gilt strips into <strong>5 securities</strong> (4 coupon strips + 1 principal strip).</p>\n<p><em>Extra practice:</em> A 5-year semi-annual coupon-paying note would strip into 10 coupon strips + 1 principal strip = <strong>11 securities</strong>.</p>\n<p>Key advantage: STRIPS let investors precisely match future liabilities, eliminating reinvestment risk (unlike a coupon-paying bond, where coupons must be reinvested at uncertain future rates). In the UK, only gilts designated strippable by the Debt Management Office (DMO) are eligible, and only gilt-edged market makers (GEMMs), the Bank of England or HM Treasury may strip or reconstitute gilts (reconstitution = exchanging STRIPS back into a conventional gilt; the holder need not hold every individual strip for that gilt to reconstitute).</p>\n<p><strong>Zero coupon bonds (ZCBs)</strong>, also called pure discount bonds or \"zeros\", pay no interest; the entire return comes from the difference between the discounted purchase price and the redemption (nominal) value.</p>\n"}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What is the maximum loss an ordinary shareholder can suffer if the company they invested in fails?", "options": ["50% of the amount invested", "100% of the purchase price plus commission paid", "The nominal value only", "There is no maximum, losses are unlimited"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "An ordinary shareholder's liability is limited to what they paid for the shares plus any commission paid on purchase — the maximum loss is 100% of that amount, while the potential gain is theoretically unlimited."}, {"question": "A preference shareholder is entitled to receive both this year's dividend and any dividends unpaid from previous years before ordinary shareholders are paid anything. What type of preference share is this?", "options": ["Participating preference share", "Redeemable preference share", "Cumulative preference share", "Zero coupon preference share"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "A cumulative preference share carries forward any unpaid dividends from prior years, all of which must be paid (along with the current year's dividend) before ordinary shareholders receive a dividend."}, {"question": "Which type of preference share allows the holder to benefit from additional distributions beyond the fixed dividend, effectively also sharing in profits like an ordinary shareholder?", "options": ["Cumulative preference share", "Participating preference share", "Convertible preference share", "Redeemable preference share"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Participating preference shares entitle the holder to additional distributions (including on liquidation) beyond the fixed preference dividend, once other preferred shareholders have been paid."}, {"question": "From the issuing company's perspective, which type of preference share is most similar in character to debt?", "options": ["Convertible preference share", "Participating preference share", "Redeemable preference share", "Cumulative preference share"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Redeemable preference shares can be bought back by the company at an agreed price and date, similar to how debt is repaid, removing the firm's ongoing obligation to those shareholders."}, {"question": "A preference share pays no dividend at all but redeems at a price above its issue price. What is this called?", "options": ["Cumulative preference share", "Zero coupon preference share", "Participating preference share", "Convertible preference share"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Zero coupon preference shares pay no dividend; the investor's entire return comes from the redemption price being set above the issue price."}, {"question": "In the event of a company's insolvency, in which order are claims typically settled?", "options": ["Ordinary shareholders, then bondholders, then preference shareholders", "Bondholders/creditors, then preference shareholders, then ordinary shareholders", "Preference shareholders, then bondholders, then ordinary shareholders", "All classes are paid pro-rata simultaneously"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Bondholders and other creditors are repaid first, followed by preference shareholders, with ordinary shareholders receiving only what remains — the residual claim."}, {"question": "Which statement correctly distinguishes bearer securities from registered securities?", "options": ["Bearer securities require entry in an issuer's register to transfer ownership", "Registered securities are transferred simply by handing over the certificate", "For bearer securities, physical possession of the certificate is proof of ownership", "Registered securities cannot be held in a CSD"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "With bearer securities, whoever holds (bears) the certificate has title to it, like a banknote; registered securities instead require a change of entry in the issuer's register to transfer ownership."}, {"question": "What is the nominal (par) value of a bond?", "options": ["The current market price of the bond", "The amount the issuer will repay the holder at the redemption/maturity date", "The annual coupon payment in currency terms", "The price the bond was first issued at"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The nominal or par value is the amount the borrower agrees to repay the bondholder when the bond matures (is redeemed), regardless of the price at which it currently trades."}, {"question": "A floating-rate note (FRN) resets its coupon periodically with reference to which type of benchmark?", "options": ["A fixed percentage set at issue that never changes", "A published reference rate such as SOFR", "The issuer's share price", "The consumer prices index (CPI)"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "FRNs reset their coupon against a published reference interest rate, historically LIBOR and now more commonly SOFR (Secured Overnight Financing Rate)."}, {"question": "A 5% gilt is redeeming in six years and is priced at £104.40. What is its flat yield?", "options": ["4.79%", "5.00%", "5.21%", "4.40%"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Flat yield = (Annual coupon / Price) x 100 = (5 / 104.40) x 100 = 4.79%."}, {"question": "A 7% gilt redeeming in three years is priced at £108.60. What is its flat yield (to two decimal places)?", "options": ["7.00%", "6.45%", "6.89%", "7.66%"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Flat yield = (7 / 108.60) x 100 = 6.45%."}, {"question": "Which measure of bond return takes into account both the coupon income and any capital gain or loss to redemption, but ignores the effect of taxation?", "options": ["Flat yield", "Net redemption yield", "Gross redemption yield (GRY)", "Modified duration"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The GRY (also called yield to maturity) is the bond's internal rate of return, capturing both income and capital gain/loss, but it is quoted gross of tax."}, {"question": "Why is the gross redemption yield especially relevant to pension funds and charities?", "options": ["Because they are exempt from paying tax, so the gross figure is directly relevant to them", "Because they only hold bonds for very short periods", "Because they cannot use present value techniques", "Because they only invest in floating-rate notes"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "As non-taxpaying long-term investors, pension funds and charities are not affected by the fact that GRY ignores tax, making it a directly relevant and accurate measure of their expected return."}, {"question": "A government bond is priced at $95.84 and has a modified duration of 1.02. If interest rates rise by one percentage point, what is the approximate change in the bond's price?", "options": ["Falls by $1.02", "Rises by $0.98", "Falls by $0.98", "Falls by $9.78"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Price change = (Modified duration / 100) x Price = (1.02 / 100) x $95.84 = $0.98. Since interest rates rose, the bond's price falls by $0.98."}, {"question": "Using the same bond (price $95.84, modified duration 1.02), what is the approximate price change if interest rates rise by only half a percentage point (0.5%)?", "options": ["$0.98", "$1.96", "$0.49", "$4.89"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Price change = 1.02/100 x $95.84 x 0.5 = $0.49, since the rate change is half of one percentage point."}, {"question": "All else being equal, which bond will show the greatest price volatility (highest modified duration) for a given change in interest rates?", "options": ["A short-dated, high-coupon bond", "A long-dated, low-coupon bond", "A long-dated, high-coupon bond", "A short-dated, low-coupon bond"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Lower-coupon and longer-dated bonds are more sensitive (more volatile) to a given change in interest rates, i.e. they have a higher modified duration."}, {"question": "XYZ Inc bonds pay an 8% annual coupon and are priced at 156. An investor buys $5,000 nominal value. What is the investor's annual interest income?", "options": ["$400", "$624", "$320", "$500"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Interest income = nominal value x coupon rate = $5,000 x 8% = $400. The current market price (156) does not affect the interest income calculation."}, {"question": "A convertible bond issued by XYZ Inc trades at $142.5 (i.e. $1,000 nominal costs $1,425). $1,000 nominal converts into 3 shares, and XYZ shares trade at $380. What is the conversion premium, expressed as a percentage?", "options": ["20%", "25%", "15%", "30%"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Conversion value = 3 x $380 = $1,140. Premium = $1,425 - $1,140 = $285. Premium % = (285/1,140) x 100 = 25%."}, {"question": "ABC plc's convertible bonds trade at £110. Each £100 nominal converts into 15 ordinary shares, currently trading at £6.40. What is the conversion premium, expressed as a percentage?", "options": ["12.50%", "20.00%", "14.58%", "10.00%"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Conversion value = 15 x £6.40 = £96. Premium = £110 - £96 = £14. Premium % = (14/96) x 100 = 14.58%."}, {"question": "£100 nominal value of a convertible bond can convert into shares at £4.46 each. What is the conversion ratio?", "options": ["4.46 shares", "44.60 shares", "22.42 shares", "100 shares"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Conversion ratio = Nominal value / Conversion price = £100 / £4.46 = 22.42 shares."}, {"question": "A company with convertible bonds in issue undertakes a 1-for-1 bonus issue of ordinary shares. What is the effect on the conversion price and conversion ratio?", "options": ["Conversion price doubles, conversion ratio halves", "Conversion price halves, conversion ratio doubles", "Neither the conversion price nor the ratio changes", "Conversion price and ratio both double"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A 1-for-1 bonus issue doubles the number of shares in issue and roughly halves the share price, so the conversion price is adjusted down by half, which doubles the conversion ratio."}, {"question": "Why does a convertible bond generally trade at a premium to the value of the shares it can convert into?", "options": ["Because convertible bonds always have higher coupons than ordinary bonds", "Because it contains an embedded option of value (upside from conversion plus downside protection from redemption value)", "Because convertible bonds are always issued at a discount to par", "Because conversion rights expire after one year"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The conversion right is effectively an embedded option: the holder gets upside if the shares perform well and downside protection (redemption value) if they do not — this optionality has value, reflected in the premium. Without a premium there would be a risk-free arbitrage."}, {"question": "A corporate bond has a face value of $1,000, pays interest semi-annually on 15 February and 15 August at a nominal yield of 8%, and settles on 15 September at a listed (clean) price of 100.25. What is the accrued interest?", "options": ["$3.37", "$13.48", "$6.74", "$40.00"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Semi-annual coupon = $40. Days since last payment (15 Aug to 15 Sep) = 31. Days between coupons (15 Feb to 15 Aug) = 184. Accrued interest = $40 x (31/184) = $6.74."}, {"question": "Using the same bond as above (clean price 100.25, accrued interest $6.74), what is the dirty (purchase) price the investor must pay?", "options": ["$1,002.50", "$1,006.74", "$1,009.24", "$1,040.00"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Dirty price = Clean price value + Accrued interest = ($100.25 x $1,000/100) + $6.74 = $1,002.50 + $6.74 = $1,009.24."}, {"question": "Which day count convention is typically used for UK gilts and US Treasuries, counting both the actual number of days between coupon payments and the actual number of days in the year (365 or 366)?", "options": ["30/360", "ACT/360", "ACT/365", "ACT/ACT"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "ACT/ACT counts the actual number of days elapsed and the actual number of days in the year, so leap years affect the calculation. It is used for UK gilts and US Treasuries."}, {"question": "Which day count convention treats every month as having 30 days and the year as always having 360 days, and is commonly used for US agency/corporate bonds and eurobonds?", "options": ["ACT/ACT", "ACT/365", "30/360", "ACT/360"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The 30/360 convention assumes every month has 30 days and the year has 360 days, chosen for simplicity and predictable, regular payment amounts."}, {"question": "What is the difference between a bond's 'clean' (flat) price and its 'dirty' price?", "options": ["The clean price includes accrued interest, the dirty price does not", "The dirty price is the clean price plus accrued interest", "There is no difference, the terms are interchangeable", "The dirty price only applies to floating-rate notes"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The clean/flat price is the quoted price excluding accrued interest; the dirty price (what the buyer actually pays) equals the clean price plus accrued interest."}, {"question": "UBX Inc bonds (about 10 years to maturity) yield 3.40%. Comparable 10-year government bonds yield 3.15%. What is the spread of UBX bonds over the government bond benchmark, in basis points?", "options": ["3.40 basis points", "25 basis points", "315 basis points", "0.25 basis points"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Spread = 3.40% - 3.15% = 0.25%, which equals 25 basis points (1 basis point = 0.01%)."}, {"question": "Corporate Bond X yields 5.75% and the relevant swap rate is 5.10%. What is Bond X's spread over the swap rate?", "options": ["55 basis points", "65 basis points", "5.10 basis points", "110 basis points"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Spread = 5.75% - 5.10% = 0.65%, equivalent to 65 basis points."}, {"question": "What does an upward-sloping (normal) yield curve primarily reflect?", "options": ["Investors' expectation of an imminent recession", "Investors' liquidity preference — demanding higher yields for less liquid, longer-dated bonds", "A guarantee that short-term rates will fall", "The fact that all bonds carry identical credit risk"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The normal, upward-sloping yield curve reflects liquidity preference theory: investors prefer more liquid, shorter-dated bonds and require a higher yield to compensate for holding less liquid, longer-dated bonds."}, {"question": "What does an inverted yield curve typically signal?", "options": ["An expectation that interest rates will rise significantly in future", "An expectation that interest rates will fall significantly in future, often associated with a forecast economic slowdown", "That government bonds have become risk-free", "That the bond in question has defaulted"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "An inverted yield curve arises when the market expects a significant future fall in interest rates; investors accept lower yields on longer bonds that will benefit from that future fall, and this is often read as signalling weaker economic conditions ahead."}, {"question": "Why might an investor be willing to buy a government bond priced to produce a negative yield?", "options": ["Because negative yields guarantee the highest possible return", "Because the investor prioritises capital preservation over generating a positive return", "Because negative yields are only available on corporate bonds", "Because it eliminates all credit risk from the investment"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "In periods of extreme uncertainty, some investors are willing to accept a small loss (negative yield) in order to keep their capital safe with a very secure borrower such as a government, prioritising capital preservation over return."}, {"question": "What is $500 receivable in two years' time worth today (its present value), given an annual interest rate of 4%?", "options": ["$480.77", "$462.28", "$500.00", "$416.67"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "PV = 500 / (1.04)^2 = 500 / 1.0816 = $462.28."}, {"question": "A $1,000 nominal, 2-year bond pays an annual coupon of 10%. Using a market interest rate of 5%, what is the present value (price) of the bond?", "options": ["$1,000.00", "$1,073.41", "$1,092.97", "$1,113.00"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "PV of year 1 cash flow ($100) at 5% = $95.24; PV of year 2 cash flow ($1,100) at 5% = $997.73. Sum = $1,092.97, which is the bond's price."}, {"question": "In the present value calculation for a bond, what does the discount rate that equates the sum of discounted cash flows to the bond's current market price represent?", "options": ["The bond's flat yield", "The bond's coupon rate", "The bond's gross redemption yield (GRY)", "The bond's modified duration"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The GRY is, by definition, the internal rate of return (discount rate) that makes the present value of all future cash flows equal to the bond's current market price."}, {"question": "If the nominal interest rate is 6% and the inflation rate is 4%, what is the approximate real interest rate using the rule-of-thumb method?", "options": ["10%", "2%", "1.5%", "24%"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The rule-of-thumb approximation is Real rate ≈ Nominal rate - Inflation rate = 6% - 4% = 2%."}, {"question": "If the nominal interest rate is 7% and inflation is 9%, what does this imply for savers?", "options": ["Savers are earning a healthy positive real return", "The real interest rate is negative, so savers are not being rewarded for saving", "The nominal rate is irrelevant to savers", "Inflation has no effect since the nominal rate is positive"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "When inflation exceeds the nominal rate, the real interest rate is negative (approximately 7% - 9% = -2%), meaning the real value of savings is being eroded rather than rewarded."}, {"question": "How are the coupon and principal of a US Treasury Inflation-Protected Security (TIPS) adjusted for inflation?", "options": ["Only the coupon rate changes; the principal remains fixed at par", "The principal is adjusted by the CPI, and the coupon is the fixed rate applied to the adjusted principal", "TIPS pay no coupon at all, only an inflation-linked redemption amount", "The coupon rate itself changes every year in line with CPI"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "With US TIPS, the CPI adjusts the outstanding principal (up in inflation, down in deflation); the coupon paid is then the fixed coupon rate multiplied by that adjusted principal."}, {"question": "What is a key difference between UK index-linked gilts and some other sovereign index-linked bonds (e.g. in the US and France) regarding a fall in prices (deflation)?", "options": ["UK gilts have a deflation floor guaranteeing repayment of at least par value; US/French bonds do not", "US and French index-linked bonds have a deflation floor guaranteeing at least par value at redemption; UK gilts do not", "Both UK gilts and US/French bonds always guarantee at least par value", "Deflation floors apply only to conventional (non-index-linked) bonds"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Some sovereign index-linked bonds, such as those issued by the US and France, have a deflation floor guaranteeing redemption at no less than par value. UK index-linked gilts have no such floor and could, in theory, redeem below nominal value in a period of sustained deflation."}, {"question": "What does STRIPS stand for?", "options": ["Standard Trading of Registered Interest Payment Securities", "Separate Trading of Registered Interest and Principal of Securities", "Structured Trading of Repayable Investment Principal Securities", "Selective Trading of Retail Investment Principal Securities"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "STRIPS is an acronym for Separate Trading of Registered Interest and Principal of Securities — the process of splitting a bond's coupons and principal into individually tradeable zero-coupon securities."}, {"question": "A ten-year US Treasury note paying semi-annual coupons is fully stripped. How many separate securities (strips) are created?", "options": ["10", "20", "21", "22"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "A 10-year note has 20 semi-annual coupon payments plus one principal repayment, so stripping creates 21 separate securities: 20 coupon strips and 1 principal strip."}, {"question": "What is the main advantage of STRIPS for an investor seeking to match a specific future liability, such as the repayment of a mortgage principal in five years?", "options": ["STRIPS pay a higher coupon than conventional bonds", "STRIPS eliminate reinvestment risk because there are no coupons to reinvest", "STRIPS are always cheaper to buy than conventional bonds of the same maturity", "STRIPS carry no credit risk regardless of the issuer"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Because a STRIP pays a single lump sum at a known future date with no interim coupons to reinvest at uncertain rates, it precisely matches a known future liability and removes reinvestment risk — unlike a coupon-paying bond."}, {"question": "Where does the entire return on a zero coupon bond (ZCB) come from?", "options": ["Regular coupon payments made throughout its life", "The difference between the discounted price paid and the value received at redemption", "Dividends declared by the issuer", "Currency movements only"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A ZCB pays no interest; it is bought at a discount to its face (nominal) value, and the investor's entire return is the difference between the discounted purchase price and the amount received at redemption."}, {"question": "Why is the yield on US Treasuries generally considered the 'risk-free rate' for dollar-denominated bonds?", "options": ["Because Treasuries always offer the highest yield in the market", "Because the US government controls the printing of dollars, making Treasuries effectively free of credit (default) risk", "Because Treasuries are exempt from all forms of interest rate risk", "Because Treasuries are not traded on secondary markets"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Since the US government ultimately controls the issuance of its own currency, US Treasuries are considered effectively free of credit/default risk, making their yield the benchmark risk-free rate for USD instruments."}, {"question": "In the UK, which measure of consumer price inflation is still used to calculate the inflation uplift on legacy index-linked gilts, despite CPI being the Bank of England's preferred target measure?", "options": ["The Producer Price Index (PPI)", "The GDP deflator", "The Retail Prices Index (RPI)", "The Consumer Price Index (CPI)"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "RPI continues to be used for UK index-linked gilts because it was specified in the prospectuses of older issues and industry consultation favoured its continued use, even though CPI is now the BoE's targeted inflation measure."}], "flashcards": [{"front": "What is the nominal (par) value of a bond?", "back": "The amount the issuer will repay the holder at the redemption/maturity date; also the base on which the coupon percentage is calculated."}, {"front": "What is a bond's coupon?", "back": "The interest rate the issuer pays the bondholder, usually expressed as a fixed percentage of nominal value; can also be floating-rate or index-linked."}, {"front": "What is a floating-rate note (FRN)?", "back": "A bond whose coupon resets periodically against a published reference rate (e.g. SOFR, historically LIBOR)."}, {"front": "What is an index-linked bond?", "back": "A bond where both the coupon and the principal repayment are adjusted in line with a published inflation index (e.g. RPI for UK gilts, CPI for US TIPS)."}, {"front": "Cumulative preference share — definition", "back": "A preference share where any unpaid dividends from previous years roll forward and must be paid, along with the current year's dividend, before ordinary shareholders receive anything."}, {"front": "Participating preference share — definition", "back": "A preference share entitling the holder to additional distributions beyond the fixed dividend (including on liquidation), effectively also sharing in profits like an ordinary shareholder."}, {"front": "Redeemable preference share — definition", "back": "A preference share the company can buy back from the holder at an agreed price/date; economically similar to debt from the issuer's perspective."}, {"front": "Convertible preference share — definition", "back": "A preference share giving the holder the right (not obligation) to convert into a predetermined number of ordinary shares."}, {"front": "Zero coupon preference share — definition", "back": "A preference share that pays no dividend but redeems at a price above its issue price, with the uplift providing the return."}, {"front": "Ordinary shareholder — maximum loss and maximum gain", "back": "Maximum loss = 100% of the purchase price plus commission paid. Maximum gain = theoretically unlimited."}, {"front": "Bearer securities vs registered securities", "back": "Bearer: physical possession of the certificate is proof of ownership, easily transferred by handing it over. Registered: the issuer maintains a register recording the holder's ownership; transfer requires a change of register entry."}, {"front": "Flat (running/interest) yield — formula", "back": "Flat yield = (Annual coupon / Market price) x 100. Ignores capital gain/loss to redemption and the time value of money."}, {"front": "Gross redemption yield (GRY) / yield to maturity (YTM) — definition", "back": "The internal rate of return (IRR) of a bond's cash flows — the single discount rate that equates the PV of all future coupons and redemption proceeds to the current price. Ignores tax."}, {"front": "Net redemption yield (NRY) — definition", "back": "Same concept as GRY but calculated using after-tax cash flows; relevant to taxpaying investors holding to maturity."}, {"front": "Modified duration — definition", "back": "A measure of a bond's price sensitivity: the approximate % change in price for a 1% change in interest rates. Higher modified duration = more volatile price."}, {"front": "Modified duration — price change formula", "back": "Approximate price change = (Modified duration / 100) x Bond price x (change in interest rate, as a decimal/whole number of percentage points)."}, {"front": "Which bonds have the highest modified duration (most price-volatile), all else equal?", "back": "Lower-coupon bonds and longer-dated (longer maturity) bonds are more volatile — they have higher modified duration — than higher-coupon or shorter-dated bonds."}, {"front": "Conversion value of a convertible bond — formula", "back": "Conversion value = Number of shares receivable on conversion x Current share price."}, {"front": "Conversion premium — formula", "back": "Conversion premium (%) = ((Convertible bond price − Conversion value) / Conversion value) x 100."}, {"front": "Conversion ratio — formula", "back": "Conversion ratio = Nominal value / Conversion price of shares."}, {"front": "Effect of a 1-for-1 bonus issue on a convertible bond's conversion terms", "back": "The conversion price halves and the conversion ratio doubles (the number of shares each unit of nominal value converts into)."}, {"front": "Why do convertible bonds trade at a premium to their conversion value?", "back": "Because the conversion right is an embedded option — upside if shares perform well, downside protection via redemption value if they do not. Without a premium there would be a risk-free arbitrage opportunity."}, {"front": "Accrued interest — formula", "back": "Accrued interest = Coupon payment x (Number of days since last payment / Number of days between payments)."}, {"front": "Clean (flat) price vs dirty price", "back": "Clean/flat price = the quoted price excluding accrued interest. Dirty price = clean price + accrued interest = the actual amount the buyer pays."}, {"front": "Ex-dividend period — definition (gilts)", "back": "A short period (typically seven working days) before a coupon payment date during which the bond trades without entitlement to the imminent coupon. Outside this period the bond trades cum-dividend."}, {"front": "ACT/360 day count convention", "back": "Actual number of days between dates, but the year is always assumed to be 360 days (even in leap years). Used for US Treasury bills and money market instruments."}, {"front": "30/360 day count convention", "back": "Every month is treated as having 30 days; the year is always 360 days. Used by US agency/corporate issuers and eurobonds for simplicity/predictability."}, {"front": "ACT/365 day count convention", "back": "Actual number of days between dates, but the year is always assumed to be 365 days, regardless of leap years."}, {"front": "ACT/ACT day count convention", "back": "Actual number of days between dates and the actual number of days in the year (365 or 366); leap years affect the result. Used for US Treasuries and UK gilts."}, {"front": "Spread — definition", "back": "The difference between two yields, usually expressed in basis points (1bp = 1/100 of 1% = 0.01%); it measures the relative risk of a bond versus a benchmark."}, {"front": "Government bond benchmark for corporate bond spreads", "back": "Typically the most recently issued government bond closest in maturity to the corporate bond — gilts (UK), bunds (Europe), Treasuries (US)."}, {"front": "Swap rate benchmark for spreads", "back": "Rates from the actively traded fixed-for-floating interest rate swaps market, used as an alternative benchmark to compare bond yields against."}, {"front": "What replaced LIBOR as a key interbank reference rate benchmark?", "back": "SOFR — the Secured Overnight Financing Rate — is now commonly used in place of LIBOR."}, {"front": "Normal (upward-sloping) yield curve — explanation", "back": "Reflects investors' liquidity preference: they demand a lower yield for holding more liquid short-dated bonds and a higher yield for less liquid, longer-dated bonds."}, {"front": "Inverted yield curve — what does it signal?", "back": "Short-term yields exceed long-term yields, implying the market expects a significant future fall in interest rates — often read as a signal of an expected economic slowdown/recession."}, {"front": "Why might investors accept negative bond yields?", "back": "In periods of extreme uncertainty, investors may prioritise capital preservation over generating a positive return, accepting a small guaranteed loss to keep funds safe with a very secure borrower (e.g. a government)."}, {"front": "Present value (PV) — formula", "back": "PV of a sum receivable in n years at rate r = Sum / (1+r)^n. The process is called discounting; r is the discount rate."}, {"front": "How is the price of a bond derived using present value techniques?", "back": "The price of a bond equals the sum of the present values of all its future cash flows (coupons and redemption proceeds), each discounted at the prevailing market interest rate."}, {"front": "How does the GRY relate to present value calculations?", "back": "The GRY is the discount rate that, when applied to a bond's future cash flows, produces a present value exactly equal to its current market price — it is the bond's internal rate of return (IRR)."}, {"front": "Real interest rate — exact formula", "back": "Real rate = ((1 + Nominal rate) / (1 + Inflation rate)) − 1."}, {"front": "Real interest rate — rule-of-thumb approximation", "back": "Real rate ≈ Nominal rate − Inflation rate."}, {"front": "What does a negative real interest rate mean for savers?", "back": "It means inflation exceeds the nominal interest rate, so savers are not being rewarded for saving — the real (inflation-adjusted) value of their money falls over time."}, {"front": "UK CPI — base year and BoE target", "back": "UK CPI base year is 2015 (index = 100); the Bank of England's Monetary Policy Committee targets CPI inflation of 2%."}, {"front": "RPI vs CPI in the UK", "back": "RPI (Retail Prices Index) is an older measure, once published never revised, still used for legacy UK index-linked gilts. CPI (Consumer Price Index) is the BoE's current targeted inflation measure."}, {"front": "Producer Price Index (PPI) — definition", "back": "Measures inflation earlier in the production process; includes input price indices (raw materials/'wholesale prices') and output/factory gate indices (prices as goods leave production for retail)."}, {"front": "Index ratio for an index-linked gilt — formula", "back": "Index ratio = Reference RPI applicable to a given date / Reference RPI applicable to the gilt's original issue date. Used to adjust the coupon and redemption payment for inflation."}, {"front": "How does a US TIPS adjust for inflation?", "back": "The CPI adjusts the outstanding principal (up in inflation, down in deflation); the coupon paid equals the fixed coupon rate applied to that adjusted principal. At maturity, the investor receives the greater of the adjusted or original principal."}, {"front": "Deflation floor on index-linked bonds — which markets have it?", "back": "Some sovereign index-linked bonds (e.g. US TIPS, French OATi) guarantee redemption at no less than par value (a deflation floor). UK index-linked gilts have no such floor."}, {"front": "STRIPS — what does the acronym stand for?", "back": "Separate Trading of Registered Interest and Principal of Securities — splitting a bond's coupons and principal into individually tradeable zero-coupon securities."}, {"front": "How many strips result from a 10-year US Treasury note with semi-annual coupons?", "back": "21 securities: 20 individual coupon strips plus 1 principal strip."}, {"front": "Key advantage of STRIPS for liability matching", "back": "STRIPS let an investor precisely match a known future liability with a single maturing lump sum, eliminating the reinvestment risk associated with reinvesting coupons from a conventional bond."}, {"front": "Who is permitted to strip or reconstitute UK gilts?", "back": "Only gilt-edged market makers (GEMMs), the Bank of England, or HM Treasury; only gilts designated 'strippable' by the Debt Management Office (DMO) are eligible."}, {"front": "Zero coupon bond (ZCB) — how does it generate a return?", "back": "A ZCB pays no coupons; it is bought at a discount to its face value, and the entire return is the difference between the discounted purchase price and the amount received at redemption."}, {"front": "Why are US Treasuries considered the 'risk-free rate' for USD bonds?", "back": "Because the US government controls the issuance of its own currency, US Treasuries are effectively free of credit (default) risk, making their yield the benchmark risk-free rate against which other USD bonds are priced with a credit spread."}, {"front": "Relationship between interest rates and bond prices", "back": "There is an inverse relationship: when interest rates rise, bond prices fall; when interest rates fall, bond prices rise (since coupons are typically fixed, price must adjust for the yield to stay competitive)."}]}, {"id": "sec-m3", "number": 3, "title": "Debt Instruments, Money Markets & Credit Ratings", "examWeight": "Core module", "summaryHtml": "<p>Corporate debt can be <strong>secured</strong> or <strong>unsecured</strong>. Secured debt (a UK <em>debenture</em>) gives bondholders a claim over company assets via a <strong>fixed charge</strong> (a specific asset, e.g. a building) or a <strong>floating charge</strong> (a class of assets that <em>crystallises</em> over whatever is available on default). Asset-backed securities (ABS) and mortgage-backed securities (MBS) pool receivables/mortgages and pass interest and principal through to investors in tranches, usually via a bankruptcy-remote <strong>special purpose vehicle (SPV)</strong> that takes assets off the originator's balance sheet. <strong>Covered bonds</strong> are similar but the assets stay on the issuing bank's balance sheet (no SPV) and continue paying to schedule even after issuer default, provided collateral is sufficient. A <strong>trustee</strong> (bank or trust company, paid by the issuer but acting for bondholders) enforces the trust deed, monitors covenants, and provides unified, cost-effective enforcement. Debt ranks in tiers: <strong>senior</strong> (lowest risk/rate, often secured), <strong>subordinated</strong> (ranks behind senior), and <strong>mezzanine</strong> (highest risk, may carry payment-in-kind (PIK) interest and equity warrants). Unsecured debt has no asset backing, so pays a higher rate; variants include subordinated, guaranteed (backed by a third party, typically the parent), and convertible bonds (right to convert into shares, usually at a lower coupon). Floating-rate notes (FRNs) pay a margin (in basis points) over a reference rate such as SONIA (UK) or SOFR (US), which replaced LIBOR after the rate-rigging scandal.</p><p><strong>Credit ratings</strong> from S&amp;P, Moody's and Fitch assess default risk. Ratings from AAA/Aaa down to BBB-/Baa3 are <strong>investment grade</strong>; BB+/Ba1 and below are <strong>non-investment grade</strong>, also called <strong>high-yield</strong> or <strong>junk</strong>. Lower ratings mean higher coupons and lower prices for existing bonds if downgraded. Credit enhancement (e.g. monoline insurance) can lift a rating, but ratings are a lagging indicator and were criticised after the 2007-08 crisis for being too generous on structured products.</p><p>The <strong>money market</strong> covers cash and instruments with under one year to maturity. <strong>Cash deposits</strong> pay interest only (no capital growth); fixed-term/notice accounts pay more than instant-access or current accounts; gross interest is pre-tax, net is post-tax; deposit protection schemes cover losses up to a set limit. <strong>Treasury bills (T-bills)</strong> are government-issued, 1-12 month, zero-coupon discount instruments — the investor's return is simply the discount to par. <strong>Commercial paper (CP)</strong> is the corporate/unsecured equivalent, issued at a discount, maturing up to 270 days (US) or one year (Europe), sold as direct paper or via dealers, and carrying refinancing (rollover) risk since it is continually reissued. <strong>Repo</strong> transactions are a sale-and-agreed-repurchase of a security (e.g. a gilt), economically a secured loan; the difference between sale and repurchase price is the repo rate. The buyer's position is a reverse repo. The UK DMO's standing repo facility helps cover gilt shortages in the market.</p>", "sections": [{"heading": "3.1 Secured Debt: Charges, ABS, MBS and Covered Bonds", "html": "<p>Secured debt (called a <strong>debenture</strong> in the UK; in the US 'debenture' actually means <em>unsecured</em> loan stock) offers company assets as security against default. There are two structures:</p><ul><li><strong>Fixed charge</strong> — the debt is secured over a specific, identified asset (e.g. a building). The company cannot dispose of that asset without the lender's consent.</li><li><strong>Floating charge</strong> — the debt is secured over a class of assets (e.g. stock, receivables) that changes in the ordinary course of business. On default, the charge <strong>crystallises</strong> over whatever assets are available at that point.</li></ul><p><strong>Asset-backed securities (ABS)</strong> are bonds backed by a pool of assets such as mortgage loans, credit card receivables or car loans. Cash generated by the pool services interest and repays principal. <strong>Mortgage-backed securities (MBS)</strong> are the original form of ABS, pooling mortgage loans; in the US the major issuers/guarantors are Ginnie Mae, Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac. ABS/MBS issues are usually split into <strong>tranches</strong> with different priority: interest is paid across all tranches first, then principal is repaid tranche by tranche (first tranche first), creating different risk/maturity profiles per tranche.</p><p>ABS issuance typically uses a <strong>special purpose vehicle (SPV)</strong>, usually a trust. The originator sells the assets to the SPV, which issues the bonds. This achieves: (1) an <strong>off-balance-sheet</strong> arrangement for the originator, and (2) a <strong>bankruptcy-remote</strong> structure — if the originator fails, the SPV and its asset pool remain intact, potentially giving the ABS a higher rating than the originator itself.</p><p>Where no SPV is used, the assets stay on the issuing bank's balance sheet and the securities are called <strong>covered bonds</strong> — senior secured debt of a regulated financial institution. A key feature: provided collateral is sufficient, covered bonds continue to be paid on their original schedule even after issuer default. In the sub-prime crisis (2007-08), some originators transferred distressed SPV assets back onto their own balance sheet, converting them to covered bonds. European covered bonds typically keep assets on-balance-sheet; in the US an SPV structure is still used as legislation does not permit direct issuance."}, {"heading": "3.2 The Role of the Trustee and Tiers of Debt", "html": "<p>When secured debt is issued to a large number of holders, a <strong>trustee</strong> (typically a bank or trust company) is appointed under a trust deed. Company property is mortgaged to the holders via the trustee, who holds the company's repayment covenant on trust for the security holders. Under English law the trustee has wide discretionary powers; under US law duties are more narrowly defined. Core trustee roles:</p><ul><li><strong>Representation</strong> — represents holders' interests and guides the issuer.</li><li><strong>Security agent</strong> — for pledge-secured issues, holds the charge for the benefit of secured parties and determines priority of payment per the governing documents.</li><li><strong>Share trustee</strong> — holds shares in an issuing SPV to preserve off-balance-sheet treatment.</li><li><strong>Successor/replacement trustee</strong> — steps in where a bank must resign due to conflicts of interest (e.g. on default) or capacity constraints.</li></ul><p>Benefits of a trust deed: unified, coherent enforcement (rather than disparate individual actions), parity of treatment/pro-rata payment among holders, and lower administration/enforcement cost than multiple separate actions. The trustee also monitors compliance with the bond's <strong>covenants</strong> (terms of the indenture, e.g. asset/liability ratios, debt limits) and tracks total issuance against the indenture.</p><p>Corporate borrowing is divided into three <strong>tiers</strong>, reflecting repayment priority on insolvency:</p><table><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Ranking</th><th>Typical rate</th><th>Notes</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Senior debt</strong></td><td>Ranks above all other debt and equity</td><td>Lowest</td><td>Often secured; strict covenants (minimum financial ratios)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Subordinated debt</strong></td><td>Ranks behind senior debt</td><td>Higher than senior</td><td>Fewer/less stringent covenants than senior debt</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Mezzanine debt</strong></td><td>Ranks behind senior and unsecured debt (highest risk)</td><td>Highest</td><td>May include payment-in-kind (PIK) interest (rolled into principal, not paid in cash) and warrants/options for equity upside</td></tr></table>"}, {"heading": "3.3 Unsecured Debt: Subordinated, Guaranteed, Convertible and Floating-Rate Bonds", "html": "<p><strong>Unsecured debt</strong> is not backed by company assets, so holders have no special protection on default and are compensated with a higher coupon than equivalent secured debt. Unsecured debtholders still rank ahead of shareholders in insolvency (lenders before owners).</p><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Subordinated</th><th>Guaranteed</th><th>Convertible</th></tr><tr><td>Maturity</td><td>No difference — typically 2-30 years</td><td>No difference</td><td>No difference</td></tr><tr><td>Ranking vs plain unsecured</td><td>Below unsecured bonds</td><td>Alongside other unsecured bonds</td><td>Alongside other unsecured bonds</td></tr><tr><td>Credit rating</td><td>Lower (greater default risk)</td><td>Higher (depends on guarantor's standing)</td><td>No difference from unsecured</td></tr><tr><td>Coupon</td><td>Higher than unsecured</td><td>Lower (due to guarantee)</td><td>Potentially lower (upside from conversion)</td></tr><tr><td>Rationale</td><td>Extra risk premium for subordination</td><td>Guarantor (often the parent company) lowers cost of finance</td><td>Conversion upside restricts coupon needed and lowers effective repayment risk</td></tr></table><p><strong>Subordinated debt</strong>: on failure, repaid only after other creditors, if funds remain — hence a higher coupon than non-subordinated unsecured debt. <strong>Guaranteed debt</strong>: a third party (typically the parent or a group company) guarantees payment. <strong>Convertible bonds</strong>: give the holder the right, but not the obligation, to convert into a predetermined number of ordinary shares.</p><p><strong>Fixed-coupon bonds</strong> pay coupon on the nominal value regardless of interest-rate moves (price falls if rates rise, and vice versa). <strong>Floating-rate notes (FRNs)</strong> pay a coupon that resets against a reference rate plus a margin in basis points (1bp = 0.01%). Reference rates historically were LIBOR; following manipulation findings, the FCA retired LIBOR in favour of <strong>SONIA</strong> (UK), <strong>SOFR</strong> (US) and <strong>EURIBOR</strong> (EU). Example: an FRN pays SONIA + 175bps; if SONIA = 2%, the coupon = 2% + 1.75% = <strong>3.75%</strong>."}, {"heading": "3.4 Credit Ratings: Agencies, the Full Rating Scale and Investment Grade vs High Yield", "html": "<p>Credit (default) risk is assessed by independent <strong>credit rating agencies</strong>, chiefly <strong>Standard &amp; Poor's (S&amp;P)</strong>, <strong>Moody's</strong> and <strong>Fitch Ratings</strong> (regional specialists include Japan Credit Rating Agency (JCR) and Global Credit Ratings (GCR) in Africa). Ratings split issues into <strong>investment grade</strong> (greatest liquidity/security, lower yield) and <strong>non-investment grade / speculative</strong>, also called <strong>high-yield</strong> or <strong>junk</strong> bonds (higher coupon to compensate for higher risk). A rating change re-prices already-issued bonds, altering their effective return.</p><p><strong>Long-term rating scale (highest to lowest):</strong></p><table><tr><th>Moody's</th><th>S&amp;P</th><th>Fitch</th><th>Grade / description</th></tr><tr><td>Aaa</td><td>AAA</td><td>AAA</td><td>Prime — highest quality</td></tr><tr><td>Aa1</td><td>AA+</td><td>AA+</td><td rowspan='3'>High grade</td></tr><tr><td>Aa2</td><td>AA</td><td>AA</td></tr><tr><td>Aa3</td><td>AA-</td><td>AA-</td></tr><tr><td>A1</td><td>A+</td><td>A+</td><td rowspan='3'>Upper-medium grade</td></tr><tr><td>A2</td><td>A</td><td>A</td></tr><tr><td>A3</td><td>A-</td><td>A-</td></tr><tr><td>Baa1</td><td>BBB+</td><td>BBB+</td><td rowspan='3'>Lower-medium grade (<strong>investment grade</strong>)</td></tr><tr><td>Baa2</td><td>BBB</td><td>BBB</td></tr><tr><td>Baa3</td><td>BBB-</td><td>BBB-</td></tr><tr><td colspan='4'><strong>— Investment grade / non-investment grade (high-yield) boundary —</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Ba1</td><td>BB+</td><td>BB+</td><td rowspan='3'>Non-investment grade speculative (highest of the junk tiers)</td></tr><tr><td>Ba2</td><td>BB</td><td>BB</td></tr><tr><td>Ba3</td><td>BB-</td><td>BB-</td></tr><tr><td>B1</td><td>B+</td><td>B+</td><td rowspan='3'>Highly speculative</td></tr><tr><td>B2</td><td>B</td><td>B</td></tr><tr><td>B3</td><td>B-</td><td>B-</td></tr><tr><td>Caa1</td><td>CCC+</td><td rowspan='3'>CCC</td><td rowspan='3'>Substantial risks / extremely speculative</td></tr><tr><td>Caa2</td><td>CCC</td></tr><tr><td>Caa3</td><td>CCC-</td></tr><tr><td>Ca</td><td>CC</td><td>CC</td><td rowspan='2'>In default, or with little prospect of recovery</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>C</td><td>C</td></tr><tr><td>—</td><td>D</td><td>D</td><td>In default</td></tr></table><p><strong>Short-term rating scale:</strong></p><table><tr><th>Moody's</th><th>S&amp;P</th><th>Fitch</th></tr><tr><td>P-1</td><td>A-1+ / A-1</td><td>F1+ / F1</td></tr><tr><td>P-2</td><td>A-2</td><td>F2</td></tr><tr><td>P-3</td><td>A-3</td><td>F3</td></tr><tr><td>Not Prime</td><td>B / C / D</td><td>B / C / D</td></tr></table><p><strong>Key exam point:</strong> the lowest investment-grade rating is <strong>Baa3 (Moody's) / BBB- (S&amp;P and Fitch)</strong>; the highest non-investment-grade (junk) rating is <strong>Ba1 (Moody's) / BB+ (S&amp;P and Fitch)</strong>.</p><p><strong>Credit enhancement</strong> (e.g. insurance from a monoline insurer, or a line of credit) can raise a rating by covering shortfalls if the underlying pool is insufficient. In 2007-08, under-capitalised monoline insurers backing risky ABS/MBS could not support them on default, and agencies were criticised for overly generous ratings. A further <strong>limitation</strong>: ratings are a <strong>lagging indicator</strong> — they may not be updated quickly enough if an issuer's conditions deteriorate rapidly, leaving investors exposed to true risk not yet reflected in the rating."}, {"heading": "3.5 Cash Deposits", "html": "<p>Cash deposits are accounts held with banks/savings institutions by retail investors, companies, governments and institutions. Return is <strong>interest income only</strong> (no capital growth), and capital is returned in full at term end. Rates vary by institution, deposit size (larger deposits earn better rates) and access terms:</p><ul><li><strong>Fixed-term accounts</strong> (e.g. 1-3 years) or notice accounts (e.g. 30/60/90 days' notice) pay higher rates for tying up funds.</li><li><strong>Instant-access accounts</strong> pay the lowest rates among savings accounts.</li><li><strong>Current/transactional accounts</strong> (US: checking accounts) pay even less, sometimes nothing.</li></ul><p><strong>Gross interest</strong> is the quoted rate before tax; <strong>net interest</strong> is after tax is deducted (often at source by the deposit-taker).</p><p><strong>Advantages:</strong> liquidity (fast conversion to cash for spending needs/emergencies), predictable return, no exposure to market price volatility. <strong>Disadvantages:</strong> counterparty (bank default) risk; inflation erodes real return (after-tax return can be negative); interest rates fluctuate and cash generally offers the lowest returns of any asset class. Government-sponsored <strong>deposit protection/compensation schemes</strong> repay lost deposits up to a set maximum on institution failure — meaningful for retail depositors but limited protection for large depositors. For overseas deposits, also consider currency conversion costs/FX risk, the foreign banking system's creditworthiness, whether a protection scheme covers non-residents, local tax treatment, and any exchange controls restricting repatriation."}, {"heading": "3.6 Treasury Bills (T-bills)", "html": "<p>Governments manage short-term liquidity needs by issuing <strong>Treasury bills (T-bills)</strong> — short-term IOUs guaranteed by the government, with maturities of <strong>1 to 12 months</strong>. The US issues T-bills weekly with 4-, 13-, 26- and 52-week maturities. T-bills pay <strong>no coupon</strong> and are issued at a <strong>discount to nominal (par) value</strong>; the investor's return is entirely the difference between the discounted purchase price and par value received at redemption. As government-guaranteed instruments, T-bills are considered close to a <strong>risk-free</strong> short-term investment.</p><p><strong>Worked example:</strong> An investor pays <strong>$990,000</strong> for <strong>$1,000,000</strong> nominal of a 13-week US T-bill.</p><ul><li>Gain = $1,000,000 − $990,000 = <strong>$10,000</strong></li><li>Return over the period = $10,000 / $990,000 × 100 = <strong>1.01%</strong> (approx. 3 months)</li><li>Annualised (compounded) = approximately <strong>4.10%</strong></li></ul><p><strong>UK T-bills</strong> are issued weekly by the DMO via competitive <strong>tenders</strong>, usually on the last business day of the week (Friday), open to eligible bidders including major banks. Only bidders offering a high enough price are allocated bills, and each successful bidder pays their own bid price. Minimum bid: <strong>£500,000 nominal</strong>, in multiples of <strong>£50,000</strong> above that. In secondary trading, the minimum denomination is <strong>£25,000</strong>."}, {"heading": "3.7 Commercial Paper (CP)", "html": "<p><strong>Commercial paper (CP)</strong> is an <strong>unsecured</strong>, short-term promissory note issued primarily by corporations (also municipal/sovereign issuers) — the corporate equivalent of a government T-bill. Like T-bills, CP is issued at a <strong>discount</strong> to nominal value (no coupon). Maximum maturity is <strong>270 days in the US</strong> and <strong>up to one year in Europe</strong>, though 3-month issuance is most common in both markets.</p><p>Large companies run ongoing <strong>CP programmes</strong> to manage liquidity, rather than borrowing directly from banks. Issues are rated by the major credit rating agencies, and pricing is differentiated accordingly. Because a CP programme continually rolls old issues into new ones, the principal risk is <strong>refinancing (rollover) risk</strong> — the issuer's inability to issue new paper to repay maturing paper. Many issuers arrange <strong>credit enhancements</strong> (e.g. a bank line of credit) to mitigate this. A missed CP payment, even for one day, can trigger bankruptcy proceedings, so issuers prioritise timely repayment.</p><p>Two issuance routes: <strong>direct paper</strong>, sold straight to buy-and-hold investors (typically by large, frequent-borrowing finance companies who save the dealer fee — around 5bps, i.e. $50,000 per $100 million outstanding, in the US); and <strong>dealer paper</strong>, placed through bond dealers/broker-dealers who sell into the market (dealer fees tend to be lower outside the US).</p><p><strong>Asset-backed CP</strong> is issued by a bank/financial institution, typically maturing in 90-180 days, backed by receivables (e.g. auto loans, personal loans, credit card receivables) as collateral — used by finance companies (e.g. Ally Financial, formerly GMAC) to fund consumer/dealer financing."}, {"heading": "3.8 Repo Markets", "html": "<p>A <strong>repo (sale and repurchase agreement)</strong> is a legally binding contract combining an immediate sale of a financial instrument (e.g. a government bond/gilt) with a simultaneous agreement to repurchase the same instrument at a specified price on a set future date. Economically, it is a way of <strong>borrowing cash using the bond as collateral</strong>.</p><ul><li>The seller of the bond (borrower of cash) has entered a <strong>repo</strong>.</li><li>The buyer of the bond (lender of cash) has entered the matching <strong>reverse repo</strong> — the equivalent of a short-term secured loan.</li></ul><p>The cash paid at the start is less than the cash repaid at the end; the difference, expressed as a percentage, is the <strong>repo rate</strong> (the effective interest rate on the transaction). Benefits: the bond seller obtains relatively cheap short-term secured finance; the cash provider gains security via holding the bond as collateral, or (if a market-maker) can use the repo to source bonds it has sold short to meet settlement obligations — supporting smooth secondary-market functioning. Repos are an important general source of liquidity across debt markets, not just gilts.</p><p>In the UK, the DMO operates a <strong>standing repo facility</strong>: any Gilt-Edged Market Maker (GEMM) or other DMO counterparty may enter a reverse repo with the DMO (after signing the relevant documentation) to cover a shortage of a specific gilt, requesting any amount above <strong>£5 million nominal</strong>. The facility settles next day and can be rolled forward for up to two weeks; the DMO charges a slightly higher-than-normal repo rate for its use."}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What is the key difference between a fixed charge and a floating charge over company assets?", "options": ["A fixed charge is secured over a specific identified asset, while a floating charge covers a class of changing assets that crystallises on default", "A fixed charge is unsecured, while a floating charge is always secured", "A fixed charge applies only to government bonds, while a floating charge applies only to corporate bonds", "A fixed charge pays a floating coupon, while a floating charge pays a fixed coupon"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "A fixed charge attaches to a specific asset (e.g. a building); a floating charge attaches to a changing class of assets (e.g. stock) and only crystallises over the available assets at the point of default."}, {"question": "In the UK, a bond secured with a fixed charge over company assets is generally referred to as a:", "options": ["Eurobond", "Debenture", "Commercial paper", "Convertible bond"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "In the UK, secured debt is called a debenture. Note that in the US, 'debenture' instead refers to an unsecured loan agreement."}, {"question": "Which best describes a mortgage-backed security (MBS)?", "options": ["A short-term government IOU issued at a discount", "A bond created by pooling mortgage loans, with investors receiving interest and principal as the underlying loans are paid off", "An unsecured promissory note issued by a corporation", "A bond that converts into equity at the holder's option"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "MBSs pool mortgage loans (e.g. via Ginnie Mae, Fannie Mae, Freddie Mac in the US) and pass through interest and principal from underlying mortgage payments to bondholders, often split into tranches."}, {"question": "What is the primary purpose of using a special purpose vehicle (SPV) in an ABS structure?", "options": ["To increase the coupon paid to investors", "To make the assets subject to a floating charge only", "To achieve an off-balance-sheet, bankruptcy-remote structure separating the asset pool from the originator's own credit risk", "To guarantee the bonds will be rated below investment grade"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The SPV, usually a trust, buys the assets from the originator so they leave the originator's balance sheet, and the pool remains intact (bankruptcy-remote) even if the originator fails."}, {"question": "How does a covered bond differ from an SPV-based asset-backed security?", "options": ["Covered bonds pay no interest, whereas ABS always pay a fixed coupon", "With covered bonds, the underlying assets remain on the issuing bank's balance sheet rather than being transferred to an SPV", "Covered bonds are always unsecured, whereas ABS are always secured", "Covered bonds can only be issued by governments"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Covered bonds are senior secured debt of a regulated institution (e.g. a bank) where the collateral pool stays on the issuer's own balance sheet, rather than being transferred to an SPV as with typical ABS."}, {"question": "Ranking corporate borrowing tiers from highest to lowest priority on liquidation, which is the correct order?", "options": ["Mezzanine, subordinated, senior", "Senior, subordinated, mezzanine", "Subordinated, senior, mezzanine", "Mezzanine, senior, subordinated"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Senior debt ranks highest (repaid first), then subordinated debt, then mezzanine debt (highest risk, ranking behind both senior and unsecured debt)."}, {"question": "'Payment-in-kind' (PIK) interest is most closely associated with which tier of debt?", "options": ["Senior debt", "Treasury bills", "Mezzanine debt", "Covered bonds"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Mezzanine debt, being high-risk subordinated debt, sometimes rolls interest into the principal outstanding instead of paying cash — this is PIK interest."}, {"question": "A convertible bond gives the holder:", "options": ["The obligation to convert into shares at maturity", "The right, but not the obligation, to convert the bond into a predetermined number of ordinary shares", "A guaranteed fixed coupon that increases each year", "Priority ranking above senior secured debt"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Convertible bonds give holders an option (not an obligation) to convert into a set number of ordinary shares of the issuer, usually in exchange for a lower coupon."}, {"question": "An FRN pays a coupon of SONIA plus 175 basis points. If SONIA is currently 2.00%, what coupon rate is paid?", "options": ["2.00%", "1.75%", "3.75%", "0.25%"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "175 basis points = 1.75%. Coupon = reference rate + margin = 2.00% + 1.75% = 3.75%."}, {"question": "Which reference rate replaced LIBOR in the UK floating-rate market following the LIBOR manipulation scandal?", "options": ["SOFR", "EURIBOR", "SONIA", "P-1"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "SONIA (Sterling Overnight Index Average) is the UK's replacement reference rate; SOFR is the US equivalent and EURIBOR applies in the EU."}, {"question": "Which three agencies are described as the most prominent global credit rating agencies?", "options": ["S&P, Moody's and Fitch Ratings", "JCR, GCR and Fitch", "S&P, DMO and ICMA", "Moody's, Euroclear and Clearstream"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Standard & Poor's, Moody's and Fitch Ratings are the three dominant global rating agencies, alongside regional specialists such as JCR (Japan) and GCR (Africa)."}, {"question": "What is the lowest S&P/Fitch long-term rating that still qualifies as investment grade?", "options": ["BB+", "BBB-", "A-", "CCC+"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "BBB- is the lowest S&P/Fitch investment-grade rating. Anything at BB+ or below is non-investment grade (high yield/junk)."}, {"question": "What is the lowest Moody's long-term rating that still qualifies as investment grade?", "options": ["Ba1", "A3", "Baa3", "Caa1"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Baa3 is Moody's lowest investment-grade rating, equivalent to S&P/Fitch's BBB-."}, {"question": "What is the highest-rated tier within the S&P non-investment grade (high-yield) category?", "options": ["BBB-", "BB+", "B+", "CCC+"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "BB+ (Moody's equivalent Ba1) is the top of the non-investment grade/speculative scale, immediately below the investment-grade BBB- threshold."}, {"question": "On the Moody's long-term rating scale, which rating is exactly one notch below Aa3?", "options": ["Aa2", "A1", "A2", "Aaa"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The Moody's sequence descends Aa1, Aa2, Aa3, A1, A2, A3 — so A1 is one notch below Aa3."}, {"question": "On the S&P long-term rating scale, which rating is exactly one notch below A-?", "options": ["A", "BBB+", "BBB-", "BB+"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The S&P sequence descends A, A-, BBB+, BBB, BBB- — so BBB+ is one notch below A-."}, {"question": "A bond rated 'D' by S&P and Fitch indicates:", "options": ["The issue is investment grade but with a negative outlook", "The issuer is in default", "The issuer has the highest possible credit quality", "The bond is a floating-rate note"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "'D' denotes an issuer/issue in default — the lowest point on the S&P and Fitch scales."}, {"question": "What was a key criticism of credit rating agencies following the 2007-08 global financial crisis?", "options": ["They rated all Treasury bills as non-investment grade", "They stopped rating corporate bonds entirely", "They had given overly generous ratings to complex, opaque structured products such as MBSs, partly supported by under-capitalised monoline insurers", "They eliminated the investment grade category altogether"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Highly leveraged, opaque ABS/MBS structures, some backed by under-capitalised monoline insurers, received generous ratings that could not be supported on default, prompting increased regulatory oversight of rating agencies."}, {"question": "Credit ratings are sometimes criticised as being a 'lagging indicator'. What does this mean?", "options": ["Ratings are updated in real time, ahead of market prices", "A rating may not be promptly updated if an issuer's financial condition deteriorates quickly, leaving the rating out of step with true risk", "Ratings only apply to instruments with less than one year to maturity", "Ratings cannot be assigned to government issuers"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Because ratings are not necessarily reviewed continuously, rapid deterioration in an issuer's condition may not be reflected promptly, exposing investors relying on the stale rating."}, {"question": "What is the difference between the 'gross' and 'net' interest rate quoted on a cash deposit?", "options": ["Gross interest is the rate after currency conversion costs; net interest is before", "Gross interest is the headline rate before tax deduction; net interest is the rate after tax has been deducted", "Gross interest applies only to instant-access accounts; net interest applies only to fixed-term accounts", "There is no difference; the terms are interchangeable"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Gross interest is quoted before tax is deducted; net interest is what the depositor actually receives after tax (often deducted at source)."}, {"question": "Which of the following is a genuine disadvantage of holding cash deposits, according to CISI material?", "options": ["Cash is highly exposed to market price volatility", "Cash deposits cannot be accessed quickly in an emergency", "Inflation can erode the real return, and after-tax returns can be negative", "Cash always earns a higher return than bonds or equities"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "A key drawback of cash is that inflation reduces the real return, and the after-tax real return can be negative — even though cash is liquid and not subject to market price volatility."}, {"question": "What is the typical maturity range of a Treasury bill (T-bill)?", "options": ["1 day to 1 week", "1 to 12 months", "2 to 30 years", "50 to 100 years"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "T-bills are short-term government instruments with maturities ranging from one to twelve months (e.g. the US issues 4-, 13-, 26- and 52-week bills)."}, {"question": "An investor buys $1,000,000 nominal of a 13-week US T-bill for $990,000. What is the approximate return on the investment over the holding period?", "options": ["10.0%", "1.01%", "0.10%", "4.10%"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Gain = $10,000. Return over the period = $10,000 / $990,000 x 100 = 1.01% (this equates to roughly 4.10% annualised on a compounded basis)."}, {"question": "Why are Treasury bills issued at a discount to par rather than paying a periodic coupon?", "options": ["Because they are equity instruments", "Because T-bills pay no coupon; the investor's entire return is the difference between the discounted purchase price and the par value received at redemption", "Because they always mature at a loss", "Because coupons are illegal for government issuers"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "T-bills are zero-coupon discount instruments — the investor's return is purely the discount between purchase price and par (maturity) value."}, {"question": "Commercial paper (CP) is best described as:", "options": ["A secured, long-term bond issued by governments", "An unsecured, short-term promissory note issued primarily by corporations, at a discount to nominal value", "A coupon-bearing perpetual bond", "A form of cash deposit protected by government compensation schemes"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "CP is unsecured, short-term (up to 270 days in the US / one year in Europe), and issued at a discount, similar in structure to a T-bill but issued by corporations."}, {"question": "What is the maximum maturity of commercial paper in the US market?", "options": ["30 days", "90 days", "270 days", "365 days"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "US commercial paper has a maximum maturity of 270 days, compared with up to one year in Europe, though 3-month issuance is most common in both markets."}, {"question": "What is the principal risk faced by an issuer running an ongoing commercial paper programme?", "options": ["Interest rate risk on a fixed 30-year coupon", "Refinancing (rollover) risk — the inability to issue new CP to repay maturing paper", "The risk that CP cannot legally be rated by credit agencies", "Currency risk on domestic-currency CP"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Because CP is continually rolled over (new issues fund the retirement of old issues), the key risk is that the issuer is unable to issue new paper when existing paper matures — refinancing/rollover risk."}, {"question": "In a repo transaction, what does the difference between the initial sale price and the repurchase price represent?", "options": ["A capital gains tax charge", "The effective interest rate on the transaction, known as the repo rate", "A credit rating downgrade", "The nominal value of the bond"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The cash paid at the start of a repo is less than the cash repaid at the end; the percentage difference is the repo rate, i.e. the cost of the short-term secured borrowing."}, {"question": "From the perspective of the party providing cash and receiving the bond as collateral, a repo transaction is described as a:", "options": ["Reverse repo", "Convertible bond", "Covered bond", "Floating charge"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The party lending cash against the bond collateral has entered into a reverse repo — the equivalent of a short-term secured loan — while the counterparty selling the bond has entered a repo."}, {"question": "What is the purpose of the UK DMO's standing repo facility?", "options": ["To allow retail investors to buy T-bills directly", "To allow GEMMs and other DMO counterparties to obtain gilts via reverse repo (above £5 million nominal) to cover a shortage in the market", "To set the base rate of interest for the UK economy", "To guarantee corporate bond issuance"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The standing repo facility lets GEMMs/DMO counterparties, after signing the relevant documentation, request gilts above £5 million nominal via reverse repo (next-day settlement, rollable up to two weeks) to cover shortages, at a slightly elevated repo rate."}], "flashcards": [{"front": "What is a fixed charge?", "back": "Secured debt backed by a specific, identified company asset (e.g. a building); the company cannot dispose of it without lender consent."}, {"front": "What is a floating charge?", "back": "Secured debt backed by a changing class of assets; it crystallises over whatever assets are available at the point of default."}, {"front": "What is an asset-backed security (ABS)?", "back": "A bond backed by a pool of assets (mortgages, credit card receivables, car loans); cash from the pool services interest and repays principal."}, {"front": "What is a mortgage-backed security (MBS)?", "back": "An ABS created from pooled mortgage loans; in the US, major issuers/guarantors are Ginnie Mae, Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac."}, {"front": "What is a special purpose vehicle (SPV) used for in securitisation?", "back": "It buys assets from the originator and issues the bonds, achieving an off-balance-sheet, bankruptcy-remote structure separate from the originator's own credit risk."}, {"front": "What is a covered bond?", "back": "Senior secured debt of a regulated institution (e.g. a bank) where the collateral pool stays on the issuer's own balance sheet rather than being moved to an SPV."}, {"front": "List the four core roles of a UK debenture trustee.", "back": "Representation of holders; security/collateral agent; SPV share trustee (preserves off-balance-sheet treatment); successor/replacement trustee."}, {"front": "What is senior debt?", "back": "Ranks above all other debt and equity; often secured, subject to strict covenants; carries the lowest interest rate of the tiers."}, {"front": "What is subordinated debt?", "back": "Ranks behind senior debt on liquidation; less stringent covenants than senior debt; higher interest rate than senior debt."}, {"front": "What is mezzanine debt?", "back": "High-risk subordinated debt ranking behind senior and unsecured debt; highest interest rate; may include PIK interest and equity warrants/options."}, {"front": "What is PIK (payment-in-kind) interest?", "back": "Interest that is not paid in cash but rolled up and added to the principal outstanding — common in mezzanine debt."}, {"front": "What is unsecured debt?", "back": "Debt not backed by company assets; holder has no special protection on default, compensated by a higher coupon than secured equivalents."}, {"front": "What is a convertible bond?", "back": "Gives the holder the right (not obligation) to convert into a predetermined number of ordinary shares of the issuer, usually for a lower coupon."}, {"front": "What is a floating-rate note (FRN)?", "back": "A bond whose coupon resets periodically against a reference rate (e.g. SONIA, SOFR) plus a margin quoted in basis points."}, {"front": "Who are the three most prominent global credit rating agencies?", "back": "Standard & Poor's (S&P), Moody's, and Fitch Ratings."}, {"front": "Investment grade vs high yield/junk — where's the cutoff?", "back": "Investment grade: AAA/Aaa down to BBB-/Baa3. Non-investment grade (high-yield/junk): BB+/Ba1 and below."}, {"front": "Rating tier: Aaa / AAA / AAA", "back": "Prime — the highest possible credit quality (top of the investment-grade scale)."}, {"front": "Rating tier: Aa1-Aa3 / AA+ to AA- / AA+ to AA-", "back": "High grade — second-highest investment-grade tier."}, {"front": "Rating tier: A1-A3 / A+ to A- / A+ to A-", "back": "Upper-medium grade — third investment-grade tier."}, {"front": "Rating tier: Baa1-Baa3 / BBB+ to BBB- / BBB+ to BBB-", "back": "Lower-medium grade — lowest investment-grade tier; Baa3/BBB- is the last rung before high yield."}, {"front": "Rating tier: Ba1-Ba3 / BB+ to BB- / BB+ to BB-", "back": "Non-investment grade speculative — the highest tier of high-yield/junk bonds, immediately below investment grade."}, {"front": "Rating tier: B1-B3 / B+ to B- / B+ to B-", "back": "Highly speculative — mid-tier junk bonds."}, {"front": "Rating tier: Caa1-Caa3 / CCC+ to CCC- / CCC", "back": "Substantial risks / extremely speculative."}, {"front": "Rating tier: Ca / CC / CC", "back": "In default, or with little prospect of recovery."}, {"front": "Rating tier: C / C / C", "back": "In default, or with little prospect of recovery (lowest non-default rating band)."}, {"front": "Rating: D (S&P/Fitch)", "back": "Issuer/issue is in default — the lowest possible rating; Moody's has no direct 'D' equivalent, using Ca/C for terminal distress."}, {"front": "What is credit enhancement?", "back": "A mechanism (e.g. monoline insurance, a line of credit) used to raise a bond's rating by covering potential shortfalls if the underlying pool is insufficient."}, {"front": "Gross interest vs net interest on a cash deposit?", "back": "Gross = the quoted rate before tax; net = the rate after tax has been deducted (often at source)."}, {"front": "Two key disadvantages of cash deposits?", "back": "Bank/institution default (credit) risk, and inflation eroding real returns (after-tax real return can be negative)."}, {"front": "What are Treasury bills (T-bills)?", "back": "Short-term (1-12 month) government-guaranteed IOUs; pay no coupon; issued at a discount to par, with the discount being the investor's entire return."}, {"front": "T-bill worked example: pay $990,000 for $1,000,000 nominal 13-week bill — what's the return?", "back": "Gain $10,000; return = $10,000/$990,000 x 100 = 1.01% over the period, approx. 4.10% annualised (compounded)."}, {"front": "What is commercial paper (CP)?", "back": "An unsecured, short-term promissory note issued at a discount, mainly by corporations — the corporate equivalent of a T-bill."}, {"front": "What is the main risk of a CP programme?", "back": "Refinancing/rollover risk — the issuer may be unable to issue new CP to repay maturing paper."}, {"front": "What is a repo?", "back": "A sale-and-repurchase agreement: immediate sale of a security with a simultaneous agreement to buy it back at a set price and date — effectively a secured cash loan."}, {"front": "What is a reverse repo?", "back": "The mirror-image transaction from the cash provider's side — buying a security now with an agreement to sell it back later; equivalent to a short-term secured loan made."}, {"front": "What is the repo rate?", "back": "The effective interest rate on a repo, calculated as the percentage difference between the initial sale price and the (higher) repurchase price."}, {"front": "What is the UK DMO's standing repo facility used for?", "back": "Lets GEMMs/DMO counterparties reverse repo gilts (above £5m nominal) from the DMO to cover market shortages; next-day settlement, rollable up to two weeks, at a slightly elevated rate."}]}, {"id": "sec-m4", "number": 4, "title": "Alternative Investments & Foreign Exchange", "examWeight": "Core module", "summaryHtml": "\n<p>This module covers <b>depositary receipts</b>, <b>warrants</b>, <b>property</b> as an asset class, and the <b>foreign exchange (FX) market</b> &ndash; a calculation-heavy topic that rewards careful practice.</p>\n<p><b>Depositary receipts (DRs)</b> &ndash; ADRs (US-targeted) and GDRs (broader/non-US-targeted) &ndash; are negotiable certificates representing shares of a non-domestic company, created by a depository bank (eg, Diageo ADR: 4 ordinary shares = 1 ADR on Citibank/NYSE; Infosys ADR 1:1; Reliance GDR: 2 shares = 1 GDR). Pre-release allows a DR to be created against cash collateral up to <b>three months</b> before the underlying shares are actually deposited. Shares are registered in the depository bank's name; DRs themselves are bearer securities. Dividends are converted to the DR's trading currency by the depository bank. UK stamp duty is normally 0.5% on share purchases, but DR creation instead attracts a one-off <b>1.5%</b> stamp duty charge, with no further stamp duty on subsequent DR trades.</p>\n<p><b>Warrants</b> give the holder the right, not the obligation, to subscribe for new shares at a fixed (exercise) price over a fixed period; they carry no dividend or voting rights and are highly geared. <b>Covered warrants</b> are issued by third parties (usually investment banks) rather than the underlying company, as call or put warrants, and trade on the LSE. The <b>conversion premium</b> = warrant price + exercise price &minus; share price.</p>\n<p><b>Property</b> is illiquid, indivisible, has high transaction costs and subjective valuation, with price driven mainly by demand (supply is fixed). <b>REITs</b> avoid double taxation (no corporation tax) if they hold at least three rental properties, no single property exceeding 40% of total value, and distribute at least 90% of rental profits. Open-ended property funds offer liquidity in theory but may impose redemption moratoria (as seen in 2007&ndash;08) because underlying property is slow to sell.</p>\n<p>The <b>FX market</b> is OTC, dominated by banks, with no central exchange. <b>Spot</b> deals settle in two working days; <b>forward</b> deals settle later at a rate fixed today. Rates are quoted bid (buy)/offer (sell); forward points are quoted as a <b>premium (pm, subtract from spot)</b> or <b>discount (dis, add to spot)</b>. <b>Interest rate parity (IRP)</b> mathematically links spot and forward rates via the interest rate differential between the two currencies (forward = spot &times; (1 + quote-currency rate)/(1 + base-currency rate)) &ndash; it is not a forecast. Over the long run, <b>purchasing power parity (PPP)</b> predicts exchange rates will move to equalise the price of a basket of goods across countries. Short-term rates are driven by supply/demand, speculation, central bank policy, trade flows and data releases (eg, US Non-Farm Payrolls). Cryptocurrencies (eg, Bitcoin, 2009) are a related but distinct, high-risk, largely unregulated asset class.</p>\n", "sections": [{"heading": "4.1 Depositary Receipts (ADRs and GDRs)", "html": "\n<p>A <b>depositary receipt (DR)</b> is a negotiable certificate evidencing ownership of shares (or a fraction/multiple of a share) in a corporation based outside the DR's home trading market. <b>American depositary receipts (ADRs)</b> were created to make it easier for US investors to hold overseas shares. <b>Global depositary receipts (GDRs)</b> work identically but target a broader, often non-US, investor base.</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Example</th><th>Depository bank / market</th><th>Ratio (DR : underlying share)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Diageo (UK) ADR</td><td>Citibank; NYSE</td><td>1 ADR = 4 ordinary Diageo shares</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Infosys (India) ADR</td><td>NYSE</td><td>1 ADR = 1 Infosys share</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Reliance Industries (India) GDR</td><td>London &amp; Munich</td><td>1 GDR = 2 Reliance shares</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><b>Creation:</b> DRs are typically sponsored by the foreign issuer, which works with an investment bank on the ratio/structure. A depository bank accepts underlying shares on deposit and issues DRs against them, distributed to investors via local brokers.</p>\n<p><b>Pre-release:</b> a depository bank may create and sell a DR before the underlying shares are actually placed on deposit, provided it holds cash collateral. This pre-release facility can operate for up to <b>three months</b> before the underlying shares are actually purchased.</p>\n<p><b>Registration and form:</b> the underlying shares are registered in the name of the depository bank; the DRs themselves are transferable as <b>bearer securities</b> and are quoted/traded in the DR market's currency (eg, US dollars for ADRs), governed by that market's trading and settlement rules.</p>\n<p><b>Rights attached:</b> DR holders are normally entitled to vote, exercised via the depository bank, though some DRs are non-voting &ndash; eg, Thailand's <b>non-voting depository receipts (NVDRs)</b>, a form of capital/foreign-ownership control.</p>\n<p><b>Dividends:</b> the company pays dividends in its domestic currency to the depository bank, which converts them into the DR's trading currency (eg, US dollars for ADR holders) before distribution &ndash; removing currency-conversion hassle for the investor, though currency movements can still cause the DR to outperform/underperform the underlying local shares (unless a currency hedge is built in).</p>\n<p><b>Tax:</b> HMRC's UK stamp duty on ordinary share purchases is <b>0.5%</b> of the purchase price. DRs traded outside the UK escape this ongoing stamp duty, but HMRC instead charges a one-off <b>1.5%</b> stamp duty fee when the DR is created.</p>\n<p><b>Exchange for underlying shares:</b> an investor may sell the DR itself, or cancel it by delivering the certificate to the depository bank, which releases the corresponding number of underlying shares in the home market. GDRs are generally convertible into local shares, which can be attractive if GDR liquidity dries up after listing while the local shares remain actively traded.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "4.2 Warrants", "html": "\n<p>A <b>warrant</b> is an instrument, traditionally issued by a company itself, giving the holder the <b>right, but not the obligation</b>, to subscribe for new shares in that company at a fixed (exercise) price, over a fixed period (often several years, ending at the warrant's expiry date). If exercised, the company issues new shares. Warrants are listed and traded on stock exchanges.</p>\n<p>Warrants raise cash for the issuing company when sold, and raise further capital if/when exercised. Because the warrant holder receives no dividends and has no voting rights until exercise, the capital raised is effectively \"free\" until then. Warrants offer <b>highly geared</b> exposure and are often issued alongside other securities (eg, bonds) rather than sold outright.</p>\n<p><b>Covered warrants</b> are issued by a third party &ndash; usually an investment bank &ndash; rather than by the company whose shares underlie the warrant. They come as <b>call warrants</b> (right to buy) or <b>put warrants</b> (right to sell), and in the UK are traded on the London Stock Exchange.</p>\n<h4>Worked example &ndash; conversion premium</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Cambridge Investment Trust plc (fictional)</th><th>Value</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Current share price</td><td>77p</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Exercise price (right to buy at)</td><td>100p (&pound;1)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Warrant expiry</td><td>2027</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Warrant price</td><td>4p</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><b>Conversion premium</b> = warrant price + exercise price &minus; prevailing share price:</p>\n<p>4p + 100p &minus; 77p = <b>27p</b> conversion premium.</p>\n<p>(If the result were negative, the warrant would be trading at a <b>conversion discount</b> instead.)</p>\n<h4>Worked example &ndash; warrants attached to bonds</h4>\n<p>CBC plc is raising finance via a bond issue. Its advisers suggest either a straight bond paying a <b>6% pa</b> coupon, or a bond paying a lower <b>5% pa</b> coupon if a single detachable warrant is given away with each &pound;100 nominal of bonds. Because the warrants are <b>detachable</b>, investors can sell or keep the warrants independently of whether they retain the bonds &ndash; the warrant \"sweetens\" the deal, letting CBC reduce its coupon cost.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "4.3 Property as an Investment", "html": "\n<p>Property (real estate) has distinctive features as an asset class:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Each property is <b>unique</b> (location, structure, design) &ndash; valuation is subjective; there is no centralised market or continuous, reliable pricing.</li>\n<li>Complex legal considerations and <b>high transaction costs</b> on transfer.</li>\n<li><b>Illiquid</b> &ndash; not instantly tradeable, and generally <b>indivisible</b> (a factory unit or a spare bedroom cannot usually be sold off separately).</li>\n<li>Large discrete transaction sizes make diversification difficult for individual investors.</li>\n<li>Land supply is fixed/finite (and restricted further by planning law), so price is driven predominantly by <b>changes in demand</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Residential (direct investment)</th><th>Commercial</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Access</td><td>Second homes, holiday homes, buy-to-let &ndash; open to individual investors</td><td>Large investment size limits direct ownership mainly to property companies/institutions</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tenancies</td><td>Typically short, renewable leases</td><td>Long-term contracts, often 5+ years</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Repairs</td><td>Landlord's responsibility</td><td>Tenant is usually responsible</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Returns</td><td>Largely capital growth (house prices)</td><td>Significant income return from rent</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Property offers diversification (low correlation with other asset classes), a hedge against inflation, and a reliable income stream, but suffers from illiquidity even inside a fund. During the 2007&ndash;08 financial crisis, falling property prices triggered redemption pressure and some property funds imposed <b>12-month moratoria</b> on redemptions. High maintenance/transaction costs and void (tenant-less) risk mean direct commercial property mainly suits long-term institutional investors (eg, pension funds); indirect routes make property accessible to individuals.</p>\n<h4>Real Estate Investment Trusts (REITs)</h4>\n<p>REITs are listed investment companies pooling investor funds to invest in (mainly commercial, sometimes residential) property, avoiding the <b>double taxation</b> that otherwise arises (corporation tax at fund level plus investor tax on dividends/gains). Broad UK tax-exempt conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The property rental business must contain <b>at least three</b> single rental properties (commercial or residential; each separately let unit in a multi-let property counts).</li>\n<li>No single property may represent more than <b>40%</b> of the total value of the property rental business.</li>\n<li>The REIT must distribute at least <b>90%</b> of property rental business profits as dividends each accounting period.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>REITs are quoted, freely tradeable funds, so &ndash; unlike direct property &ndash; they remove liquidity risk for the investor and provide diversified, professionally managed exposure.</p>\n<h4>Open-Ended (Property) Funds</h4>\n<p>Open-ended funds pool investor money and issue units, growing/shrinking as units are created/cancelled &ndash; normally providing liquidity. This works less well for physical property, since individual properties are slow to sell, sale prices are cyclical, and properties are indivisible. Open-ended property funds may therefore impose <b>redemption moratoria</b> or extended redemption periods, forcing investors to wait (sometimes months) to receive sale proceeds.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "4.4 Introduction to Foreign Exchange", "html": "\n<p>The <b>foreign exchange (FX/forex) market</b> covers all transactions exchanging one currency for another. It has no physical exchange (unlike London's equity market) &ndash; it is purely <b>over-the-counter (OTC)</b> and dominated by banks.</p>\n<p>Two transaction types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Spot transactions</b> &ndash; immediate currency deals settled within <b>two working days</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Forward transactions</b> &ndash; currency deals agreed now for a future date, at a rate fixed today.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both spot and forward rates are quoted as a bid (buying rate) and offer (selling rate); the spread between them is the dealer's profit margin.</p>\n<p>FX market participants fall into two broad groups:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Trade-related users</b> &ndash; eg, a Japanese exporter invoicing a US customer in dollars must convert those dollars to yen; it can wait and deal spot when the dollars arrive, or lock in a forward rate now for budgeting certainty.</li>\n<li><b>Speculators</b> &ndash; taking a view on currency movements to profit, via spot or forward deals.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Currency pairs and quoting conventions:</b> currencies are always traded in pairs, abbreviated to three-character codes (eg, USD/JPY, EUR/USD, USD/CHF, GBP/USD). The <b>first</b> currency quoted is the <b>base currency</b> (always = 1 unit); the second is the <b>counter/quote currency</b>.</p>\n<p><i>Example:</i> EUR/USD quoted 0.9937 / 39 means the market maker will buy euros at $0.9937 and sell euros at $0.9939 per euro. To <b>buy</b> &euro;100,000 you pay the higher (offer) price: 100,000 &times; 0.9939 = <b>$99,390</b>. To <b>sell</b> &euro;100,000 you receive the lower (bid) price: 100,000 &times; 0.9937 = <b>$99,370</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Cross rates:</b> exchange rates are generally quoted against the US dollar. A <b>cross rate</b> is any FX rate that does not involve the US dollar (with GBP/EUR treated as a direct quote, not a cross rate) &ndash; eg, GBP/JPY. Cross rates matter to businesses trading directly between two non-US countries.</p>\n<p><i>Worked cross-rate example:</i> if GBP/USD = 1.2500 and USD/JPY = 150.00, then GBP/JPY = 1.2500 &times; 150.00 = <b>187.50</b>.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "4.5 Spot and Forward FX Transactions (worked calculations)", "html": "\n<h4>Spot quotation and calculation</h4>\n<p>A typical sterling/dollar spot quote:</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Buyer's rate</th><th>Seller's rate</th></tr>\n<tr><td>&pound;1 buys $1.1555</td><td>$1.1645 buys &pound;1</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>The buyer's rate and seller's rate refer to buying and selling <b>dollars</b> respectively; the difference is the <b>bid-offer spread</b>, the bank's profit.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example:</b> an investor wants to sell $50,000 for sterling. Using the seller's rate ($1.1645 buys &pound;1):</p>\n<p>$50,000 &divide; 1.1645 = <b>&pound;42,937</b></p>\n<h4>Forward points: premium (pm) and discount (dis)</h4>\n<p>Forward rates are not quoted outright; dealers quote points to add/subtract from the spot rate.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Premium (pm)</b>: used when the dollar will be <b>more expensive</b> relative to sterling in future. The premium (quoted in cents) is <b>subtracted</b> from the spot rate.</li>\n<li><b>Discount (dis)</b>: used when the dollar will be <b>cheaper</b> relative to sterling in future. The discount is <b>added</b> to the spot rate.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><i>Premium example:</i> three-month points quoted 1.00 &ndash; 0.97 pm (ie, 1 cent and 0.97 cents). Applied to the spot quote above:</p>\n<p>Buyer's: 1.1555 &minus; 0.0100 = $1.1455; Seller's: 1.1645 &minus; 0.0097 = $1.1548</p>\n<p>Three-month forward quote: <b>$1.1455 &ndash; $1.1548</b>. Note the forward spread (0.0093) is wider than the spot spread (0.0090), as forward quotes always carry a wider spread than spot.</p>\n<p><i>Discount example (illustrative):</i> three-month points quoted 0.60 &ndash; 0.55 dis, added to the same spot quote: Buyer's: 1.1555 + 0.0060 = $1.1615; Seller's: 1.1645 + 0.0055 = $1.1700. Three-month forward quote: <b>$1.1615 &ndash; $1.1700</b>.</p>\n<h4>Interest rate parity (IRP)</h4>\n<p><b>Interest rate parity</b> follows from arbitrage/rational pricing: borrowing in one currency, converting to a second currency, investing at the second currency's interest rate, and simultaneously buying a forward contract to convert back, should give the same return as simply investing in the first currency &ndash; otherwise a risk-free arbitrage would exist. IRP is a purely mathematical relationship (interest rate differential), <b>not</b> a forecast of the future spot rate; actual future rates depend on all the other factors affecting the FX market.</p>\n<p>Formula: <b>Forward rate = Spot rate &times; (1 + quote-currency interest rate for the period) &divide; (1 + base-currency interest rate for the period)</b></p>\n<p><b>Worked example:</b> GBP/USD spot = 1.5220. UK 3-month interest rate = 4.88% pa; US 3-month interest rate = 3.20% pa.</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Step</th><th>Calculation</th><th>Result</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Convert pa rates to 3-month rates (&divide;4)</td><td>Sterling: 4.88%/4; Dollar: 3.20%/4</td><td>Sterling 1.22%; Dollar 0.80%</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Apply IRP formula</td><td>1.5220 &times; (1.0080 &divide; 1.0122)</td><td>&asymp; <b>1.5157</b></td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>The forward rate (1.5157) is lower than spot (1.5220) &ndash; &pound;1 buys $0.0063 fewer dollars forward, a difference of <b>63 pips</b> (1 pip = one-hundredth of 1%, ie, 0.0001). This makes sense under IRP: sterling (the higher-yielding currency) is at a forward <b>discount</b> against the dollar.</p>\n<h4>Extra practice calculation 1 &ndash; spot conversion</h4>\n<p>Quote: Buyer's rate &pound;1 buys $1.2500; Seller's rate $1.2600 buys &pound;1. An investor wants to sell $63,000 for sterling.</p>\n<p>Using the seller's rate: $63,000 &divide; 1.2600 = <b>&pound;50,000</b></p>\n<h4>Extra practice calculation 2 &ndash; interest rate parity</h4>\n<p>EUR/USD spot = 1.1000. Eurozone 3-month rate = 2.00% pa (0.50% per quarter); US 3-month rate = 5.00% pa (1.25% per quarter). EUR is the base currency, USD the quote currency.</p>\n<p>Forward = 1.1000 &times; (1.0125 &divide; 1.0050) = 1.1000 &times; 1.007463 &asymp; <b>1.1082</b></p>\n<p>The dollar (higher-yielding) trades at a forward discount against the euro &ndash; it takes more dollars to buy one euro forward than spot.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "4.6 Factors Affecting Foreign Exchange Rates", "html": "\n<h4>Historical regime</h4>\n<p>Under the 1944 <b>Bretton Woods</b> agreement, many exchange rates were fixed, changed only by formal devaluation (eg, the UK devalued sterling from &pound;1 = $2.80 to &pound;1 = $2.40 in 1968). Fixed rates ended in the 1970s after a US dollar currency crisis and the abandonment of gold convertibility (August 1971). <b>Freely floating exchange rates</b> are now the norm, though exceptions exist: Saudi Arabia and the UAE peg to the US dollar; China pegs to a currency basket; the CFA franc is pegged to the euro (Hong Kong uses a stricter currency board); Nigeria operates a managed/parallel-rate system.</p>\n<h4>Purchasing power parity (PPP)</h4>\n<p>PPP theory holds that, over the long term, exchange rates move so that equivalent amounts of different currencies have equal purchasing power.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example:</b> a basket of goods costs &pound;100 in London and $200 in New York &rArr; PPP-implied rate: <b>&pound;1 = $2.00</b>.</p>\n<p>If, after some years, UK inflation pushes the basket to &pound;150 in London while it only rises to $210 in New York, the PPP-implied rate becomes: 210 &divide; 150 = <b>&pound;1 = $1.40</b> &ndash; ie, sterling should have depreciated, reflecting its higher relative inflation.</p>\n<h4>Currency supply and demand (using USD as the example)</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Increases demand for USD</th><th>Increases supply of USD</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Overseas buyers needing dollars to pay for US imports</td><td>US importers buying foreign currency to pay for imports</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Overseas investors buying US assets</td><td>US residents buying overseas assets (selling USD)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Commodities (oil, gold) priced in USD, exporters retaining/reinvesting USD</td><td>Fed selling USD to build foreign reserves</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Speculators buying USD expecting appreciation</td><td>Speculators selling USD expecting depreciation</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Demand slopes downward and supply slopes upward with respect to price. Tariff policy, and central bank interest-rate/inflation management, also shift demand for a currency.</p>\n<h4>Trading, speculation and volatility</h4>\n<p>The Bank for International Settlements' 2022 triennial survey estimated daily FX turnover at approximately <b>$7.5 trillion</b>; the most heavily traded pair is <b>USD/EUR</b>, at just under a quarter of all trades. The market trades around the clock for roughly six days a week. Highly traded pairs (eg, EUR/USD) have narrow spreads; exotic/thinly traded pairs have wider spreads.</p>\n<p>The <b>carry trade</b> involves borrowing in a low-interest-rate currency (eg, Japanese yen, Swiss franc) to buy higher-yielding assets (eg, Australian short-term government instruments), and helps explain correlations between FX and other asset markets.</p>\n<p>Key volatility triggers: central bank commentary/minutes, the US <b>Non-Farm Payrolls (NFP)</b> report (first Friday of each month, 8:30am Eastern time), inflation/GDP/industrial production/retail sales releases (eg, from the ONS or Eurostat), government bond auction results, central bank rate announcements, and (for emerging markets) political risk such as elections or geopolitical tensions.</p>\n<h4>Cryptocurrencies (related, distinct asset class)</h4>\n<p>Emerging after the 2007&ndash;08 financial crisis, cryptocurrencies use encryption (\"cryptography\")/distributed ledger (blockchain) technology to control issuance and record ownership; <b>Bitcoin</b> (2009) was the first. A \"coin\" has its own blockchain; a \"token\" runs on a third-party blockchain. Coins can be acquired by purchase, by being earned, via <b>mining (proof of work)</b>, or via <b>proof of stake</b>. Risks include extreme volatility, counterparty/liquidity risk on decentralised networks, evolving/unclear regulation, lack of any reserve backing, high energy use, and hacking (eg, the 2025 Bybit hack, in which roughly $1.5 billion in ethereum was stolen).</p>\n"}], "mcqs": [{"question": "A GDR differs from an ADR mainly in that:", "options": ["GDRs are only issued by UK companies", "GDRs are marketed to a broader investor base, potentially outside the US, whereas ADRs specifically target US investors", "GDRs cannot be converted back into the underlying local shares", "GDRs carry voting rights while ADRs never do"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "GDRs work identically to ADRs but are marketed more broadly, including to investors outside the US, whereas ADRs were created specifically to attract US investors."}, {"question": "Under Diageo's ADR programme (depository bank Citibank, traded on the NYSE), how many ordinary Diageo shares does each ADR represent?", "options": ["One", "Two", "Four", "Ten"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Each Diageo ADR (American Depositary Share) represents four ordinary Diageo shares."}, {"question": "The pre-release facility for depositary receipts allows a depository bank to create and sell a DR before the underlying shares are placed on deposit, provided it holds:", "options": ["A bank guarantee from the issuing company", "Cash collateral, for up to three months", "Regulatory approval from the SEC only", "A matching short position in the underlying shares"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "As long as the depository bank holds cash collateral, it can create and sell the DR before the shares are actually deposited, for a period of up to three months."}, {"question": "How are the shares underlying a depositary receipt typically registered, and in what form are the DRs themselves held?", "options": ["Registered in the investor's own name; DRs are registered securities", "Registered in the name of the depository bank; DRs are transferable bearer securities", "Registered in the name of the issuing company; DRs are non-transferable", "Unregistered; DRs are held only in electronic book-entry form with no physical equivalent"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The underlying shares are registered in the depository bank's name, while the DRs themselves are transferable as bearer securities."}, {"question": "A UK company's shares are converted into a depositary receipt traded outside the UK. What is the UK stamp duty position?", "options": ["0.5% stamp duty is charged on every subsequent DR trade, as with ordinary shares", "No stamp duty applies at any stage", "A one-off 1.5% stamp duty is charged when the DR is created; no further stamp duty applies to subsequent DR trades", "Stamp duty is charged at 1.5% on every DR trade, replacing the usual 0.5% rate"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Because the DR trades outside the UK, ordinary 0.5% stamp duty on share purchases is avoided, but HMRC charges a one-off 1.5% stamp duty fee when the DR is created."}, {"question": "Thailand's non-voting depository receipts (NVDRs) are best described as:", "options": ["A tax-avoidance scheme for domestic investors", "A form of capital/foreign-ownership control, restricting foreign investors to non-voting receipts", "A type of covered warrant", "A short-term bridging facility used only during pre-release"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "NVDRs are non-voting, effectively acting as a form of capital control that restricts foreign investors' access to voting rights in the underlying Thai companies."}, {"question": "A warrant gives the holder:", "options": ["The obligation to buy shares in the issuing company at a fixed price", "The right, but not the obligation, to subscribe for new shares at a fixed price over a fixed period", "A fixed income stream paid by the issuing company", "Automatic voting rights equivalent to an ordinary shareholder"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A warrant is the right, not the obligation, to subscribe for new shares in the company at a fixed (exercise) price over a fixed period."}, {"question": "A covered warrant differs from a traditional company-issued warrant in that:", "options": ["It is issued by a third party, typically an investment bank, rather than the company whose shares are referenced", "It cannot be a put warrant", "It always has a shorter life than a traditional warrant", "It entitles the holder to dividends"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Covered warrants are issued by firms such as investment banks, not by the underlying company, and can be call warrants (right to buy) or put warrants (right to sell)."}, {"question": "Cambridge Investment Trust plc shares trade at 77p. Warrants give the right to buy shares at 100p until 2027 and trade at 4p. What is the conversion premium?", "options": ["19p", "23p", "27p", "31p"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Conversion premium = warrant price + exercise price - share price = 4p + 100p - 77p = 27p."}, {"question": "CBC plc is offered bonds paying 6% pa, or 5% pa with one detachable warrant per £100 nominal. \"Detachable\" means:", "options": ["The warrant automatically expires if the bond is sold", "The investor can sell or keep the warrant independently of whether they retain the bond", "The warrant can only be exercised once the bond matures", "The bond and warrant must always be traded together"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Because the warrant is detachable, the investor can sell or retain it separately, regardless of what they do with the bond."}, {"question": "Which characteristic best explains why property is generally considered illiquid, even beyond the time needed to find a buyer?", "options": ["Property cannot be mortgaged", "Property is usually indivisible, so investors must sell all or nothing of an individual property", "Property yields no income return", "Property prices are set by a centralised exchange"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Property is generally indivisible - an investor cannot sell part of a single property (eg, one factory unit or a spare bedroom) - which compounds its illiquidity."}, {"question": "For a UK REIT to qualify for tax-exempt treatment on its property rental business, which condition must be met?", "options": ["It must hold at least three rental properties, with no single property exceeding 40% of the total value, and distribute at least 90% of rental profits", "It must hold at least ten rental properties and distribute 100% of all profits", "It must invest only in residential property", "It must be unlisted and privately held"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Broad UK REIT conditions require at least three rental properties, no single property representing more than 40% of total value, and distribution of at least 90% of rental business profits as dividends."}, {"question": "The main tax advantage a REIT structure offers investors, compared with holding shares in an ordinary property company, is:", "options": ["Exemption from all income tax on dividends received", "Avoidance of double taxation, since the REIT itself does not pay corporation tax on qualifying rental profits", "No requirement to pay any tax on capital gains", "Automatic inheritance tax relief"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "REITs avoid double taxation: provided conditions are met, no corporation tax is paid at fund level, with distributions instead taxed on the investor."}, {"question": "Open-ended funds investing directly in physical property may need to impose redemption moratoria mainly because:", "options": ["Regulators require it by law at all times", "Individual properties are slow to sell and are generally indivisible, creating a liquidity mismatch with open-ended fund structures", "Property funds are legally prohibited from allowing redemptions", "Property always falls in value during redemptions"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Open-ended funds normally offer liquidity by creating/cancelling units, but physical property is slow to sell and indivisible, so funds may restrict redemptions (as happened in 2007-08)."}, {"question": "The foreign exchange (FX) market is best described as:", "options": ["A centralised exchange based in London", "An over-the-counter (OTC) market with no physical exchange, dominated by banks", "A market regulated exclusively by central banks with fixed daily rates", "A retail-only market for individual investors"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "There is no physical FX exchange; it operates purely OTC and is dominated by banks acting as market makers."}, {"question": "In an EUR/USD quote of 0.9937 / 39, what would it cost, in US dollars, to buy €100,000?", "options": ["$99,370", "$99,390", "$100,630", "$99,000"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "To buy euros, the buyer pays the higher (offer) price: 100,000 x 0.9939 = $99,390."}, {"question": "In FX quoting convention, the base currency in a currency pair quote:", "options": ["Is always the US dollar", "Is the second currency quoted", "Is the first currency quoted and is always equal to one unit", "Varies according to which currency has the higher interest rate"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The first currency quoted is the base currency, always equal to one unit (eg, one euro, one pound); the second is the counter/quote currency."}, {"question": "Which of the following is correctly described as a cross rate?", "options": ["EUR/USD", "USD/JPY", "GBP/JPY", "GBP/USD"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "A cross rate is any FX rate that does not involve the US dollar (with GBP/EUR treated as a direct quote); GBP/JPY is a cross rate."}, {"question": "Spot GBP/USD is quoted: Buyer's rate £1 buys $1.1555; Seller's rate $1.1645 buys £1. An investor wants to sell $50,000 for sterling. How much sterling will they receive (to the nearest £)?", "options": ["£42,937", "£43,275", "£57,775", "£50,000"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Selling dollars for sterling uses the seller's rate: $50,000 / 1.1645 = £42,937."}, {"question": "A three-month GBP/USD forward is quoted at a premium (pm) to the spot rate. This means:", "options": ["The premium points are added to the spot rate because the dollar will be cheaper in future", "The premium points are subtracted from the spot rate because the dollar will be more expensive relative to sterling in future", "The forward rate will always equal the spot rate", "The premium reflects a forecast that the pound will strengthen indefinitely"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A premium (pm) is used when the dollar will be more expensive relative to sterling in future; the premium points are subtracted from the spot rate to derive the forward rate."}, {"question": "GBP/USD spot = 1.5220. The three-month UK interest rate is 4.88% pa and the US rate is 3.20% pa. Using interest rate parity, what is the approximate three-month forward rate?", "options": ["1.5220", "1.5157", "1.5283", "1.4980"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Quarterly rates: UK 1.22%, US 0.80%. Forward = 1.5220 x (1.0080/1.0122) ≈ 1.5157 - sterling (the higher-yielding currency) trades at a forward discount."}, {"question": "A basket of goods costs £100 in London and £150 in London some years later (after inflation), while the same basket rises from $200 to $210 in New York. According to purchasing power parity, what is the new implied exchange rate?", "options": ["£1 = $2.10", "£1 = $1.40", "£1 = $1.60", "£1 = $2.00"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "PPP-implied rate = $210 / £150 = £1 = $1.40, implying sterling should have depreciated due to its higher relative inflation."}, {"question": "The UK devalued sterling against the US dollar in 1968 under the Bretton Woods system, moving the rate from:", "options": ["£1 = $2.80 to £1 = $2.40", "£1 = $4.00 to £1 = $2.80", "£1 = $1.60 to £1 = $1.20", "£1 = $2.40 to £1 = $1.80"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The UK Government devalued the pound against the dollar from £1 = $2.80 to £1 = $2.40 in 1968."}, {"question": "The 'carry trade' in FX markets typically involves:", "options": ["Borrowing in a high-interest-rate currency and investing in a low-interest-rate currency", "Borrowing in a low-interest-rate currency (eg, yen or Swiss franc) and investing in higher-yielding assets (eg, Australian short-term instruments)", "Simultaneously buying and selling the same currency pair to lock in the spread", "Only trading in pegged currencies to avoid volatility"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The carry trade involves borrowing in a low-interest-rate currency, such as the yen or Swiss franc, and investing the proceeds in higher-yielding assets, such as Australian government short-term instruments."}, {"question": "According to the Bank for International Settlements' 2022 triennial survey, approximately how much was traded daily in the global FX market, and which currency pair was most heavily traded?", "options": ["$7.5 trillion; USD/EUR", "$1 trillion; GBP/USD", "$500 billion; USD/JPY", "$50 trillion; USD/CNY"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The BIS 2022 survey estimated daily FX turnover at approximately $7.5 trillion, with USD/EUR the most widely traded pair (just under a quarter of all trades)."}, {"question": "The US Non-Farm Payrolls (NFP) report, a key driver of FX volatility, is released:", "options": ["On the last trading day of each month", "On the first Friday of each month at 8:30am Eastern time", "Quarterly, alongside GDP data", "Only when requested by the Federal Reserve"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The NFP report is released by the US Labour Department on the first Friday of each month at 8:30am Eastern time and is a significant mover of FX rates."}], "flashcards": [{"front": "What is a depositary receipt (DR)?", "back": "A negotiable certificate evidencing ownership of shares (or a fraction/multiple) in a company based outside the DR's home trading market."}, {"front": "ADR vs GDR - key difference?", "back": "ADRs target US investors specifically; GDRs are marketed more broadly, including outside the US."}, {"front": "Diageo ADR ratio", "back": "1 ADR = 4 ordinary Diageo shares (depository bank Citibank, traded on NYSE)."}, {"front": "Reliance Industries GDR ratio", "back": "1 GDR = 2 Reliance shares, traded in London and Munich."}, {"front": "What is DR pre-release?", "back": "Creating and selling a DR before the underlying shares are actually placed on deposit, backed by cash collateral, for up to three months."}, {"front": "How are DR dividends handled?", "back": "The company pays dividends in its domestic currency to the depository bank, which converts them to the DR's trading currency before distribution to holders."}, {"front": "UK stamp duty on DR creation vs ordinary share purchase", "back": "Ordinary share purchases: 0.5% stamp duty. DR creation: one-off 1.5% stamp duty charge; no further stamp duty on subsequent DR trades outside the UK."}, {"front": "Define a warrant", "back": "The right, but not the obligation, to subscribe for new shares in a company at a fixed exercise price over a fixed period."}, {"front": "What is a covered warrant?", "back": "A warrant issued by a third party (usually an investment bank), not the underlying company, offered as call warrants (right to buy) or put warrants (right to sell); traded on the LSE."}, {"front": "Conversion premium formula", "back": "Warrant price + exercise price - prevailing share price."}, {"front": "Cambridge Investment Trust example: warrant 4p, exercise 100p, share price 77p - conversion premium?", "back": "4p + 100p - 77p = 27p."}, {"front": "What does 'detachable' mean for a bond warrant?", "back": "The warrant can be sold or kept independently of the bond it was issued alongside."}, {"front": "Why is property considered illiquid beyond just time-to-sell?", "back": "Property is generally indivisible - an investor must sell the whole property (or none), and units are large, making diversification hard."}, {"front": "Residential vs commercial property: typical lease length", "back": "Residential: short, renewable leases. Commercial: long-term, often 5+ years."}, {"front": "UK REIT minimum property/value conditions", "back": "At least three rental properties; no single property may exceed 40% of the total value of the rental business."}, {"front": "UK REIT minimum profit distribution requirement", "back": "At least 90% of property rental business profits distributed as dividends each accounting period."}, {"front": "Main tax benefit of REIT structure", "back": "Avoids double taxation - no corporation tax at fund level (subject to conditions); distributions taxed on the investor instead."}, {"front": "Why might open-ended property funds impose redemption moratoria?", "back": "Physical property is slow to sell and indivisible, creating a liquidity mismatch with the fund's open-ended (unit creation/cancellation) structure - eg, 12-month moratoria seen in 2007-08."}, {"front": "How is the FX market structured?", "back": "Purely over-the-counter (OTC), with no physical exchange, dominated by banks."}, {"front": "Forward FX transaction - definition", "back": "A currency deal agreed now for a future date, at an exchange rate fixed today."}, {"front": "Base currency vs quote/counter currency", "back": "Base currency is quoted first and always equals one unit; the quote (counter) currency is the second currency."}, {"front": "What is a cross rate?", "back": "Any FX rate not involving the US dollar (GBP/EUR is treated as a direct quote, not a cross rate), eg GBP/JPY."}, {"front": "GBP/USD spot: seller's rate $1.1645 buys £1. Investor sells $50,000 - GBP received?", "back": "$50,000 / 1.1645 = £42,937."}, {"front": "Forward premium (pm) - applied how, and when used?", "back": "Subtracted from the spot rate; used when the dollar will be more expensive relative to sterling in future."}, {"front": "Interest rate parity (IRP) formula", "back": "Forward rate = Spot rate x (1 + quote-currency interest rate) / (1 + base-currency interest rate), for the relevant period."}, {"front": "IRP worked example: spot GBP/USD 1.5220, UK 3-month rate 1.22%, US 3-month rate 0.80% - forward rate?", "back": "1.5220 x (1.0080/1.0122) ≈ 1.5157 (63 pip discount for sterling)."}, {"front": "Purchasing power parity (PPP) theory", "back": "Over the long term, exchange rates move so that equivalent amounts of different currencies have equal purchasing power for the same basket of goods."}, {"front": "PPP example: basket costs £100 in London, $200 in New York - implied rate?", "back": "£1 = $2.00."}, {"front": "Carry trade strategy", "back": "Borrow in a low-interest-rate currency (eg, JPY, CHF) and invest in higher-yielding assets (eg, Australian short-term instruments)."}, {"front": "BIS 2022 survey: daily FX turnover and most-traded pair", "back": "Approximately $7.5 trillion daily; USD/EUR most widely traded (just under a quarter of trades)."}, {"front": "US Non-Farm Payrolls (NFP) report - release timing", "back": "First Friday of each month, 8:30am Eastern time - a major FX volatility driver."}, {"front": "Bitcoin - creation and blockchain basics", "back": "First established cryptocurrency, created in 2009; a 'coin' has its own blockchain, a 'token' runs on a third-party blockchain."}]}, {"id": "sec-m5", "number": 5, "title": "Collective Investment Schemes", "examWeight": "Core module", "summaryHtml": "<p>A collective investment scheme (CIS) pools money from many investors into a single fund managed by a professional manager. The IOSCO definition requires a CIS to invest in transferable securities, be publicly marketed, and be open-ended. In the UK, schemes that meet FCA requirements for retail marketing are <strong>regulated</strong> (either authorised domestically or recognised if overseas); those that do not are <strong>unregulated collective investment schemes (UCISs)</strong>. UCISs are typically illiquid, highly leveraged, volatile and speculative, suitable for only a small part of a sophisticated investor's portfolio. Key UCIS risks include limited liquidity (redemption can take up to two and a half months), long investment horizons, higher/performance-based charges (subject to a high-water mark), gearing (leverage), currency/political risk if offshore, and poor diversification.</p><p>The second major split is between <strong>open-ended</strong> and <strong>closed-ended</strong> funds. Open-ended funds (unit trusts, OEICs, US mutual funds, European SICAVs) create and cancel units/shares on demand, so fund size expands and contracts with investor flows; the price is always directly tied to net asset value (NAV). <strong>Unit trusts</strong> are constituted by a trust deed between an independent, FCA-authorised trustee (capital over £4 million) and an FCA-authorised manager; pricing occurs at a daily valuation point, with a typical dual-price spread of 5-7% or single pricing with separate charge disclosure, and charges comprise an initial charge (3-6.5% typically, lower for trackers), an annual management charge (0.5-1.5%) and sometimes a sliding-scale exit charge. <strong>OEICs (ICVCs)</strong> are corporate structures whose share price equals NAV per share; they can be umbrella funds with separately priced sub-funds and offer income or accumulation share classes.</p><p><strong>Closed-ended</strong> investment trusts (companies, not trusts, despite the name — the first was Foreign & Colonial, 1868) issue a fixed number of shares traded on an exchange at an offer/bid price that reflects supply and demand, not always NAV — hence trusts commonly trade at a discount (sometimes a premium) to NAV. Because share numbers are fixed, managers can take a long-term view, invest in unquoted companies, provide venture capital, and borrow (gear) more freely than open-ended funds — increasing both potential return and volatility.</p><p><strong>ETFs</strong> combine features of shares and CISs: they trade continuously on exchange at prices kept close to NAV via authorised participants trading creation/redemption units, while retail investors trade on the secondary market. Most are passively managed trackers, using either physical replication (owning constituents — simple but costly) or synthetic replication (swap-based — cheaper but with counterparty risk). Related products, ETNs, are unsecured bank debt tracking an index and carry issuer credit risk. Finally, <strong>specialist closed-ended funds</strong> (private equity, property, infrastructure) exploit the closed-ended structure to hold illiquid assets without forced distressed sales, though investors must accept long lock-up periods and, in some cases, periodic redemption windows rather than daily dealing.</p>", "sections": [{"heading": "5.1 Regulated and Unregulated Collective Investment Schemes (UCIS)", "html": "<p>The <strong>IOSCO definition</strong> of a CIS requires it to: (1) invest in transferable securities, (2) be publicly marketed, and (3) be open-ended.</p><table><tr><th>Regulated CIS</th><th>Unregulated CIS (UCIS)</th></tr><tr><td>Authorised by the local regulator (domestic scheme) or recognised (overseas scheme meeting specified criteria)</td><td>Not authorised or recognised by the local regulator</td></tr><tr><td>Can be freely marketed to retail investors</td><td>Subject to marketing restrictions, particularly to retail clients</td></tr><tr><td>Subject to investment and borrowing restrictions ensuring a prudent spread of risk</td><td>Generally not subject to such restrictions</td></tr><tr><td>Lower volatility/leverage typically</td><td>High volatility, illiquidity and high leverage — regarded as speculative</td></tr></table><p><strong>Typical UCIS risks:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Liquidity</strong> — investors must serve notice to redeem; redemption takes effect at the next available redemption date (often month-end), priced at the end of the following month, with proceeds returned roughly 14 days later — up to 2.5 months elapsed time in total.</li><li><strong>Term</strong> — investment must be regarded as long term due to these liquidity constraints.</li><li><strong>Valuation</strong> — not necessarily riskier than a regulated fund, but valuations can be less frequent/transparent.</li><li><strong>Charges</strong> — some UCISs levy a performance fee if a benchmark is exceeded, subject to a <strong>high-water mark</strong> (the manager cannot earn a further performance fee until the fund price surpasses its previous peak).</li><li><strong>Gearing (leverage in the US)</strong> — borrowing to enhance returns magnifies both gains and losses, since the loan must be repaid in full before NAV is attributed to investors.</li><li><strong>Currency/political risk</strong> — offshore UCISs may have a different base currency and exposure to less stable jurisdictions.</li><li><strong>Diversification</strong> — a UCIS may represent a single project (eg, a golf course, forest, or off-plan property), increasing concentration risk.</li></ul>"}, {"heading": "5.2 Open-Ended vs Closed-Ended CISs: Core Mechanics", "html": "<p>Funds are also classified by legal structure and operating mechanism.</p><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Open-ended fund</th><th>Closed-ended fund</th></tr><tr><td>Units/shares in issue</td><td>Variable — created/cancelled on demand</td><td>Fixed at launch (subsequent issues/buybacks possible but not routine)</td></tr><tr><td>How investors deal</td><td>Directly with the fund manager, who creates or redeems units/shares</td><td>On the stock market, buying from/selling to other investors</td></tr><tr><td>Price basis</td><td>Directly proportional to NAV of the underlying portfolio</td><td>Determined by supply and demand — may diverge from NAV (discount/premium)</td></tr><tr><td>Examples</td><td>Unit trusts, OEICs (UK); mutual funds (US); SICAVs (Europe)</td><td>Investment trusts / investment companies; specialist private equity, property and infrastructure funds</td></tr></table><p>Because an open-ended fund must always stand ready to redeem, a wave of net redemptions can force the manager to sell underlying assets at an inopportune time. A closed-ended fund faces no such forced selling, since the pool of capital is fixed regardless of secondary-market trading in its shares.</p>"}, {"heading": "5.3 Unit Trusts", "html": "<p>A unit trust is a professionally managed CIS under which investors buy <strong>units</strong> representing a fraction of the trust's portfolio. An <strong>authorised unit trust (AUT)</strong> can be marketed to the public and must be constituted by a <strong>trust deed</strong> between the manager and the trustee.</p><p><strong>Trustee requirements (UK):</strong></p><ul><li>Must be authorised by the FCA and fully independent of the manager.</li><li>Must have capital in excess of £4 million — normally large banks or insurers.</li><li>Primary duty: protect the interests of unitholders (the beneficiaries).</li><li>The trust deed must state investment strategy, objectives and permitted investment limits.</li></ul><p><strong>Manager's role:</strong> marketing the trust; managing assets per the trust deed; maintaining the unit register for trustee inspection; supplying information on request; and notifying the FCA of any regulatory breaches.</p><p><strong>Pricing:</strong> buying/selling prices are struck at a daily <strong>valuation point</strong> based on the net value of underlying assets. Dual pricing typically shows a 5-7% spread between buying and selling prices; some managers use <strong>single pricing</strong> (one price for both), with charges separately disclosed.</p><table><tr><th>Charge type</th><th>When levied</th><th>Typical level</th></tr><tr><td>Initial charge</td><td>Upfront, added to the buying price</td><td>3-6.5% (active funds); lower for index trackers</td></tr><tr><td>Annual management charge (AMC)</td><td>Periodically, on fund value</td><td>0.5-1.5%</td></tr><tr><td>Exit charge</td><td>On sale, usually only within first 3-5 years, sliding scale</td><td>Varies — reduces as holding period lengthens</td></tr></table>"}, {"heading": "5.4 Open-Ended Investment Companies (OEICs)", "html": "<p>An OEIC (pronounced 'oiks') is a UK open-ended CIS structured as a <strong>corporation</strong> under the Open-Ended Investment Companies Regulations, also known as an <strong>ICVC</strong> (investment company with variable capital) — the terms are interchangeable.</p><p>A share in an OEIC entitles the holder to a share of profits, and share value equals NAV per share. Example: underlying investments worth £125 million with 100 million shares in issue gives a NAV of £1.25 per share. Investors buy new shares from, or sell shares back to, the OEIC itself (not on an exchange), with the number of shares in issue rising or falling accordingly, and dealing is done at NAV.</p><ul><li>An OEIC may be structured as an <strong>umbrella fund</strong> containing several separately priced <strong>sub-funds</strong>, each with its own investment strategy/currency, client register and asset pool.</li><li><strong>Income shares</strong> pay a dividend; <strong>accumulation shares</strong> retain and reinvest income within net assets.</li><li>Unlike ordinary companies, OEICs can issue and redeem their own shares continuously — this flexibility is what makes them 'open-ended'.</li></ul>"}, {"heading": "5.5 Investment Trusts", "html": "<p>Investment trusts originated in the UK; the <strong>Foreign & Colonial Investment Trust (1868)</strong> was the first. Despite the name, investment trusts are <strong>companies, not trusts</strong> — their sole business is investing, and shareholders hope for dividends plus capital growth.</p><p><strong>Buying and selling:</strong> shares are quoted with an <strong>offer price</strong> (higher, at which investors buy) and a <strong>bid price</strong> (lower, at which holders sell); the difference is the <strong>spread</strong>. Media quotes often show the mid-market price. Deals go through a stockbroker, with commission depending on advisory vs execution-only service.</p><p><strong>Net Asset Value (NAV):</strong> NAV = (listed investments at mid-market price + unlisted investments at directors' valuation + cash and other net current assets) − liabilities (including preference capital at nominal value). NAV per share = NAV ÷ number of ordinary shares.</p><p><em>Example:</em> assets $10m, liabilities $4m → NAV = $6m. With 12 million shares outstanding, NAV per share = $6m ÷ 12m = 50c.</p><p>Because the share price is driven by market supply and demand rather than directly by NAV, most investment trust shares trade at a <strong>discount</strong> to NAV (occasionally a premium); the discount/premium narrows or widens with demand. A discount enhances income yield, since dividend income reflects the (higher) underlying portfolio value while the investor paid a lower price for the shares.</p><p><strong>Case study — Woodford Patient Capital Trust:</strong> launched 2015 by Neil Woodford, it initially traded at a <strong>premium</strong> to NAV amid investor excitement, then moved to a widening <strong>discount</strong> following poor performance. The trust changed manager (to Schroders) and was renamed the Schroder UK Public Private Trust (2019), then the Schroder Capital Global Innovation Trust (2023), and is now in an orderly wind-down, still trading at a discount.</p>"}, {"heading": "5.6 Comparing Fund Structures", "html": "<table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Unit trust</th><th>OEIC</th><th>Investment trust</th><th>ETF</th></tr><tr><td>Legal form</td><td>Trust (trust deed, trustee + manager)</td><td>Company (corporate/ICVC)</td><td>Company (closed-ended)</td><td>Company/fund trading like a share</td></tr><tr><td>Open- or closed-ended</td><td>Open-ended</td><td>Open-ended</td><td>Closed-ended</td><td>Open-ended (creation/redemption) but exchange-traded</td></tr><tr><td>Price basis</td><td>NAV-based, single daily valuation point</td><td>NAV per share, dealt directly with fund</td><td>Supply/demand — may be at discount/premium to NAV</td><td>Real-time market price, kept close to NAV by authorised participants</td></tr><tr><td>Dealing venue</td><td>Direct with manager</td><td>Direct with fund</td><td>Stock exchange</td><td>Stock exchange (secondary market) / creation-redemption with fund (authorised participants only)</td></tr><tr><td>Gearing/borrowing</td><td>Limited</td><td>Limited</td><td>Greater freedom — can gear to enhance returns</td><td>Not geared itself (though leveraged/inverse ETFs exist)</td></tr><tr><td>Can hold unquoted/private companies</td><td>Restricted</td><td>Restricted</td><td>Yes — wider investment freedom</td><td>Generally no — tracks listed/liquid benchmarks</td></tr><tr><td>Pricing frequency</td><td>Once daily (valuation point)</td><td>Once daily (valuation point)</td><td>Continuous (market hours)</td><td>Continuous (market hours)</td></tr></table><p>Key exam distinction: open-ended fund prices always move in direct proportion to the value of the underlying portfolio; closed-ended (investment trust) share prices are set by market supply and demand and can diverge from NAV. Closed-ended structures give managers more freedom to gear and to hold illiquid/unquoted assets since they never face forced redemptions.</p>"}, {"heading": "5.7 Exchange-Traded Funds (ETFs) and Exchange-Traded Notes (ETNs)", "html": "<p>ETFs combine features of shares (traded in real time on an exchange) and CISs (holding a basket of investments). Most are <strong>passively managed</strong> trackers, though actively managed ETFs have been permitted in the US since the SEC's 2008 authorisation. ETFs have existed since 1993 in the US and 1999 in Europe.</p><p><strong>Mechanics:</strong> only <strong>authorised participants</strong> (large institutions) deal directly with the fund manager, exchanging large blocks — <strong>creation/redemption units</strong> — in kind for baskets of underlying securities. This activity keeps the ETF's intra-day market price close to its NAV. Retail investors trade smaller amounts on the secondary market (eg, NYSE/Arca, LSE), unlike unit trusts/OEICs, which are priced only once a day.</p><table><tr><th>Replication method</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Trade-off</th></tr><tr><td>Physical</td><td>ETF buys/owns most or all index constituents</td><td>Simple, transparent; labour-intensive and costly → higher charges</td></tr><tr><td>Synthetic</td><td>ETF provider enters a swap with a counterparty bank to deliver the index return</td><td>Lower cost, reduced tracking error; introduces <strong>counterparty risk</strong></td></tr></table><p><strong>Related products and risks:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>ETNs</strong> — senior unsecured debt notes issued by banks, trade like ETFs and track indices, but carry issuer credit/counterparty risk and different tax treatment.</li><li><strong>Inverse ETFs</strong> — designed to move opposite to an underlying (eg, TBT tracks the yield, and moves inversely to the price, of 20yr+ US Treasuries; TLT tracks the price of long Treasuries — TBT and TLT move almost exactly inversely to each other).</li><li><strong>Leveraged ETFs</strong> — use derivatives to target 2x or 3x the index return.</li><li><strong>Tracking impact</strong> — large tracker flows can distort prices of index constituents around index inclusion/exclusion events.</li><li><strong>Liquidity risk</strong> — ETF shares are liquid on exchange, but underlying holdings (eg, non-investment-grade bonds, small international equities) can be illiquid, risking large price impact on major redemptions.</li></ul>"}, {"heading": "5.8 Specialist / Alternative Closed-Ended Funds", "html": "<p>Closed-ended structures suit alternative assets — <strong>private equity, property, and infrastructure</strong> — because they turn otherwise illiquid or hard-to-access investments into vehicles available to institutional and retail investors, without the need to sell assets at distressed prices to meet redemptions. The trade-off is that investors may need to commit capital for long periods, and some closed-ended vehicles are not exchange-traded at all, instead offering only periodic redemption windows.</p><p><strong>Private equity funds:</strong></p><ul><li>Usually structured as a <strong>limited partnership</strong>, targeting institutional and high-net-worth investors; ordinary retail investors can gain access via listed, closed-ended funds.</li><li>Invest directly in companies, or indirectly via other funds, providing growth capital or turnaround finance.</li><li>Often employ high levels of debt, creating exposure to interest rate rises and credit squeezes.</li><li>Profits are realised via an <strong>exit</strong> — a trade sale or flotation — the timing of which is opportunistic and hard to predict.</li><li>Highly correlated with the economic cycle: discounts to NAV narrow in stable conditions (easier exits) and widen as the economic environment deteriorates.</li><li>Fund size affects the liquidity of the fund's own shares — relevant when buying or realising an investment.</li></ul>"}], "mcqs": [{"question": "Under the IOSCO definition, a collective investment scheme (CIS) must satisfy which combination of characteristics?", "options": ["It invests in transferable securities, is publicly marketed, and is open-ended", "It is closed-ended, listed on an exchange, and pays a fixed dividend", "It is only available to institutional investors and uses leverage", "It invests solely in unquoted private companies and property"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "IOSCO defines a CIS as an instrument that invests in transferable securities, is publicly marketed, and is open-ended."}, {"question": "In the UK, what distinguishes a 'regulated' CIS from an 'unregulated collective investment scheme' (UCIS)?", "options": ["Regulated schemes are always closed-ended, UCISs are always open-ended", "Regulated schemes are authorised or recognised by the FCA and can be marketed to retail investors; UCISs are not and face marketing restrictions", "UCISs must be constituted by a trust deed; regulated schemes cannot be", "Regulated schemes cannot hold overseas assets"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A scheme is 'regulated' if it is authorised (domestic) or recognised (overseas) by the FCA, permitting retail marketing. UCISs lack this status and face restrictions on marketing, particularly to retail clients."}, {"question": "A client redeeming an investment from a typical unregulated CIS serves notice of redemption. Approximately how long can the total elapsed time be from serving notice to receiving proceeds?", "options": ["24 hours", "One week", "Up to two and a half months", "A minimum of twelve months"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Redemption takes effect at the next available redemption date (often month-end), the investment is sold at the price prevailing at the end of the following month, and proceeds are returned around 14 days later — up to 2.5 months in total."}, {"question": "What is the key structural difference between an open-ended fund and a closed-ended fund?", "options": ["Open-ended funds can only invest in bonds, closed-ended funds only in equities", "An open-ended fund can create and redeem units/shares in response to investor demand, whereas a closed-ended fund has a fixed number of shares traded between investors", "Closed-ended funds must be authorised by the FCA, open-ended funds need not be", "Open-ended funds are always exchange-traded, closed-ended funds are not"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Open-ended funds expand/contract as units are created or cancelled on investor demand. Closed-ended funds issue a fixed number of shares at launch, subsequently traded on the stock market between investors."}, {"question": "Which of the following is an example of an open-ended collective investment scheme?", "options": ["An investment trust", "A private equity limited partnership", "A UK OEIC", "An exchange-traded note (ETN)"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "OEICs are open-ended, alongside unit trusts, US mutual funds and European SICAVs. Investment trusts and private equity vehicles are closed-ended; ETNs are debt instruments, not CISs."}, {"question": "For an authorised unit trust (AUT), what legal document sets out its investment strategy, objectives and permitted investment limits?", "options": ["The prospectus filed with the SEC", "The trust deed made between the manager and the trustee", "The listing particulars filed with the exchange", "The custody agreement"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The trust deed, made between manager and trustee, must clearly state investment strategy, objectives and permitted investment limits for an AUT."}, {"question": "Under FCA rules, what minimum level of capital must a unit trust trustee hold?", "options": ["£500,000", "£1 million", "£4 million", "£10 million"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Trustees of a UK unit trust must be FCA-authorised, independent of the manager, and hold capital in excess of £4 million — hence they are normally large banks or insurers."}, {"question": "A unit trust uses dual pricing with a typical spread between buying and selling prices. What range is typical?", "options": ["0.1%-0.5%", "1%-2%", "5%-7%", "15%-20%"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "A typical spread between buying and selling prices under dual pricing in the market is 5%-7%; alternatively, managers may use single pricing with separately disclosed charges."}, {"question": "Initial charges on actively managed unit trusts are typically in what range, compared with index trackers?", "options": ["0.1-0.5%, higher than trackers", "3-6.5%, higher than trackers", "10-15%, lower than trackers", "There is no typical range; all initial charges are identical"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Initial charges tend to be higher on actively managed funds, often 3-6.5%, with lower initial charges typically levied on index trackers."}, {"question": "What does 'OEIC' stand for, and what alternative acronym is used interchangeably?", "options": ["Open-Ended Investment Company; also known as ICVC (investment company with variable capital)", "Offshore Equity Investment Certificate; also known as SICAV", "Overseas Exchange Investment Corporation; also known as AUT", "Open Equity Income Contract; also known as UCIS"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "An OEIC (pronounced 'oiks') is an Open-Ended Investment Company, also known as an ICVC — investment company with variable capital. The terms are used interchangeably."}, {"question": "An OEIC's underlying investments are valued at £125 million and there are 100 million shares in issue. What is the NAV per share?", "options": ["£0.80", "£1.00", "£1.25", "£12.50"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "NAV per share = total value of underlying investments ÷ number of shares in issue = £125m ÷ 100m = £1.25."}, {"question": "In an OEIC structured as an umbrella fund, what characterises each sub-fund?", "options": ["Sub-funds share a single client register and asset pool with the umbrella", "Each sub-fund has its own separate client register and asset pool, and may adopt different strategies or currencies", "Sub-funds must all be denominated in the same base currency", "Sub-funds cannot be separately priced from one another"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Each sub-fund of an umbrella OEIC has a separate client register and asset pool, and may pursue different investment strategies or be denominated in different currencies."}, {"question": "What is the difference between income shares and accumulation shares in an OEIC?", "options": ["Income shares pay a dividend; accumulation shares retain and reinvest income within net assets", "Accumulation shares pay a dividend; income shares retain income", "Income shares are closed-ended; accumulation shares are open-ended", "There is no difference other than the name"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Income shares pay out a dividend to holders, whereas accumulation shares do not distribute income — it is added to net assets instead."}, {"question": "Which statement about investment trusts is correct?", "options": ["Investment trusts are legally structured as trusts, governed by a trust deed like unit trusts", "Despite their name, investment trusts are companies, not trusts, and make their profit solely from investments", "Investment trusts must redeem shares directly with investors, like an OEIC", "Investment trusts cannot borrow money under any circumstances"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Despite the name, investment trusts are companies, not trusts. Unlike other companies that sell goods/services, they make their profit solely from investments."}, {"question": "Which historic vehicle is regarded as the first investment trust, and when was it founded?", "options": ["Woodford Patient Capital Trust, 2015", "Foreign & Colonial Investment Trust, 1868", "SPDR S&P 500 ETF Trust, 1993", "Schroder UK Public Private Trust, 2019"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The Foreign & Colonial Investment Trust, founded in 1868, was the first investment trust, aiming to give investors of moderate means the same advantages as large capitalists."}, {"question": "An investment trust has assets worth $10 million and liabilities of $4 million, with 12 million ordinary shares outstanding. What is the NAV per share?", "options": ["33 cents", "50 cents", "60 cents", "83 cents"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "NAV = $10m − $4m = $6m. NAV per share = $6m ÷ 12m shares = 50 cents per share."}, {"question": "Why do investment trust share prices commonly trade at a discount to NAV, unlike open-ended fund prices?", "options": ["Because investment trust shares are priced by supply and demand on the stock market, which need not track the underlying portfolio value exactly", "Because investment trusts are required by the FCA to price below NAV", "Because investment trusts hold only cash", "Because open-ended funds are also priced by market supply and demand"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "As a closed-ended, exchange-traded vehicle, an investment trust's share price is set by supply and demand, which can diverge from the underlying NAV — commonly resulting in a discount (or occasionally a premium)."}, {"question": "In the Woodford Patient Capital Trust case study, what ultimately happened to the trust?", "options": ["It converted into an OEIC to allow daily dealing", "It changed manager to Schroders, was renamed (eventually Schroder Capital Global Innovation Trust), and entered an orderly wind-down while trading at a discount", "It merged with the Foreign & Colonial Investment Trust", "It was suspended permanently with no wind-down process"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "After poor performance caused a widening discount, the trust switched manager to Schroders, was renamed twice, and is now in an orderly wind-down, still trading at a discount to NAV."}, {"question": "Compared with authorised open-ended funds such as unit trusts and OEICs, closed-ended investment trusts generally have greater freedom to do which of the following?", "options": ["Offer daily redemption to retail investors only", "Invest in unquoted private companies, provide venture capital, and borrow (gear) to enhance returns", "Avoid all FCA regulation entirely", "Guarantee a fixed dividend regardless of performance"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Closed-ended investment trusts can invest in unquoted companies, provide venture capital, and borrow more freely than open-ended funds, whose powers to borrow are more limited — enabling gearing but also increasing volatility."}, {"question": "Why might an open-ended fund manager be forced to sell portfolio investments at an inopportune time, unlike a closed-ended fund manager?", "options": ["Open-ended funds must pay a fixed annual dividend", "Net redemptions in an open-ended fund reduce the number of units and require the fund to pay out cash, potentially forcing asset sales", "Closed-ended funds are never permitted to hold cash", "Open-ended funds are legally required to liquidate annually"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "If redemptions exceed subscriptions in an open-ended fund, the number of units falls and the fund must pay out cash, which may force the manager to sell investments even when it is not strategically ideal — a risk closed-ended funds do not face."}, {"question": "How do ETF shares typically get created or redeemed?", "options": ["Any retail investor can create or redeem ETF shares directly with the manager at NAV", "Only authorised participants deal directly with the fund manager, exchanging large creation/redemption units, usually in kind for baskets of underlying securities", "ETF shares cannot be created or redeemed once the fund is launched", "Creation and redemption occur only once a year, similar to a private equity fund"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Only authorised participants (typically large institutions) create or redeem ETF shares directly with the manager, in large blocks (creation/redemption units), usually exchanged in kind with underlying securities. Retail investors trade on the secondary market."}, {"question": "What is the main practical difference in pricing between an ETF and a traditional unit trust?", "options": ["ETFs are priced only once a day; unit trusts are priced continuously", "ETFs trade and are priced in real time throughout market hours; unit trusts are typically priced once a day at a valuation point", "There is no difference — both are priced continuously", "Unit trusts are priced by authorised participants; ETFs are priced by trustees"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "ETFs price and trade in real time whenever the market is open, unlike unit trusts and similar CISs, which are typically priced only once at the end of each day."}, {"question": "What is the key difference between physical and synthetic replication in an ETF?", "options": ["Physical replication uses a swap with a counterparty bank; synthetic replication buys the underlying constituents", "Physical replication involves the ETF buying/owning most or all index constituents; synthetic replication uses a swap contract with a counterparty bank, introducing counterparty risk", "There is no cost difference between the two methods", "Synthetic replication is always used for equity ETFs and physical replication only for bond ETFs"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Physical replication buys the actual index constituents (transparent but costly); synthetic replication uses a swap with a counterparty bank to deliver the index return, which can lower costs but introduces counterparty risk."}, {"question": "What distinguishes an exchange-traded note (ETN) from a typical ETF?", "options": ["An ETN is a senior unsecured debt note issued by a bank, carrying issuer/counterparty credit risk, whereas an ETF represents a pooled basket of assets", "An ETN cannot track an index, only individual shares", "ETNs are always physically replicated funds", "ETNs are regulated CISs, while ETFs are not"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "ETNs are structured products issued as senior debt notes by banks. They trade like ETFs and often track indices, but they carry counterparty credit risk to the issuing bank, unlike a fund holding a basket of assets."}, {"question": "An inverse ETF such as TBT, which tracks the yield on 20-year-plus US Treasuries, will typically move in which direction when Treasury bond prices rise?", "options": ["TBT rises, since it moves with bond prices", "TBT falls, since it moves inversely to bond prices (as yields fall when prices rise)", "TBT is unaffected by Treasury bond prices", "TBT tracks the S&P 500 instead"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "TBT tracks Treasury yields, which move inversely to Treasury prices. When bond prices rise, yields fall, and TBT (an inverse/short-yield product) falls."}, {"question": "What is a key liquidity risk associated with certain ETFs, despite the ETF shares themselves being highly liquid on exchange?", "options": ["The ETF manager cannot legally redeem shares", "The underlying holdings (eg, non-investment-grade bonds or small international equities) may be illiquid or OTC-traded, risking a large price impact during major redemptions", "ETFs are not allowed to hold any bonds", "ETF prices cannot be quoted during market hours"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Although ETF shares trade with high liquidity during market hours, the underlying assets can be illiquid; large redemptions may force authorised participants and managers to exit positions at a significant price impact."}, {"question": "Why are private equity, property and infrastructure funds typically structured as closed-ended vehicles?", "options": ["Because regulators prohibit these assets from being held in open-ended funds under any circumstances", "Because the closed-ended structure avoids the need to sell illiquid assets at distressed prices to meet investor redemptions", "Because closed-ended structures are always cheaper to run than open-ended structures", "Because closed-ended funds are exempt from FCA oversight"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The fixed capital pool of a closed-ended fund means the manager is never forced to sell illiquid assets (like private companies or property) at distressed prices to fund redemptions, making it well suited to alternative/illiquid assets."}, {"question": "How do most retail investors typically gain access to private equity investments?", "options": ["By joining the limited partnership directly as a general partner", "Through listed, closed-ended funds providing access to private equity strategies", "Only through structured deposits", "Private equity is entirely inaccessible to retail investors"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Private equity is usually structured as a limited partnership targeting institutional and very wealthy investors, but ordinary retail investors can gain access via listed, closed-ended funds."}, {"question": "What is the typical relationship between economic conditions and the discount on private equity fund shares?", "options": ["Discounts always remain constant regardless of the economic cycle", "Discounts tend to narrow in stable economic environments and widen as the economic environment deteriorates", "Discounts widen in stable environments and narrow in deteriorating environments", "Private equity funds never trade at a discount"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "In stable economic conditions, drivers of outperformance and exit routes are more favourable, so discounts narrow; as conditions deteriorate, discounts tend to widen."}, {"question": "Which of the following is NOT typically listed as a risk of investing in an unregulated collective investment scheme (UCIS)?", "options": ["Illiquidity and long redemption notice periods", "Gearing (leverage) magnifying gains and losses", "Daily FCA-guaranteed compensation for capital losses", "Concentration in a single project reducing diversification"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "UCISs offer no FCA compensation guarantee for capital losses; their key risks include illiquidity, long redemption periods, gearing, currency/political risk and poor diversification."}], "flashcards": [{"front": "What three features define a CIS under the IOSCO definition?", "back": "It invests in transferable securities, is publicly marketed, and is open-ended."}, {"front": "What is the difference between a regulated CIS and a UCIS?", "back": "A regulated CIS is authorised (domestic) or recognised (overseas) by the local regulator and can be marketed to retail investors; a UCIS is not, and faces marketing restrictions, especially to retail clients."}, {"front": "What characterises a UCIS in terms of risk?", "back": "High volatility, illiquidity, high leverage — generally regarded as a speculative investment suitable for only a small part of a portfolio."}, {"front": "How long can the total elapsed time be for redemption from a typical UCIS?", "back": "Up to two and a half months from serving notice to receiving proceeds."}, {"front": "What is a 'high-water mark' in relation to fund performance fees?", "back": "A mechanism ensuring the manager only earns a further performance fee once the fund price surpasses its previous maximum (peak) value."}, {"front": "What is 'gearing' and what is it called in the US?", "back": "Gearing is a fund borrowing money to enhance the funds available for investment, magnifying gains and losses; in the US it is called leverage."}, {"front": "Define an open-ended fund.", "back": "A fund that can issue and redeem shares or units at any time; price is directly proportional to the value of the underlying portfolio."}, {"front": "Define a closed-ended fund.", "back": "A fund with a fixed number of shares issued at launch, subsequently traded between investors on a stock market; capital does not expand or contract with investor demand."}, {"front": "Name three examples of open-ended CISs from different jurisdictions.", "back": "UK OEICs, US mutual funds, and mainland Europe's SICAVs (sociétés d'investissement à capital variable)."}, {"front": "What document constitutes an authorised unit trust (AUT)?", "back": "A trust deed made between the manager and the trustee."}, {"front": "What are the FCA requirements for a unit trust trustee?", "back": "Must be FCA-authorised, fully independent of the manager, and hold capital in excess of £4 million (normally large banks or insurers)."}, {"front": "What is the primary duty of a unit trust trustee?", "back": "To protect the interests of the unitholders (the beneficiaries of the trust)."}, {"front": "List four responsibilities of a unit trust manager.", "back": "Marketing the trust; managing assets per the trust deed; maintaining the unit register for trustee inspection; supplying required information; informing the FCA of regulatory breaches."}, {"front": "What is the typical spread between buying and selling prices for a dual-priced unit trust?", "back": "5%-7%."}, {"front": "What is 'single pricing' for a unit trust?", "back": "The same price is quoted for buying and selling units, with charges separately disclosed."}, {"front": "What are the three types of unit trust charges?", "back": "Initial charge (upfront), annual management charge (periodic), and exit charge (on sale, often only within the first 3-5 years, sliding scale)."}, {"front": "Typical initial charge range on actively managed unit trusts?", "back": "3-6.5% (lower for index trackers)."}, {"front": "Typical annual management charge (AMC) range?", "back": "0.5-1.5% of the underlying fund."}, {"front": "What does OEIC stand for and what is its alternative name?", "back": "Open-Ended Investment Company; also called an ICVC (investment company with variable capital)."}, {"front": "How is the NAV per share of an OEIC calculated?", "back": "Value of underlying investments ÷ number of shares in issue. E.g., £125m ÷ 100m shares = £1.25 per share."}, {"front": "What is an OEIC umbrella fund?", "back": "An OEIC structured with several separately priced sub-funds, each with its own client register, asset pool, and possibly different strategy or currency."}, {"front": "Distinguish income shares from accumulation shares.", "back": "Income shares pay a dividend to holders; accumulation shares retain and reinvest income within net assets rather than distributing it."}, {"front": "Are investment trusts legally trusts?", "back": "No — despite the name, investment trusts are companies, not trusts, and their sole business is investing (unlike companies selling goods/services)."}, {"front": "Which was the first investment trust and when was it founded?", "back": "The Foreign & Colonial Investment Trust, founded in 1868."}, {"front": "What are the offer price, bid price and spread for an investment trust?", "back": "Offer price = higher price at which investors buy; bid price = lower price at which holders sell; spread = the difference between the two."}, {"front": "How is an investment trust's NAV calculated?", "back": "Listed investments at mid-market prices + unlisted investments at directors' valuation + cash/net current assets − liabilities (including preference capital at nominal value) = NAV for ordinary shareholders."}, {"front": "Investment trust: assets $10m, liabilities $4m, 12m shares outstanding — what is NAV per share?", "back": "NAV = $6m; NAV per share = $6m ÷ 12m = 50 cents."}, {"front": "Why do investment trust shares commonly trade at a discount to NAV?", "back": "Because their price is set by market supply and demand rather than tracking NAV directly, and demand often falls short of matching underlying asset values."}, {"front": "What happened to Woodford Patient Capital Trust?", "back": "Launched 2015; traded at a premium then a widening discount; changed manager to Schroders, renamed twice (finally Schroder Capital Global Innovation Trust), and is now in an orderly wind-down, still at a discount."}, {"front": "What investment freedoms do closed-ended investment trusts have that open-ended unit trusts/OEICs generally lack?", "back": "They can invest in unquoted private companies, provide venture capital, and borrow (gear) more freely to enhance returns."}, {"front": "Why can closed-ended fund managers take a longer-term view than open-ended fund managers?", "back": "Because the number of shares in issue is unaffected by day-to-day investor buying/selling, so there is no forced selling of assets to meet redemptions."}, {"front": "How do authorised participants keep an ETF's market price close to its NAV?", "back": "By trading creation/redemption units directly with the fund manager (usually exchanged in kind for baskets of underlying securities) and acting as market makers on the open market."}, {"front": "How does ETF pricing differ from unit trust pricing?", "back": "ETFs trade and are priced in real time throughout market hours; unit trusts are typically priced once daily at a valuation point."}, {"front": "Distinguish physical from synthetic ETF replication.", "back": "Physical replication buys/owns the actual index constituents (transparent but costly). Synthetic replication uses a swap contract with a counterparty bank to deliver index returns (cheaper, but introduces counterparty risk)."}, {"front": "What is an ETN and what risk does it carry that a fund does not?", "back": "An exchange-traded note is a senior unsecured debt note issued by a bank, tracking an index; it carries counterparty/issuer credit risk since it is a liability of the issuing bank."}, {"front": "What is a key liquidity risk of certain ETFs?", "back": "ETF shares are highly liquid on exchange, but underlying holdings (eg, illiquid or OTC bonds) may not be, risking large price impact during major redemptions."}, {"front": "Why are private equity, property and infrastructure funds usually closed-ended?", "back": "The closed-ended structure avoids the need to sell illiquid assets at distressed prices to meet investor redemptions, though investors must accept long lock-up periods."}, {"front": "How do most retail investors access private equity?", "back": "Via listed, closed-ended funds, since direct private equity limited partnerships target institutional and very wealthy investors."}]}, {"id": "sec-m6", "number": 6, "title": "Primary Markets & New Issues", "examWeight": "Core module", "summaryHtml": "\n<p><strong>Primary markets</strong> are where new securities are created and sold for the first time; <strong>secondary markets</strong> are where existing securities are subsequently bought and sold. In the primary market, <strong>issuers</strong> (companies raising capital, or selling shareholders such as governments in privatisations) are the suppliers of new securities, while the principal purchasers are large <strong>institutional investors</strong> (pension funds, insurers, ETFs, mutual funds), with individuals able to subscribe directly via a brokerage. An IPO that attracts more orders than shares available is <strong>oversubscribed</strong>. The secondary market provides <strong>liquidity</strong>: existing holders trade with willing buyers, spreads (ask minus bid) narrow as liquidity improves, and activity is boosted by short-term speculators and <strong>high-frequency trading (HFT)</strong>. Each jurisdiction has a <strong>governing/listing authority</strong>: in the UK this is the <strong>FCA</strong> (UK Listing Rules; the LSE runs day-to-day trading but the FCA can suspend a listing); in the US the <strong>SEC</strong> registers issuers and requires ongoing filings; in China the <strong>CSRC</strong> plus the exchanges themselves regulate new listings.</p>\n<p>A company seeking its first listing chooses between an <strong>IPO</strong> (offer for sale, fixed-price or tender/strike-price basis), a <strong>placing</strong> (selective marketing to chosen clients — cheapest, least democratic, no underwriting needed), or an <strong>introduction</strong> (no shares issued or sold — purely to gain secondary-market liquidity, eg Spotify 2018). An already-listed company can raise further capital through a <strong>follow-on (secondary) offering</strong>, which is quicker/cheaper than an IPO because the prospectus process is already familiar. All these deals may carry a <strong>greenshoe/over-allotment option</strong> (up to an extra 15% of the base deal, named after Green Shoe Manufacturing Co) used for price stabilisation, and may be <strong>underwritten</strong> on a <strong>firm</strong> (guaranteed) or <strong>best-efforts</strong> (no guarantee) basis. <strong>SPACs</strong> (blank-cheque companies) offer an alternative route to going public via a two-year-life shell that merges with a private target (\"de-SPAC\"), often backed by a further PIPE funding round. <strong>Convertible</strong> and <strong>exchangeable bonds</strong> are hybrid debt/equity instruments giving bondholders the right to convert into the issuer's shares (convertible) or another company's shares held by the issuer (exchangeable), issued more cheaply than plain debt because of the equity upside.</p>\n<p>Bringing a company to market needs an <strong>origination team</strong>: a <strong>sponsor/listing agent</strong> (nominated adviser or \"nomad\" on AIM) assesses suitability and coordinates the prospectus; a <strong>corporate broker</strong> liaises with the market; <strong>reporting accountants</strong> and <strong>legal advisers</strong> provide due diligence; PR consultants manage perception. Listed companies accept ongoing obligations — corporate governance and regular (audited annual, half-yearly) reporting. For large deals, a <strong>syndicate</strong> of banks is assembled, led by a <strong>lead manager/book runner</strong> (sometimes co-leads by region), which conducts <strong>book-building</strong> via roadshows to set a price range, then a final price. <strong>Underwriting</strong> guarantees the issuer a minimum outcome; <strong>stabilisation</strong> (typically via the greenshoe) lets the lead manager support the price for a limited period post-listing, subject to FCA disclosure rules.</p>\n<p>Each exchange sets <strong>admission criteria</strong>: the UK Main Market (Official List, FCA Listing Rules) requires a plc, sponsor, 3-year trading record, £30m minimum market cap, 10% free float, prospectus and fee; AIM (LSE-regulated, less stringent) requires only a nomad and broker, with no minimum size, track record or free float. Frankfurt offers General Standard, Prime Standard (highest transparency, English-language) and Scale (SME) segments. Bond issuers include supranationals, sovereigns, sub-sovereigns/municipals (often guaranteed by monoline insurers), agencies (Fannie Mae, Sallie Mae), corporates and financial institutions/SPVs. Bonds are issued via traditional single issuance, or flexible <strong>shelf registration</strong> supporting MTN programmes, sold via <strong>auction</strong> (DMO gilts — successful bidders pay their own bid price) or <strong>tender</strong> (all successful bidders pay the lowest accepted/strike price), or via <strong>reverse inquiry</strong>. A bond origination team runs through pitching (\"beauty pageant\"), indicative bid, mandate announcement, credit rating, roadshow, listing/prospectus and syndication.</p>\n", "sections": [{"heading": "6.1 Users, Motivations and the Governing Authority (Primary & Secondary Markets)", "html": "\n<p><strong>Users of the primary market:</strong> issuers (companies undertaking IPOs/follow-ons, and debt issuers) supply new securities. Principal buyers are large institutional investors (pension funds, insurance companies, collective investment vehicles including ETFs and mutual funds) buying on behalf of clients/the general public; individuals can also subscribe directly via a brokerage. Heavy demand can cause an issue to be <strong>oversubscribed</strong>, so not every applicant receives their full order (or any allocation).</p>\n<p><strong>Users of the secondary market:</strong> the same institutions trade existing holdings as portfolio priorities shift (a large part of daily volume on exchanges such as the LSE and NYSE). Also present are short-term speculators/traders (who may hold positions for seconds to hours and often use leverage) and members of the public via brokerages, robo-advisers or direct-investing platforms. Increasingly, <strong>algorithmic trading</strong> and <strong>high-frequency trading (HFT)</strong> dominate secondary-market activity, exploiting transitory price discrepancies with rapid execution.</p>\n<p><strong>Motivations:</strong> the primary market lets issuers raise capital (expansion, acquisitions) and lets investors allocate capital to attractive opportunities. Proceeds do not always go to the issuer — in an offer for sale of existing shares (eg government privatisations, or Saudi Aramco's 2019 IPO selling a government stake), proceeds go to the <strong>selling shareholder</strong>. Buying newly issued shares purely to resell quickly amid IPO hype is called <strong>flipping</strong> — an extreme form of short-term trading. The secondary market's key benefit is <strong>liquidity</strong>: sellers can exit and buyers can enter readily; narrow bid/ask spreads and HFT activity are hallmarks of a liquid market, providing depth beyond long-term institutional reallocation.</p>\n<p><strong>Role of the governing authority:</strong> each jurisdiction has rules for gaining (and retaining) a listing. UK: the <strong>Financial Conduct Authority (FCA)</strong> is the competent authority for listing decisions (which shares/bonds, including gilts, may be admitted to trade on the LSE), set out in the <strong>UK Listing Rules</strong>. The LSE itself runs the exchange/secondary trading, but the FCA can suspend a listing (removing secondary trading). US: the <strong>SEC</strong> requires registration/disclosure before listing on NYSE/Nasdaq, plus ongoing filings (financial/trading performance). China: the <strong>China Securities Regulatory Commission (CSRC)</strong> regulates new listings, alongside significant authority held by the Shanghai, Shenzhen and Beijing exchanges themselves.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "6.2 Initial Public Offerings (IPOs) and SPACs", "html": "\n<p>An <strong>IPO</strong> (\"going public\") is a company's first public sale of shares, previously held by a small group (founders, family, PE/VC funds). New shareholders gain voting rights (eg electing the board), so original owners give up significant control; public shareholders can also freely sell on the secondary market. Advantages of an IPO: can raise substantial capital, generates publicity, and the capital raised is <strong>equity/risk capital</strong> — company assets are not encumbered/hypothecated as they would be under debt financing.</p>\n<p><strong>Structure:</strong> a base number of shares plus an optional <strong>greenshoe/over-allotment clause</strong> allowing extra shares to be issued if demand is strong (terms set out in the underwriting documents).</p>\n<p><strong>Three stages of an IPO:</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Decision</strong> — issuer and advisers (notably the investment bank) weigh the pros/cons of a public offer.</li><li><strong>Prospectus preparation</strong> — the official document detailing the company and IPO terms; prepared with investment bank, reporting accountants and legal advisers.</li><li><strong>Marketing and sale</strong> — the lead-managing investment bank (possibly with a syndicate of co-managers) sells the securities to clients.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Underwriting of the offer:</strong> <strong>firm underwriting</strong> = a guarantee to buy any unsold shares; <strong>best-efforts underwriting</strong> = the underwriter/co-managers try their best to sell but give no formal guarantee, avoiding financial loss on a shortfall but risking reputational damage and reduced future mandates.</p>\n<h4>Special Purpose Acquisition Companies (SPACs)</h4>\n<p>SPACs (\"blank cheque companies\") are listed shells, typically with a two-year life, formed solely to merge with a private company to take it public — a quicker/easier alternative to a traditional IPO. Investors may withdraw their investment (cash back plus interest) once a merger target is identified. Key stages: (1) a <strong>sponsor</strong> team sets up the SPAC and funds initial expenses, receiving <strong>founder shares</strong> (~20% of the SPAC); (2) capital is raised from investors (often hundreds of $m), redeemable with interest if no merger completes in time; (3) investors may also redeem if they dislike the eventual merger target/terms; (4) the SPAC lists, typically offering units of shares plus warrants (US standard: shares $10, warrants exercisable at $11.50, tradeable separately); (5) the SPAC has up to two years to find and agree a target; (6) once terms are agreed, a further funding round markets the deal to institutional investors — a <strong>PIPE deal</strong> (private investment in public equity); (7) investor approval is sought and, if granted, the merger completes (<strong>\"de-SPAC\"</strong>) — the SPAC invests its funds (plus PIPE proceeds) in the target, its governance is replaced, and it is renamed as the operating company. Example: BowX Acquisition Corp (SPAC) merged with WeWork in October 2021, raising $402.5m at IPO plus an $800m PIPE, injecting $1.3bn into WeWork; WeWork later filed for Chapter 11 in 2023 and re-emerged as a private company in 2024. The world's biggest ever IPO was Saudi Aramco (December 2019), raising ~$25.6bn (rising to ~$29.4bn once the greenshoe was exercised) by selling around 1.5% of the company (a base 3bn shares) to mainly local/regional investors via a 25-bank underwriting syndicate.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "6.3 Follow-on Offerings", "html": "\n<p>A <strong>follow-on offering</strong> (secondary offer) lets an already-listed company raise further capital. It is only pursued when equity markets are sufficiently robust — in a falling/bear market there is unlikely to be sufficient demand at the price wanted. Like an IPO it has a base number of shares plus an optional greenshoe. Being quicker, easier and cheaper than an IPO (the company has already been through the process), it follows the same three broad stages: (1) decision to issue additional shares; (2) prospectus preparation (relatively easy, as one exists already); (3) marketing and sale, lead-managed by an investment bank possibly with a co-manager syndicate. As with an IPO, a follow-on may be <strong>underwritten</strong> (firm undertaking to buy any shortfall) or sold on a <strong>best-efforts</strong> basis (no guarantee, common for higher-risk securities), or a combination of both. Example: Saudi Aramco's June 2024 follow-on offer sold a base 1.545bn shares at SAR27.25, with a 10% greenshoe (154.5m shares) exercised, taking total proceeds to $12.355bn.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "6.4 Offers for Sale", "html": "\n<p>An <strong>offer for sale</strong> is the most common of the three main first-time listing methods (offer for sale, placing, introduction). The issuing company sells its shares to an <strong>issuing house</strong> (usually an investment bank), which then invites public applications at a slightly higher price, supported by a detailed <strong>prospectus/offer document</strong>. The document is prepared by the company's directors and assessed by the sponsor for regulatory suitability. Offers for sale need not involve newly created shares — they are often used to release existing owners'/founders' equity, or for government privatisation programmes.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Basis</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Allocation on oversubscription</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>Fixed price</strong></td><td>Price set just below expected full-subscription level to encourage active secondary trading; applicants apply for a set number of shares at the fixed price.</td><td>Scaling down applications, or randomly satisfying a proportion of applications in full (method specified in offer document).</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Tender (strike price)</strong></td><td>No fixed price; issuer sets a minimum tender price; investors state quantity and price they are willing to pay.</td><td>A single settlement/<strong>strike price</strong> is set once the offer closes, satisfying all bids at or above that price. More efficient/maximises proceeds but more complex to administer — hence less common than fixed price.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h4>Over-allotment options (greenshoe)</h4>\n<p>Named after the Green Shoe Manufacturing Company (1919, now Stride Rite Corporation) — the first company permitted to use this practice. Legally an <strong>over-allotment option</strong>: gives underwriters the right to sell up to an additional <strong>15%</strong> of the original share number at the IPO price if demand exceeds the original offer. Used as a stabilisation tool — smoothing price fluctuations on strong demand, or supporting the price in adverse conditions. Example: ABC plans to sell 100,000 shares with a 15,000-share (15%) greenshoe; if 115,000 shares are found via book-building and the price holds/rises post-listing, the bank exercises the greenshoe to cover the extra 15,000; if the price falls, the bank buys back 15,000 shares in the market instead (greenshoe not exercised).</p>\n"}, {"heading": "6.5 Selective Marketing, Placing, Private Placements and Direct Listings", "html": "\n<p>In a <strong>placing</strong>, the company markets the issue directly to a broker, issuing house or other financial institution, which places the shares with <strong>selected clients</strong> — hence also called <strong>selective marketing</strong>. It is the least democratic of the three IPO routes (the general public has no initial access) but the <strong>least expensive</strong>, since the prospectus is less detailed than for an offer for sale/introduction and <strong>no underwriting is required</strong>. Placings can be used for IPOs or follow-on/secondary issues; where pre-emptive shareholder rights apply, a shareholders' resolution is needed.</p>\n<h4>Private placements</h4>\n<p>A special provision allows offerings to a restricted class of <strong>sophisticated/qualified/accredited investors</strong> (and the banks advising them) with either no formal prospectus or much lighter disclosure than a public offer requires. UK: governed by the Prospectus Regulations (reflecting the EU Prospectus Directive). US: SEC exemptions, most commonly <strong>Regulation D (\"Reg D\")</strong>.</p>\n<h4>Direct listings</h4>\n<p>An alternative to a traditional IPO where there are <strong>no underwriters</strong> — the company itself creates demand at first trading, and the trading price is set purely by actual demand at listing (no stabilisation activity). Suited to companies that do not need to raise capital (eg already cash-flow positive) and are already well understood by investors, so do not need banks to \"educate\" the market. Example: Spotify listed directly on the NYSE in April 2018 — existing shares only, no new issuance, no underwriters; opened at $165.90 (a $29.5bn valuation).</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>New shares raised?</th><th>Underwriting?</th><th>Public access?</th><th>Cost/complexity</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Offer for sale</td><td>Often, but can be existing shares (eg privatisations)</td><td>Usually yes</td><td>Yes — general public</td><td>Highest (detailed prospectus)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Placing</td><td>Yes or existing</td><td>No</td><td>No — selected clients only</td><td>Lowest (less detailed prospectus)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Introduction</td><td>No shares sold/issued</td><td>No</td><td>N/A — no marketing operation</td><td>Low — just gains a listing</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Direct listing</td><td>No underwriters; price set by demand</td><td>No</td><td>Yes, direct to public</td><td>Lower than IPO — no bank marketing</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n"}, {"heading": "6.6 Introductions", "html": "\n<p>An <strong>introduction</strong> is not actually an issue of securities at all — it is simply a way for a company to gain access to secondary-market trading on an exchange, common on many emerging-market exchanges. Unlike most listings (used to raise funds, or to meet minimum free-float rules), an introduction is used by a company that does <strong>not need to raise capital</strong> but wants the added liquidity a listing provides. Typical candidates: a company already listed on an overseas exchange, a newly merged entity formed from two previously listed companies, or a demutualised organisation. Because no funds are raised, an introduction is <strong>not a marketing operation</strong> in the way an offer for sale or placing is.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "6.7 Exchangeable/Convertible Bond Offerings", "html": "\n<p>Both are hybrid instruments combining bond and equity characteristics.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Convertible bond</th><th>Exchangeable bond</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Pays coupon, has nominal/redemption value</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Conversion right into</td><td>Shares of the <strong>bond issuer itself</strong></td><td>Shares of <strong>another company</strong>, held by the issuer</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Both give the holder coupon/repayment safety plus potential equity upside, and allow the issuer to raise borrowed funds more cheaply (the conversion upside compensates for a lower coupon). Structure mirrors an equity offering — a base amount of bonds plus a possible greenshoe. Stages mirror an IPO: (1) decision to raise capital via convertible/exchangeable; (2) prospectus preparation detailing terms; (3) marketing/sale, lead-managed by an investment bank with a possible co-manager syndicate; may be firm- or best-efforts-underwritten. Example: XYZ Co. holds ABC plc shares and issues 6% exchangeable bonds redeeming in 5 years; £100 nominal converts into 20 ABC shares — the holder will convert only if ABC shares trade above £5 each (£100 ÷ 20) at redemption.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "6.8 Listing Advisers and Continuing Obligations", "html": "\n<p>A company seeking a listing must appoint advisers (precise roles set by local/exchange regulations):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sponsor / listing agent</strong> — usually an investment bank, stockbroker or accountancy practice; assesses suitability for listing, the best route to market, and coordinates prospectus production. On the LSE's <strong>AIM</strong>, this role is called the <strong>nominated adviser (\"nomad\")</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Reporting accountants</strong> — attest to the validity of the prospectus's financial information.</li>\n<li><strong>Legal advisers</strong> — ensure all relevant matters are covered and statements justified. Together with reporting accountants they provide <strong>due diligence</strong> on the prospectus.</li>\n<li><strong>PR consultants</strong> — optimise public perception ahead of listing.</li>\n<li><strong>Corporate broker</strong> — interfaces between the company and the market/investors; advises on market conditions and sentiment; may be the same firm as the listing agent.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Continuing obligations</strong> for listed issuers cover: <strong>corporate governance</strong> (laws, rules, customs and internal/external policies guiding direction/management — eg splitting chairman/CEO roles, appointing non-executive directors (NEDs), a qualified finance director) and <strong>reporting</strong> (audited annual accounts, plus less detailed half-yearly or quarterly reports) to keep investors informed.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "6.9 Deal Syndication, Underwriting and Stabilisation", "html": "\n<h4>Deal syndication</h4>\n<p>For large listings, the sponsor assembles a <strong>syndicate</strong> of investment banks/brokers to market shares to institutional and retail clients. The sponsoring bank generally acts as <strong>lead manager</strong>, appointing co-managers; very large deals may have multiple lead managers (<strong>co-leads</strong>), often split by geography. The syndicate gauges institutional interest and, with the issuer, agrees an indicative <strong>price range</strong> (the gap between top and bottom can be 10–15%). Marketing then proceeds via <strong>roadshows</strong>, conferences and investor meetings. Gathering subscription requests to gauge overall demand is <strong>book-building</strong>, coordinated by the lead manager acting as <strong>book runner</strong>; the final price is set just before listing.</p>\n<h4>Underwriting</h4>\n<p>Underwriting is an agreement with financial institutions (banks, insurers, asset managers) that they will buy the shares if public demand is insufficient — effectively insuring the issue's success. Underwriters also help determine demand/pricing and assist distribution. The guaranteed buy price may be at a discount to the public offer price (eg offered at £5, underwritten at £4.75). Benefit to issuer: guaranteed minimum proceeds. Risk to underwriter: may be forced to buy shares worth less than paid; in return, underwriters earn fees regardless of outcome.</p>\n<h4>Stabilisation</h4>\n<p>Stabilisation prevents a substantial post-issue price fall by having the lead manager buy back newly issued securities in the market if price falls below a predefined level, giving the market time to absorb the extra supply, reducing volatility and discouraging panic-selling; bought-back securities are resold over time. The typical stabilisation tool is the <strong>greenshoe</strong> — issuing more shares if demand is strong, or not exercising it (buying back instead) if demand is weak. Regulation: the FCA requires <strong>disclosure</strong> to the market that stabilisation is occurring and that the price may not be representative. Exchanges can also use <strong>circuit breakers</strong> to suspend trading temporarily during volatility.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "6.10 Admission Criteria for Listing", "html": "\n<p>Stock exchange criteria ensure only viable, well-governed, solvent, transparent and reputable companies list, protecting investor confidence. Listing is typically a <strong>two-stage process</strong>: (1) filing a <strong>prospectus</strong> with the regulator (coordinated by the sponsor, supported by reporting accountants and legal advisers); (2) an application to the <strong>exchange</strong> itself, which checks for sufficient <strong>free float</strong> (shares held outside directors/dominant investors) and market-maker/bank support.</p>\n<p>Developed markets typically run a senior (\"main\") market alongside a junior market for smaller/faster-growing companies: UK — <strong>Main Market</strong> (FCA Official List) vs <strong>AIM</strong>; Hong Kong — <strong>Main Board</strong> vs <strong>Growth Enterprise Market</strong>. The FCA must recognise an exchange before it can operate in the UK (LSE = a <strong>Recognised Investment Exchange, RIE</strong>), assessing its systems/controls and setting Official List admission rules; the LSE separately sets its own membership/trading rules.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>UK Main Market (Official List)</th><th>UK AIM</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Regulator/rulebook</td><td>FCA — UK Listing Rules</td><td>LSE's own AIM rules</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Company form</td><td>Must be a plc, represented by a sponsor</td><td>No plc/sponsor requirement as such; requires a <strong>nomad</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Trading record</td><td>Minimum 3 years</td><td>No minimum period</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Market capitalisation</td><td>At least £30 million</td><td>No minimum</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Free float (public hands)</td><td>At least 10% (excludes directors/associates and 5%+ holders)</td><td>No restriction</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Prospectus</td><td>Required</td><td>Required (lighter than Official List)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Key adviser(s)</td><td>Sponsor</td><td>Nominated adviser (\"nomad\") + broker (can be same firm)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Share transferability</td><td>N/A specified</td><td>No restriction on transferability required</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Consequence of losing adviser</td><td>N/A</td><td>Shares suspended immediately; removed from AIM after 1 month without a broker/adviser</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Debt securities minimum (aggregate market value)</td><td>At least £200,000</td><td>N/A</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Continuing obligations</td><td>Half-yearly report + annual accounts; notify price-sensitive information</td><td>Same continuing obligations apply</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Note: UK Listing Rules were amended in summer 2024 to permit greater flexibility for dual-class share structures for commercial companies.</p>\n<h4>Frankfurt Stock Exchange (FWB) — an EU/exchange-regulated example</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>General Standard</strong> (EU-regulated): for medium/large corporations seeking cost-effective access to national investors — audited prospectus (BaFin-compliant), ≥3 years reporting history, ≥€1.25m probable total price value, ≥10,000 shares admitted, ≥25% free float.</li>\n<li><strong>Prime Standard</strong> (EU-regulated): the premium segment — highest transparency requirements in Europe; all requirements must be met in English; attractive for issuers targeting international investors.</li>\n<li><strong>Scale</strong> (exchange-regulated): for German/European SMEs seeking growth capital.</li>\n</ul>\n"}, {"heading": "6.11 Bond Issuance: Types of Issuer and Methods", "html": "\n<h4>Types of issuer</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Supranationals</strong> — eg World Bank, Asian Development Bank.</li>\n<li><strong>Governments (sovereigns)</strong> — long-term borrowing via bonds, eg UK gilts, US Treasury bonds.</li>\n<li><strong>Sub-sovereigns</strong> — may offer tax advantages to resident investors.</li>\n<li><strong>Municipals</strong> — states/cities (US \"munis\") financing local borrowing, often tax-efficient and guaranteed by a third party (US: <strong>monoline insurer</strong>), enhancing credit quality/terms.</li>\n<li><strong>Agencies</strong> — often government-backed, for specific purposes, eg Fannie Mae (mortgage finance) and Sallie Mae (student loans) in the US.</li>\n<li><strong>Corporates</strong> — large companies use bonds (including convertibles) to lower borrowing costs for capex and other needs.</li>\n<li><strong>Financial institutions / SPVs</strong> — issue bonds to finance borrowing; also arrange <strong>off-balance-sheet finance</strong> via special purpose vehicles (SPVs), particularly used in asset-backed securities markets.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Methods of issuance</h4>\n<p>Historically, bond issuance only made sense for large single capital raises (to justify issuance costs), marketed via <strong>auction</strong> or <strong>tender</strong>. Companies needing regular funding often preferred pre-arranged bank credit lines instead. <strong>Shelf registration</strong> (a US innovation, now widely adopted) allows a single regulatory registration to cover multiple bond issues over up to two years, heavily used in the <strong>medium-term note (MTN) market</strong> (typically 2–10 year maturities), letting issuers tap the market flexibly as demand dictates. A <strong>reverse inquiry</strong> is where a dealer's client requests a specific maturity/coupon MTN, and the issuer decides whether to accept.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Price paid by successful bidders</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>Auction</strong> (current DMO/UK gilt method)</td><td>DMO announces auction, receives bids, allocates to highest bidders; GEMMs expected to bid; DMO can retain unsold gilts on its own book.</td><td>Each bidder pays the <strong>price they individually bid</strong> (non-uniform pricing).</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Tender</strong> (UK gilt method pre-1987)</td><td>DMO sets a minimum price; investors bid; gilts awarded to highest bidders.</td><td>All successful bidders pay a single common <strong>strike price</strong> — the highest price at which all gilts on offer can be sold.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h4>Role of retail/commercial banks — bond origination team stages</h4>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Pitching</strong> — competing banks present to the issuer (\"beauty pageant\"); issuer selects bank(s).</li>\n<li><strong>Indicative bid</strong> — banks estimate the finance likely to be raised on the proposed terms.</li>\n<li><strong>Mandate announcement</strong> — the issuer announces which bank(s) have been mandated.</li>\n<li><strong>Credit rating</strong> — from a rating agency or investment bank; vital to how much can be raised; may need credit enhancement (eg insurance) for a higher rating; some institutional investors (pension funds/endowments) are restricted from holding lower-rated bonds.</li>\n<li><strong>Roadshow</strong> — visits to potential buyers across major financial centres.</li>\n<li><strong>Listing</strong> — prospectus prepared/submitted if the bond is to be listed.</li>\n<li><strong>Syndication</strong> — for larger issues, a lead manager (primary issuer contact) plus co-managers (often by geographic region) form the syndicate selling into their client bases.</li>\n</ol>\n"}], "mcqs": [{"question": "Who are the principal suppliers of new securities in the primary market?", "options": ["Retail brokerages", "Issuers of new securities, such as companies undertaking IPOs or follow-on offerings", "Central banks", "Stock exchanges"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Issuers, including corporates issuing shares via IPOs/follow-ons and issuers of debt instruments, are the main suppliers of new securities in primary markets."}, {"question": "In the UK, which body is the competent authority responsible for listing decisions?", "options": ["The London Stock Exchange (LSE)", "The Bank of England", "The Financial Conduct Authority (FCA)", "HM Treasury"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The FCA is the UK's competent authority for listing, setting the UK Listing Rules; the LSE operates the exchange and secondary trading, but the FCA can suspend a listing."}, {"question": "In the US, which regulator requires companies to register and file ongoing reports in order to list on exchanges such as the NYSE and Nasdaq?", "options": ["The Federal Reserve", "The Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC)", "FINRA only", "The Treasury Department"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The SEC requires registration/disclosure before listing and ongoing filings (financial and trading performance) once listed."}, {"question": "A short-term trading strategy where an investor buys shares in a newly issued IPO and quickly sells them amid market excitement is known as:", "options": ["Flipping", "Stabilisation", "Book-building", "Shelf registration"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Flipping is buying a new issue and selling it shortly afterwards, an extreme form of short-term trading, often seen with highly publicised IPOs."}, {"question": "What is the greenshoe (over-allotment) option typically limited to, as a percentage of the original number of shares offered?", "options": ["5%", "10%", "15%", "25%"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The greenshoe/over-allotment option gives underwriters the right to sell up to an additional 15% of the original offer at the IPO price if demand is high."}, {"question": "What are the three broad stages of an IPO?", "options": ["Marketing, listing, delisting", "Decision, prospectus preparation, marketing and sale", "Pitching, roadshow, syndication", "Registration, auction, settlement"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "An IPO progresses through: (1) the decision to raise capital via an IPO, (2) preparation of the prospectus, and (3) marketing and sale of the securities."}, {"question": "Under a 'best efforts' underwriting arrangement, what is the underwriter's obligation?", "options": ["To guarantee purchase of any unsold shares", "To do its best to sell the shares, with no formal guarantee of full placement", "To buy back all shares at the offer price after six months", "To set the final listing price unilaterally"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Best-efforts underwriting involves no unconditional commitment to purchase unplaced securities; the underwriter avoids immediate monetary loss but risks reputational damage if the sale fails."}, {"question": "What is a SPAC (Special Purpose Acquisition Company) alternatively known as?", "options": ["A unit investment trust", "A blank cheque company", "A monoline insurer", "A gilt-edged market maker"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "SPACs are alternatively referred to as 'blank cheque companies' — listed shells formed to merge with a private company to take it public."}, {"question": "In a typical SPAC structure, what proportion of the SPAC do the sponsors' 'founder shares' usually represent?", "options": ["Around 5%", "Around 20%", "Around 50%", "Around 80%"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "In return for funding the SPAC's initial operating expenses, sponsors receive founder shares typically representing around 20% of the SPAC."}, {"question": "What term describes the process where a SPAC completes its merger with the target company?", "options": ["Book-building", "De-SPAC", "Stabilisation", "Reverse inquiry"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "'De-SPAC' describes the completion of the merger: the SPAC invests its funds in the target, its governance is dissolved and replaced, and it is renamed as the operating entity."}, {"question": "What does 'PIPE' stand for in the context of SPAC funding rounds?", "options": ["Public Investment in Private Equity", "Private Investment in Public Equity", "Pooled Institutional Public Equity", "Primary Issue of Public Equity"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A PIPE deal (private investment in public equity) is a further funding round used to help finance the SPAC's merger with its target."}, {"question": "Why is a follow-on (secondary) offering typically quicker, easier and cheaper than an IPO for the same company?", "options": ["Because follow-ons never require underwriting", "Because the company has already been through the listing/prospectus process before", "Because follow-ons are exempt from prospectus rules entirely", "Because follow-ons do not require an investment bank"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Since the company is already listed and has previously prepared a prospectus, a follow-on offering is relatively straightforward compared with an IPO."}, {"question": "In an offer for sale, who typically buys the shares from the issuing company before they are offered to the public?", "options": ["The stock exchange", "The issuing house (usually an investment bank)", "The FCA", "Retail investors directly"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "In an offer for sale, the issuing company sells its shares to an issuing house (typically an investment bank), which then invites public applications at a slightly higher price."}, {"question": "In a tender offer for sale, how is the final settlement (strike) price determined?", "options": ["It equals the average of all bids received", "It is fixed by the exchange before the offer opens", "It is set to satisfy all applications tendered at or above that price, once the offer closes", "It is always set at the minimum tender price"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "In a tender offer, once the offer closes a single settlement price is determined that satisfies all applications tendered at, or above, that price."}, {"question": "Why is a placing (selective marketing) generally the least expensive of the three main first-time listing methods?", "options": ["Because it always avoids the need for a sponsor", "Because the prospectus required is less detailed and no underwriting is needed", "Because it is exempt from all regulatory requirements", "Because placings only ever apply to bonds, not shares"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "In a placing, shares are placed with selected clients via a broker/issuing house; the prospectus is less detailed than for an offer for sale or introduction, and no underwriting is required."}, {"question": "Which type of investor is typically eligible to participate in a private placement without a full public prospectus?", "options": ["Any member of the public", "Sophisticated, qualified or accredited investors", "Only pension funds", "Only government entities"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Private placements are marketed to a restricted class of sophisticated, qualified or accredited investors, with either no formal prospectus or much lighter disclosure requirements."}, {"question": "What is a key distinguishing feature of a direct listing compared with a traditional IPO?", "options": ["It always raises new capital for the company", "There are no underwriters, and the trading price is set by actual demand at listing", "It requires a longer prospectus than an IPO", "It can only be used by companies going public for the first time on AIM"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "In a direct listing, there is no underwriting; demand is generated by the company itself, and the trading price is set based on actual demand at listing with no stabilisation activity."}, {"question": "What is the defining feature of an 'introduction' as a listing method?", "options": ["It raises the maximum possible amount of new capital", "It involves selling shares only to institutional investors", "No shares are actually issued or sold — it simply gains the company secondary-market access", "It requires the highest level of underwriting of the three methods"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "An introduction is not an issue at all; it is a way for a company to gain access to secondary-market trading without raising capital, so it is not a marketing operation."}, {"question": "What is the key difference between a convertible bond and an exchangeable bond?", "options": ["Convertible bonds pay no coupon, exchangeable bonds do", "A convertible bond converts into the issuer's own shares; an exchangeable bond converts into shares of another company held by the issuer", "Exchangeable bonds have no redemption date", "Convertible bonds can only be issued by governments"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A convertible bond gives the right to convert into shares of the bond's own issuer, while an exchangeable bond gives the right to exchange into shares of another company that the issuer holds."}, {"question": "On the LSE's AIM market, what is the sponsor/listing agent role commonly referred to as?", "options": ["The book runner", "The nominated adviser ('nomad')", "The monoline insurer", "The gilt-edged market maker"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "On AIM, the listing agent/sponsor role is referred to as the nominated adviser, or 'nomad'."}, {"question": "What is the primary function of a corporate broker in the listing process?", "options": ["To audit the company's financial statements", "To act as an interface between the company and the stock market/investors, advising on market conditions", "To set the company's credit rating", "To act as the sole regulator of the exchange"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The corporate broker advises the company on market conditions, sentiment and general direction, acting as an interface between the company and the market/investors."}, {"question": "What is 'book-building' in the context of a large syndicated share issue?", "options": ["The process of auditing the company's balance sheet before listing", "The process of gathering subscription requests from investors to gauge demand across the syndicate", "A method used solely for gilt auctions", "The process of registering a bond prospectus with the SEC"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Book-building is the process by which the syndicate gathers investor demand for the issue, coordinated by the lead manager acting as book runner."}, {"question": "In deal syndication for a very large issue, what term describes multiple lead managers each responsible for a geographic region?", "options": ["Co-leads", "Nomads", "GEMMs", "Reverse inquirers"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Very large issues may warrant more than one lead manager, described as co-leads, each potentially responsible for a particular geographical area."}, {"question": "What is the primary purpose of stabilisation activity following a new issue?", "options": ["To maximise the underwriters' profit through arbitrage", "To prevent a substantial fall in the price of newly issued securities by supporting the price in the market", "To increase the free float requirement", "To eliminate the need for a listing agent"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Stabilisation is designed to prevent a substantial price fall by having the lead manager buy back newly issued securities if the price falls below a predefined level, giving the market time to adjust."}, {"question": "What does the FCA require in relation to stabilisation activity in the UK?", "options": ["That stabilisation is banned outright", "Disclosure to the market that stabilisation is occurring and that the price may not be representative", "That only retail investors may participate in stabilised trades", "That stabilisation must continue indefinitely"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "UK FCA rules require disclosure to the market that stabilisation is taking place and that the market price may not be a representative one because of that activity."}, {"question": "Under the UK Listing Rules, what is the minimum expected market capitalisation for a commercial company to be admitted to the Official List?", "options": ["£10 million", "£30 million", "£100 million", "£200 million"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The main requirements for admission to the UK Official List include an expected market capitalisation of at least £30 million."}, {"question": "Which of the following is NOT a requirement for admission to AIM, unlike the Official List?", "options": ["Appointment of a nominated adviser", "Appointment of a broker", "A minimum market capitalisation", "No restriction on share transferability"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Unlike the Official List, AIM has no minimum market capitalisation, no minimum trading history and no minimum free-float requirement — it only requires a nomad, a broker, and unrestricted share transferability."}, {"question": "On AIM, what happens if a company is without a broker or nominated adviser for one month?", "options": ["Nothing changes", "The company's shares are automatically re-priced", "The company is removed from AIM", "The company is fined but remains listed"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "If an AIM company is without a broker or nominated adviser for a period of one month, it is removed from AIM (trading is suspended immediately if it loses either at any time)."}, {"question": "Which Frankfurt Stock Exchange segment is aimed specifically at German and European SMEs seeking growth capital?", "options": ["Prime Standard", "General Standard", "Scale", "Main Board"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Scale is the exchange-regulated segment targeting German and European small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) seeking capital for growth."}, {"question": "Which type of bond issuer is exemplified by the World Bank or the Asian Development Bank?", "options": ["Municipal", "Agency", "Supranational", "Corporate"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Supranational organisations such as the World Bank and Asian Development Bank raise money by issuing bonds."}, {"question": "What is 'shelf registration' in the context of bond issuance?", "options": ["A requirement to register each bond issue separately with the regulator every time", "A process allowing a single registration to cover multiple bond issues over a period of up to two years", "A rule restricting municipal bond issuance to domestic investors only", "A method of allocating gilts at auction"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Shelf registration, a US innovation now widely adopted, enables a single registration to be used for a number of bond issues over a period of up to two years, heavily used in the MTN market."}, {"question": "In the UK gilt auction method used by the DMO, what price does a successful bidder pay?", "options": ["A single common strike price for all successful bidders", "The price they individually bid", "The average of all bids submitted", "A price set in advance by the government"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Under the auction method, successful bidders each pay the price they bid, unlike the tender method where all successful bidders pay a common strike price."}, {"question": "What is a 'reverse inquiry' in the context of the MTN market?", "options": ["A request by the issuer for a credit rating from an agency", "A request from a dealer's client for a specific maturity/coupon MTN, which the issuer can accept or decline", "The process of returning unsold gilts to the DMO", "A regulator's request for additional prospectus disclosure"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A reverse inquiry occurs when a dealer's client requests a particular maturity and coupon for an MTN; the issuer then decides whether to accept the terms and issue the bonds."}], "flashcards": [{"front": "Who supplies new securities in the primary market?", "back": "Issuers — companies undertaking IPOs/follow-on offerings and issuers of debt instruments."}, {"front": "Who are the principal purchasers of newly issued securities?", "back": "Large institutional investors (pension funds, insurance companies, ETFs, mutual funds), buying on behalf of clients; individuals can also subscribe via a brokerage."}, {"front": "What is the UK's competent authority for listing decisions?", "back": "The Financial Conduct Authority (FCA), under the UK Listing Rules; the LSE runs day-to-day exchange trading."}, {"front": "What is the US regulator for listings and ongoing disclosure?", "back": "The Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) — requires registration before listing and ongoing filings after."}, {"front": "What is a greenshoe / over-allotment option?", "back": "An option allowing underwriters to sell up to an additional 15% of the original offer at the IPO price if demand is strong; used for price stabilisation."}, {"front": "What are the three broad stages of an IPO?", "back": "1) Decision, 2) Prospectus preparation, 3) Marketing and sale."}, {"front": "What is 'firm' underwriting?", "back": "A guarantee by the underwriter(s) to buy any shares not sold to the public."}, {"front": "What is 'best efforts' underwriting?", "back": "The underwriter agrees to try its best to sell the offering, with no formal guarantee of full placement."}, {"front": "What is a SPAC?", "back": "A Special Purpose Acquisition Company ('blank cheque company') — a listed shell, typically with a 2-year life, formed to merge with a private company to take it public."}, {"front": "What are 'founder shares' in a SPAC?", "back": "Shares given to the sponsors in return for funding initial operating expenses, typically around 20% of the SPAC."}, {"front": "What is 'de-SPAC'?", "back": "The completion of the SPAC's merger with its target: funds are invested in the target, governance is replaced, and the entity is renamed."}, {"front": "What is a follow-on offering?", "back": "An already-listed company issuing further shares to raise capital; also called a secondary offer. Follows the same 3 stages as an IPO but is quicker/cheaper."}, {"front": "What is an offer for sale?", "back": "The company sells shares to an issuing house (usually an investment bank), which invites public applications at a slightly higher price via a prospectus."}, {"front": "Fixed-price offer for sale: how is oversubscription handled?", "back": "By scaling down applications, or by randomly satisfying a proportion of applications in full."}, {"front": "Tender offer for sale: how is the price set?", "back": "Investors bid a quantity and price above a set minimum; a single strike price is set after close, satisfying all bids at or above it."}, {"front": "What is a placing?", "back": "A company markets its issue directly to a broker/issuing house/institution, which places shares with selected clients — also called selective marketing."}, {"front": "Who can invest in a private placement?", "back": "Sophisticated, qualified or accredited investors, under lighter disclosure rules (no full public prospectus needed)."}, {"front": "What is a direct listing?", "back": "A listing route with no underwriters; the company creates its own demand, and the trading price is set by actual demand at listing, with no stabilisation."}, {"front": "What is an introduction (as a listing method)?", "back": "Not an issue at all — a way for a company to gain secondary-market access without raising capital or issuing/selling shares."}, {"front": "Convertible bond — conversion right into whose shares?", "back": "The bond issuer's own shares."}, {"front": "Exchangeable bond — conversion right into whose shares?", "back": "Shares of another company (not the issuer), which the issuer holds."}, {"front": "What is a sponsor/listing agent called on AIM?", "back": "The nominated adviser, or 'nomad'."}, {"front": "What is the role of the corporate broker?", "back": "Acts as interface between the company and the stock market/investors; advises on market conditions and sentiment."}, {"front": "What do reporting accountants and legal advisers together provide?", "back": "Due diligence on the prospectus — ensuring accuracy and regulatory compliance."}, {"front": "What is book-building?", "back": "The process of gathering investor subscription requests to gauge demand, coordinated by the lead manager acting as book runner."}, {"front": "What is stabilisation?", "back": "The lead manager buying back newly issued securities in the market if the price falls below a set level, to prevent a substantial price fall post-issue."}, {"front": "What must the FCA require to be disclosed during stabilisation activity?", "back": "That stabilisation is taking place and that the market price may not be representative because of it."}, {"front": "UK Official List: minimum market capitalisation requirement?", "back": "At least £30 million."}, {"front": "UK Official List: minimum free float (shares in public hands)?", "back": "At least 10% (excludes directors/associates and holders of 5%+)."}, {"front": "UK Official List: minimum trading record required?", "back": "At least 3 years of operating history."}, {"front": "What two advisers must an AIM company appoint?", "back": "A nominated adviser (nomad) and a broker (can be the same firm)."}, {"front": "What happens to an AIM company without a broker/nomad for one month?", "back": "It is removed from AIM (shares are suspended immediately upon losing either adviser)."}, {"front": "Frankfurt Stock Exchange: which segment has the highest transparency requirements and requires English disclosure?", "back": "The Prime Standard."}, {"front": "Frankfurt Stock Exchange: which segment targets SMEs?", "back": "Scale (exchange-regulated segment for German/European SMEs)."}, {"front": "What guarantees credit quality on US municipal bonds?", "back": "A monoline insurer (a third-party guarantor)."}, {"front": "Name two US agency bond issuers mentioned in the text.", "back": "Fannie Mae (mortgage finance) and Sallie Mae (student loan finance)."}, {"front": "What is shelf registration?", "back": "A single regulatory registration covering multiple bond issues over up to two years, widely used in the MTN market."}, {"front": "UK gilt auction method: what price do successful bidders pay?", "back": "The price they individually bid (non-uniform)."}, {"front": "UK gilt tender method (pre-1987): what price do successful bidders pay?", "back": "A single common strike price — the highest price at which all gilts on offer could be sold."}, {"front": "What is a reverse inquiry?", "back": "A request from a dealer's client for a specific maturity/coupon MTN, which the issuer can choose to accept or decline."}, {"front": "What is the 'beauty pageant' in bond origination?", "back": "The competitive pitching stage where banks present to the issuer to win the mandate."}, {"front": "List the 7 stages of bond origination.", "back": "Pitching, indicative bid, mandate announcement, credit rating, roadshow, listing, syndication."}]}, {"id": "sec-m7", "number": 7, "title": "Secondary Markets, Trading & Indices", "examWeight": "Core module", "summaryHtml": "<p>Secondary markets are where already-issued securities change hands. <strong>Developed markets</strong> (US, UK, Japan, Germany) are dominated by regulated exchanges plus alternative venues introduced under MiFID: <strong>multilateral trading facilities (MTFs)</strong>, <strong>systematic internalisers (SIs)</strong>, and (post-MiFID II) <strong>organised trading facilities (OTFs)</strong> for non-equity instruments. Off-exchange OTC trading, <strong>dark pools</strong> (anonymous, price/size hidden until after execution) and <strong>private placements</strong> (Regulation D in the US) supplement exchange trading. <strong>Emerging/frontier markets</strong> are less liquid and sophisticated; China's SSE/SZSE, with A-shares (mainland) and H-shares (Hong Kong), illustrate rapid but structurally complex development.</p><p>Trading systems are <strong>quote-driven</strong> (market makers post firm two-way prices, e.g. Nasdaq) or <strong>order-driven</strong> (buy/sell orders matched directly on an order book, e.g. NYSE, TSE, LSE large-caps); hybrid systems (LSE's SETSqx) combine both. Key participants include <strong>market makers</strong> (obliged to quote continuous two-way prices), <strong>inter-dealer brokers (IDBs)</strong> (anonymous agency between dealers, settle as principal, cannot take positions), <strong>prime brokers</strong> (stock lending, margin financing, cash management, custody, rehypothecation for hedge funds), and <strong>systematic internalisers</strong>. <strong>Algorithmic trading</strong> removes emotion and speeds execution; <strong>high-frequency trading (HFT)</strong>, aided by co-location and low latency, accounts for over 50% of volume but is blamed for the May 2010 and April 2013 flash crashes and criticised for manipulative strategies and systemic contagion risk.</p><p>Exchange order books rank orders by <strong>price then time priority</strong>. Opening auctions use an uncrossing algorithm to set a single opening price; automatic execution can be interrupted by price-tolerance circuit breakers. Order types include market, limit, iceberg, fill-or-kill, execute-and-eliminate, and all-or-none orders, with unmatched balances creating multiple fills or residuals. A <strong>central counterparty (CCP)</strong> interposes itself between buyer and seller via <strong>novation</strong>, reducing counterparty/default risk, preserving anonymity, enabling netting, and improving settlement efficiency and liquidity, in exchange for a fee and margin. Trading costs include the bid-offer spread, commission (full-service vs discount brokers), account fees, and stamp duty (0.5% in the UK on share purchases). Listed companies disclose price-sensitive information via <strong>primary information providers (PIPs)</strong> such as the LSE's RNS, which feed <strong>secondary information providers (SIPs)</strong> like Bloomberg and Refinitiv.</p><p><strong>Stock indices</strong> are mostly <strong>capitalisation-weighted</strong> (larger companies have greater influence), with the DJIA a rare <strong>price-weighted</strong> exception (sum of prices ÷ Dow Divisor). Indices exist at national level (FTSE 100, S&P 500, Nikkei 225, DAX, CAC 40) and sector level (Nasdaq Biotechnology, FTSE EPRA/NAREIT). <strong>Total return indices</strong> reinvest dividends (price return excludes dividends; total/gross return reinvests gross dividends; net total return reinvests after withholding tax). <strong>Free-float adjustment</strong> excludes strategic/insider/restricted holdings (generally 5%+ stakes), rounding the free-float factor to the nearest 5%, giving a more investable benchmark.</p><p><strong>Government bond markets</strong> rely on issuing agencies (UK DMO, US Treasury, Japan MoF, EU Commission/national treasuries), <strong>primary dealers</strong> (UK GEMMs, obliged to make two-way prices and bid at auctions in exchange for exclusive DMO access), broker-dealers, and gilt IDBs. <strong>Corporate bonds</strong> trade in a decentralised OTC dealer market (unlike centralised equity exchanges), priced off the equivalent government bond yield plus a credit spread; trading methods include voice, IDB-brokered, and electronic platforms (dealer-to-dealer: MTS Cash, BrokerTec; dealer-to-customer RFQ: Tradeweb, MTS BondVision). Bond pricing reflects issuer factors (credit rating, seniority, YTM) and market factors (liquidity, quote type, repo market depth).</p>", "sections": [{"heading": "7.1 Developed Markets: Exchanges, Trading Venues & Roles", "html": "<p>Stock exchanges (NYSE, Nasdaq, LSE, FWB, TSE) evolved from informal physical meeting places into regulated, largely electronic venues that provide <strong>liquidity</strong>, enable <strong>price formation/price discovery</strong>, and match buyers with sellers via brokers. Many exchanges now offer bonds, futures, options and ETFs alongside equities and are termed 'securities exchanges' or 'exchange groups' after consolidation.</p><p><strong>Broker vs dealer:</strong> a <strong>broker</strong> arranges deals for clients (dealing as agent) and earns commission, never taking ownership of the securities. A <strong>dealer</strong> buys/sells for its own account (dealing as principal), profiting from price movement. Firms doing both are <strong>broker-dealers</strong>.</p><p><strong>MiFID venue classification (EU):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Regulated markets</strong> — traditional stock exchanges.</li><li><strong>Multilateral trading facilities (MTFs)</strong> — bring together third-party buy/sell interests outside a regulated market.</li><li><strong>Systematic internalisers (SIs)</strong> — investment firms dealing on own account against client orders on an organised, frequent, systematic, substantial basis.</li><li><strong>Organised trading facilities (OTFs)</strong> — added by MiFID II (2018) for non-equity instruments (bonds, derivatives).</li></ul><p>In the US, equivalent venues are 20+ national securities exchanges (dominated by NYSE and Nasdaq) and 75+ alternative trading systems (ATS).</p><p><strong>OTC trading</strong> is dealer-to-dealer trading outside formal exchange procedures. <strong>Dark pools</strong> are private trading systems enabling anonymous trading; price/size are not displayed until after execution (opposite of 'lit pools'); in the US they are a form of ATS, in Europe a form of MTF. <strong>Private placements</strong> (non-public offerings, e.g. US Regulation D exemption from SEC registration) allow companies — often pre-IPO tech firms — to raise capital from sophisticated/institutional investors without a prospectus (illustrated by WeWork's Series A–H funding rounds).</p>"}, {"heading": "7.2 Emerging and Frontier Markets", "html": "<p>Emerging and frontier markets are less developed than mature markets; <strong>frontier markets</strong> are even less sophisticated, smaller and less liquid than emerging markets ('pre-emerging'). China illustrates rapid but structurally distinctive development: the <strong>Shanghai Stock Exchange (SSE)</strong> (large-cap, SOEs, banks, energy) and <strong>Shenzhen Stock Exchange (SZSE)</strong> (small/mid-cap) both began trading in 1990 and are now among the world's largest by market cap; the <strong>Beijing Stock Exchange (BSE)</strong> launched in 2021. China has multiple share classes: <strong>A-shares</strong> (mainland-listed, gradually opened to foreign investors via quotas) and <strong>H-shares</strong> (mainland companies listed in Hong Kong, popular with global investors). <strong>Shanghai-Shenzhen Connect</strong> lets foreign investors access A-shares via the Hong Kong Stock Exchange.</p><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Developed Markets</th><th>Emerging/Frontier Markets</th></tr><tr><td>Liquidity</td><td>High</td><td>Lower (frontier lowest)</td></tr><tr><td>Regulation/infrastructure</td><td>Mature, sophisticated</td><td>Developing</td></tr><tr><td>Trading venues</td><td>Multiple exchanges + MTFs/SIs/dark pools</td><td>Mostly a single dominant exchange</td></tr><tr><td>Foreign access</td><td>Generally open</td><td>Often restricted/quota-based</td></tr><tr><td>Examples</td><td>US, UK, Japan, Germany</td><td>China (emerging), smaller African/Asian markets (frontier)</td></tr></table>"}, {"heading": "7.3 Quote-Driven vs Order-Driven Trading Systems", "html": "<p><strong>Quote-driven systems:</strong> market makers agree to buy and sell at least a minimum number of shares at quoted prices — the <strong>bid</strong> (buying price) and <strong>offer</strong> (selling price). Nasdaq is the prime example. Market makers provide continuous liquidity even when order flow is thin.</p><p><strong>Order-driven systems:</strong> investors (or agents) submit orders stating quantity and price; the system matches buyers and sellers directly with no obligatory market maker. Examples: NYSE, TSE (Japan), and trading in the largest LSE stocks. Liquidity depends entirely on the presence of matching orders — order-driven markets can lack liquidity if orders are insufficient.</p><p><strong>Hybrid systems</strong> combine both features, e.g. the LSE's SETSqx (Stock Exchange Electronic Trading Service — quotes and crosses).</p><p>Orders awaiting matching sit in the <strong>order book</strong>: the buy side lists bids, the sell side lists offers; new orders are checked against the opposite side for a match.</p>"}, {"heading": "7.4 Market Participants: Market Makers, IDBs, Prime Brokers, Systematic Internalisers", "html": "<p><strong>Market makers</strong> apply to the exchange, disclose the securities they will deal in, and must quote two-way prices for a minimum size throughout the trading day, smoothing volatility and providing liquidity. They need sufficient capital and skilled staff given the added risk.</p><p><strong>Inter-dealer brokers (IDBs)</strong> are exchange members registered to act as agent between dealers (e.g. market makers) so that a dealer's identity remains hidden. The IDB acts as agent but <strong>settles as if it were principal</strong> to preserve anonymity; it cannot take principal positions and must be a separate firm, not a division of a broker-dealer.</p><p><strong>Equity and fixed income prime brokers</strong> provide hedge funds with: <strong>stock lending</strong> (to cover shorts), <strong>margin financing</strong> (leveraged trading), <strong>cash management</strong>, <strong>trade execution/settlement</strong>, <strong>custody</strong> (including corporate action processing), and hold a <strong>right of rehypothecation</strong> — the right to re-use, re-charge or dispose of a client's pledged assets (e.g. lending them on or using them as collateral for cheaper funding); rehypothecated assets become the property of the prime broker while so used.</p><p><strong>Systematic internalisers (SIs)</strong> — MiFID term for investment firms dealing on own account against client orders on an organised, frequent, systematic, substantial basis instead of routing to other venues.</p>"}, {"heading": "7.5 Algorithmic Trading and High-Frequency Trading (HFT)", "html": "<p><strong>Algorithmic trading</strong> (algo-trading, automated/black-box trading) uses pre-built platforms to buy/sell automatically when predefined conditions are met. Reasons for its popularity: (1) <strong>removal of emotion/discipline</strong> — predefined entry/exit rules prevent overtrading or panic; (2) <strong>speed and cost</strong> — faster execution, fewer manual-entry errors, lower cost — though glitches/data errors can cause rapid, large losses.</p><p><strong>High-frequency trading (HFT)</strong> is a major sub-category using powerful, co-located computers to transmit huge numbers of small orders that are rapidly reversed for a small 'turn' per trade; estimated to represent 50%+ of exchange turnover. <strong>Latency</strong> (time to interact with the market) is minimised via <strong>co-location</strong> (servers physically close to/in the exchange's data centre).</p><p><strong>Criticisms of HFT:</strong> seen as 'vultures' exploiting long-term investors; dominant market share gives outsized pricing impact; blamed (SEC/CFTC report) for the <strong>6 May 2010 'flash crash'</strong> (DJIA fell ~600 points in 5 minutes, recovered in ~20 minutes, triggered by S&P futures selling and a CME circuit breaker) and the <strong>23 April 2013 'mini flash crash'</strong> (hoax tweet about an attack on the White House, DJIA fell ~130 points); potential for market manipulation (e.g. quote stuffing/spoofing near the market then withdrawing on execution); and systemic risk from arbitrage spreading price shocks across linked exchanges.</p>"}, {"heading": "7.6 Exchange Rules, Order Book Procedures and Order Types", "html": "<p><strong>Order book priority:</strong> orders are ranked first by <strong>price</strong>, then by <strong>time</strong> of entry. The best (highest) buy price and best (lowest) sell price sit at the top and are matched/executed first.</p><p><strong>Opening auctions:</strong> before trading starts, member firms submit orders (no trading yet); an <strong>uncrossing algorithm</strong> executes overlapping orders at the single price that maximises volume traded, simultaneously setting the opening price. Automatic execution then continues; a <strong>trading halt</strong> (price tolerance breach, typically 5%–25% depending on exchange/share) can temporarily suspend automatic execution to let investors react to large price moves. The exchange can also suspend a security's listing entirely (trading halt) at its discretion; pre-suspension unsettled trades still settle normally.</p><p>Viewing the order book requires no membership, but interacting with it (placing orders) does; non-members may need a paid data subscription to see beyond best bid/offer.</p><table><tr><th>Order Type</th><th>Key Feature</th></tr><tr><td>Market order</td><td>No price specified; executes immediately at best available price (variants: market-on-open (MOO), market-on-close (MOC))</td></tr><tr><td>Limit order</td><td>Price limit and time limit specified (e.g. 'sell at 360 by Tuesday'); can be partially filled; only limit orders are displayed on the order book; default good for the day if no time limit given</td></tr><tr><td>Iceberg order</td><td>Large order with only a small visible 'tip' on the order book; hidden bulk is released in tranches as each tranche executes, reducing market impact</td></tr><tr><td>Execute and eliminate (at best)</td><td>Executes as much as possible immediately at a specified price, cancels the unfilled remainder</td></tr><tr><td>Fill or kill</td><td>Entire order filled immediately at the specified price or better, otherwise entirely cancelled</td></tr><tr><td>All or none</td><td>Must be filled entirely or not at all; no partial/multiple fills permitted</td></tr></table><p><strong>Multiple fills</strong> occur when one order is matched against several opposing orders at different prices (weighted average price paid). A <strong>residual</strong> is the unfilled balance of a partially matched limit order, which the broker may continue working or the client may cancel.</p>"}, {"heading": "7.7 The Central Counterparty (CCP) and Costs of Trading", "html": "<p>A <strong>central counterparty (CCP)</strong> interposes itself between the two original parties to a trade through <strong>novation</strong>: the single A-to-B contract is replaced by two new contracts (A-to-CCP and CCP-to-B). If either A or B defaults, only the CCP is directly affected.</p><p><strong>Benefits of a CCP:</strong> (1) <strong>reduced counterparty risk</strong> — the well-capitalised, insured CCP absorbs default risk, lowering systemic risk; (2) <strong>anonymity</strong> — neither side discovers the original counterparty; (3) <strong>operational efficiency</strong> — all trades settle with one CCP; (4) <strong>netting</strong> — receipts/payments in the same security settling the same day can be netted; (5) <strong>improved pricing</strong> — greater willingness to transact anonymously improves price formation. The CCP charges a flat fee and requires margin from participants.</p><p><strong>Costs of trading:</strong> the <strong>bid-offer spread</strong> (implicit cost); <strong>commission</strong> (percentage of value or flat fee — higher for full-service/advisory brokers, lower/zero for discount brokers, generally higher in less-developed markets); <strong>account fees</strong> for custody/administration (often waived above a minimum trading threshold); and <strong>stamp duty</strong>, a government transaction tax on the buyer (0.5% on UK electronic share purchases, also levied in Hong Kong and Switzerland; typically not levied in the US; some instruments like ETFs may be exempt).</p><p><strong>Regulatory disclosure:</strong> listed companies must disclose price-sensitive information promptly (UK: FCA Disclosure Guidance and Transparency Rules; EU: Transparency Directive) via a <strong>primary information provider (PIP)</strong>, e.g. the LSE's <strong>Regulatory News Service (RNS)</strong>, which processes ~300,000 UK announcements a year. PIPs disseminate information to <strong>secondary information providers (SIPs)</strong> such as Bloomberg, Refinitiv and Dow Jones, who pass it to the wider market (brokers, analysts) to inform research.</p>"}, {"heading": "7.8 Stock Market Indices: Purpose and Construction/Weighting Methodologies", "html": "<p>A stock market index measures the performance of a defined segment of the market (national, sector, or global) and is used as a benchmark for portfolio performance and general market sentiment. Most indices are <strong>market-capitalisation-weighted</strong> (constituent importance based on shares in issue × price); a minority, notably the <strong>DJIA</strong>, are <strong>price-weighted</strong> (weighted purely by per-share price).</p><table><tr><th>Weighting Method</th><th>Description</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td>Capitalisation-weighted (full)</td><td>Weighted by total market cap</td><td>Nikkei-style variants, many national indices</td></tr><tr><td>Free-float capitalisation-weighted</td><td>Weighted by market cap adjusted for free-float factor (excludes strategic/insider/restricted holdings)</td><td>S&P 500, FTSE 100, TOPIX, TASI</td></tr><tr><td>Price-weighted</td><td>Weighted purely by share price; divided by an adjusted divisor</td><td>DJIA (Dow)</td></tr><tr><td>Modified market-value-weighted</td><td>Cap-weighted with adjustments/caps on individual constituent influence</td><td>Nasdaq-100</td></tr></table><p><strong>DJIA calculation:</strong> Index = Σ(prices of 30 component stocks) ÷ Dow Divisor. The divisor is adjusted for stock splits, spin-offs and similar structural changes so such events don't distort the index level; the current divisor is less than one, so the index value exceeds the raw sum of prices. Criticism: a $1 move in a low-priced stock has the same index impact as a $1 move in a high-priced stock, ignoring market cap/industry size — critics prefer the free-float cap-weighted S&P 500 or Wilshire 5000.</p><p><strong>S&P 500 eligibility:</strong> US company, minimum market cap (~$22.7bn, 2025), highly liquid, public float ≥10% of shares outstanding, positive recent-quarter and trailing-four-quarter earnings; weighted by free-float market cap; 504 stocks (multiple share classes) represent 500 companies.</p>"}, {"heading": "7.9 National and Sector Indices, Total Return Indices, Free-Float", "html": "<p><strong>National indices</strong> reflect the performance/sentiment of a nation's stock market and its largest listed companies (FTSE 100 — UK, DJIA/S&P 500/Wilshire 5000 — US, Nikkei 225/TOPIX — Japan, DAX — Germany, CAC 40 — France, Hang Seng — Hong Kong). <strong>Sector indices</strong> track specific industries, e.g. FTSE EPRA/NAREIT (real estate), Nasdaq Biotechnology Index. Broader benchmarks include MSCI World/Emerging Markets and STOXX Europe 600.</p><p><strong>Total return index versions</strong> (illustrated by the S&P 500's three variants):</p><ul><li><strong>Price return</strong> — measures only price performance; dividends excluded.</li><li><strong>Total return (gross)</strong> — reflects price performance plus reinvestment of gross dividends on the ex-dividend date.</li><li><strong>Net total return</strong> — reflects dividend reinvestment after deduction of withholding tax.</li></ul><p><strong>Free-float</strong> is the estimated proportion of a company's shares available for public trading, excluding: shares held by 5%+ owners (government, institutional, strategic, founder/executive/insider holdings), restricted stock, and long-term insider holdings. The <strong>free-float factor</strong> (rounded to the nearest 5%) is applied to full market cap to derive free-float market cap — e.g. a 23.2% free float is rounded to 25% for index inclusion. Free-float weighting better reflects the investable universe and is used by most major indices (S&P 500, FTSE series, TASI). Example: SABIC has ~SAR 180bn full market cap but only ~22% free float (Saudi Aramco holds 70%), hugely reducing its index weight versus full-cap weighting.</p>"}, {"heading": "7.10 Participants in Government Bond Markets", "html": "<p>Beyond the government itself, four groups facilitate government bond market activity: (1) the <strong>government's issuing agency</strong>, (2) <strong>primary dealers</strong>, (3) <strong>broker-dealers</strong>, (4) <strong>inter-dealer brokers</strong>.</p><p><strong>UK:</strong> the <strong>Debt Management Office (DMO)</strong> issues gilts; the LSE oversees secondary market rules. <strong>Gilt-edged market makers (GEMMs)</strong> are primary dealers vetted by the DMO, obliged to: make effective two-way prices to customers up to an agreed size; bid competitively at DMO auctions (informally underwriting them); and report closing prices/positions/turnover to the DMO. In return GEMMs get exclusive rights to bid directly at auctions, trade as DMO counterparty in secondary operations, and exclusive access to gilt IDBs. GEMMs can register for all gilts, gilts excluding index-linked, or index-linked only. <strong>Broker-dealers</strong> (non-GEMM LSE members) deal as principal or agent, subject to best execution; must flag 'small' deals (&lt;£1m nominal) to GEMMs. <strong>Gilt IDBs</strong> arrange anonymous deals between GEMMs, act as agent but settle as principal, cannot take principal positions, and must be a separate firm.</p><p><strong>EU:</strong> the European Commission issues euro-denominated EU-Bonds/EU-Bills (e.g. funding NextGenerationEU, Western Balkans Reform and Growth Facility, Macro-Financial Assistance, SAFE defence instrument). Eurozone member states (e.g. Germany, France) each issue their own sovereign debt at their own credit rating. <strong>Germany</strong>: Bubills (6/12mo), Schatz (2yr), Bobls (5yr), Bunds (10/30yr); multiple-price auctions restricted to EU-domiciled credit institutions, who must subscribe ≥0.05% of annual issuance to remain in the auction group. <strong>France</strong>: OATs (2–50yr, mostly fixed-rate) and BTFs (short-term discount T-bills, weekly auctions); bid-price (multiple-price) auction system open to Euroclear France-affiliated, Banque de France account-holding institutions; BTANs (2/5yr) were phased out by 2017.</p><p><strong>US:</strong> Treasury Department auctions bills (&lt;1yr), notes (2,3,5,7,10yr, interest every 6 months), and bonds (30yr) via appointed primary dealers; the NY Fed regulates primary dealer banks. Cash management bills (CMBs) are irregular short-term issues sold when Treasury cash is low.</p><p><strong>Japan:</strong> Ministry of Finance (MoF) issues JGBs across maturities from 2 to 40 years (including inflation-indexed and floating-rate); semi-annual interest, principal at maturity; separate strips programme available.</p>"}, {"heading": "7.11 Corporate Bond Markets: Trading Methods and Pricing Factors", "html": "<p>Corporate bonds are priced off the equivalent government bond yield less a discount (i.e. plus a credit spread) reflecting default risk. Unlike centralised equity exchanges, corporate bonds trade in a <strong>decentralised dealer market</strong> — multiple 'pools of liquidity' with dealers providing liquidity by standing ready to buy/sell, rather than exchange-based market makers. Key differences from equities: bonds have a defined coupon income stream and bondholders rank senior to equity holders on insolvency; equity dividends are discretionary/variable and shareholders rank last on liquidation. A modest <strong>retail bond market</strong> also exists via exchanges, e.g. the LSE's <strong>Order Book for Retail Bonds (ORB)</strong>, an order-driven service for gilts, supranational and corporate bonds with continuous two-way market-maker pricing (typical minimum denomination £1,000).</p><p><strong>Bond trading methods:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Dealer-to-dealer</strong>: direct telephone contact; via an IDB (voice-broked); or via an electronic inter-dealer platform (e.g. MTS Cash, BrokerTec).</li><li><strong>Dealer-to-customer</strong>: voice trading; or via electronic platforms (Tradeweb, MTS BondVision, single-dealer bank platforms, Bloomberg FIT, Liquidnet, MarketAxess).</li></ul><p><strong>Trend:</strong> electronic trading has grown fastest in liquid government bonds (US Treasuries, European sovereigns, JGBs), reducing reliance on voice brokers. Dealer-to-customer platforms increasingly use a <strong>request for quote (RFQ)</strong> model — investors solicit simultaneous quotes from multiple dealers, improving speed, competition and price.</p><p><strong>Factors influencing bond pricing:</strong></p><p><em>Issuer factors:</em> credit rating; seniority/structure of the issue (e.g. secured/mortgage-backed vs subordinated); yield to maturity (YTM) relative to benchmark government yields; macroeconomic/credit environment (contagion, risk aversion).</p><p><em>Market factors:</em> <strong>liquidity premium</strong> (more liquid bonds are more expensive, with narrower bid/offer spreads); <strong>indicative vs firm two-way quotes</strong> (some bonds only get indicative pricing, requiring negotiation); <strong>repo market depth</strong> — bonds with an active repo market and easy short-covering react faster to interest rate/yield changes; illiquid bonds are hard to price and hard to short (inability to borrow/cover).</p>"}], "mcqs": [{"question": "Under the MiFID venue classification originally introduced in the EU, which term describes an investment firm that deals on its own account against client orders on an organised, frequent, systematic and substantial basis instead of routing them elsewhere?", "options": ["Multilateral trading facility", "Systematic internaliser", "Organised trading facility", "Regulated market"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A systematic internaliser (SI) is an investment firm dealing on own account against client orders on an organised, frequent, systematic and substantial basis, as defined under MiFID."}, {"question": "A private trading system that enables clients to trade anonymously, with price and order size not displayed until after the trade is complete, is best described as a:", "options": ["Lit pool", "Dark pool", "Regulated market", "Systematic internaliser"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A dark pool hides price and order size information until after execution; trading in dark pools is described as 'dark liquidity', the opposite of 'lit pools'."}, {"question": "Which venue type was added by MiFID II in 2018, specifically for instruments other than equities such as bonds or derivatives?", "options": ["Multilateral trading facility (MTF)", "Systematic internaliser (SI)", "Organised trading facility (OTF)", "Regulated market"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "MiFID II added the organised trading facility (OTF) as a fourth venue type for non-equity instruments where multiple third-party buy/sell interests can interact to form a contract."}, {"question": "Which pair of Chinese share classes correctly distinguishes mainland-listed shares from those listed in Hong Kong?", "options": ["A-shares (mainland) and H-shares (Hong Kong)", "B-shares (mainland) and A-shares (Hong Kong)", "H-shares (mainland) and A-shares (Hong Kong)", "N-shares (mainland) and A-shares (Hong Kong)"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "A-shares trade on mainland exchanges (Shanghai/Shenzhen) with gradually opened foreign access; H-shares are mainland companies listed on the Hong Kong Stock Exchange, popular with global investors."}, {"question": "In a quote-driven trading system such as Nasdaq, what are market makers obliged to do?", "options": ["Match buy and sell orders without taking a position", "Agree to buy and sell at least a set minimum number of shares at quoted prices", "Only execute trades once a year at auction", "Act solely as agents for a commission"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "In quote-driven systems, market makers commit to buy and sell at least a minimum number of shares at their quoted bid and offer prices, providing continuous liquidity."}, {"question": "Which of the following is a hybrid trading system combining features of both order-driven and quote-driven markets?", "options": ["Nasdaq", "NYSE floor trading", "LSE's SETSqx", "Tokyo Stock Exchange's main board"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The LSE's SETSqx (Stock Exchange Electronic Trading Service — quotes and crosses) is a named example of a hybrid system combining order-driven and quote-driven features."}, {"question": "An inter-dealer broker (IDB) arranging a trade between two market makers:", "options": ["Takes a principal position in the trade to guarantee performance", "Acts as agent but settles the transaction as if it were principal, to preserve anonymity", "Must be a division of one of the dealing broker-dealers", "Is only permitted to arrange trades in government bonds"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "An IDB acts as agent for the dealer but settles as if it were principal specifically to preserve the anonymity of the dealers, and must be a separate firm, not a division of a broker-dealer."}, {"question": "Which of the following is NOT a typical service provided by an equity and fixed income prime broker?", "options": ["Stock lending to cover short positions", "Margin financing of trades", "Underwriting the client's initial public offering", "Custody and safekeeping of securities"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Prime brokers provide stock lending, margin financing, cash management, trade execution/settlement and custody services to hedge fund clients — underwriting an IPO is not a prime brokerage service."}, {"question": "A prime broker's contractual right to re-use, re-charge or otherwise dispose of a client's pledged securities is known as:", "options": ["Novation", "Rehypothecation", "Uncrossing", "Free-float adjustment"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The right of rehypothecation allows a prime broker to re-use client collateral, for example by depositing it with a third party for cheaper funding or lending it to another client."}, {"question": "What is a key benefit claimed for algorithmic trading in terms of trading discipline?", "options": ["It allows traders to overtrade during volatile markets", "It removes the possibility of the trader diverting from predefined entry/exit rules", "It guarantees profits on every trade", "It eliminates the need for any regulatory oversight"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Because entry and exit rules are predefined and coded into the algorithm, there is no opportunity for the trader to deviate from the plan during volatile or opportunistic conditions."}, {"question": "What technique do high-frequency traders use to minimise latency by placing servers physically close to an exchange's own servers?", "options": ["Novation", "Co-location", "Rehypothecation", "Uncrossing"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Co-location involves siting trading servers physically close to (often in the same data centre as) the exchange's servers to minimise data transmission time and latency."}, {"question": "The May 2010 'flash crash' in US markets, during which around 600 points were wiped off a major index in five minutes before recovering, was primarily attributed by an SEC/CFTC report to:", "options": ["A cyberattack on the NYSE", "High-frequency traders' algorithms", "A failure of the central counterparty", "Stamp duty changes"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "An official SEC/CFTC report placed most of the blame for the 6 May 2010 flash crash's volatility on high-frequency traders' algorithms, triggered initially by S&P futures market movements."}, {"question": "In an exchange's electronic order book, orders are given priority based on:", "options": ["Size, then price", "Price, then time of entry", "Time of entry only", "Broker identity, then price"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Orders in the order book are prioritised first by price (best price at the top) and then by the time at which the order was entered."}, {"question": "The algorithm used at the start of the trading day to execute overlapping orders at the single price that maximises the number of shares traded is known as the:", "options": ["Uncrossing algorithm", "Rehypothecation algorithm", "Free-float algorithm", "Novation algorithm"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The opening auction uses an uncrossing algorithm to execute overlapping orders at the price that maximises volume traded, simultaneously establishing the opening price."}, {"question": "An order that does not specify a price and is designed to execute immediately at the best available price on the order book is a:", "options": ["Limit order", "Iceberg order", "Market order", "Fill or kill order"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Market orders do not specify a price and are executed immediately at the best available price; variants include market-on-open (MOO) and market-on-close (MOC)."}, {"question": "Which order type only shows a small visible portion on the order book while hiding the bulk of the order, releasing further tranches as each is executed?", "options": ["Iceberg order", "All-or-none order", "Execute and eliminate order", "Fill or kill order"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "An iceberg order displays only a small 'tip' on the order book, with the hidden bulk released in tranches as each visible portion is filled, reducing market impact."}, {"question": "An order that requires the entire order to be filled immediately at a price at least as good as specified, or the whole order to be cancelled, is known as:", "options": ["A limit order", "A fill or kill order", "An all-or-none order", "An iceberg order"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A fill or kill order is either filled entirely at a price at least as good as specified, or the entire order is cancelled — unlike an all-or-none order, it normally has a specified price."}, {"question": "A buy order for 5,000 shares is filled against three separate sell orders at different prices. This outcome is known as:", "options": ["A residual", "A multiple fill", "An iceberg execution", "A novation"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "When an order is matched against several opposing orders, this is described as a multiple fill, and the buyer pays a weighted average price across the fills."}, {"question": "The legal mechanism by which a central counterparty (CCP) replaces a single bilateral trade obligation with two new obligations (buyer-to-CCP and CCP-to-seller) is called:", "options": ["Novation", "Rehypothecation", "Uncrossing", "Netting"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Novation is the transfer of obligation whereby the CCP steps in between the two original parties, creating two new contracts and eliminating the original bilateral counterparty exposure."}, {"question": "Which of the following is NOT typically cited as a benefit of using a central counterparty (CCP)?", "options": ["Reduced counterparty/default risk", "Anonymity between the original counterparties", "Elimination of stamp duty on the trade", "Netting of receipts and payments in the same security settling on the same day"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "CCP benefits include reduced counterparty risk, anonymity, operational efficiency, netting, and improved pricing — stamp duty is a separate government transaction tax unaffected by CCP use."}, {"question": "In the UK, stamp duty on electronic share purchases is typically charged at what rate, and is payable by whom?", "options": ["1.0%, payable by the seller", "0.5%, payable by the buyer", "0.25%, payable by both parties equally", "2.0%, payable by the broker"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "UK stamp duty on electronic share purchases is typically 0.5%, payable by the buyer and usually embedded within the broker's commission; some instruments such as ETFs may be exempt."}, {"question": "The LSE's Regulatory News Service (RNS) is an example of which type of entity in the disclosure of price-sensitive company information?", "options": ["A secondary information provider (SIP)", "A primary information provider (PIP)", "A gilt-edged market maker (GEMM)", "An inter-dealer broker (IDB)"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The RNS is an example of a primary information provider (PIP), which receives and disseminates regulatory news from companies to secondary information providers such as Bloomberg and Refinitiv."}, {"question": "Which major US stock index is calculated as the sum of its 30 constituent share prices divided by an adjusted divisor, rather than being weighted by market capitalisation?", "options": ["S&P 500", "Nasdaq 100", "Dow Jones Industrial Average (DJIA)", "Wilshire 5000"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The DJIA is a price-weighted index calculated as the sum of the prices of its 30 component stocks divided by the Dow Divisor, which is adjusted for splits and similar structural changes."}, {"question": "A criticism commonly made of the DJIA's price-weighting methodology is that:", "options": ["It ignores dividend payments entirely", "A $1 move in a low-priced constituent has the same index impact as a $1 move in a high-priced constituent, ignoring market cap", "It only includes financial sector companies", "It cannot be adjusted for stock splits"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Because the DJIA is price-weighted, a $1 price move has equal index impact regardless of the company's size, giving higher-priced stocks disproportionate influence relative to market capitalisation."}, {"question": "A company has a free float of 23.2% of its issued shares. For the purposes of a free-float adjusted index, this free float would typically be rounded to:", "options": ["20%", "23%", "25%", "30%"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Free-float factors are typically rounded to the nearest multiple of 5% for calculation purposes, so 23.2% would be rounded up to 25%."}, {"question": "Which of the following would typically be EXCLUDED from a company's free-float when calculating a free-float adjusted index weighting?", "options": ["Shares held by retail investors owning less than 1% each", "Shares held by an institutional investor owning 8% of the company", "Shares held in a widely diversified index tracker fund", "Shares available for trading on the open market with no restrictions"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Free-float excludes shares held by owners of 5% or more (government, institutional, strategic, founder/insider holdings) plus restricted stock, since these are not readily available for public trading."}, {"question": "The three versions of the S&P 500 Index — price return, total return and net total return — differ primarily in how they treat:", "options": ["Free-float adjustment", "Dividend income and reinvestment", "Currency conversion", "Stock splits"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Price return excludes dividends; total (gross) return reinvests gross dividends; net total return reinvests dividends after deduction of withholding tax."}, {"question": "In the UK gilt market, which entity is responsible for issuing new UK government securities, while a separate body sets the secondary market dealing rules?", "options": ["The Debt Management Office (DMO) issues gilts; the LSE sets secondary market dealing rules", "The Bank of England issues gilts; the FCA sets dealing rules", "The LSE issues gilts; the DMO sets dealing rules", "HM Treasury issues gilts; the PRA sets dealing rules"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The DMO, an executive agency of HM Treasury, is the UK's gilt issuing agency; once issued, secondary market dealing is overseen jointly by the DMO and the LSE, which prescribes the dealing rules."}, {"question": "A gilt-edged market maker (GEMM) is obliged to do all of the following EXCEPT:", "options": ["Make effective two-way prices to customers up to an agreed size", "Bid competitively in DMO gilt auctions", "Provide information to the DMO on closing prices and positions", "Guarantee the DMO a minimum resale price for all gilts in issue"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "GEMM obligations include two-way pricing, competitive auction bidding, and reporting to the DMO — there is no obligation to guarantee any minimum resale price for gilts in issue."}, {"question": "Under the German government bond auction system, bids priced above the lowest accepted price are:", "options": ["Rejected entirely", "Allotted in full at the price quoted in the respective bid", "Allotted at a single uniform clearing price", "Automatically converted to non-competitive bids"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The Bund uses a multiple price auction: bids above the lowest accepted price are allotted in full at the price quoted in each bid (not a uniform price); bids below the lowest accepted price receive nothing."}, {"question": "US Treasury securities are issued in three forms based on maturity. Which of the following correctly matches the instrument to its maturity range?", "options": ["T-bills: 30 years; T-notes: 2-10 years; T-bonds: less than 1 year", "T-bills: less than 1 year; T-notes: 2-10 years; T-bonds: 30 years", "T-bills: 2-10 years; T-notes: 30 years; T-bonds: less than 1 year", "T-bills: 1-5 years; T-notes: 5-10 years; T-bonds: 10-30 years"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "US Treasury bills are issued for terms under a year, Treasury notes for 2, 3, 5, 7 and 10 years, and Treasury bonds for a 30-year term."}, {"question": "Why is trading in corporate bonds commonly described as taking place in a 'decentralised dealer market', in contrast to equity trading?", "options": ["Because corporate bonds cannot legally be traded on any exchange", "Because there is no single central location; dealers provide liquidity across multiple separate pools of liquidity", "Because corporate bonds are only traded once a year at auction", "Because corporate bonds are always settled through a central counterparty, unlike equities"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Unlike centralised equity exchanges, corporate bond trading occurs across numerous separate dealer systems/pools of liquidity, with dealers providing liquidity by standing ready to buy or sell."}, {"question": "A key difference between bondholders and equity holders in the event of an issuer's insolvency is that:", "options": ["Equity holders always receive their capital back in full before bondholders", "Bondholders have a more senior claim against the issuer's assets than equity holders", "Bondholders receive dividends while equity holders receive coupons", "There is no difference in seniority between bondholders and equity holders"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Bondholders have a specified income stream and a more senior claim on the issuer's assets in a bankruptcy or restructuring, while equity holders rank last and may receive little or no residual value."}, {"question": "Which of the following is an example of a dealer-to-customer electronic bond trading platform typically operating on a request for quote (RFQ) basis?", "options": ["BrokerTec", "MTS Cash", "Tradeweb", "The LSE's main order book"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Tradeweb (along with MTS BondVision and single-dealer platforms) is a dealer-to-customer electronic platform; investors request quotes simultaneously from multiple dealers on an RFQ basis. MTS Cash and BrokerTec are dealer-to-dealer platforms."}, {"question": "Which market factor most directly explains why bonds with an active repo market and easy short-covering react more quickly to interest rate changes?", "options": ["Their credit rating is always higher", "The ability to cover short positions readily allows faster response to yield changes", "They are always government bonds", "They are exempt from stamp duty"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Bonds with active markets and an available liquid repo market for covering short positions can react more quickly to underlying interest rate and yield changes than illiquid bonds."}], "flashcards": [{"front": "What are the three MiFID-defined trading venues (pre-MiFID II)?", "back": "Regulated markets, multilateral trading facilities (MTFs), and systematic internalisers (SIs)."}, {"front": "What venue type did MiFID II add in 2018, and for what instruments?", "back": "The organised trading facility (OTF), for non-equity instruments such as bonds and derivatives."}, {"front": "What is a dark pool?", "back": "A private trading system enabling anonymous trading where price/size information is hidden until after execution; opposite of a 'lit pool'."}, {"front": "What US regulation exempts private placements from SEC registration?", "back": "Regulation D under the Securities Act of 1933."}, {"front": "What distinguishes A-shares from H-shares in China?", "back": "A-shares trade on mainland exchanges (Shanghai/Shenzhen) with restricted foreign access; H-shares are mainland companies listed in Hong Kong, popular with global investors."}, {"front": "What is a quote-driven trading system? Give an example.", "back": "Market makers agree to buy/sell a minimum number of shares at quoted bid/offer prices; Nasdaq is the prime example."}, {"front": "What is an order-driven trading system? Give examples.", "back": "Investors indicate quantity/price and the system matches buyers and sellers directly; examples: NYSE, TSE, large-cap LSE stocks."}, {"front": "Name a hybrid trading system.", "back": "LSE's SETSqx (Stock Exchange Electronic Trading Service — quotes and crosses), combining order-driven and quote-driven features."}, {"front": "What is the key difference between a broker and a dealer?", "back": "A broker acts as agent, arranging deals for commission without taking ownership; a dealer acts as principal, buying/selling for its own account."}, {"front": "What is an inter-dealer broker (IDB) and how does it settle trades?", "back": "An agent arranging deals anonymously between dealers/market makers; it settles as if it were principal to preserve anonymity, but cannot itself take principal positions."}, {"front": "List five services a prime broker provides to hedge funds.", "back": "Stock lending, margin financing, cash management, trade execution/settlement, and custody (plus rehypothecation rights)."}, {"front": "What is rehypothecation?", "back": "A prime broker's right to re-use, re-charge or dispose of a client's pledged securities, e.g. by lending them to another client or using them as collateral for cheaper funding."}, {"front": "What is a systematic internaliser (SI)?", "back": "An investment firm dealing on its own account against client orders on an organised, frequent, systematic, substantial basis, instead of routing orders elsewhere."}, {"front": "Give two reasons algorithmic trading is popular.", "back": "It removes emotion/discipline issues (rules are predefined) and offers faster, cheaper, more accurate execution than manual trading."}, {"front": "What is high-frequency trading (HFT)?", "back": "A sub-category of algo-trading using powerful, low-latency, often co-located computers to enter and rapidly reverse large numbers of small orders for small profits per trade."}, {"front": "What is co-location and why does it matter for HFT?", "back": "Placing trading servers physically close to (often in the same data centre as) the exchange's servers, to minimise latency and gain speed advantage."}, {"front": "What happened in the 6 May 2010 'flash crash'?", "back": "US markets fell around 600 points in 5 minutes and recovered in ~20 minutes; an SEC/CFTC report blamed HFT algorithms, triggered by S&P futures selling."}, {"front": "What triggered the 23 April 2013 'mini flash crash'?", "back": "A hoax Twitter post claiming an attack on the White House and injury to President Obama; the DJIA fell ~130 points before rapidly recovering."}, {"front": "How are orders prioritised in an exchange order book?", "back": "First by price (best price at top), then by time of entry."}, {"front": "What does the opening auction's uncrossing algorithm do?", "back": "Executes overlapping orders at the single price that maximises the number of shares traded, and sets the opening price."}, {"front": "What triggers a trading halt/suspension of automatic execution during the day?", "back": "When a trade price moves beyond the exchange's price tolerance level (typically 5%-25%) versus the previous trade price."}, {"front": "Define a market order.", "back": "An order with no specified price that executes immediately at the best available price; variants include market-on-open (MOO) and market-on-close (MOC)."}, {"front": "Define a limit order.", "back": "An order with a specified price limit and time limit (e.g. good for the day); can be partially filled; only limit orders appear on the order book."}, {"front": "Define an iceberg order.", "back": "A large order that only displays a small visible portion on the order book, with the hidden bulk released in tranches as each visible tranche executes."}, {"front": "Define a fill or kill order.", "back": "An order that is either filled entirely at the specified price or better immediately, or entirely cancelled."}, {"front": "Define an execute and eliminate (at best) order.", "back": "Executes as much of the order as possible immediately at a specified price, then cancels the unfilled remainder."}, {"front": "Define an all-or-none order.", "back": "An order that must be filled entirely or not at all; no partial or multiple fills are permitted."}, {"front": "What is a 'multiple fill'?", "back": "When a single order is matched against several opposing orders at different prices, resulting in a weighted average execution price."}, {"front": "What is novation in the context of a CCP?", "back": "The legal process by which a CCP replaces a single bilateral trade obligation with two new obligations: buyer-to-CCP and CCP-to-seller."}, {"front": "List five benefits of using a central counterparty (CCP).", "back": "Reduced counterparty/default risk, anonymity, operational efficiency, netting of same-day same-security flows, and improved price formation."}, {"front": "What is UK stamp duty on electronic share purchases?", "back": "Typically 0.5%, paid by the buyer, usually embedded in broker commission; some instruments like ETFs may be exempt."}, {"front": "What is a primary information provider (PIP)? Give a UK example.", "back": "A service that receives regulatory/price-sensitive information from listed companies and disseminates it to secondary information providers; example: the LSE's Regulatory News Service (RNS)."}, {"front": "What is a secondary information provider (SIP)? Give examples.", "back": "An entity that further disseminates PIP information to the wider financial community; examples: Bloomberg, Refinitiv, Dow Jones."}, {"front": "How is the DJIA calculated?", "back": "Sum of the prices of its 30 component stocks divided by the Dow Divisor, which is adjusted for splits/spin-offs so such events don't distort the index level."}, {"front": "What is the main criticism of price-weighted indices like the DJIA?", "back": "A $1 move in any constituent has equal index impact regardless of market cap, giving high-priced stocks disproportionate influence versus low-priced or larger-cap stocks."}, {"front": "How is a company's free-float factor typically applied in index calculations?", "back": "The free-float percentage is rounded to the nearest 5% and multiplied by the full market cap to give free-float market capitalisation."}, {"front": "What is excluded from a company's free-float?", "back": "Shares held by owners of 5%+ stakes (government, institutional, strategic, founders, executives, insiders) and restricted stock."}, {"front": "What are the three S&P 500 index versions and how do they differ?", "back": "Price return (excludes dividends), total/gross return (reinvests gross dividends), and net total return (reinvests dividends after withholding tax)."}, {"front": "Who is the UK's gilt issuing agency, and who else oversees the secondary market?", "back": "The Debt Management Office (DMO) issues gilts; the DMO and the LSE jointly oversee the secondary market, with the LSE setting dealing rules."}, {"front": "What are the core obligations of a gilt-edged market maker (GEMM)?", "back": "Make two-way prices to customers up to an agreed size, bid competitively at DMO auctions, and report closing prices/positions/turnover to the DMO."}, {"front": "What privileges does GEMM status confer?", "back": "Exclusive rights to bid directly at DMO gilt auctions, trade as DMO counterparty in secondary operations, and exclusive access to gilt inter-dealer brokers."}, {"front": "What auction method does the German Bund use, and how are bids allotted?", "back": "A multiple price auction: bids above the lowest accepted price are allotted in full at their quoted price; bids below the lowest accepted price get nothing; non-competitive bids get the weighted average accepted price."}, {"front": "What are the maturity ranges of US T-bills, T-notes and T-bonds?", "back": "T-bills: less than 1 year; T-notes: 2, 3, 5, 7, 10 years; T-bonds: 30 years."}, {"front": "Why is the corporate bond market described as a 'decentralised dealer market'?", "back": "Trading occurs through multiple separate dealer-provided pools of liquidity rather than via a single centralised exchange, unlike equities."}, {"front": "What is the LSE's Order Book for Retail Bonds (ORB)?", "back": "An order-driven trading service offering retail access to a selected number of gilts, supranational and UK corporate bonds with continuous two-way market-maker pricing."}, {"front": "What is request for quote (RFQ) trading in the bond market?", "back": "A dealer-to-customer trading model where investors request quotes simultaneously from multiple dealers electronically, improving speed and price competition."}]}, {"id": "sec-m8", "number": 8, "title": "Corporate Actions", "examWeight": "Core module — calculation-intensive", "summaryHtml": "<p>Corporate actions are events initiated by an issuer that affect its securities and shareholders. This module is one of the most calculation-heavy in the syllabus, so formulas must be memorised cold.</p><p><strong>Income vs capital events.</strong> Bond coupons are contractual and predictable (fixed or floating, e.g. FRNs referencing SOFR/EURIBOR); equity dividends are discretionary and variable, though large established companies try to maintain/grow them. Preference shares are a hybrid: a specified dividend rate like a bond coupon, but the issuer retains the right to skip payment.</p><p><strong>Bond capital repayment</strong> is usually a single <strong>bullet</strong> repayment at maturity; <strong>sinking funds</strong> set aside cash annually to reduce this risk; <strong>callable</strong> bonds let the issuer redeem early (favours issuer when rates fall); <strong>putable</strong> bonds let the investor demand early redemption.</p><p><strong>Equity capital restructuring</strong> — bonus issues (scrip/capitalisation issues), stock splits, and reverse stock splits — involve no cash flow and leave market capitalisation theoretically unchanged, but reduce (bonus/split) or increase (reverse split) the price per share to aid marketability. A bonus issue converts reserves into share capital; a split simply subdivides existing share capital (no reserves movement).</p><p><strong>Rights issues</strong> raise new cash while preserving shareholders' proportionate ownership (avoiding dilution) via <strong>pre-emptive rights</strong>. New shares are offered at a discount to existing holders in proportion to their holding. The key formula is the <strong>theoretical ex-rights price (TERP)</strong>:</p><p style='text-align:center'><strong>TERP = (N × P<sub>cum</sub> + R × P<sub>issue</sub>) ÷ (N + R)</strong></p><p>where N = shares held cum-rights, P<sub>cum</sub> = cum-rights market price, R = new (rights) shares issued, P<sub>issue</sub> = rights issue subscription price. The <strong>nil-paid value</strong> of a right = TERP − P<sub>issue</sub>. A shareholder can take up rights in full, sell them nil-paid in full, do nothing (rights sold on their behalf), or sell just enough rights to fund taking up the rest — known as <strong>'swallowing the tail'</strong> — where the number of nil-paid rights to sell = (P<sub>issue</sub> × rights allocated) ÷ TERP, rounded up.</p><p><strong>Share buybacks</strong> return surplus cash or re-gear the balance sheet toward debt; restricted by rules protecting the 'creditors' buffer' and requiring AGM shareholder approval. Mechanics include block trades, accelerated bookbuilds (best-efforts vs backstop price), and bought deals (underwriter takes the risk).</p><p><strong>Stakebuilding</strong> may be strategic (protect supply chain/block a takeover) or pre-acquisition, direct (dawn raid) or indirect (CFDs — economic exposure without voting rights). UK disclosure: 3%+ triggers notification, then every whole 1% change. Under PTM rules, 30%+ voting rights (or increasing a 30–50% stake) triggers a mandatory cash offer.</p><p><strong>M&A</strong>: an acquisition/takeover is one company buying >50% of another (parent/subsidiary; friendly or hostile); a merger is a combination of similarly-sized firms. Benefits: increased market share, economies of scale/synergies, market/product diversification. Risks: overpayment, culture clashes, and — from a regulatory standpoint — monopoly power, policed by competition/antitrust authorities (UK: CMA; US: FTC/DOJ under antitrust law).</p>", "sections": [{"heading": "8.1 Bonds vs Equities — Income Events Recap", "html": "<table><tr><th></th><th>Bonds</th><th>Equities</th></tr><tr><td>Nature of payment</td><td>Contractual coupon — issuer must pay</td><td>Discretionary dividend — board can cut/omit</td></tr><tr><td>Predictability</td><td>High (fixed coupon, known frequency, known nominal)</td><td>Lower — depends on profitability/cash flow, though many large-cap firms aim to maintain/grow DPS</td></tr><tr><td>Frequency</td><td>Usually semi-annual or annual (FRNs often quarterly)</td><td>Often quarterly (US) or semi-annual (UK)</td></tr><tr><td>Investor benefit</td><td>Known income; can calculate accrued interest to any date</td><td>Upside potential if issuer performs well</td></tr><tr><td>Issuer benefit</td><td>Knows exact funding obligation; can lock in low coupon before rates rise</td><td>Can reduce/stop dividends in stress to conserve cash</td></tr></table><p><strong>Floating-rate notes (FRNs):</strong> coupon is set as a margin above a reference rate (historically LIBOR/EURIBOR, now increasingly SOFR), reducing predictability but allowing quarterly resets. <em>Example: on 27 August 2025 the US Treasury issued $28bn face value of a two-year FRN (indexed to the 13-week T-bill discount rate) that tapped an existing $30bn issue maturing 31 July 2027.</em></p><p><strong>Preference shares</strong> are a hybrid instrument: the dividend rate is specified in advance (like a bond coupon), increasing predictability versus ordinary shares, but as they remain equity the issuer can still suspend the dividend in times of stress — an option not available on a bond's contractual coupon.</p><p><strong>Worked example — ExxonMobil dividend growth:</strong> ExxonMobil's total annual dividend per share rose from $2.70 (10 years prior) to $4.00 (latest full year), an average annual growth rate of 5.8% sustained over the prior 42 years — illustrating a 'progressive dividend policy' even through the 2020–21 pandemic.</p>"}, {"heading": "8.2 Bond Repayment Events", "html": "<table><tr><th>Structure</th><th>Description</th><th>Risk/rate implication</th></tr><tr><td>Bullet (bullet issue)</td><td>Entire principal repaid in one lump sum at maturity</td><td>Riskier — large single payment far in the future — so usually pays a higher coupon</td></tr><tr><td>Non-bullet issue</td><td>Principal repaid via a series of payments rather than a lump sum</td><td>Lower risk profile than bullet</td></tr><tr><td>Sinking fund</td><td>Issuer sets aside funds each year (often via a trustee) toward the eventual maturity repayment, or the trustee buys back bonds in the market if trading below par</td><td>Reduces risk that issuer lacks funds to repay principal at maturity</td></tr><tr><td>Callable bond</td><td>Issuer has the right to redeem some/all bonds early, at specified points, usually at par</td><td>Favours the issuer — used to refinance if the bond becomes 'expensive' (rates fall)</td></tr><tr><td>Putable bond</td><td>Investor has the right to demand early redemption, after due notice</td><td>Favours the investor</td></tr></table><p>A sinking fund is often combined with a callable structure so the issuer can buy back bonds cheaply in the open market if they trade below par.</p>"}, {"heading": "8.3 Equity Capital Restructuring: Bonus Issues, Splits & Reverse Splits", "html": "<p>Three equity capital events involve <strong>no cash flow</strong> between shareholder and company; they restructure the equity section of the accounts and change the share price to improve marketability, leaving market capitalisation theoretically unchanged.</p><table><tr><th>Event</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Effect on share capital account</th><th>Effect on price</th></tr><tr><td>Bonus issue (scrip/capitalisation issue)</td><td>New shares issued free, pro rata to existing holdings; funded by converting reserves into share capital</td><td>Share capital line increases, reserves fall by the same amount</td><td>Price falls proportionately (more shares, same net worth)</td></tr><tr><td>Stock split</td><td>Each existing share subdivided into a larger number of shares with a smaller nominal value</td><td>Total share capital unchanged — same total, just more shares each with smaller nominal value</td><td>Price falls proportionately</td></tr><tr><td>Reverse stock split (consolidation)</td><td>A number of existing shares combined into one share with a larger nominal value</td><td>Total share capital unchanged — same total, fewer shares each with larger nominal value</td><td>Price rises proportionately</td></tr></table><p><strong>Real-world example:</strong> Amazon's 20-for-1 stock split (announced 2022): shares closed at $2,785; post-split theoretical price = $2,785 ÷ 20 = $139.25. Shareholders end up holding 20× more shares of equivalent total value.</p><p><em>Rationale:</em> UK shares trading well into double-digit pounds, or US shares above roughly $200, are considered to suffer reduced marketability — a bonus issue/split can improve liquidity. A reverse split is used when the price has fallen to a very low level (e.g. below $1.00), which can damage investor confidence.</p>"}, {"heading": "8.4 Impact of Capital Restructuring on Share Price — Worked Examples", "html": "<p><strong>Example 1 — 1-for-3 scrip (bonus) issue.</strong> Company has 750,000 $1 shares in issue, trading at $3.00 (the 'cum-bonus' price). Market cap = 750,000 × $3 = $2.25m.</p><table><tr><th></th><th>Shares (000)</th><th>Price $</th><th>Value ($000)</th></tr><tr><td>Before</td><td>750</td><td>3.00</td><td>2,250</td></tr><tr><td>Scrip issue</td><td>+250</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>After</td><td>1,000</td><td><strong>2.25</strong></td><td>2,250</td></tr></table><p>$0.25m is transferred from retained profits to share capital to cover the 250,000 free shares. Theoretical ex-bonus price = $2.25m ÷ 1,000,000 = <strong>$2.25</strong>. Cross-check using the minimum qualifying holding (3 shares for a 1-for-3 issue): 3 × $3 = $9 portfolio value; after bonus, 4 shares worth $9 ÷ 4 = <strong>$2.25</strong> each.</p><p><strong>Example 2 — XYZ plc 1-for-4 bonus issue.</strong> 1m shares of $0.25 nominal, trading at $2.00, bonus issue 1-for-4:</p><ul><li>Shares before = 1m; shares after = 1m + 0.25m = 1.25m</li><li>Nominal share capital before = 1m × $0.25 = $250,000; after = 1.25m × $0.25 = $312,500</li><li>Theoretical price = $2.00 × (4/5) = <strong>$1.60</strong></li><li>Market cap before = 1m × $2.00 = $2m; market cap after = 1.25m × $1.60 = <strong>$2m</strong> (unchanged)</li></ul><p><strong>Example 3 — 5-for-1 stock split.</strong> 1m shares of $1 nominal replaced by 5m shares of $0.20 nominal; pre-split price $3.00.</p><table><tr><th></th><th>Shares (000)</th><th>Price $</th><th>Value ($000)</th></tr><tr><td>Before</td><td>1,000</td><td>3.00</td><td>3,000</td></tr><tr><td>After</td><td>5,000</td><td><strong>0.60</strong></td><td>3,000</td></tr></table><p>New price = $3.00 ÷ 5 = <strong>$0.60</strong> (above the $0.20 nominal, so no issuance problem).</p><p><strong>Example 4 — 1-for-2 reverse stock split.</strong> 4m shares of $1 nominal replaced by 2m shares of $2 nominal; pre-split price $1.20.</p><table><tr><th></th><th>Shares (000)</th><th>Price $</th><th>Value ($000)</th></tr><tr><td>Before</td><td>4,000</td><td>1.20</td><td>4,800</td></tr><tr><td>After</td><td>2,000</td><td><strong>2.40</strong></td><td>4,800</td></tr></table><p>New price = $1.20 × 2 = <strong>$2.40</strong>.</p><p><strong>Extra practice:</strong> A company has a 1-for-1 bonus issue; cum-bonus price is $10.00. Using 1 share before + 1 bonus share = 2 shares: ex-bonus price = $10 ÷ 2 = <strong>$5.00</strong>. If instead the company did a 2-for-1 split (2 shares replace each 1), the theoretical price is identical, $5.00 — only the accounting treatment differs (split: no reserves movement; bonus: reserves converted to share capital). A 5-into-1 reverse split on a $10 cum price would instead give a theoretical price of $10 × 5 = $50.00.</p>"}, {"heading": "8.5 Rights Issues — Pre-emptive Rights & Mechanics", "html": "<p><strong>Pre-emptive rights</strong> give existing shareholders first refusal on new share issues for cash, protecting them from dilution of their proportionate control unless they agree otherwise (a special resolution waiving pre-emption, best-practice limited to 5% p.a. absent a specified purpose).</p><p><strong>Dilution example:</strong> Investor X holds 400 of ABC plc's 10,000 shares (a 4% stake). If ABC issues 10,000 new shares to <em>other</em> parties only, Investor X's stake falls to 400/20,000 = 2% — dilution. A rights issue avoids this: ABC offers Investor X the pre-emptive right to 4% of the new shares (400 of the 10,000 new shares), so after taking them up Investor X holds 800 of 20,000 shares — still 4%.</p><table><tr><th></th><th>Before</th><th>New shares offered</th><th>After (if taken up)</th></tr><tr><td>Investor X</td><td>400 (4%)</td><td>400</td><td>800 (4%)</td></tr><tr><td>Other shareholders</td><td>9,600 (96%)</td><td>9,600</td><td>19,200 (96%)</td></tr><tr><td>Total</td><td>10,000</td><td>10,000</td><td>20,000</td></tr></table><p><strong>Real example:</strong> National Grid plc's 7-for-24 Rights Issue of 1,085,448,980 new ordinary shares at 645 pence, announced 23 May 2024, closed 10 June 2024 with ~91% valid acceptances (fully underwritten so the company still received all the capital sought); new shares began trading fully paid around 12 June 2024.</p><p><strong>Timeline:</strong> Shares trade <em>cum-rights</em> until the ex-rights date (minimum ~10 business days from announcement to acceptance date); a provisional (renounceable, transferable) allotment letter is sent, and the ex-rights period begins the day after it is posted. A shareholder has four choices by the acceptance date: (1) take up rights in full; (2) sell all rights nil-paid; (3) sell part nil-paid to fund taking up the rest ('swallowing the tail'); (4) do nothing — the company sells the rights on their behalf and distributes net proceeds pro rata.</p><p>Rights issues are usually <strong>underwritten</strong> so the issuer is guaranteed to raise the full amount sought regardless of shareholder take-up.</p>"}, {"heading": "8.6 Rights Issues — TERP, Nil-Paid Value & Swallowing the Tail (Worked Calculations)", "html": "<p><strong>TERP formula:</strong> TERP = (N × P<sub>cum</sub> + R × P<sub>issue</sub>) ÷ (N + R), where N = shares held cum-rights, R = new rights shares.</p><p><strong>Worked example — 1-for-4 rights issue at £1.50.</strong> Company: 1m £1 nominal shares, share premium £0.5m, net assets £2m, retained profits £0.5m, market price £3.00. Raises £375,000 via a 1-for-4 rights issue at £1.50 (250,000 new shares).</p><table><tr><th>Accounts</th><th>Before ($000)</th><th>Movement</th><th>After ($000)</th></tr><tr><td>Net assets</td><td>2,000</td><td>+375</td><td>2,375</td></tr><tr><td>Share capital (£1 shares)</td><td>1,000</td><td>+250</td><td>1,250</td></tr><tr><td>Share premium</td><td>500</td><td>+125</td><td>625</td></tr><tr><td>Retained profit</td><td>500</td><td>—</td><td>500</td></tr></table><table><tr><th></th><th>Shares (000)</th><th>Price £</th><th>Value (£000)</th></tr><tr><td>Before</td><td>1,000</td><td>3.00</td><td>3,000</td></tr><tr><td>Rights issue</td><td>250</td><td>1.50</td><td>375</td></tr><tr><td>After</td><td>1,250</td><td><strong>2.70</strong></td><td>3,375</td></tr></table><p>Using the formula with the minimum qualifying holding (4 shares): TERP = [(4 × £3.00) + (1 × £1.50)] ÷ 5 = £13.50 ÷ 5 = <strong>£2.70</strong>.</p><p><strong>Nil-paid value</strong> = TERP − issue price = 270p − 150p = <strong>120p</strong>. (It would be irrational to pay more than 120p for the right to buy a 270p share for 150p.)</p><p><strong>Swallowing the tail — number of nil-paid rights to sell:</strong> = (Issue price × rights allocated) ÷ TERP, rounded <em>up</em>. Example: investor holds 2,000 shares cum-rights (same 1-for-4 at 150p, cum price 300p, TERP 270p).</p><table><tr><th></th><th>Shares</th><th>Price (p)</th><th>Value (£)</th></tr><tr><td>Shares held cum-rights</td><td>2,000</td><td>300</td><td>6,000.00</td></tr><tr><td>Rights allocated</td><td>500</td><td>150</td><td>750.00</td></tr><tr><td>Post rights issue</td><td>2,500</td><td>—</td><td>6,750.00</td></tr><tr><td>TERP</td><td>—</td><td>270</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Nil-paid value</td><td>—</td><td>120</td><td>—</td></tr></table><p>No. of nil-paid rights to sell = (150 × 500) ÷ 270 = 277.78 → rounded up to <strong>278</strong>. Proceeds raised = 278 × 120p = £333.60. Rights taken up = 500 − 278 = 222; cost = 222 × 150p = £333.00 (a 60p cash gain). Total position value post-rights = (2,000 + 222) × £2.70 = £5,999.40, plus £0.60 cash = <strong>£6,000.00</strong> — unchanged from the pre-rights £6,000.00 (2,000 × £3.00), confirming no net change in the investor's invested capital.</p><p><strong>Extra practice examples (source exercises, worked):</strong></p><ul><li>Shares trade at $5.00 cum-rights; 1-for-3 rights issue at $3.00. TERP = [(3 × $5.00) + (1 × $3.00)] ÷ 4 = $18.00 ÷ 4 = <strong>$4.50</strong>.</li><li>Shares trade at $8.00 cum-rights; 1-for-6 rights issue at $4.50. TERP = [(6 × $8.00) + (1 × $4.50)] ÷ 7 = $52.50 ÷ 7 = $7.50. Nil-paid value = $7.50 − $4.50 = <strong>$3.00</strong>.</li><li>Investor with 3,000 shares, same 1-for-3 at $3.00 (TERP $4.50, nil-paid $1.50): rights allocated = 1,000. Rights to sell to swallow the tail = ($3.00 × 1,000) ÷ $4.50 = 666.67 → <strong>667</strong>. Rights taken up = 1,000 − 667 = 333; cost = 333 × $3.00 = $999.00; proceeds from sale = 667 × $1.50 = $1,000.50 (≈$1.50 cash gain).</li></ul>"}, {"heading": "8.7 Share Buybacks & Stakebuilding", "html": "<p><strong>Share buybacks</strong> — a company uses cash to repurchase its own shares. Two common rationales: (1) surplus cash after disposing of a business segment; (2) re-gearing the capital structure toward more debt/less equity by borrowing to fund the buyback.</p><p><strong>Restrictions:</strong> regulation protects the 'creditors' buffer' (the capital originally paid into the company) by capping the value that can be used for buybacks; shareholder approval via an AGM resolution is required, which also sets the % limit and price parameters.</p><table><tr><th>Buyback mechanism</th><th>How it works</th></tr><tr><td>Block trade</td><td>Investment bank executes a small number of large trades with investors, e.g. via an exchange</td></tr><tr><td>Accelerated bookbuild — best-efforts basis</td><td>Bank(s)/syndicate contact institutions seeking willingness to sell at price points; junior syndicate members may only commit on a best-efforts basis (attempt to buy, no guarantee, not liable if they fail)</td></tr><tr><td>Accelerated bookbuild — backstop price</td><td>Maximum price the syndicate will not exceed</td></tr><tr><td>Bought deal</td><td>Underwriter(s) purchase the shares themselves outright, then resell to clients, retaining unsold inventory (opposite of best-efforts)</td></tr></table><p><strong>Buyback effect on EPS (worked example):</strong> A company earns $10m profit with 10m shares in issue: EPS = $10m ÷ 10m = $1.00. It buys back and cancels 1m shares (funded from surplus cash), leaving 9m shares: EPS = $10m ÷ 9m = <strong>$1.11</strong> (approx.) — buybacks mechanically raise EPS by shrinking the share count, even with unchanged profit.</p><p><strong>Stakebuilding</strong> is accumulating shares beyond pure investment purposes — strategic (protect a supply chain, block a rival's takeover) or preparatory to a takeover bid. Predators seek secrecy to avoid pushing up the price: <em>direct</em> stakebuilding (outright purchase, including a 'dawn raid' — buying heavily as soon as the market opens) is visible; <em>indirect</em> stakebuilding via CFDs gives the same economic exposure (price gain/loss) without voting rights or board influence, and is potentially leveraged.</p><p><strong>UK disclosure (FCA Disclosure & Transparency Rules):</strong> a holding of <strong>3% or more</strong> of a public company is 'notifiable'; above 3%, any change through a further whole percentage point must also be disclosed. CFD/derivative exposure with similar economic effect counts.</p><p><strong>Panel on Takeovers and Mergers (PTM/POTAM) rules:</strong> a <strong>mandatory offer</strong> is triggered if a person's holding reaches <strong>30% or more</strong> of voting rights, or if they increase a stake that is already between 30% and 50%. The mandatory offer must be (or include) a cash consideration no lower than the highest price the offeror paid in the prior 12 months. During an offer, dealings in relevant securities (offeror/offeree shares and derivatives) by the offeror, offeree or associates must be disclosed to the Panel and a Regulatory Information Service (RIS, e.g. LSE's RNS) by noon the next business day; anyone holding more than 1% must similarly disclose further dealings.</p>"}, {"heading": "8.8 Mergers & Acquisitions", "html": "<p><strong>Takeover/acquisition (US: 'takeover' often called 'acquisition'):</strong> one company (the predator/offeror) buys a majority (>50%) of another's shares (the target/offeree), gaining control; the target becomes a subsidiary of the acquirer (parent). Can be <strong>friendly</strong> (target's directors recommend acceptance) or <strong>hostile</strong> (directors recommend rejection) — shareholders ultimately decide regardless of the board's recommendation.</p><p><strong>Real example:</strong> BHP's attempted takeover of UK-listed Anglo American in 2024 was unsuccessful — despite BHP raising its bid three times, Anglo American's directors refused to engage or recommend the offer, and BHP abandoned the bid. This illustrates that a hostile bid can fail even after repeated increases.</p><p><strong>Merger:</strong> two companies of broadly similar size combine into one larger entity, typically via a share exchange, under joint ownership of both original shareholder groups (often reflected in a combined name, e.g. Disney Pixar, JPMorgan Chase, ExxonMobil).</p><table><tr><th>Benefits of M&A</th><th>Disadvantages/risks of M&A</th></tr><tr><td>Greater scale — increased revenue and market share</td><td>Acquirer may overpay (excessive premium)</td></tr><tr><td>Economies of scale — bulk buying, cost synergies (HR, finance rationalisation)</td><td>Cultural clashes between merging organisations hurt performance</td></tr><tr><td>Geographic/product diversification and growth</td><td>Limited overlap can mean few realisable synergies, adding cost via geographic dispersion</td></tr></table><p><strong>Monopoly & competition regulation:</strong> a monopoly is where one entity controls an entire market for a good/service, allowing it to set (not accept) prices and exploit consumers. Governments use competition ('antitrust' in the US) authorities to review substantial M&A deals — approving, blocking, or imposing conditions. UK: the <strong>Competition and Markets Authority (CMA)</strong>. US: enforced via antitrust law by agencies including the FTC and DOJ.</p>"}], "mcqs": [{"question": "Why is bond coupon income generally more predictable than equity dividend income?", "options": ["Bond coupons are contractual obligations of the issuer, whereas dividends are discretionary and depend on profitability and cash flow", "Bonds are only issued by governments", "Equities never pay any income", "Bond coupons are always higher than dividend yields"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The coupon rate, frequency and nominal value are fixed contractually at issue, so investors know in advance what income to expect; dividends are variable and at the board's discretion."}, {"question": "Which reference rate has increasingly replaced LIBOR as the basis for floating-rate note (FRN) coupons in the US?", "options": ["EURIBOR", "The Bank of England base rate", "SOFR (Secured Overnight Financing Rate)", "The prime lending rate"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "SOFR has increasingly replaced USD LIBOR as a reference rate for FRN coupons, alongside continuing use of EURIBOR for Eurozone issues."}, {"question": "Why are preference shares described as a 'hybrid' instrument?", "options": ["They can be converted into bonds at any time", "The dividend rate is specified in advance like a bond coupon, but as equity the issuer can still choose to stop paying it in times of stress", "They pay no income at all", "They are only issued by governments"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Preference shares specify a dividend rate in advance (bond-like predictability) but remain equity, so the issuer retains the flexibility to suspend payment — unlike a bond's contractual coupon."}, {"question": "A bond structured to repay its entire principal in a single lump sum at maturity is known as a:", "options": ["Sinking fund bond", "Bullet issue", "Putable bond", "Convertible bond"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Bullet issues repay the whole principal in one payment at maturity; because this concentrates repayment risk on a single date, they typically carry a higher coupon than non-bullet issues."}, {"question": "A sinking fund arrangement primarily exists to:", "options": ["Increase the coupon paid to bondholders", "Allow the issuer to skip coupon payments", "Reduce the risk that the issuer lacks sufficient funds to repay the principal at maturity, by setting aside funds annually", "Convert the bond into equity"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "A sinking fund has the issuer (often via a trustee) set aside money each year toward the maturity repayment, or buy back bonds trading below par, reducing the risk of a shortfall at maturity."}, {"question": "A callable bond gives which party a right, and how does it typically benefit them?", "options": ["The investor; it lets them demand redemption early if rates rise", "The issuer; it lets them redeem the bond early, useful if the bond has become relatively expensive (e.g. rates have fallen)", "The custodian; it lets them liquidate the position", "Neither party has any right in a callable bond"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A call provision gives the issuer the right to redeem some or all bonds early, typically at par, which is valuable to the issuer when the bond's fixed coupon becomes expensive relative to prevailing rates."}, {"question": "What is the key accounting difference between a bonus issue and a stock split?", "options": ["There is no difference — both are treated identically", "A bonus issue increases the share capital line with an offsetting reduction in reserves; a stock split leaves total share capital unchanged, merely subdividing it into more shares of smaller nominal value", "A stock split increases reserves; a bonus issue reduces net assets", "A bonus issue requires shareholder cash payment; a stock split does not"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A bonus issue capitalises reserves into new share capital (increasing the share capital account), whereas a split just re-slices the existing share capital total into a larger number of shares with lower nominal value — no reserves movement."}, {"question": "A company has 750,000 $1 nominal shares in issue trading at $3.00 (cum-bonus). It undertakes a 1-for-3 scrip issue. What is the theoretical ex-bonus share price?", "options": ["$3.00", "$2.25", "$2.00", "$1.50"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Market cap = 750,000 × $3.00 = $2.25m, unchanged after the bonus. New shares = 750,000/3 = 250,000, giving 1,000,000 shares total. $2.25m ÷ 1,000,000 = $2.25."}, {"question": "XYZ plc has 1 million shares trading at $2.00 and announces a 1-for-4 bonus issue. What is the new theoretical market price?", "options": ["$1.60", "$2.00", "$1.75", "$2.50"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "New theoretical price = $2.00 × (4/5) = $1.60, since 1.25m shares now represent the same $2m market capitalisation as the original 1m shares."}, {"question": "A company undertakes a 5-for-1 stock split on shares trading at $3.00. What is the theoretical post-split price?", "options": ["$15.00", "$3.00", "$0.60", "$1.00"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "$3.00 ÷ 5 = $0.60. Market capitalisation is unchanged; there are simply five times as many shares."}, {"question": "A company carries out a 1-for-2 reverse stock split (2 old shares become 1 new share) on shares trading at $1.20. What is the theoretical post-split price?", "options": ["$0.60", "$1.20", "$2.40", "$3.60"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "$1.20 × 2 = $2.40. Combining two shares into one doubles the price per share while leaving total value unchanged."}, {"question": "Which best describes the purpose of pre-emptive rights?", "options": ["To guarantee a minimum dividend to shareholders", "To give existing shareholders first refusal on new share issues for cash, so their proportionate ownership/control is not diluted without their agreement", "To prevent a company from ever issuing new shares", "To fix the share price for a rights issue at par value"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Pre-emptive rights require companies (absent shareholder waiver) to offer new cash share issues to existing shareholders first, in proportion to their holdings, protecting against dilution."}, {"question": "In the TERP (theoretical ex-rights price) formula, what does 'R' represent?", "options": ["The rights issue discount percentage", "The number of new (rights) shares to be issued", "The retained earnings of the company", "The rate of return required by shareholders"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "TERP = (N × Pcum + R × Pissue) ÷ (N + R), where N is shares held cum-rights and R is the number of new rights shares issued."}, {"question": "A company's shares trade at £3.00 cum-rights. It launches a 1-for-4 rights issue at £1.50. What is the theoretical ex-rights price (TERP)?", "options": ["£2.40", "£2.70", "£3.00", "£1.50"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "TERP = [(4 × £3.00) + (1 × £1.50)] ÷ 5 = £13.50 ÷ 5 = £2.70."}, {"question": "Using the same 1-for-4 rights issue at £1.50 with a TERP of £2.70 (270p), what is the nil-paid value of one right?", "options": ["150p", "270p", "120p", "420p"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Nil-paid value = TERP − issue price = 270p − 150p = 120p."}, {"question": "A company's shares trade at $5.00 cum-rights. It announces a 1-for-3 rights issue at $3.00. What is the theoretical ex-rights price?", "options": ["$4.00", "$4.50", "$5.00", "$3.75"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "TERP = [(3 × $5.00) + (1 × $3.00)] ÷ 4 = $18.00 ÷ 4 = $4.50."}, {"question": "A company's shares trade at $8.00 cum-rights. It announces a 1-for-6 rights issue at $4.50. What is the nil-paid value of a right?", "options": ["$4.50", "$8.00", "$3.00", "$0.50"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "TERP = [(6 × $8.00) + (1 × $4.50)] ÷ 7 = $52.50 ÷ 7 = $7.50. Nil-paid value = $7.50 − $4.50 = $3.00."}, {"question": "An investor holds 2,000 shares cum-rights in a 1-for-4 rights issue at 150p (cum-rights price 300p, TERP 270p, nil-paid value 120p). Approximately how many nil-paid rights must be sold to 'swallow the tail' (fund taking up the remaining rights at no extra cost)?", "options": ["500", "222", "278", "120"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Rights to sell = (issue price × rights allocated) ÷ TERP = (150p × 500) ÷ 270p = 277.78, rounded up to 278."}, {"question": "In the 'swallowing the tail' calculation, why must the number of nil-paid rights sold be rounded up rather than down?", "options": ["Regulation requires overselling of rights", "Nil-paid rights cannot be sold in fractions, and rounding up ensures sufficient proceeds are raised to cover taking up the remaining whole rights", "Rounding down would breach pre-emption rules", "It has no effect on the outcome"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Since fractional rights cannot be sold, the number sold is rounded up to the nearest whole right, which ensures (with a small residual cash gain) that the proceeds are sufficient to fund the shares taken up."}, {"question": "An investor holds 3,000 shares cum-rights at $5.00; the company announces a 1-for-3 rights issue at $3.00 (TERP $4.50, nil-paid value $1.50). Approximately how many rights must be sold to swallow the tail?", "options": ["1,000", "333", "667", "1,500"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Rights allocated = 3,000 ÷ 3 = 1,000. Rights to sell = ($3.00 × 1,000) ÷ $4.50 = 666.67, rounded up to 667."}, {"question": "Which of the following is NOT one of the four choices available to a shareholder in a rights issue?", "options": ["Take up the rights in full", "Sell all of the rights nil-paid", "Sell some rights nil-paid to fund taking up the rest ('swallowing the tail')", "Demand the company cancel the rights issue"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "The four choices are: take up in full; sell all nil-paid; sell some nil-paid to fund taking up the balance; or take no action (rights sold on the shareholder's behalf, proceeds distributed pro rata)."}, {"question": "National Grid plc's 2024 rights issue was structured as a:", "options": ["1-for-4 rights issue at 300 pence", "7-for-24 rights issue at 645 pence, fully underwritten", "20-for-1 stock split", "Reverse stock split at 500 pence"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "National Grid's rights issue of 1,085,448,980 new ordinary shares at 645 pence was announced 23 May 2024 on a 7-for-24 basis and was fully underwritten, receiving ~91% valid acceptances."}, {"question": "What is the primary regulatory concern that limits the monetary value a company may use for a share buyback in a given year?", "options": ["Preventing the share price from rising too quickly", "Protecting the 'creditors' buffer' — the capital originally paid into the company — from erosion", "Ensuring the company pays a dividend instead", "Preventing insider dealing"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Buyback regulation (via accounting tests) is designed partly to prevent unfair prejudice to creditors by limiting erosion of the capital originally contributed to the company."}, {"question": "In an accelerated bookbuild buyback structure, what does a 'best-efforts basis' mean for junior syndicate members?", "options": ["They guarantee to purchase the full target number of shares regardless of market conditions", "They will attempt to buy back shares but do not guarantee any financial compensation and are not liable if the target is not met", "They must buy shares only above the backstop price", "They act only as custodians, never as buyers"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A best-efforts basis means dealers try to buy back shares without guaranteeing results or being liable for shortfalls — contrasted with a bought deal, where underwriters buy the shares outright themselves."}, {"question": "How does a bought deal differ from a best-efforts buyback arrangement?", "options": ["In a bought deal the underwriter purchases the shares itself and then resells them to clients, retaining unsold inventory; best-efforts involves no such guarantee", "A bought deal is only used for bond issuance", "There is no meaningful difference", "Best-efforts always achieves a higher price than a bought deal"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "In a bought deal, the underwriter(s) commit their own capital, buying the shares outright and bearing the risk of reselling them; a best-efforts deal offers no such guarantee to the seller."}, {"question": "A company earns $10m annual profit with 10 million shares in issue (EPS = $1.00). It buys back and cancels 1 million shares using surplus cash, with profit unchanged. What is the new EPS (approximately)?", "options": ["$0.90", "$1.00", "$1.11", "$1.25"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "New EPS = $10m ÷ 9m shares ≈ $1.11. Reducing the share count via buyback mechanically raises EPS even with unchanged total profit."}, {"question": "Why might a company use contracts for difference (CFDs) rather than buying shares directly when stakebuilding?", "options": ["CFDs are cheaper to trade in all cases", "CFDs give the same economic exposure to price movements in a less visible, potentially leveraged way, but without voting rights or board influence", "CFDs automatically confer board representation", "CFDs are required by law for all stakebuilding"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "CFDs provide economic exposure to a share's price movement (indirect stakebuilding) with more secrecy and potential leverage, but unlike direct share ownership, they do not confer voting rights or the ability to demand board seats."}, {"question": "Under the FCA's Disclosure and Transparency Rules, at what threshold does a shareholding in a UK public company first become 'notifiable'?", "options": ["1%", "3%", "5%", "10%"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A holding of 3% or more is a notifiable interest; above 3%, the investor must also disclose subsequent changes of a whole percentage point."}, {"question": "Under PTM (Panel on Takeovers and Mergers) rules, at what threshold of voting rights does a mandatory cash offer for the target company generally become required?", "options": ["10%", "20%", "30%", "50%"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "A mandatory offer is triggered when a person's holding reaches 30% or more of the target's voting rights, or when a stake already between 30% and 50% is increased further."}, {"question": "A predator purchasing a large number of shares as soon as the market opens, in order to build a stake before its intentions become widely known, is engaging in a:", "options": ["Bought deal", "Dawn raid", "Sinking fund purchase", "Rights issue"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A dawn raid is a direct stakebuilding tactic — purchasing a large volume of shares over a short period, typically at market open, before the market becomes aware of the predator's intentions."}, {"question": "What is the key structural distinction between a 'merger' and an 'acquisition/takeover'?", "options": ["There is no distinction — the terms are always interchangeable in a legal sense", "A merger typically combines two similarly-sized companies via a share exchange into a jointly-owned entity; an acquisition/takeover involves one company buying more than 50% of another, gaining control (parent/subsidiary)", "A merger always involves a hostile bid, while an acquisition is always friendly", "A merger requires regulatory approval; an acquisition never does"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "In a takeover/acquisition, the predator buys a majority stake and gains control (target becomes a subsidiary); in a merger, two similarly-sized firms combine, typically through a share exchange, under joint shareholder ownership."}, {"question": "In BHP's 2024 attempted takeover of Anglo American, why did the bid ultimately fail despite BHP raising its offer three times?", "options": ["Anglo American's shareholders voted to reject it in a binding poll", "Anglo American's directors remained unwilling to engage with BHP or recommend the bid to shareholders, and BHP abandoned its bid", "UK competition regulators blocked the deal", "BHP ran out of financing"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Despite three increased bids, Anglo American's directors refused to engage with or recommend BHP's offer, and BHP ultimately abandoned the hostile approach — illustrating that a hostile bid can fail even after repeated sweetening."}, {"question": "Which of the following is a commonly cited benefit of a merger or acquisition?", "options": ["Guaranteed avoidance of cultural integration issues", "Economies of scale from bulk purchasing and cost synergies (e.g. rationalising HR and finance functions)", "Automatic exemption from competition regulation", "Elimination of all future need to raise capital"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A larger combined entity may achieve cost reductions through economies of scale (bulk buying) and synergies from rationalising overlapping functions such as HR and finance."}, {"question": "Which UK body is responsible for reviewing substantial mergers and acquisitions to protect against monopoly/anti-competitive effects?", "options": ["The Financial Conduct Authority (FCA)", "The Competition and Markets Authority (CMA)", "The Panel on Takeovers and Mergers (PTM)", "The Bank of England"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The CMA is the UK's competition regulator, fulfilling a role broadly similar to antitrust agencies (e.g. the FTC/DOJ) in the US, reviewing mergers for monopoly-type effects on consumers."}, {"question": "A monopoly is best described as a market situation in which:", "options": ["Many firms compete on price", "One entity controls the entire market for a good or service and can set, rather than accept, the market price", "Government sets all prices directly", "Firms are legally required to merge"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "In a monopoly, a single supplier faces no competitors and can dictate price, potentially exploiting consumers — the core reason competition authorities scrutinise large mergers."}, {"question": "Which statement about putable bonds is correct?", "options": ["They give the issuer the right to redeem the bond early", "They give the investor the flexibility to require early redemption after giving the issuer due notice", "They can never be redeemed before maturity", "They are only issued as sinking-fund bonds"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A put provision benefits the bondholder, who can demand early redemption after providing notice — the opposite of a call provision, which benefits the issuer."}], "flashcards": [{"front": "Why are bond coupons more predictable than equity dividends?", "back": "Coupons are a contractual obligation (fixed rate/frequency/nominal set at issue); dividends are discretionary, dependent on profitability and cash flow."}, {"front": "What is an FRN and what does its coupon reference?", "back": "A floating-rate note; coupon = margin above a reference rate (historically LIBOR/EURIBOR, increasingly SOFR), often paid quarterly."}, {"front": "Why are preference shares called a 'hybrid' instrument?", "back": "Dividend rate is pre-specified like a bond coupon (predictable), but as equity the issuer can still choose to suspend payment in stress."}, {"front": "What is a bullet bond issue?", "back": "A bond structured to repay 100% of principal in a single lump sum at maturity; carries higher risk/coupon than non-bullet issues."}, {"front": "What is a sinking fund?", "back": "Issuer sets aside funds annually (often via trustee) toward maturity repayment, or buys back bonds below par, reducing default risk at maturity."}, {"front": "Callable bond — whose right, and when exercised?", "back": "Issuer's right to redeem some/all bonds early (usually at par); exercised when the bond has become relatively expensive (rates have fallen)."}, {"front": "Putable bond — whose right, and when exercised?", "back": "Investor's right to demand early redemption (with notice); benefits the investor, e.g. if rates rise or credit concerns increase."}, {"front": "What is a bonus issue (scrip/capitalisation issue)?", "back": "Free pro-rata issue of new shares to existing shareholders, funded by converting reserves into share capital; no cash raised."}, {"front": "Accounting difference: bonus issue vs stock split?", "back": "Bonus issue increases share capital account, decreases reserves. Stock split: total share capital unchanged, just subdivided into more shares of lower nominal value."}, {"front": "1-for-3 scrip issue: 750,000 $1 shares @ $3.00 cum-bonus — theoretical ex-bonus price?", "back": "$2.25 (market cap $2.25m unchanged ÷ 1,000,000 shares after issue)."}, {"front": "XYZ 1-for-4 bonus issue, price $2.00 before — theoretical price after?", "back": "$1.60 ($2.00 × 4/5); market cap unchanged at $2m."}, {"front": "5-for-1 stock split on a $3.00 share — new theoretical price?", "back": "$0.60 ($3.00 ÷ 5)."}, {"front": "1-for-2 reverse stock split on a $1.20 share — new theoretical price?", "back": "$2.40 ($1.20 × 2)."}, {"front": "Why might a company do a reverse stock split?", "back": "Share price has fallen to a very low level (e.g. below $1.00), risking investor confidence; consolidating shares raises the price per share."}, {"front": "What are pre-emptive rights?", "back": "Existing shareholders' legal right to be offered new cash share issues first, in proportion to their holding, before they go to outside investors."}, {"front": "Rights issue — key advantage over other fundraising for shareholders?", "back": "No dilution of proportionate ownership (if rights are taken up); shares offered at a discount to market price."}, {"front": "TERP formula", "back": "TERP = (N × P(cum-rights) + R × P(issue price)) ÷ (N + R), where N = shares held cum-rights, R = new rights shares."}, {"front": "1-for-4 rights issue at £1.50, cum-rights price £3.00 — TERP?", "back": "£2.70: [(4×£3.00)+(1×£1.50)]÷5 = £13.50÷5."}, {"front": "Nil-paid value formula", "back": "Nil-paid value = TERP − rights issue price."}, {"front": "TERP £2.70, issue price £1.50 — nil-paid value?", "back": "120p (270p − 150p)."}, {"front": "1-for-3 rights issue at $3.00, cum-rights price $5.00 — TERP?", "back": "$4.50: [(3×$5.00)+(1×$3.00)]÷4."}, {"front": "1-for-6 rights issue at $4.50, cum-rights price $8.00 — nil-paid value?", "back": "$3.00 (TERP = $7.50, minus $4.50 issue price)."}, {"front": "What does 'swallowing the tail' mean?", "back": "Selling just enough nil-paid rights to fund taking up the remaining rights, without investing any new cash."}, {"front": "Formula: number of nil-paid rights to sell to swallow the tail", "back": "(Issue price × rights allocated) ÷ TERP, rounded UP to the nearest whole right (fractional rights can't be sold)."}, {"front": "2,000 shares cum-rights, 1-for-4 at 150p, TERP 270p — rights to sell to swallow tail?", "back": "278: (150p × 500) ÷ 270p = 277.78, rounded up."}, {"front": "What is a provisional allotment letter?", "back": "Renounceable, transferable document sent to shareholders in a rights issue setting out existing holding, new rights allotted, and acceptance date."}, {"front": "Four choices for a rights issue shareholder", "back": "Take up in full; sell all nil-paid; sell some nil-paid to fund the rest (swallow the tail); do nothing (rights sold on their behalf, proceeds distributed pro rata)."}, {"front": "Why are rights issues usually underwritten?", "back": "To guarantee the company raises the full capital sought regardless of shareholder take-up."}, {"front": "National Grid plc 2024 rights issue terms", "back": "7-for-24 rights issue, 1,085,448,980 new shares at 645p, fully underwritten, ~91% valid acceptances."}, {"front": "Two common reasons for a share buyback", "back": "(1) Return surplus cash after disposing of a business segment; (2) re-gear capital structure toward more debt/less equity."}, {"front": "What is the 'creditors' buffer' in buyback regulation?", "back": "The capital originally paid into the company, protected by rules limiting the value of shares that can be repurchased, to avoid unfairly prejudicing creditors."}, {"front": "Accelerated bookbuild: best-efforts basis vs bought deal", "back": "Best-efforts: dealer attempts to buy, no guarantee/liability for shortfall. Bought deal: underwriter buys shares outright itself, then resells, keeping unsold inventory."}, {"front": "Backstop price in a buyback syndicate", "back": "The maximum price the syndicate has agreed it will not exceed when buying back shares."}, {"front": "Buyback impact on EPS — 10m shares, $10m profit, buy back 1m shares", "back": "EPS rises from $1.00 (10m/10m) to ≈$1.11 (10m/9m) — fewer shares over unchanged profit."}, {"front": "Direct vs indirect stakebuilding", "back": "Direct: outright share purchase (e.g. dawn raid). Indirect: CFDs — same economic exposure, no voting rights/board influence, more secretive, can be leveraged."}, {"front": "UK notifiable shareholding disclosure threshold", "back": "3% or more of a public company; above 3%, must also disclose changes of a further whole percentage point (FCA Disclosure & Transparency Rules)."}, {"front": "PTM mandatory offer threshold", "back": "30% or more of voting rights, or any increase to a stake already between 30% and 50%, triggers a mandatory cash offer under PTM rules."}, {"front": "Mandatory offer consideration requirement under PTM rules", "back": "Must be cash, or have a cash alternative, at no less than the highest price the offeror paid in the previous 12 months."}, {"front": "Disclosure timing during a takeover offer under PTM rules", "back": "Dealings in relevant securities by offeror/offeree/associates must be disclosed to the Panel and an RIS by noon the next business day; also applies to anyone holding over 1%."}, {"front": "Merger vs acquisition/takeover — key difference", "back": "Acquisition: predator buys >50% of target, gains control (parent/subsidiary). Merger: similarly-sized firms combine via share exchange into a jointly-owned entity."}, {"front": "Friendly vs hostile takeover", "back": "Friendly: target directors recommend acceptance. Hostile: target directors recommend rejection. Shareholders decide either way."}, {"front": "BHP / Anglo American 2024 — outcome and lesson", "back": "BHP raised its bid three times but Anglo American's directors refused to engage/recommend; BHP abandoned the bid — a hostile approach can fail despite repeated sweetening."}, {"front": "Key disadvantage risk in M&A", "back": "Acquirer may overpay (excessive premium); cultural clashes; limited synergies if companies have little in common, adding cost rather than reducing it."}, {"front": "What defines a monopoly, and why do regulators care about M&A?", "back": "One entity controls the entire market and can set (not accept) prices, exploiting consumers; competition authorities review large M&A to prevent this."}, {"front": "UK vs US competition/antitrust regulator", "back": "UK: Competition and Markets Authority (CMA). US: antitrust laws enforced by agencies including the FTC and DOJ."}]}, {"id": "sec-m9", "number": 9, "title": "Custody, Registration & Stock Lending", "examWeight": "Core module", "summaryHtml": "<p>A <strong>custodian</strong> exists primarily to keep client assets safe: it provides robust safekeeping of physical and digital assets, releases them only against authorised instructions, and, critically, segregates client assets from its own so that a financial or external shock to the custodian does not expose clients to creditor claims. Beyond safekeeping, custodians maintain inventory records, verify trade details, arrange settlement of cash and securities, ensure securities are properly registered and legal title transferred, supply market information, collect dividend and interest income, manage client cash flows, process corporate actions and voting entitlements, handle tax reclaims, and meet regulatory reporting obligations.</p><p>Investors choose between a <strong>global custodian</strong> (cross-border administration for the full portfolio; the term dates from 1970s US ERISA legislation), a <strong>sub-custodian</strong> (a global custodian's local agent, its 'eyes and ears' in a foreign market, which may be an own branch, a local specialist bank, or a regional provider), a <strong>local/single-market custodian</strong> (deep local expertise but weaker credit rating and technology), or a <strong>regional custodian</strong> (multi-market reach and economies of scale, but less local nuance than a pure local player).</p><p>Ownership of shares is proved not by a share certificate but by entry on the company's <strong>share register</strong>. Most shares today are registered; <strong>bearer shares</strong> (ownership by physical possession, now banned in the UK) are being phased out globally under OECD pressure for beneficial-ownership transparency. To avoid administrative burden, investors commonly appoint a <strong>nominee</strong>, which takes <strong>legal/registered title</strong> while the client retains <strong>beneficial ownership</strong> (dividends, capital growth). Nominee arrangements are <strong>pooled/omnibus</strong> (multiple clients under one name, only the nominee can vote, no separate client entitlement), <strong>designated</strong> (unique identifier per client under one nominee name), or <strong>sole nominee</strong> (a dedicated name for one client, at slightly higher cost). A <strong>corporate nominee</strong>, sponsored by the issuer itself, sits between pooled and designated: one register entry overall, but the registrar can identify and pay each underlying small shareholder individually.</p><p><strong>Stock lending</strong> is the temporary transfer of securities from lender to borrower against equivalent (fungible) securities returned later, driven by securities-driven or cash-driven motivations and governed by a securities lending agreement requiring collateral (cash, government securities or a letter of credit) and a fee (or rebate, if collateral is cash). Its main historical driver was covering settlement failures and short sales; benefits include enhanced liquidity, extra income, support for trading/derivative strategies and improved price discovery. Risks include failure to return securities/collateral, mitigated by credit evaluation, exposure limits, collateralisation with a 'haircut', daily mark-to-market/margin calls, and master legal agreements such as the GMSLA. <strong>Stock borrowing and lending intermediaries (SBLIs)</strong>, often custodian banks, connect long-term holders (pension/insurance funds) with borrowers (hedge funds), pooling assets and splitting revenue. On loan, legal title (and voting rights) passes to the borrower, who must return economic benefits to the lender as a manufactured dividend; lenders must recall stock to vote. Stock lending differs from repo mainly in that lending earns a fee while repo involves interest on cash.</p>", "sections": [{"heading": "9.1 The Role and Responsibility of a Custodian", "html": "<p>The primary responsibility of a custodian is to keep clients' assets <strong>safe</strong>, providing robust safekeeping facilities for physical and digital assets and releasing investments from its care only in accordance with authorised client instructions.</p><p>Two features are essential:</p><ul><li><strong>Segregation</strong> - each client's assets must be properly segregated from those of the custodian itself.</li><li><strong>Legal protection</strong> - appropriate legal arrangements must ensure that a financial or external shock to the custodian does not expose client assets to claims from the custodian's creditors or any other party.</li></ul><p>A custodian is typically a large, reputable third-party firm holding securities or other assets belonging to an investment institution, in either physical or electronic (dematerialised) form.</p>"}, {"heading": "9.2 Services Provided by Custodians", "html": "<p>In addition to core safekeeping, custodians provide a broad range of services to clients:</p><ul><li>Safekeeping of the investor's assets in the local market</li><li>Maintaining inventory lists</li><li>Verifying trade details against securities available for sale from that institution</li><li>Arranging delivery and receipt of cash and securities to support settlement</li><li>Ensuring securities are registered and legal title transfer proceeds effectively</li><li>Providing market information on developments and reforms in the local market</li><li>Collecting dividend income, bond interest and other income payments locally</li><li>Managing the client's cash flows</li><li>Monitoring and managing entitlements through corporate actions and voting rights</li><li>Managing tax reclaims and other local tax services</li><li>Ensuring regulatory and other reporting obligations are discharged effectively</li></ul>"}, {"heading": "9.3 Types of Custodians: Global, Sub-, Local and Regional", "html": "<p>An investor with a global portfolio can appoint a local custodian in each market (direct custody), appoint a single <strong>global custodian</strong> across all markets, or settle/hold via a CSD or international CSD (ICSD) directly.</p><table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Role</th><th>Key points</th></tr><tr><td>Global custodian</td><td>Cross-border investment administration and safekeeping outside the investor's home country</td><td>Term originated from US ERISA (1974), which required pension fund assets to be held by an independent bank custodian. Largest global custodians (2025) include JPMorgan Chase, Citi, BNY Mellon and State Street. May also offer investment accounting, treasury/FX, securities lending, collateral management and risk analytics</td></tr><tr><td>Sub-custodian</td><td>Local agent appointed by a global custodian to provide settlement/custody in a foreign market</td><td>The 'eyes and ears' of the global custodian locally; may be the global custodian's own branch, a local agent bank, or a regional provider (eg, Brown Brothers Harriman's sub-custody network)</td></tr><tr><td>Local (single market) custodian</td><td>Specialist providing custody only in its home market</td><td>Strengths: country specialism, regulator/political relationships, local market/language/culture knowledge, reciprocal business. Weaknesses: weaker credit rating, technology lag, fewer price discounts. Examples: MUFG, Mizuho, SMBC (Japan), Maybank (Malaysia), UOB (Singapore)</td></tr><tr><td>Regional custodian</td><td>Agent bank services across multiple markets in a region</td><td>Strengths: higher credit rating, cross-fertilised best practice, tech/scale economies, standardised reporting, regulatory leverage, price discounts. Weaknesses: less local nuance, spread focus, shorter track record than local incumbents. Examples: Standard Chartered/HSBC (Asia-Pacific); Bank Austria, Deutsche Bank, Raiffeisen, Citi (CEE); Citi/Itau Unibanco (Latin America); Standard Chartered/Standard Bank (Africa)</td></tr></table><p><strong>Reciprocal arrangements</strong> can influence appointment of local providers: global custodian A appoints local provider B in market B, and in return offers sub-custody in market A to pension/insurance funds that use B as their global custodian.</p>"}, {"heading": "9.4 The Share Register, Registered Title and Bearer Shares", "html": "<p>When shares are settled, the change of ownership must be communicated to the company registrar, who maintains the register of members (shareholders and addresses) and grants the new owner <strong>registered title</strong>. A share certificate is only evidence of ownership; the register entry is the proof that counts.</p><p><strong>Bearer shares</strong> are unregistered: whoever physically holds the certificate is treated as the owner, like cash, with no register entry to confirm or contradict ownership. Because of the secrecy this affords, the OECD has pressured jurisdictions to eliminate or restrict bearer shares - the UK has banned them entirely, while others (eg, Luxembourg) require they be 'immobilised' with an authorised depository.</p><p>Until sale, both registered and bearer holders receive dividends, may vote (where applicable), and can participate in corporate actions such as rights issues.</p>"}, {"heading": "9.5 Nominees: Legal Title versus Beneficial Ownership", "html": "<p>To avoid the administrative burden of registered title (receiving all company documentation, signing all transfer forms and corporate action instructions), investors commonly appoint a <strong>nominee</strong> - typically a stockbroker, custodian or specially created nominee company - to hold registered/legal title on their behalf.</p><ul><li>The nominee signs a <strong>declaration of trust</strong> confirming it holds the shares on behalf of the beneficial owner and will return them, or transfer them elsewhere, on request.</li><li>The <strong>nominee (legal owner)</strong> appears on the share register, receives company documentation, and, for voting purposes, is the party the company recognises.</li><li>The <strong>beneficial owner</strong> retains the economic benefits: dividends and capital growth, but no automatic right to attend company meetings.</li></ul><p>This separation of legal and beneficial ownership allows custodians, under proper client authority, to transfer shares to meet market transactions without the registrar needing sight of the underlying client's signature or seal. Institutional (and increasingly private client) holdings are overwhelmingly held via nominee accounts run by specialist custodians rather than in the investor's own name.</p>"}, {"heading": "9.6 Types of Nominee Companies", "html": "<table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th><th>Voting/visibility</th></tr><tr><td>Pooled (omnibus/commingled)</td><td>Multiple clients grouped under a single nominee registration</td><td>No individual client visibility to the company; only the single bulk nominee is entitled to vote - no separate entitlement accrues to underlying clients</td></tr><tr><td>Designated (numbered accounts)</td><td>Nominee name includes a unique identifier per client, eg, XYZ Nominees Account 1, Account 2</td><td>Individual client identifiable to the custodian; still one nominee name on the register</td></tr><tr><td>Sole nominee</td><td>A single nominee name dedicated to one client, eg, ABC Pension Fund Nominees Ltd</td><td>Full separate identification; useful where the client needs dividends mandated to a specific bank account or wants an audit-friendly, individually controlled holding</td></tr></table><p>There is no real security advantage between the three structures, but pooled nominees can obscure true ownership (useful where an institution does not want to publicise a stake) and, historically, funds have been harder to remove from omnibus accounts in a bankruptcy than from individually registered holdings. Company shareholder perks (eg, product discounts) may be unavailable through a nominee, since brokers may be unwilling to administer them. Designated registration adds minimal cost for the custodian; a sole nominee involves setting up a nameplate company, appointing directors and filing annual returns - costs usually absorbed within the standard custody tariff.</p>"}, {"heading": "9.7 The Corporate Nominee", "html": "<p>A <strong>corporate nominee</strong> (or corporate sponsored nominee) is provided by the issuing company itself as a facility for its smaller shareholders. It is a halfway house between pooled and designated structures: there is a single entry for all participating shareholders on the company's register (like a pooled nominee), but the issuing company (or its registrar) retains awareness of each individual underlying holding (like a designated nominee) - enabling separate dividend payments, voting rights and shareholder perks to be passed through to each individual. Holding shares within a corporate nominee in <strong>dematerialised form</strong> enables quick and easy transfer through the settlement system.</p>"}, {"heading": "9.8 Stock Lending: Purpose, Mechanics and Benefits", "html": "<p><strong>Stock lending</strong> is the temporary transfer of securities from a lender to a borrower, with the borrower agreeing to return equivalent (fungible) securities at a pre-agreed time. It is most common in developed, liquid markets that permit short selling.</p><p>Two motivations drive stock lending:</p><ul><li><strong>Securities-driven</strong> transactions - borrowers need specific securities to facilitate trading operations (eg, to cover a short sale or settlement obligation).</li><li><strong>Cash-driven</strong> transactions - lenders enhance portfolio returns by earning a fee for making securities available.</li></ul><p>The transaction is governed by a securities lending agreement under which the borrower provides collateral (cash, government securities, or a letter of credit) of value equal to or greater than the loaned securities. The parties negotiate a fee, quoted as an annualised percentage of the loaned securities' value; where collateral is cash, the fee is quoted as a <strong>rebate</strong> - the lender keeps all interest on the cash collateral and rebates an agreed rate back to the borrower.</p><p>The original driver of stock lending was covering <strong>settlement failure</strong> - borrowing stock for a fee to meet a delivery commitment, then returning equivalent shares once the original stock arrives. Other benefits include: increased market liquidity and reduced failed settlements; extra security for lenders via collateralisation; support for trading/derivative strategies otherwise hard to execute; income generation for lenders; and improved market efficiency through arbitrage of price differentials.</p><table><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Stock lending</th><th>Repo</th></tr><tr><td>Nature</td><td>Temporary transfer of securities for a fee</td><td>Sale of securities against cash, repurchased later, with interest paid</td></tr><tr><td>Compensation</td><td>Fee (annualised % of loan value, or rebate if cash collateral)</td><td>Rate of interest on the cash</td></tr><tr><td>Primary purpose</td><td>Obtain specific securities (eg, to cover a short sale/settlement)</td><td>Obtain/lend cash cheaply on a secured basis</td></tr><tr><td>Collateral</td><td>Cash, government securities or letter of credit, marked to market</td><td>Securities collateralise the cash; marked to market with variation margin/margin calls</td></tr></table>"}, {"heading": "9.9 Stock Lending Risks, SBLIs and Legalities", "html": "<p><strong>Risks:</strong> securities or collateral may not be returned on the agreed date due to settlement delays, market liquidity problems, counterparty default or legal challenge, potentially forcing the lender to repurchase securities in the market at a high cost.</p><table><tr><th>Risk mitigation technique</th><th>Purpose</th></tr><tr><td>Detailed credit evaluation of the borrower</td><td>Assess counterparty risk before lending</td></tr><tr><td>Credit exposure limits per borrower</td><td>Cap concentration risk</td></tr><tr><td>Collateralisation (cash or securities)</td><td>Secure the loan; lender chooses acceptable collateral quality</td></tr><tr><td>Daily mark-to-market and margin calls</td><td>Keep collateral and exposure aligned to current market values</td></tr><tr><td>Master legal agreements (eg, GMSLA)</td><td>Set clear legal parameters and default compensation mechanics</td></tr><tr><td>Haircut</td><td>Collateral value exceeds loaned securities' market value by an agreed % to absorb adverse price moves</td></tr></table><p><strong>Stock Borrowing and Lending Intermediaries (SBLIs)</strong> separate underlying long-term owners (pension funds, insurance companies) from borrowers (hedge funds, asset managers), pooling securities from many clients to achieve economies of scale and splitting commercial revenue with owners. Custodian banks are natural SBLIs because custody is often low-margin: they already have banking relationships, technology and global market coverage, the ability to pool many smaller funds, local operational experience, and the capacity to provide indemnities and manage cash collateral.</p><p><strong>Administration:</strong> an SBLI must have adequate documentation with borrowing counterparties covering terms, conditions and risks; must only lend assets of clients who have consented; must maintain separate accounts/systems distinguishing consenting from non-consenting clients; must assess collateral timing and type against borrower creditworthiness and market risk; and must hold cash/assets relating to lending (dividends, fees, etc) in accordance with custody rules.</p><p><strong>Legalities:</strong> stock lending is legal and regulated in most major markets, generally restricted to specific permitted purposes: facilitating settlement of a trade, facilitating delivery of a short sale, financing the security, or facilitating a loan to another borrower motivated by one of these purposes. Short-selling disclosure thresholds: in Europe, private disclosure to the regulator begins at net short positions of 0.1% of issued share capital; in the UK, 0.2%; both regimes require public disclosure at 0.5%.</p><p><strong>Effect on the lender's rights:</strong> when a security is loaned, legal title transfers to the borrower, who receives all coupon/dividend payments and voting rights during the loan. Economic benefits must be passed back to the lender as a <strong>manufactured dividend</strong>. A lender wishing to vote must recall the stock in time to complete a proxy form before the deadline. Costs/benefits of other corporate actions (eg, capitalisation or rights issues) customarily flow back to the lender as set out in the securities lending agreement. The <strong>Global Master Securities Lending Agreement (GMSLA)</strong> is the market-standard legal agreement covering these obligations.</p>"}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What is the primary responsibility of a custodian?", "options": ["Providing investment advice to retail clients", "Keeping clients' assets safe through robust safekeeping and proper segregation", "Executing trades on behalf of institutional investors", "Setting the market price of securities held in custody"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The custodian's core responsibility is safekeeping of client assets, ensuring they are fully protected and properly segregated from the custodian's own assets so that a shock to the custodian does not expose clients to creditor claims."}, {"question": "Which of the following is NOT typically listed as a service provided by a custodian?", "options": ["Collecting dividend income and bond interest in the local market", "Managing tax reclaims in the local market", "Underwriting new issues of equity capital", "Ensuring regulatory reporting obligations are discharged"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Underwriting new issues is an investment banking function, not a custody service. Custodians handle safekeeping, settlement support, registration, income collection, tax reclaims, corporate actions/voting, and regulatory reporting."}, {"question": "The term 'global custody' became widely used following which piece of legislation?", "options": ["The Dodd-Frank Act", "The Employee Retirement Income Security Act (ERISA)", "The Investment Company Act", "The Sarbanes-Oxley Act"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "ERISA (1974) required US pension funds to hold assets with an independent bank custodian rather than acting as custodian of their own funds, popularising the term 'global custody'."}, {"question": "A sub-custodian is best described as:", "options": ["A CSD that settles trades for all market participants", "A local agent appointed by a global custodian to provide settlement and custody services in a foreign market", "An institutional investor that lends securities to hedge funds", "A regulator that oversees custody arrangements"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A sub-custodian is employed by a global custodian as its local agent, acting as the 'eyes and ears' of the global custodian in a given foreign market, providing clearing, settlement and asset servicing there."}, {"question": "Which of the following is generally considered a strength of a local (single-market) custodian compared with a regional custodian?", "options": ["A stronger credit rating", "Ability to offer price discounts across multiple markets", "Expert knowledge of local market practice, language and culture", "Faster adoption of new technology across its network"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Local custodians' key strength is deep local market, language and cultural expertise, plus close ties to regulators and politicians. Credit rating, price discounts and technology leverage tend to favour regional custodians."}, {"question": "A reciprocal custody arrangement typically involves:", "options": ["A global custodian appointing a local provider in market B, which in turn is offered sub-custody business in market A", "Two global custodians merging their operations", "A regulator requiring custodians to share client data", "A local custodian outsourcing all its functions to a global custodian"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Under a reciprocal arrangement, global custodian A appoints local provider B for sub-custody in market B, and in return custodian A offers sub-custody in its own home market to clients of B."}, {"question": "What is the key characteristic of a bearer share?", "options": ["It can only be held by institutional investors", "Ownership is evidenced solely by physical possession of the certificate, with no register entry", "It automatically carries enhanced voting rights", "It must be held through a nominee at all times"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Bearer shares are unregistered: whoever physically holds the certificate is treated as the owner, similar to cash, with no register entry to confirm or contradict this."}, {"question": "For a registered shareholding, what constitutes definitive proof of ownership?", "options": ["The physical share certificate", "The entry on the company's share register", "A signed contract note from the broker", "The custodian's internal ledger only"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A share certificate is merely evidence of ownership; the name and address recorded on the company's share register is the proof that legally counts."}, {"question": "In a nominee arrangement, who holds legal title to the shares?", "options": ["The beneficial owner", "The nominee", "The company registrar", "The stock exchange"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The nominee takes registered/legal title to the shares and the associated administrative responsibilities, while the beneficial owner retains the economic benefits of ownership."}, {"question": "Which right does a beneficial owner NOT automatically retain when shares are held via a nominee?", "options": ["Entitlement to dividends", "Entitlement to capital growth", "The automatic right to attend and vote at company meetings", "Economic benefit from the shares"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The beneficial owner receives dividends and capital growth but does not retain the automatic right to attend company meetings, since the nominee (legal owner) is the party recognised for voting purposes."}, {"question": "What document does a nominee sign to confirm it holds shares on behalf of the beneficial owner?", "options": ["A declaration of trust", "A power of attorney", "A prospectus", "A collateral agreement"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Each nominee shareholder signs a declaration of trust confirming it holds the shares on behalf of the beneficial owner and will return or transfer them as instructed."}, {"question": "In a pooled (omnibus) nominee account, who is entitled to vote from the registrar's perspective?", "options": ["Each underlying client individually, in proportion to their holding", "Only the single nominee name appearing on the register", "No one, as pooled accounts forfeit voting rights entirely", "The company's board of directors"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "In an omnibus/pooled nominee account, only the single bulk nominee is entitled to vote; no separate voting entitlement accrues to individual underlying clients from the registrar's standpoint."}, {"question": "A designated nominee account is best described as:", "options": ["A single name covering multiple unrelated clients with no distinguishing feature", "A nominee name that includes a unique identifier for each individual client", "A nominee name reserved exclusively for a single client", "An account only used for bearer shares"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Designated (numbered) nominee accounts use a common nominee name with a unique identifier per client, eg, XYZ Nominees Account 1, Account 2, allowing individual client identification."}, {"question": "Why might a client insist on a sole nominee name rather than a pooled or designated arrangement?", "options": ["It is always the cheapest option for the custodian", "It allows dividends to be mandated directly to a particular bank account, which is impractical via an omnibus account", "It removes the need for a declaration of trust", "It guarantees enhanced security compared with other nominee types"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "One key reason for a sole nominee name is that the underlying investor requires dividends mandated to a specific account, which is not practicable through a pooled/omnibus account. There is no inherent security advantage between nominee types."}, {"question": "A corporate nominee (corporate sponsored nominee) is best described as:", "options": ["A nominee facility set up by a stockbroker for institutional clients only", "A facility provided by the issuing company itself for its smaller shareholders", "A regulator-mandated nominee used only for bearer shares", "A nominee structure exclusive to sovereign wealth funds"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A corporate nominee is provided by the issuing company itself as a facility for its smaller shareholders, sitting between the pooled and designated structures typically offered by stockbrokers."}, {"question": "How does a corporate nominee differ from a standard pooled nominee?", "options": ["It offers no register entry at all", "Beneath a single register entry, the company/registrar retains awareness of individual underlying holdings, enabling separate dividends and voting rights", "It cannot process dividend payments", "It requires each shareholder to be individually listed on the main register"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Like a pooled nominee, a corporate nominee has one register entry overall, but like a designated nominee, the company/registrar can identify individual holdings, enabling separate dividends, voting rights and shareholder perks."}, {"question": "What is stock lending?", "options": ["A permanent sale of securities with no obligation to return them", "The temporary transfer of securities from a lender to a borrower, who agrees to return equivalent securities at a pre-agreed time", "A method of raising unsecured debt finance", "An arrangement where a broker guarantees a minimum share price"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Stock lending is the temporary transfer of securities by a lender to a borrower, with agreement that the borrower will return equivalent (fungible) securities to the lender at a pre-agreed time."}, {"question": "What was the original historical driver of the securities lending business?", "options": ["Facilitating mergers and acquisitions", "Covering settlement failure, allowing a party to borrow stock to meet a delivery commitment", "Providing retail investors with margin loans", "Funding government bond auctions"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The initial driver for securities lending was to cover settlement failure - borrowing stock for a fee to meet a delivery commitment when the lending party did not have the stock available in time."}, {"question": "In a stock loan collateralised with cash, how is the lending fee typically expressed?", "options": ["As a fixed lump sum only", "As a rebate, where the lender keeps the interest on the cash collateral and rebates an agreed rate to the borrower", "As a percentage of the borrower's total assets", "It cannot be expressed in fee terms at all"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Where collateral is cash, the fee is often quoted as a rebate: the lender earns all interest accruing on the cash collateral and pays back (rebates) an agreed rate of interest to the borrower."}, {"question": "What is the principal reason a market participant borrows securities?", "options": ["To increase its voting power at AGMs", "To cover a short sale, where the seller does not hold the securities being sold", "To avoid paying transaction taxes", "To reduce its custody fees"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The principal reason for borrowing securities is to cover a short position - sellers who do not hold the securities they have sold must borrow them to make delivery."}, {"question": "Under UK short-selling disclosure rules, at what level of net short position (as a percentage of issued share capital) does public disclosure become required?", "options": ["0.1%", "0.2%", "0.5%", "1.0%"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "In both the UK and Europe, net short positions are publicly disclosed once they reach 0.5% of a listed company's issued share capital, although private disclosure to the regulator starts earlier (0.2% in the UK, 0.1% in Europe)."}, {"question": "What is a 'haircut' in the context of securities lending collateral?", "options": ["A reduction in the lender's fee", "The amount by which collateral value exceeds the market value of the borrowed securities, to protect against adverse price movements", "A penalty charged for late return of securities", "The custodian's administration charge"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A haircut means the collateral taken will typically exceed the market value of the borrowed securities by an agreed percentage, protecting the lender against adverse price movements in the collateral."}, {"question": "Which of the following is NOT a risk mitigation technique used by lenders in securities lending?", "options": ["Detailed credit evaluation of the borrower", "Daily marking of exposures and collateral to market with margin calls", "Setting limits on credit exposure to individual borrowers", "Accepting collateral only in the form of the borrower's own equity"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "Lenders decide what quality of collateral to accept and typically diversify away from the borrower's own securities; the standard mitigations are credit evaluation, exposure limits, collateralisation, daily mark-to-market/margin calls and master legal agreements."}, {"question": "What is a stock borrowing and lending intermediary (SBLI)?", "options": ["A regulator that licenses short sellers", "An intermediary that separates underlying securities owners (eg, pension funds) from borrowers (eg, hedge funds), often pooling assets for economies of scale", "A clearing house that guarantees settlement of all trades", "A type of bearer share depository"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "SBLIs undertake principal and/or agency roles that separate long-term underlying owners of securities (typically pension funds/insurers) from borrowers (typically hedge funds/asset managers), pooling assets for economies of scale."}, {"question": "Why do many custodian banks act as SBLIs in addition to their core custody business?", "options": ["Custody is a highly profitable activity that funds securities lending", "Custody is often a low-margin activity, so acting as an SBLI provides an additional revenue stream leveraging existing relationships and infrastructure", "Regulation requires all custodians to offer securities lending", "It allows them to avoid segregating client assets"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Custody is often run as a loss-making or low-margin activity, so acting as an SBLI adds a new revenue stream, leveraging the custodian's existing banking relationships, technology, global coverage and cash collateral management capability."}, {"question": "Securities borrowing is generally only permitted, by law, for which purposes?", "options": ["Any purpose the borrower chooses, without restriction", "Facilitating settlement of a trade, delivery of a short sale, financing the security, or facilitating a loan to another borrower with one of these purposes", "Only to fund government bond purchases", "Only for retail investor margin accounts"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Most markets restrict borrowing of securities to specifically permitted purposes: facilitating settlement of a trade, facilitating delivery of a short sale, financing the security, or facilitating a loan to another borrower motivated by one of these purposes."}, {"question": "What happens to legal title and voting rights when a security is loaned?", "options": ["They remain with the lender throughout the loan", "They transfer to the borrower for the duration of the loan", "They are suspended entirely until the loan ends", "They transfer to the SBLI acting as intermediary"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "When a security is loaned, legal title transfers to the borrower, who receives all coupon/dividend payments and voting rights during the loan period, though economic benefits must be passed back to the lender."}, {"question": "What is a 'manufactured dividend' in the context of stock lending?", "options": ["A dividend paid by the issuer directly to the lender despite the loan", "A payment made by the borrower to the lender to replicate the dividend/coupon income the lender would otherwise have received", "A dividend that is entirely forfeited during the loan period", "A special dividend declared only for lent securities"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Because legal title (and the right to receive dividends/coupons) transfers to the borrower during the loan, the borrower must pass the economic value of those payments back to the lender as a manufactured dividend."}, {"question": "If a lender wants to exercise its voting rights on shares that are currently out on loan, what must it do?", "options": ["Nothing - voting rights remain automatically with the lender", "Recall the stock in good time to complete and return a proxy voting form before the registrar's deadline", "Notify the SBLI, who will vote on the lender's behalf automatically", "Wait until the loan naturally matures, as recall is not possible"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "If the lender wants to exercise its right to vote, it should recall the stock in good time so a proxy voting form can be completed and returned to the registrar by the required deadline."}, {"question": "What is the Global Master Securities Lending Agreement (GMSLA)?", "options": ["A regulatory body overseeing securities lending", "A market-standard legal agreement covering the obligations of borrowers and lenders in securities lending transactions", "A clearing system for FX-denominated stock loans", "A tax treaty covering cross-border dividend withholding"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The GMSLA is a market-standard legal agreement developed to cover the matters a securities lending legal agreement ought to address, kept under periodic review with amendments applied by mutual party agreement."}, {"question": "What is the key difference between stock lending and a repurchase agreement (repo)?", "options": ["Stock lending always involves government bonds only, while repo involves equities only", "A stock lender charges a fee, while a repo counterparty pays or receives a rate of interest", "Repo never requires collateral, while stock lending always does", "Stock lending is illegal in most markets, while repo is universally permitted"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Stock lending and repo are similar secured transactions, but the key difference is that a stock lender charges a fee to the borrower, whereas a repo counterparty pays (or receives) a rate of interest on the cash involved."}, {"question": "In a repurchase agreement (repo), what does the cash lender receive as protection?", "options": ["A personal guarantee from the cash borrower's directors", "Securities as collateral, which it can utilise if the cash borrower defaults", "Nothing - repos are unsecured by design", "An insurance policy issued by the central bank"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "In a repo, the cash lender receives securities as collateral throughout the loan period and is free to utilise (sell) these securities if the cash borrower fails to repay the cash plus interest."}], "flashcards": [{"front": "What is the primary responsibility of a custodian?", "back": "To keep clients' assets safe through robust safekeeping and proper segregation from the custodian's own assets, releasing assets only against authorised instructions."}, {"front": "Name four services provided by custodians beyond safekeeping.", "back": "Any four of: inventory maintenance, trade verification, settlement arrangement, registration/legal title transfer, market information, income collection, cash flow management, corporate action/voting administration, tax reclaims, regulatory reporting."}, {"front": "What legislation popularised the term 'global custody'?", "back": "The US Employee Retirement Income Security Act (ERISA), 1974, which required pension fund assets to be held by an independent (initially only US) bank custodian."}, {"front": "Name four of the largest global custodian banks as of 2025.", "back": "JPMorgan Chase, Citi, BNY Mellon and State Street."}, {"front": "What is a sub-custodian?", "back": "A local agent appointed by a global custodian to provide settlement and custody services for assets held in a foreign market - the 'eyes and ears' of the global custodian locally."}, {"front": "What is a reciprocal custody arrangement?", "back": "Global custodian A appoints local provider B as its sub-custodian in market B; in return, A offers sub-custody in its own home market to funds using B as their global custodian."}, {"front": "List two strengths of a local (single-market) custodian.", "back": "Deep local market/language/culture expertise and strong relationships with local regulators/politicians (also: reciprocal business opportunities, acts as eyes and ears)."}, {"front": "List two weaknesses of a local custodian versus a regional custodian.", "back": "Weaker credit rating, and inability to leverage technology/service developments across multiple markets (also: fewer price discounts)."}, {"front": "List two strengths of a regional custodian.", "back": "Higher credit rating than a single-market provider, and ability to leverage technology/best practice and offer price discounts across multiple markets (also: standardised reporting, regulatory leverage)."}, {"front": "What proves ownership of registered shares?", "back": "The entry on the company's share register, not the physical share certificate (which is only evidence)."}, {"front": "What is a bearer share?", "back": "An unregistered share where the person physically holding the certificate is treated as the owner, like cash, with no register entry."}, {"front": "Why are bearer shares being phased out?", "back": "OECD pressure to strengthen beneficial ownership transparency; the UK has banned them, others (eg, Luxembourg) require immobilisation at authorised depositories."}, {"front": "What is a nominee?", "back": "A party (stockbroker, custodian or specially created nominee company) that takes registered/legal title to shares on behalf of a beneficial owner, removing the administrative burden of registered title from the investor."}, {"front": "What document does a nominee sign confirming its role?", "back": "A declaration of trust, confirming it holds the shares on behalf of, and will return or transfer them at the request of, the beneficial owner."}, {"front": "Who is the legal owner in a nominee arrangement, and what does that mean for voting?", "back": "The nominee is the legal owner; it is recognised by the company for voting purposes, while the beneficial owner does not have the automatic right to attend/vote at meetings."}, {"front": "What benefits does the beneficial owner retain when shares are held via a nominee?", "back": "Dividends and capital growth (the economic benefits of ownership), but not the automatic right to vote/attend meetings."}, {"front": "Name the three types of nominee company.", "back": "Pooled (omnibus/commingled), designated (numbered accounts), and sole nominee."}, {"front": "What is a pooled/omnibus nominee account?", "back": "Multiple clients grouped together within a single nominee registration; the company sees only the single nominee name, and only that nominee can vote (no separate client voting entitlement)."}, {"front": "What is a designated nominee account?", "back": "A nominee account where the nominee name includes a unique identifier per client, eg, XYZ Nominees Account 1, Account 2, allowing individual client identification under a common nominee name."}, {"front": "What is a sole nominee?", "back": "A single nominee name used exclusively for one specific client, eg, ABC Pension Fund Nominees Ltd."}, {"front": "Why might a client prefer a sole/designated nominee over a pooled account?", "back": "To have dividends mandated to a specific bank account (not practicable via omnibus), to aid auditing/control, or to avoid duplicate identical trades causing confusion."}, {"front": "What is a corporate nominee?", "back": "A nominee facility provided by the issuing company itself for its smaller shareholders - a halfway house between pooled and designated structures offered by stockbrokers."}, {"front": "How does a corporate nominee combine features of pooled and designated nominees?", "back": "Single register entry overall (like pooled), but the company/registrar retains awareness of individual holdings beneath it (like designated), enabling separate dividends, voting rights and perks."}, {"front": "Define stock lending.", "back": "The temporary transfer of securities by a lender to a borrower, with the borrower agreeing to return equivalent (fungible) securities to the lender at a pre-agreed time."}, {"front": "What are the two main motivations for stock lending?", "back": "Securities-driven transactions (borrower needs specific securities) and cash-driven transactions (lender seeks to enhance portfolio income via fees)."}, {"front": "What was the original historical driver of the securities lending business?", "back": "Covering settlement failure - borrowing stock for a fee to meet a delivery commitment, then returning equivalent shares once the original stock is obtained."}, {"front": "What forms of collateral can a stock lending borrower provide?", "back": "Cash, government securities, or a letter of credit, of value equal to or greater than the loaned securities."}, {"front": "How is the stock lending fee expressed when collateral is cash?", "back": "As a rebate - the lender earns all interest on the cash collateral and rebates an agreed rate of interest back to the borrower."}, {"front": "What is the principal reason securities are borrowed?", "back": "To cover a short sale - a seller who does not hold the securities sold must borrow them to make delivery, then return equivalent securities later."}, {"front": "What are the UK and European private disclosure thresholds for net short positions?", "back": "UK: 0.2% of issued share capital. Europe: 0.1% of issued share capital. Both require public disclosure at 0.5%."}, {"front": "What is a 'haircut' in securities lending collateral?", "back": "The amount by which collateral value exceeds the market value of the borrowed securities, protecting the lender against adverse price movements in the collateral."}, {"front": "List four risk mitigation techniques used by securities lenders.", "back": "Credit evaluation of the borrower, exposure limits per borrower, collateralisation, daily mark-to-market with margin calls, and master legal agreements (eg, GMSLA)."}, {"front": "What is an SBLI?", "back": "A stock borrowing and lending intermediary that separates long-term underlying owners (eg, pension funds) from borrowers (eg, hedge funds), pooling assets and splitting revenue with owners."}, {"front": "Why do custodian banks commonly act as SBLIs?", "back": "Custody is often low-margin; acting as an SBLI adds a revenue stream, leveraging existing banking relationships, technology, global coverage, and cash collateral management capability."}, {"front": "What must an SBLI ensure when pooling client assets for lending?", "back": "That only assets of clients who have consented to securities lending are lent out, using separate accounts or systems to distinguish consenting from non-consenting clients."}, {"front": "For what purposes is securities borrowing generally permitted by law?", "back": "To facilitate settlement of a trade, facilitate delivery of a short sale, finance the security, or facilitate a loan to another borrower motivated by one of these purposes."}, {"front": "What happens to legal title and voting rights when a security is loaned?", "back": "They transfer to the borrower for the duration of the loan; the borrower receives dividends/coupons and can vote, but must pass economic value back to the lender."}, {"front": "What is a manufactured dividend?", "back": "A payment made by the borrower to the lender replicating the dividend or coupon income the lender would have received had the securities not been on loan."}, {"front": "What is the GMSLA?", "back": "The Global Master Securities Lending Agreement - the market-standard legal agreement covering the obligations of borrowers and lenders in securities lending transactions."}, {"front": "What is the key difference between stock lending and repo?", "back": "A stock lender charges a fee to the borrower; a repo counterparty pays or receives a rate of interest on the cash involved."}]}, {"id": "sec-m10", "number": 10, "title": "Financial Statements & Ratio Analysis", "examWeight": "Core module — heavily calculation-intensive", "summaryHtml": "\n<p>This is the most calculation-heavy module in the Securities syllabus and demands fluency with formulas, not just definitions. The three core financial statements are: the <strong>statement of financial position</strong> (SOFP/balance sheet) — a snapshot at a point in time where assets = equity + liabilities; the <strong>statement of profit or loss</strong> (income statement) — a summary of income and expenses over a period, ending in earnings per share; and the <strong>statement of cash flows</strong> — cash movements split into operating, investing and financing activities (per IAS 7). Accounting standards are set by the IASB (IFRSs, and legacy IASs) and the ISSB (sustainability/ESG standards), together forming GAAP. Where a parent controls a subsidiary, <strong>group (consolidated) accounts</strong> are required, combining 100% of subsidiary assets/liabilities, recognising <strong>goodwill</strong> (cost of investment minus fair value of net assets acquired) and <strong>non-controlling interests</strong> (the subsidiary equity not owned by the parent).</p>\n<p>On the SOFP, non-current assets (tangible/PPE, intangible, investments) are depreciated/amortised using methods such as straight line: (cost − disposal value) / useful economic life. Equity = share capital + capital reserves (share premium, revaluation reserve) + revenue reserves (retained earnings) ± non-controlling interests. Liabilities split into current (due &lt;12 months) and non-current. <strong>Capital expenditure</strong> buys non-current assets (SOFP); <strong>revenue expenditure</strong> hits the income statement immediately. The cash flow statement reconciles profit to cash: depreciation/amortisation are added back (non-cash), and working-capital movements (receivables, inventory, payables) adjust operating cash flow. <strong>Free cash flow</strong> = net income + D&amp;A − capital expenditure, with enterprise cash flow (before financing) distinct from equity cash flow (after financing costs, before dividends).</p>\n<p>Ratio analysis is tested heavily. Key formulas (all must be memorised exactly):</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Category</th><th>Ratio</th><th>Formula</th></tr>\n<tr><td rowspan=\"5\">Profitability/Return</td><td>ROCE</td><td>Operating profit / Capital employed (equity + non-current liabilities)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ROA</td><td>Operating profit / Total net assets (non-current + current assets − current liabilities)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ROE</td><td>Net income / Shareholders' equity</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gross/Operating/Net profit margin</td><td>Gross profit, Operating profit, or PBT ÷ Revenue</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Asset turnover</td><td>Revenue / Total net assets</td></tr>\n<tr><td rowspan=\"3\">Gearing</td><td>Debt to equity</td><td>Debt / Equity</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Net debt to equity</td><td>(Debt − cash &amp; short-term investments) / Equity</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Interest cover</td><td>Operating profit / Interest costs</td></tr>\n<tr><td rowspan=\"2\">Liquidity</td><td>Current ratio</td><td>Current assets / Current liabilities</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Quick (acid test) ratio</td><td>(Current assets − inventory) / Current liabilities</td></tr>\n<tr><td rowspan=\"8\">Investors'</td><td>EPS</td><td>(Net profit − preference dividends) / Weighted avg ordinary shares</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Diluted EPS</td><td>Adjusted earnings / (shares + dilutive shares from converts/options)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>P/E (historic/prospective)</td><td>Share price / Historic or forecast EPS</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gross dividend yield</td><td>(Dividend per share / Share price) × 100</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gross dividend cover</td><td>EPS / Dividend per share</td></tr>\n<tr><td>EV</td><td>Market cap + debt + NCI + preference shares − cash</td></tr>\n<tr><td>EV/EBIT</td><td>EV / Operating profit (PBIT)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>EV/EBITDA</td><td>EV / (Operating profit + D&amp;A)</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Ratio analysis reveals trends and is useful for comparison across periods and peer companies, but is limited by its reliance on historic data, differing accounting policies/standards between jurisdictions, and the judgement involved in the underlying figures. Share prices reflect expectations, so published financial statements only move prices when they diverge from what the market already anticipated. Expect the exam to give you a mini set of accounts and require you to calculate several of the above ratios in sequence — practise the mechanics until they are automatic.</p>\n", "sections": [{"heading": "10.1 Purpose of Financial Statements, Regulation and Group Accounts", "html": "\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> financial statements (the \"accounts\") record, measure and report a company's economic activities to stakeholders (owners, creditors, investors, employees, analysts, exchanges, governments) in a standardised, comparable format, over a typical 12-month accounting period. Directors must prepare and publish an annual report and accounts, independently verified by an <strong>audit</strong>. An <strong>unqualified</strong> audit report confirms the accounts give a true and fair view and comply with law/regulation; a <strong>qualified</strong> report flags a departure and states the reason.</p>\n<p>The three core statements are the statement of financial position, the statement of profit or loss (income statement), and the statement of cash flows, supplemented by a statement of comprehensive income, a statement of changes in equity, and explanatory notes.</p>\n<p><strong>Five elements of financial statements:</strong></p>\n<table><tr><th>Element</th><th>Definition</th><th>Statement</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Assets</td><td>Items owned/controlled expected to yield future economic benefit</td><td>SOFP</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Liabilities</td><td>Money/obligations owed to others</td><td>SOFP</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Equity</td><td>Owners' invested capital plus retained (undistributed) earnings</td><td>SOFP</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Income (revenue)</td><td>Increase in assets/decrease in liabilities from trading</td><td>Income statement</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Expenses</td><td>Costs of doing business (cash and non-cash, e.g. depreciation)</td><td>Income statement</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Regulation:</strong> financial statement form/content is governed by law and accounting standards, collectively \"GAAP\". The <strong>IFRS Foundation</strong> oversees two standard-setting boards: the <strong>IASB</strong> (International Accounting Standards Board), which issues IFRSs (and retains legacy IASs from its predecessor body), and the <strong>ISSB</strong> (International Sustainability Standards Board), which sets climate/ESG disclosure standards. Many countries mandate IFRS for listed companies; the US still uses its own US GAAP, so cross-border profit comparisons require adjustment.</p>\n<p><strong>Group vs company accounts:</strong> where an investing company (the <strong>parent</strong>) controls another (the <strong>subsidiary</strong>), the parent must prepare <strong>group (consolidated) accounts</strong> in addition to its own individual company accounts, presenting parent + subsidiaries as a single economic entity (100% of each subsidiary's assets, liabilities, revenues, expenses and cash flows are added in, not just the parent's proportionate share). Two special issues arise:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Goodwill</strong> — an intangible asset recognised when the cost of acquiring a subsidiary exceeds the fair value of its net assets (assets less liabilities); the excess is recorded as goodwill, an asset on the consolidated SOFP. Goodwill is not amortised but is tested for impairment.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-controlling interests (NCI)</strong> (formerly \"minority interests\") — arise where the parent owns less than 100% of a subsidiary. E.g. a 75%-owned subsidiary has a 25% NCI; a 51%-owned subsidiary has a 49% NCI. Since 100% of the subsidiary's net assets/income are consolidated, the NCI's share must be separately shown in group equity and group income statement.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Extra practice:</strong> Parent P acquires 80% of Subsidiary S for £900,000. S's identifiable net assets at acquisition have a fair value of £950,000. Cost of investment (£900,000) is less than 100% of fair value (£950,000), but goodwill is calculated on the amount attributable to the controlling interest's cost versus its share of fair value: 80% × £950,000 = £760,000; since £900,000 (cost) > £760,000 (share of FV), goodwill = £900,000 − £760,000 = £140,000. NCI at acquisition = 20% × £950,000 = £190,000.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "10.2 Statement of Financial Position — Purpose, Format and Assets", "html": "\n<p>The <strong>statement of financial position</strong> (SOFP/balance sheet) is a snapshot at a specific date (usually year-end), split into assets, equity and liabilities, with assets = equity + liabilities always balancing. Assets and liabilities are each split into <strong>current</strong> (due/realisable within 12 months) and <strong>non-current</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example SOFP (€000s)</strong> — used throughout this module for ratio calculations:</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Non-current assets</th><th>€</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Property, plant and equipment</td><td>8,900</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Intangible assets</td><td>2,100</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Investments</td><td>300</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Total non-current assets</strong></td><td><strong>11,300</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><th>Current assets</th><th></th></tr>\n<tr><td>Inventories</td><td>3,600</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Trade and other receivables</td><td>2,600</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Prepayments</td><td>120</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cash</td><td>860</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Total current assets</strong></td><td><strong>7,180</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Total assets</strong></td><td><strong>18,480</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><th>Equity</th><th></th></tr>\n<tr><td>Share capital — 10m 50c ordinary shares</td><td>5,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Share capital — preference shares</td><td>100</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Share premium account</td><td>120</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Revaluation reserve</td><td>180</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Retained earnings</td><td>6,880</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Total equity</strong></td><td><strong>12,280</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><th>Non-current liabilities</th><th></th></tr>\n<tr><td>Bank loans</td><td>2,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Provisions</td><td>2,000</td></tr>\n<tr><th>Current liabilities</th><th></th></tr>\n<tr><td>Trade and other payables</td><td>2,200</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Total liabilities</strong></td><td><strong>6,200</strong></td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Check: total equity (12,280) + total liabilities (6,200) = 18,480 = total assets. Balances correctly.</p>\n<p><strong>Non-current assets</strong> are split into: <em>tangible</em> (property, plant and equipment — PPE — recorded at cost/book value, then depreciated, except land which is normally revalued rather than depreciated); <em>intangible</em> (no physical substance — software, patents, trademarks, capitalised development costs, purchased goodwill; goodwill is not amortised); and <em>non-current investments</em> (equity/debt holdings not maturing/sold within a year, held at cost less impairment).</p>\n<p><strong>Current assets</strong> are those expected to convert to cash or be resold within 12 months: inventories (stock), trade receivables, short-term investments, cash, and prepayments (an expense paid in advance, e.g. rent paid ahead of the period it relates to). Listed in ascending order of liquidity and valued at the lower of cost or net realisable value (NRV).</p>\n<p><strong>Prepayment example:</strong> Company XYZ pays €25,000 on 31 December for the next three months' rent (to end March). This is not an expense of the current year — it is shown as a prepayment within current assets.</p>\n<p><strong>Extra practice:</strong> A company has PPE €12,000, intangibles €1,500, investments €400, inventories €2,900, receivables €3,100, prepayments €80, cash €620. Total non-current assets = 12,000+1,500+400 = €13,900. Total current assets = 2,900+3,100+80+620 = €6,700. Total assets = €20,600.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "10.3 Depreciation, Amortisation, Equity and Liabilities", "html": "\n<p><strong>Depreciation</strong> applies to tangible non-current assets (not freehold land, not non-current investments) to spread their cost over their useful economic life, reflecting wear/use. <strong>Amortisation</strong> is the equivalent treatment for intangible assets (e.g. licences, patents — but not purchased goodwill, which is not amortised, only tested for impairment). Both are non-cash expenses charged to the income statement.</p>\n<p><strong>Straight-line depreciation formula:</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><strong>Straight-line depreciation = (Cost − Disposal value) / Useful economic life (years)</strong></p>\n<p>Statement of financial position value = Net book value (NBV) = Cost − accumulated depreciation to date. NBV does not necessarily equal market value.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> A machine costs €25,000, useful life 6 years, estimated disposal value €1,000.<br>\nAnnual depreciation = (25,000 − 1,000) / 6 = <strong>€4,000 p.a.</strong></p>\n<table><tr><th>Year</th><th>Opening NBV</th><th>Depreciation</th><th>Closing NBV</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>25,000</td><td>4,000</td><td>21,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2</td><td>21,000</td><td>4,000</td><td>17,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3</td><td>17,000</td><td>4,000</td><td>13,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4</td><td>13,000</td><td>4,000</td><td>9,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5</td><td>9,000</td><td>4,000</td><td>5,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td>6</td><td>5,000</td><td>4,000</td><td>1,000 (= disposal value)</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Extra practice:</strong> A delivery van costs €18,000, useful life 4 years, disposal value €2,000. Annual depreciation = (18,000 − 2,000) / 4 = <strong>€4,000 p.a.</strong> NBV after year 2 = 18,000 − (2 × 4,000) = <strong>€10,000</strong>.</p>\n<p>Where an asset (e.g. land) is revalued upward instead of depreciated, the increase is credited to a <strong>revaluation reserve</strong> within equity, keeping the SOFP balanced.</p>\n<p><strong>Equity</strong> = Share capital + Capital reserves + Revenue reserves (± NCI in group accounts):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Share capital</strong> — nominal value of called-up ordinary and preference shares.</li>\n<li><strong>Capital reserves</strong> — revaluation reserve and share premium account (premium received on shares issued above nominal value; \"additional paid-in capital\" in some jurisdictions). Not distributable as dividends but may fund a bonus issue.</li>\n<li><strong>Revenue reserves</strong> — chiefly retained earnings: accumulated distributable profits not paid out as dividends. Not the same as cash held, or the current period's income statement profit.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-controlling interests</strong> — equity attributable to minority shareholders of a subsidiary in group accounts.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Liabilities</strong> split into: <strong>non-current liabilities</strong> — borrowings not repayable within 12 months (bonds, long-term bank loans, long-term leases); and <strong>current liabilities</strong> — trade payables, bank overdraft, tax payable and other amounts due within 12 months. <strong>Provisions</strong> are liabilities of uncertain timing/amount (e.g. restructuring costs) — a prudent estimate is recognised and classified current or non-current based on expected payment date, then adjusted as the true cost becomes known.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "10.4 Statement of Profit or Loss (Income Statement)", "html": "\n<p>The income statement (profit and loss statement; under IFRS \"statement of profit or loss and other comprehensive income\") summarises revenue earned and expenses incurred over the accounting period, ending in EPS (\"the bottom line\").</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example income statement (€000s, current year / prior year):</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Current</th><th>Prior</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Revenue</td><td>9,500</td><td>8,750</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cost of sales</td><td>(7,000)</td><td>(6,600)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Gross profit</strong></td><td><strong>2,500</strong></td><td><strong>2,150</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Distribution costs</td><td>(110)</td><td>(90)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Administrative expenses</td><td>(30)</td><td>(20)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Loss on disposal of plant</td><td>(260)</td><td>–</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Operating profit (PBIT/EBIT)</strong></td><td><strong>2,100</strong></td><td><strong>2,040</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Financial costs</td><td>(230)</td><td>(250)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Financial income</td><td>120</td><td>112</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Profit before taxation</strong></td><td><strong>1,990</strong></td><td><strong>1,902</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Taxation</td><td>(555)</td><td>(548)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Net income</strong></td><td><strong>1,435</strong></td><td><strong>1,354</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>EPS</td><td>16.1c</td><td>15.9c</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Key lines:</strong> <em>Revenue</em> (turnover) — total sales regardless of cash receipt. <em>Cost of sales/COGS</em> — direct costs of producing goods sold (materials, direct labour); excludes distribution/admin/finance costs. <em>Gross profit</em> = Revenue − COGS. <em>Operating profit</em> (= PBIT = EBIT) = Gross profit − other operating expenses (distribution, admin, including depreciation/amortisation, plus/minus disposal gains/losses). Adding back D&amp;A to operating profit gives <strong>EBITDA</strong>, useful for assessing debt capacity. <em>Finance costs</em> — interest on borrowings; <em>Finance income</em> — interest on surplus cash. <em>Profit before tax (PBT)</em>. <em>Taxation</em> — corporation tax charge. <strong>Net income</strong> (net profit/profit for the period) — theoretically fully distributable to shareholders as dividends.</p>\n<p><strong>Extra practice:</strong> Revenue €12,000, cost of sales €8,400, distribution costs €300, admin expenses €500, finance costs €200, finance income €50, tax €650. Gross profit = 12,000 − 8,400 = €3,600. Operating profit = 3,600 − 300 − 500 = €2,800. PBT = 2,800 − 200 + 50 = €2,650. Net income = 2,650 − 650 = €2,000.</p>\n<p><strong>Dividends</strong>: preference dividends are paid first, then ordinary dividends (interim + final, or quarterly). Movements are shown in the <strong>statement of changes in equity</strong>, which reconciles opening to closing equity (share issues, revaluation gains, net income for the year, dividends paid).</p>\n"}, {"heading": "10.5 Capital versus Revenue Expenditure", "html": "\n<p><strong>Capital expenditure</strong> is money spent to acquire non-current assets (e.g. PPE); it appears on the statement of financial position and is depreciated/amortised over time rather than expensed immediately.</p>\n<p><strong>Revenue expenditure</strong> is money spent that immediately impacts the income statement — wages, rent, professional/audit fees and similar operating costs.</p>\n<table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Capital expenditure</th><th>Revenue expenditure</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Statement impact</td><td>SOFP (asset)</td><td>Income statement (expense)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Timing of charge</td><td>Spread via depreciation/amortisation over useful life</td><td>Charged in period incurred</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Examples</td><td>Buying machinery, buildings, vehicles</td><td>Wages, rent, insurance, audit fees, repairs</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Extra practice:</strong> A company spends €50,000 buying a new machine and €8,000 on staff wages and €3,000 on repairing an existing machine (routine maintenance). The €50,000 is capital expenditure (asset, depreciated over its life); the €8,000 wages and €3,000 routine repair are revenue expenditure (expensed in full this year).</p>\n<p><strong>Annual report and accounts</strong> — beyond the three core statements, a listed company's annual report includes: the <em>strategic report</em> (directors' strategy and outlook), a <em>stakeholder/section 172 report</em>, a <em>financial/CFO review</em> (performance metrics, key risks — this is where most ratio disclosure appears), a <em>corporate governance report</em> (board committees, including the audit committee, which oversees external auditor independence/appointment), a <em>directors' remuneration report</em> (aiming for goal congruence between directors and shareholders), an <em>ESG/sustainability section</em>, the <em>independent auditor's report</em> (unqualified/qualified opinion), the <em>accounts and notes</em>, and <em>shareholder information</em> (share price and dividend history).</p>\n"}, {"heading": "10.6 Statement of Cash Flows", "html": "\n<p>The statement of cash flows (cash flow statement), required under <strong>IAS 7</strong>, summarises cash receipts and payments over the year, split into three headings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Operating activities</strong> — cash generated from trading; interest paid may be classified as operating or financing under international standards.</li>\n<li><strong>Investing activities</strong> — dividends/interest received, cash paid to acquire non-current assets, proceeds from disposals/sale of investments.</li>\n<li><strong>Financing activities</strong> — dividends paid, new borrowing/repayments, proceeds from share issues (less buybacks); interest paid may also appear here.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Worked example (€000s):</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tr><td>Cash receipts from customers</td><td>4,528</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cash paid to suppliers and employees</td><td>(2,441)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Cash generated from operations</strong></td><td><strong>2,527</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tax paid</td><td>–</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Interest paid</td><td>(150)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Interest received</td><td>80</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Dividends received</td><td>40</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Purchase of PPE</td><td>(1,890)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Proceeds on sale of investments</td><td>120</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Dividends paid</td><td>(435)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Repayments of borrowings</td><td>(200)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Proceeds on issue of shares</td><td>650</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Net increase in cash and cash equivalents</strong></td><td><strong>2,829</strong>*</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cash at beginning of year</td><td>425</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cash at end of year</td><td>3,254</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Many short-term investments (e.g. T-bills) count as cash equivalents. The total net cash flow explains the change in the cash balance between two SOFP dates.</p>\n<p><strong>Extra practice:</strong> Operating cash flow €1,800; investing cash outflow €(900); financing cash inflow €300. Net change in cash = 1,800 − 900 + 300 = <strong>€1,200</strong> increase. If opening cash was €500, closing cash = €1,700.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "10.7 Profit versus Cash and Free Cash Flow", "html": "\n<p><strong>Profit</strong> (income statement) measures revenues earned less expenses incurred in a period, regardless of cash timing (credit sales, depreciation vs actual cash outlay). <strong>Cash</strong> (cash flow statement) is generated when cash received exceeds cash paid. A profitable company can still run out of cash — cash is the \"lifeblood\"; failure to pay a due liability can force closure, even while reporting profit.</p>\n<p><strong>Reconciling net income to operating cash flow</strong> (indirect method) — starting point net income, then adjust for non-cash items and working-capital movements:</p>\n<table><tr><th>Item</th><th>€</th><th>Effect on cash</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Net income after tax</td><td>240,000</td><td>Starting point</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Depreciation and amortisation</td><td>+35,000</td><td>Non-cash expense, add back</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Decrease in accounts receivable</td><td>+17,000</td><td>More cash collected — addition</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Decrease in other current assets</td><td>+19,000</td><td>Addition</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Increase in accounts payable</td><td>+26,000</td><td>Cash retained (bills unpaid) — addition</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Total additions to cash from operations</td><td>97,000</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Increase in inventory</td><td>(33,000)</td><td>Cash spent on stock — subtraction</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Decrease in accrued expenses</td><td>(19,000)</td><td>Subtraction</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Decrease in other current liabilities</td><td>(23,000)</td><td>Subtraction</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Total subtractions from cash from operations</td><td>(75,000)</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Total operating cash flow</strong></td><td><strong>262,000</strong></td><td>= 240,000 + 97,000 − 75,000</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Rule of thumb: a <em>decrease</em> in a current asset or an <em>increase</em> in a current liability is an addition to cash; an <em>increase</em> in a current asset or a <em>decrease</em> in a current liability is a subtraction from cash.</p>\n<p><strong>Free cash flow (income-statement approach):</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><strong>Free cash flow = Net income + Depreciation &amp; amortisation − Capital expenditure</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Enterprise vs equity cash flow example (€):</strong> Total operating cash flow 262,000; additions from investments 162,000; subtractions from investments (314,000) → <strong>Total enterprise cash flow = 262,000 + 162,000 − 314,000 = 110,000</strong> (free cash flow before any payments to finance providers). Additions from financing 50,000, no subtractions → <strong>Total equity cash flow = 110,000 + 50,000 = 160,000</strong> (after debt financing, before dividends). Dividends paid (100,000) → <strong>Total free cash flow = 160,000 − 100,000 = 60,000</strong>. Cash at start 450,000; cash at end 510,000 (change = 60,000, consistent).</p>\n<p><strong>Extra practice:</strong> Net income €500,000, D&amp;A €60,000, capital expenditure €150,000. Free cash flow (income-statement method) = 500,000 + 60,000 − 150,000 = <strong>€410,000</strong>.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "10.8 Return and Profitability Ratios", "html": "\n<p>Profitability ratios express profit as a percentage of revenue or of capital invested. Using the worked SOFP (section 10.2: equity €12,280, non-current liabilities €4,000 [bank loans 2,000 + provisions 2,000], total net assets €16,280) and income statement (section 10.4: revenue €9,500, operating profit €2,100, net income €1,435):</p>\n<table><tr><th>Ratio</th><th>Formula</th><th>Calculation</th><th>Result</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Capital employed</td><td>Equity + Non-current liabilities</td><td>12,280 + 4,000</td><td>€16,280</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ROCE</td><td>Operating profit / Capital employed</td><td>2,100 / 16,280</td><td><strong>12.9%</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Total net assets</td><td>Non-current assets + Current assets − Current liabilities</td><td>11,300 + 7,180 − 2,200</td><td>€16,280</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ROA</td><td>Operating profit / Total net assets</td><td>2,100 / 16,280</td><td><strong>12.9%</strong> (identical to ROCE)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ROE</td><td>Net income / Shareholders' equity</td><td>1,435 / 12,280</td><td><strong>11.7%</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gross profit margin</td><td>Gross profit / Revenue</td><td>2,500 / 9,500</td><td><strong>26.3%</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Operating profit margin</td><td>Operating profit / Revenue</td><td>2,100 / 9,500</td><td><strong>22.1%</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Net profit margin (PBT basis)</td><td>Profit before tax / Revenue</td><td>1,990 / 9,500</td><td><strong>20.9%</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Asset turnover</td><td>Revenue / Total net assets</td><td>9,500 / 16,280</td><td><strong>0.58 times</strong></td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Key relationship:</strong> ROCE (= ROA) = Asset turnover × Operating profit margin = 0.58 × 22.1% ≈ 12.9%. This decomposition (DuPont-style) shows ROCE improves either by generating more revenue per € of assets, or by earning a higher margin on each € of revenue.</p>\n<p><em>Note:</em> ROCE and ROA are described in the CISI syllabus as giving \"exactly the same result\" because capital employed (equity + non-current liabilities) equals total net assets (non-current + current assets − current liabilities) by definition of the balance sheet.</p>\n<p><strong>Extra practice:</strong> A company has operating profit €3,600,000, equity €18,000,000, non-current liabilities €6,000,000, revenue €40,000,000, net income €2,200,000.<br>\nCapital employed = 18,000,000 + 6,000,000 = €24,000,000.<br>\nROCE = 3,600,000 / 24,000,000 = <strong>15.0%</strong>.<br>\nROE = 2,200,000 / 18,000,000 = <strong>12.2%</strong>.<br>\nOperating profit margin = 3,600,000 / 40,000,000 = <strong>9.0%</strong>.<br>\nAsset turnover = 40,000,000 / 24,000,000 = <strong>1.67 times</strong> (check: 1.67 × 9.0% ≈ 15.0% ROCE ✓).</p>\n"}, {"heading": "10.9 Financial Gearing Ratios", "html": "\n<p>Financial gearing (financial leverage) measures the risk arising from a company's mix of debt versus equity funding. Higher debt means fixed interest obligations must be paid regardless of profitability and the principal must eventually be repaid, whereas dividends are discretionary and share capital need not be repaid. Excessive gearing raises the risk of default; but gearing can benefit equity holders in good years since fixed interest leaves any surplus return for shareholders.</p>\n<table><tr><th>Ratio</th><th>Formula</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Debt to equity</td><td>Debt / Equity</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Net debt to equity</td><td>(Debt − Cash and short-term investments) / Equity</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Interest cover</td><td>Operating profit / Interest costs</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>\"Debt\" = interest-bearing non-current liabilities (from the SOFP). Ratios can be expressed as a decimal or a percentage.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked examples using section 10.2/10.4 figures</strong> (debt = bank loans €2,000; equity €12,280; cash €860; operating profit €2,100; interest costs €230):</p>\n<table><tr><th>Ratio</th><th>Calculation</th><th>Result</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Debt to equity</td><td>2,000 / 12,280</td><td><strong>0.163 (16.3%)</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Net debt to equity</td><td>(2,000 − 860) / 12,280</td><td><strong>0.093 (9.3%)</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Interest cover</td><td>2,100 / 230</td><td><strong>9.1 times</strong></td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>A net debt/equity ratio well below the gross debt/equity ratio signals the company holds significant cash/short-term investments that could be used to pay down debt if needed. A falling interest cover ratio over time is a warning sign of rising credit risk.</p>\n<p><strong>Extra practice:</strong> Debt (non-current interest-bearing borrowings) €5,000,000; equity €10,000,000; cash and short-term investments €1,200,000; operating profit €2,400,000; interest costs €400,000.<br>\nDebt to equity = 5,000,000 / 10,000,000 = <strong>0.50 (50%)</strong>.<br>\nNet debt to equity = (5,000,000 − 1,200,000) / 10,000,000 = <strong>0.38 (38%)</strong>.<br>\nInterest cover = 2,400,000 / 400,000 = <strong>6.0 times</strong>.</p>\n"}, {"heading": "10.10 Liquidity Ratios", "html": "\n<p>Liquidity ratios assess a company's ability to meet its short-term (current) liabilities as they fall due, using SOFP data.</p>\n<table><tr><th>Ratio</th><th>Formula</th><th>Interpretation</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Current ratio</td><td>Current assets / Current liabilities</td><td>Higher = more comfortably able to meet near-term obligations</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Quick ratio (acid test)</td><td>(Current assets − Inventory) / Current liabilities</td><td>&gt;1 generally indicates sufficient liquid assets without relying on inventory sale; &lt;1 may signal a need for new finance</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Worked example</strong> (current assets €7,180,000; inventory €3,600,000; current liabilities €2,200,000):</p>\n<table><tr><th>Ratio</th><th>Calculation</th><th>Result</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Current ratio</td><td>7,180,000 / 2,200,000</td><td><strong>3.26</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Quick ratio</td><td>(7,180,000 − 3,600,000) / 2,200,000</td><td><strong>1.63</strong></td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>The quick ratio strips out inventory because it can be slow to sell or hard to value at short notice, giving a tighter test of immediate liquidity than the current ratio.</p>\n<p><strong>Extra practice:</strong> Current assets €4,500,000 (of which inventory €1,800,000); current liabilities €3,000,000.<br>\nCurrent ratio = 4,500,000 / 3,000,000 = <strong>1.5</strong>.<br>\nQuick ratio = (4,500,000 − 1,800,000) / 3,000,000 = <strong>0.9</strong> (below 1 — may indicate a need for additional short-term finance if inventory cannot be converted to cash quickly).</p>\n"}, {"heading": "10.11 Investors' Ratios", "html": "\n<p>Investors' ratios assess how expensive shares are (capital growth potential) and how much/how sustainably they pay dividends (income potential).</p>\n<p><strong>10.11.1 Earnings per Share (EPS)</strong> — defined under <strong>IAS 33</strong>:</p>\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><strong>EPS = (Net profit attributable to ordinary shareholders − Preference dividends) / Weighted average number of ordinary shares in issue</strong></p>\n<p>For group accounts, earnings are also stated after deducting non-controlling interests.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> XYZ has net income €898,000 and 5,000,000 ordinary shares outstanding.<br>\nEPS = 898,000 / 5,000,000 = <strong>18c per share</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>10.11.2 Diluted EPS</strong> — warns shareholders of the potential EPS impact if convertible instruments (convertible bonds/preference shares, options, warrants) are converted/exercised, assuming the worst case (maximum dilution). Required disclosure under IAS 33.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example (continuing XYZ):</strong> €250,000 of 10% convertible loan stock is in issue; conversion terms are 115 ordinary shares per €100 nominal. Corporation tax rate 30%. Basic earnings €898,000; basic shares 5,000,000.<br>\nInterest saved on conversion = 250,000 × 10% = €25,000.<br>\nExtra tax payable on that saved interest = 25,000 × 30% = €7,500.<br>\nDiluted earnings = 898,000 + 25,000 − 7,500 = <strong>€915,500</strong>.<br>\nNew shares issuable = (250,000 / 100) × 115 = <strong>287,500</strong>.<br>\nDiluted shares = 5,000,000 + 287,500 = <strong>5,287,500</strong>.<br>\nDiluted EPS = 915,500 / 5,287,500 = <strong>17.3c</strong> (lower than the basic 18c, as expected).</p>\n<p><strong>10.11.3 Price Earnings (P/E) Ratio:</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><strong>P/E = Share price / EPS</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Trailing (historic) P/E</strong> uses the last 12 months' reported EPS; <strong>forward (prospective) P/E</strong> uses forecast/consensus EPS for the next 12 months. A higher P/E generally implies the market expects higher/more certain earnings growth; loss-making companies have an undefined P/E (N/A or \"nm\").</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> Shares trade at €2.50, EPS 20c → P/E = 2.50 / 0.20 = <strong>12.5</strong> (12.5 years of current earnings priced into the share).</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example (XYZ):</strong> shares trading at €1.60; forecast EPS for next year 17c; historic (pre-dilution) EPS 18c.<br>\nHistoric P/E = 160 / 18 = <strong>8.89</strong>.<br>\nProspective P/E = 160 / 17 = <strong>9.41</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>10.11.4 Enterprise Value (EV) to EBIT</strong> — EV = market capitalisation + debt + NCI + preference shares − cash and cash equivalents (the theoretical cost of buying the whole company, settling all claimants). EBIT = PBIT = Operating profit.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><strong>EV/EBIT = Enterprise value / EBIT</strong></p>\n<p>Unlike P/E (which values only the equity), EV/EBIT values the whole company (debt + equity). A lower EV/EBIT suggests the company is cheaper, all else equal.</p>\n<p><strong>10.11.5 Enterprise Value (EV) to EBITDA</strong> — EBITDA = EBIT + depreciation + amortisation added back (a proxy for operating cash generation, but a non-GAAP measure that can vary company to company and excludes working-capital/capex needs).</p>\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><strong>EV/EBITDA = Enterprise value / EBITDA</strong></p>\n<p><strong>10.11.6 Gross Dividend Yield:</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><strong>Gross dividend yield = (Dividend per share / Share price) × 100</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> A company with 5 million shares distributes €400,000 as dividends. DPS = 400,000 / 5,000,000 = 8c. Shares trade at €1.60.<br>\nGross dividend yield = (0.08 / 1.60) × 100 = <strong>5.0%</strong>. A high yield may signal the market doubts the dividend is sustainable; a low yield signals confidence in future dividend growth.</p>\n<p><strong>10.11.7 Gross Dividend Cover:</strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><strong>Gross dividend cover = EPS / Dividend per share</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> EPS 18c, DPS 8c → Dividend cover = 18 / 8 = <strong>2.25 times</strong> (just over half of profits retained). Cover below 1 = an \"uncovered\" dividend (current profits did not fully fund the payout).</p>\n<p><strong>Extra practice (all ratios, one company):</strong> Net income €3,000,000; preference dividends €0; 10,000,000 ordinary shares; share price €4.50; forecast EPS next year 32c; total dividends paid €1,200,000; EV €55,000,000; EBIT €4,000,000; D&amp;A €500,000.<br>\nEPS = 3,000,000 / 10,000,000 = <strong>30c</strong>.<br>\nHistoric P/E = 450 / 30 = <strong>15.0</strong>; Prospective P/E = 450 / 32 = <strong>14.1</strong>.<br>\nDPS = 1,200,000 / 10,000,000 = 12c; Gross dividend yield = (0.12 / 4.50) × 100 = <strong>2.67%</strong>.<br>\nDividend cover = 30 / 12 = <strong>2.5 times</strong>.<br>\nEV/EBIT = 55,000,000 / 4,000,000 = <strong>13.75</strong>; EV/EBITDA = 55,000,000 / 4,500,000 = <strong>12.2</strong>.</p>\n"}], "mcqs": [{"question": "What are the three principal financial statements that a company must produce each accounting period?", "options": ["Statement of financial position, income statement, statement of cash flows", "Statement of financial position, auditor's report, directors' report", "Income statement, statement of changes in equity, notes to the accounts", "Statement of cash flows, strategic report, remuneration report"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The three core financial statements are the statement of financial position, the statement of profit or loss (income statement), and the statement of cash flows."}, {"question": "What does an 'unqualified' audit report confirm?", "options": ["That the accounts give a true and fair view and comply with the relevant law and regulations", "That the auditor has identified a material misstatement in the accounts", "That the company has made a loss for the year", "That the company's shares are undervalued"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "An unqualified audit opinion confirms the accounts are true and fair and properly prepared under applicable law/regulation. A qualified report is issued if this is not the case."}, {"question": "Which two bodies operate under the IFRS Foundation to set global accounting and sustainability standards?", "options": ["The IASB and the ISSB", "The IASB and the FCA", "The ISSB and the SEC", "The FASB and the IASB"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The International Accounting Standards Board (IASB) sets IFRSs/IASs, and the International Sustainability Standards Board (ISSB) sets ESG/sustainability disclosure standards, both under the IFRS Foundation."}, {"question": "A parent company owns 70% of a subsidiary's shares. What is the non-controlling interest (NCI) percentage?", "options": ["30%", "70%", "50%", "100%"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "NCI represents the portion not owned by the parent: 100% − 70% = 30%."}, {"question": "In group (consolidated) accounts, how are a subsidiary's assets and liabilities treated?", "options": ["100% of the subsidiary's assets and liabilities are added to the parent's, with NCI shown separately", "Only the parent's proportionate percentage share of the subsidiary's assets and liabilities is added", "The subsidiary's assets and liabilities are excluded entirely from group accounts", "Only the subsidiary's cash and investments are consolidated"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Group accounts consolidate 100% of each subsidiary's assets, liabilities, revenues and expenses, then separately disclose the portion of net assets/income belonging to any non-controlling interests."}, {"question": "Goodwill on consolidation arises when:", "options": ["The cost of investment in a subsidiary exceeds the fair value of its identifiable net assets", "A company revalues its freehold land upward", "A company's inventory is written down to net realisable value", "A subsidiary pays a dividend to its parent"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Goodwill = cost of investment − fair value of the subsidiary's net assets (assets less liabilities) at acquisition; it is recognised as an intangible asset in the consolidated SOFP."}, {"question": "On a statement of financial position, which formula must always hold true?", "options": ["Assets = Equity + Liabilities", "Assets = Liabilities − Equity", "Revenue = Expenses + Profit", "Equity = Assets + Liabilities"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The statement of financial position always balances: total assets equal total equity plus total liabilities."}, {"question": "A company has non-current assets of €11,300,000 and current assets of €7,180,000. What are its total assets?", "options": ["€18,480,000", "€4,120,000", "€11,300,000", "€7,180,000"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Total assets = non-current assets + current assets = 11,300,000 + 7,180,000 = €18,480,000."}, {"question": "Which of the following is classified as a current asset?", "options": ["Trade receivables", "Property, plant and equipment", "Purchased goodwill", "Long-term bank loans"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Trade receivables are expected to be collected within 12 months, making them a current asset. PPE and goodwill are non-current assets; long-term bank loans are non-current liabilities."}, {"question": "A company pays €25,000 on 31 December for the following three months' office rent. How should this be treated in the year-end accounts?", "options": ["As a prepayment within current assets", "As an expense in the current year's income statement", "As a non-current liability", "As part of retained earnings"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Since the €25,000 relates to a future period, it is not an expense of the current year — it is a prepayment, shown within current assets on the SOFP."}, {"question": "A machine costs €25,000 and has an estimated disposal value of €1,000 after a 6-year useful life. Using the straight-line method, what is the annual depreciation charge?", "options": ["€4,000", "€4,167", "€3,833", "€25,000"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Straight-line depreciation = (cost − disposal value) / useful life = (25,000 − 1,000) / 6 = €4,000 per annum."}, {"question": "Using the same machine (cost €25,000, disposal value €1,000, 6-year life, straight-line depreciation of €4,000 p.a.), what is its net book value at the end of year 3?", "options": ["€13,000", "€17,000", "€9,000", "€21,000"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "NBV after 3 years = 25,000 − (3 × 4,000) = 25,000 − 12,000 = €13,000."}, {"question": "A delivery van costs €18,000, has a 4-year useful life and an estimated disposal value of €2,000. What is its net book value at the end of year 2 under the straight-line method?", "options": ["€10,000", "€14,000", "€8,000", "€16,000"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Annual depreciation = (18,000 − 2,000) / 4 = €4,000. NBV after 2 years = 18,000 − (2 × 4,000) = €10,000."}, {"question": "Which non-current asset is generally NOT depreciated but instead periodically revalued?", "options": ["Freehold land", "Plant and machinery", "Motor vehicles", "Office equipment"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Freehold land is not depreciated because it does not have a limited economic life; it is instead periodically revalued if necessary."}, {"question": "How is purchased goodwill treated differently from other intangible non-current assets?", "options": ["It is not amortised, but may be tested for and subject to impairment", "It must be amortised over 20 years", "It is depreciated using the reducing balance method", "It is expensed immediately in the income statement"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Unlike other intangible assets (which are typically amortised), purchased goodwill is capitalised, not amortised, and cannot be revalued upward, but can suffer impairment."}, {"question": "Which of the following forms part of a company's capital reserves?", "options": ["Share premium account", "Retained earnings", "Trade payables", "Bank overdraft"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Capital reserves comprise the revaluation reserve and the share premium account; retained earnings is a revenue reserve, and trade payables/overdrafts are liabilities."}, {"question": "A company revalues its freehold land upward by €80,000. Where does this increase appear in the accounts?", "options": ["Credited to a revaluation reserve within equity", "Recognised as revenue in the income statement", "Recorded as a current liability", "Deducted from retained earnings"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "An upward revaluation of a non-current asset is credited to a revaluation reserve, a capital reserve within equity, keeping the SOFP in balance."}, {"question": "A company has revenue of €12,000,000 and cost of sales of €8,400,000. What is its gross profit?", "options": ["€3,600,000", "€20,400,000", "€8,400,000", "€4,400,000"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Gross profit = Revenue − Cost of sales = 12,000,000 − 8,400,000 = €3,600,000."}, {"question": "Continuing from gross profit of €3,600,000, if distribution costs are €300,000 and administrative expenses are €500,000, what is the operating profit?", "options": ["€2,800,000", "€3,600,000", "€4,400,000", "€2,300,000"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Operating profit = Gross profit − distribution costs − admin expenses = 3,600,000 − 300,000 − 500,000 = €2,800,000."}, {"question": "Which term is equivalent to 'operating profit' on the income statement?", "options": ["Profit before interest and tax (PBIT/EBIT)", "Net income", "Gross profit", "Profit before tax"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Operating profit is profit before financing costs and tax, i.e. profit before interest and tax (PBIT), also called EBIT."}, {"question": "How is EBITDA derived from operating profit (EBIT)?", "options": ["By adding back depreciation and amortisation", "By deducting depreciation and amortisation", "By deducting finance costs", "By adding back cost of sales"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "EBITDA = EBIT + depreciation + amortisation, as these are non-cash charges added back to give a rough proxy for operating cash generation."}, {"question": "Which of the following is included in cost of sales (COGS)?", "options": ["Direct materials and direct labour used to produce goods sold", "Auditors' fees", "Distribution costs", "Interest on borrowings"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "COGS comprises costs directly associated with production, such as raw materials and direct labour; auditors' fees and distribution costs are operating expenses, and interest is a finance cost."}, {"question": "A company purchases a new factory machine for €50,000. How should this expenditure be classified?", "options": ["Capital expenditure, recorded as a non-current asset on the SOFP", "Revenue expenditure, expensed immediately in full", "A current liability", "A reduction in retained earnings"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Buying a non-current asset such as machinery is capital expenditure; it is capitalised on the SOFP and depreciated over its useful life, not expensed immediately."}, {"question": "Which of the following is an example of revenue expenditure?", "options": ["Staff wages", "Purchase of a new office building", "Purchase of a delivery van", "Acquisition of a subsidiary"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Revenue expenditure is expenditure that immediately impacts the income statement, such as wages, rent and professional fees. The other options are capital expenditure."}, {"question": "Under IAS 7, into which three categories must a statement of cash flows be divided?", "options": ["Operating, investing and financing activities", "Assets, liabilities and equity", "Revenue, expenses and profit", "Current, non-current and contingent"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "IAS 7 requires cash flows to be presented under operating, investing and financing activities."}, {"question": "A company's cash flow statement shows depreciation of €35,000 added back to net income when calculating operating cash flow. Why is this addition made?", "options": ["Depreciation is a non-cash expense that reduced net income but did not use any cash", "Depreciation represents an actual cash payment made during the year", "Depreciation must always be deducted twice for accuracy", "Depreciation increases the company's tax liability"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Depreciation is a non-cash accounting charge; because it reduced net income without any cash leaving the business, it must be added back to reconcile net income to cash generated from operations."}, {"question": "If a company's accounts receivable balance decreases during the year, what is the effect on operating cash flow?", "options": ["An addition to cash, as more customers have paid off their balances", "A subtraction from cash, as fewer sales were made", "No effect on cash flow", "It only affects the income statement, not cash flow"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "A decrease in accounts receivable means customers have paid down what they owed, so more cash has come into the business — an addition when reconciling net income to operating cash flow."}, {"question": "A company's net income is €240,000. Additions to cash from working-capital movements and depreciation total €97,000, and subtractions total €75,000. What is the total operating cash flow?", "options": ["€262,000", "€172,000", "€412,000", "€22,000"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Total operating cash flow = net income + total additions − total subtractions = 240,000 + 97,000 − 75,000 = €262,000."}, {"question": "Using the income-statement approach, free cash flow is calculated as:", "options": ["Net income + depreciation and amortisation − capital expenditure", "Net income − depreciation and amortisation + capital expenditure", "Revenue − cost of sales − capital expenditure", "Operating profit + tax − dividends"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The income-statement approach to free cash flow adds back non-cash D&A to net income and deducts capital expenditure (the estimated spend needed to maintain operating capacity)."}, {"question": "A company has net income of €500,000, depreciation and amortisation of €60,000, and capital expenditure of €150,000. What is its free cash flow?", "options": ["€410,000", "€710,000", "€290,000", "€590,000"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Free cash flow = Net income + D&A − Capex = 500,000 + 60,000 − 150,000 = €410,000."}, {"question": "What is the key difference between 'enterprise cash flow' and 'equity cash flow'?", "options": ["Enterprise cash flow is before any payments to finance providers; equity cash flow is after payments to lenders but before dividends to shareholders", "Enterprise cash flow is after dividends; equity cash flow is before any financing costs", "There is no difference between the two measures", "Enterprise cash flow only includes financing activities"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Enterprise (firm) cash flow is calculated before any financing costs to lenders or shareholders; equity cash flow deducts financing costs to lenders but is calculated before dividend payments to shareholders."}, {"question": "Why might a profitable company still fail?", "options": ["It may not generate sufficient cash to pay its liabilities as they fall due", "Profit and cash are always identical figures", "Profitable companies cannot legally go into liquidation", "Depreciation always equals cash outflow"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Profit (income statement) and cash (cash flow statement) can diverge significantly — e.g. due to credit sales or non-cash depreciation — so a company can be profitable yet run out of cash to pay debts as they fall due."}, {"question": "A company has operating profit of €2,100,000, total equity of €12,280,000 and non-current liabilities of €4,000,000. What is its ROCE?", "options": ["12.9%", "17.1%", "22.1%", "9.9%"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Capital employed = equity + non-current liabilities = 12,280,000 + 4,000,000 = 16,280,000. ROCE = operating profit / capital employed = 2,100,000 / 16,280,000 = 12.9%."}, {"question": "Why do ROCE and ROA typically give the same result for a given company?", "options": ["Capital employed (equity + non-current liabilities) equals total net assets (non-current assets + current assets − current liabilities)", "ROA always excludes non-current assets from the calculation", "ROCE is calculated using net income, not operating profit", "They are unrelated ratios that coincidentally produce similar figures"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "By the accounting identity underlying the SOFP, capital employed and total net assets are mathematically equivalent, so ROCE and ROA (both using operating profit in the numerator) produce the same result."}, {"question": "A company has net income of €1,435,000 and total shareholders' equity of €12,280,000. What is its return on equity (ROE)?", "options": ["11.7%", "8.6%", "17.1%", "12.9%"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "ROE = Net income / Shareholders' equity = 1,435,000 / 12,280,000 = 11.7%."}, {"question": "A company reports revenue of €9,500,000 and gross profit of €2,500,000. What is its gross profit margin?", "options": ["26.3%", "22.1%", "38.0%", "73.7%"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Gross profit margin = Gross profit / Revenue = 2,500,000 / 9,500,000 = 26.3%."}, {"question": "A company has revenue of €40,000,000 and total net assets of €24,000,000. What is its asset turnover?", "options": ["1.67 times", "0.60 times", "16.7 times", "2.4 times"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Asset turnover = Revenue / Total net assets = 40,000,000 / 24,000,000 = 1.67 times."}, {"question": "A company has operating profit of €3,600,000 on revenue of €40,000,000, and total net assets of €24,000,000. Which combination correctly derives its ROCE using margin and turnover?", "options": ["Operating margin (9.0%) × Asset turnover (1.67) = 15.0% ROCE", "Operating margin (9.0%) + Asset turnover (1.67) = 10.67% ROCE", "Gross margin (9.0%) ÷ Asset turnover (1.67) = 5.4% ROCE", "Net margin (9.0%) − Asset turnover (1.67) = 7.33% ROCE"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "ROCE = Asset turnover × Operating profit margin. Operating margin = 3,600,000/40,000,000 = 9.0%; Asset turnover = 40,000,000/24,000,000 = 1.67; 9.0% × 1.67 = 15.0%, matching ROCE = 3,600,000/24,000,000."}, {"question": "A company has interest-bearing debt of €2,000,000 and equity of €12,280,000. What is its debt-to-equity ratio?", "options": ["0.163 (16.3%)", "6.14 (614%)", "1.0 (100%)", "0.837 (83.7%)"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Debt to equity = Debt / Equity = 2,000,000 / 12,280,000 = 0.163, or 16.3%."}, {"question": "A company has debt of €2,000,000, cash and short-term investments of €860,000, and equity of €12,280,000. What is its net debt-to-equity ratio?", "options": ["0.093 (9.3%)", "0.163 (16.3%)", "0.233 (23.3%)", "0.070 (7.0%)"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Net debt = Debt − cash and short-term investments = 2,000,000 − 860,000 = 1,140,000. Net debt to equity = 1,140,000 / 12,280,000 = 0.093, or 9.3%."}, {"question": "A company has operating profit of €2,100,000 and interest costs of €230,000. What is its interest cover?", "options": ["9.1 times", "0.11 times", "2.1 times", "18.3 times"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Interest cover = Operating profit / Interest costs = 2,100,000 / 230,000 = 9.1 times."}, {"question": "A company has debt of €5,000,000, equity of €10,000,000, operating profit of €2,400,000 and interest costs of €400,000. What is its interest cover?", "options": ["6.0 times", "0.5 times", "50 times", "1.67 times"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Interest cover = Operating profit / Interest costs = 2,400,000 / 400,000 = 6.0 times."}, {"question": "Why might a company's net debt-to-equity ratio be considerably lower than its (gross) debt-to-equity ratio?", "options": ["The company holds substantial cash and short-term investments that could be used to repay debt", "The company has no equity in issue", "Interest cover is always higher than gearing", "Net debt-to-equity ignores all liabilities"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Net debt-to-equity nets off cash and short-term investments against debt, so a company holding large cash balances will show materially lower net gearing than gross gearing."}, {"question": "A company has current assets of €7,180,000 and current liabilities of €2,200,000. What is its current ratio?", "options": ["3.26", "0.31", "1.63", "4.98"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Current ratio = Current assets / Current liabilities = 7,180,000 / 2,200,000 = 3.26."}, {"question": "Using the same company (current assets €7,180,000, of which inventory is €3,600,000, and current liabilities €2,200,000), what is its quick (acid test) ratio?", "options": ["1.63", "3.26", "1.0", "0.5"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Quick ratio = (Current assets − Inventory) / Current liabilities = (7,180,000 − 3,600,000) / 2,200,000 = 3,580,000 / 2,200,000 = 1.63."}, {"question": "A company has current assets of €4,500,000, of which €1,800,000 is inventory, and current liabilities of €3,000,000. What is its quick ratio, and what does the result suggest?", "options": ["0.9 — below 1, potentially indicating a need for additional short-term finance", "1.5 — comfortably above 1, no liquidity concern", "2.5 — very strong liquidity position", "0.4 — the company has no current liabilities"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Quick ratio = (4,500,000 − 1,800,000) / 3,000,000 = 2,700,000 / 3,000,000 = 0.9. Being below 1 can indicate the company may need to raise new finance to cover short-term obligations without relying on inventory sales."}, {"question": "A company has net income of €898,000 attributable to ordinary shareholders and 5,000,000 ordinary shares in issue. What is its EPS?", "options": ["18c", "17.96c", "5.57c", "179.6c"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "EPS = Net profit attributable to ordinary shareholders / Weighted average shares = 898,000 / 5,000,000 = €0.18, i.e. 18c per share."}, {"question": "A company has €250,000 of 10% convertible loan stock, convertible at 115 shares per €100 nominal, a 30% corporation tax rate, basic earnings of €898,000, and 5,000,000 basic shares. What is the diluted EPS?", "options": ["17.3c", "18.0c", "16.5c", "19.1c"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Interest saved = 250,000×10%=25,000; extra tax=25,000×30%=7,500; diluted earnings=898,000+25,000−7,500=915,500. New shares=(250,000/100)×115=287,500; diluted shares=5,287,500. Diluted EPS=915,500/5,287,500=17.3c."}, {"question": "A company's shares trade at €1.60 and its historic (pre-dilution) EPS is 18c. What is its historic P/E ratio?", "options": ["8.89", "0.113", "28.8", "11.25"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Historic P/E = Share price / Historic EPS = 160 / 18 = 8.89."}, {"question": "Using the same company (share price €1.60) with a forecast EPS for next year of 17c, what is the prospective P/E ratio?", "options": ["9.41", "0.106", "27.2", "8.89"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Prospective P/E = Share price / Forecast EPS = 160 / 17 = 9.41."}, {"question": "A company's shares trade at €4.50 and its EPS is 30c. What is its historic P/E?", "options": ["15.0", "0.067", "13.5", "45.0"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "P/E = Share price / EPS = 450 / 30 = 15.0."}, {"question": "A company distributes total dividends of €400,000 across 5,000,000 shares, with the shares trading at $1.60. What is the gross dividend yield?", "options": ["5.0%", "8.0%", "20.0%", "2.5%"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "DPS = 400,000 / 5,000,000 = 8c. Gross dividend yield = (0.08 / 1.60) × 100 = 5.0%."}, {"question": "A company has EPS of 18c and pays a dividend of 8c per share. What is its gross dividend cover?", "options": ["2.25 times", "0.44 times", "10 times", "1.44 times"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Dividend cover = EPS / Dividend per share = 18 / 8 = 2.25 times."}, {"question": "A dividend cover ratio of less than 1 indicates:", "options": ["An 'uncovered' dividend, where the year's profits were insufficient to cover the dividend paid", "The company has no dividend policy", "The company's shares are undervalued", "The company has paid no dividend at all"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "A dividend cover below 1 means the dividend paid exceeded the year's earnings — an 'uncovered' dividend, often funded from reserves or borrowing."}, {"question": "How is Enterprise Value (EV) calculated?", "options": ["Market capitalisation + debt + non-controlling interests + preference shares − cash and cash equivalents", "Market capitalisation − debt − cash", "Net income × P/E ratio", "Total assets − total liabilities"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "EV represents the theoretical cost of buying the whole company by settling all claimants: market cap plus debt, NCI and preference shares, less cash and cash equivalents."}, {"question": "What is the key difference between the P/E ratio and the EV/EBIT ratio as valuation measures?", "options": ["P/E values only the equity of a company, while EV/EBIT values the whole company (equity plus debt)", "P/E is calculated using EBITDA, while EV/EBIT uses net income", "EV/EBIT can only be used for loss-making companies", "P/E and EV/EBIT are calculated using identical formulas"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "P/E relates share price (an equity-only measure) to EPS. EV/EBIT relates enterprise value (which captures both debt and equity claims) to operating profit, giving a whole-company valuation perspective."}, {"question": "A company has an enterprise value of €55,000,000, EBIT of €4,000,000 and D&A of €500,000. What is its EV/EBITDA?", "options": ["12.2", "13.75", "110.0", "9.2"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "EBITDA = EBIT + D&A = 4,000,000 + 500,000 = 4,500,000. EV/EBITDA = 55,000,000 / 4,500,000 = 12.2."}, {"question": "Why is EBITDA described as a potentially misleading, non-GAAP measure of cash earnings?", "options": ["It excludes the cash required to fund working capital and replace old equipment", "It always equals net income", "It includes tax and interest, which distorts comparisons", "It is defined identically by every company and cannot vary"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "EBITDA leaves out cash needed for working capital and capital expenditure to replace/maintain equipment, and companies can vary what they include, so it should not be treated as a true measure of cash earnings."}, {"question": "Which of the following best explains why a company's share price may not move significantly even after it reports a 10% rise in net income?", "options": ["The market had already anticipated the improvement, so the actual result matched or fell short of expectations", "Financial statements have no relationship whatsoever with share prices", "Net income is never relevant to investors", "Share prices only respond to changes in the statement of cash flows"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Share prices reflect what investors expect to happen. If reported results are in line with — or below — market expectations, the share price reaction will be muted or even negative, despite genuinely positive results."}, {"question": "Which of the following is a recognised limitation of ratio analysis?", "options": ["Financial statements contain historic data, so ratios are not necessarily predictive of future performance", "Ratios are calculated identically under every accounting standard worldwide", "Ratio analysis requires no judgement to interpret", "Ratios eliminate the need to compare a company against its peers"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Key limitations of ratio analysis include reliance on historic (backward-looking) data, differences in accounting policies/standards between companies and jurisdictions, and the judgement required in both preparing and interpreting the figures."}], "flashcards": [{"front": "Straight-line depreciation formula", "back": "(Cost − Disposal value) / Useful economic life (years)"}, {"front": "Net book value (NBV)", "back": "Cost of an asset less accumulated depreciation to date; does not necessarily equal market value"}, {"front": "Equity formula", "back": "Equity = Share capital + Capital reserves + Revenue reserves (± non-controlling interests in group accounts)"}, {"front": "ROCE formula", "back": "Operating profit / Capital employed, where Capital employed = Equity + Non-current liabilities"}, {"front": "ROA formula", "back": "Operating profit / Total net assets, where Total net assets = Non-current assets + Current assets − Current liabilities (gives the same result as ROCE)"}, {"front": "ROE formula", "back": "Net income / Shareholders' equity"}, {"front": "Gross profit margin", "back": "Gross profit / Revenue"}, {"front": "Operating profit margin", "back": "Operating profit / Revenue"}, {"front": "Net profit margin (per CISI definition)", "back": "Profit before tax / Revenue — profit after considering both operating and finance costs"}, {"front": "Asset turnover formula", "back": "Revenue / Total net assets, expressed as 'x times'; ROCE = Asset turnover × Operating profit margin"}, {"front": "Debt-to-equity ratio", "back": "Debt / Equity, where Debt = interest-bearing non-current liabilities"}, {"front": "Net debt-to-equity ratio", "back": "(Debt − Cash and short-term investments) / Equity"}, {"front": "Interest cover formula", "back": "Operating profit / Interest costs"}, {"front": "Current ratio formula", "back": "Current assets / Current liabilities"}, {"front": "Quick ratio (acid test) formula", "back": "(Current assets − Inventory) / Current liabilities"}, {"front": "EPS formula (IAS 33)", "back": "(Net profit attributable to ordinary shareholders − Preference dividends) / Weighted average number of ordinary shares in issue"}, {"front": "Diluted EPS — concept", "back": "Adjusted EPS assuming conversion of all dilutive instruments (convertible bonds/preference shares, options, warrants) into ordinary shares, using the worst-case (maximum dilution) assumption; required under IAS 33"}, {"front": "P/E ratio formula", "back": "Share price / EPS"}, {"front": "Historic vs prospective P/E", "back": "Historic (trailing/ttm) P/E uses the last 12 months' reported EPS; Prospective (forward) P/E uses forecast EPS for the next 12 months"}, {"front": "Enterprise Value (EV) formula", "back": "Market capitalisation + Debt + Non-controlling interests + Preference shares − Cash and cash equivalents"}, {"front": "EV/EBIT formula", "back": "Enterprise value / EBIT (operating profit / PBIT)"}, {"front": "EV/EBITDA formula", "back": "Enterprise value / EBITDA, where EBITDA = EBIT + Depreciation + Amortisation"}, {"front": "Gross dividend yield formula", "back": "(Dividend per share / Share price) × 100"}, {"front": "Gross dividend cover formula", "back": "EPS / Dividend per share"}, {"front": "EBIT / PBIT", "back": "Earnings (or profit) before interest and tax = Operating profit"}, {"front": "EBITDA", "back": "Earnings before interest, tax, depreciation and amortisation = EBIT + D&A; a non-GAAP proxy for operating cash generation"}, {"front": "Free cash flow formula (income-statement approach)", "back": "Net income + Depreciation and amortisation − Capital expenditure"}, {"front": "Enterprise cash flow vs equity cash flow", "back": "Enterprise cash flow is free cash flow before any payments to finance providers; equity cash flow is after financing costs to lenders but before dividends to shareholders"}, {"front": "Capital employed", "back": "Equity + Non-current liabilities (equivalent to total net assets)"}, {"front": "Capital expenditure vs revenue expenditure", "back": "Capital expenditure buys non-current assets and sits on the SOFP (depreciated over time); revenue expenditure (wages, rent, fees) is expensed immediately in the income statement"}, {"front": "Goodwill (on consolidation)", "back": "Cost of investment in a subsidiary minus the fair value of its identifiable net assets acquired; recognised as an intangible asset, not amortised, but subject to impairment"}, {"front": "Non-controlling interests (NCI)", "back": "The share of a subsidiary's equity/profit not owned by the parent (formerly 'minority interests'); e.g. 75% parent ownership = 25% NCI"}, {"front": "Group accounts vs company accounts", "back": "Group (consolidated) accounts combine 100% of a parent's and its subsidiaries' assets, liabilities, revenues and expenses as a single entity; company accounts show only the parent's individual results, with subsidiary investments at cost"}, {"front": "IFRS vs IAS", "back": "IFRSs are standards currently issued by the IASB; IASs are legacy standards issued by the IASB's predecessor body (the IASC) that remain in force where not superseded"}, {"front": "IASB vs ISSB", "back": "IASB (International Accounting Standards Board) sets financial reporting standards (IFRS/IAS); ISSB (International Sustainability Standards Board) sets climate/ESG disclosure standards; both operate under the IFRS Foundation"}, {"front": "Prepayment", "back": "An expense paid in advance of the period it relates to; shown as a current asset on the SOFP, not as an expense in the current period"}, {"front": "Provisions", "back": "Liabilities of uncertain timing or amount arising from a past event (e.g. restructuring); recognised at a prudent estimate and classified current or non-current based on expected payment date"}, {"front": "Share premium account (capital reserve)", "back": "The excess received over the nominal value of shares issued; also called 'additional paid-in capital'; not distributable as dividends"}, {"front": "Revenue reserves (retained earnings)", "back": "Accumulated distributable profits not paid out as dividends; distinct from cash held and from the current period's reported profit"}, {"front": "Statement of financial position equation", "back": "Assets = Equity + Liabilities (must always balance)"}, {"front": "Cost of sales (COGS)", "back": "Direct costs of producing goods/services sold (materials, direct labour); excludes distribution, admin and finance costs"}, {"front": "Gross profit", "back": "Revenue − Cost of sales"}, {"front": "Operating profit (PBIT/EBIT)", "back": "Gross profit − other operating expenses (distribution costs, admin expenses, including depreciation/amortisation)"}, {"front": "Unqualified vs qualified audit report", "back": "Unqualified = accounts give a true and fair view, properly prepared; Qualified = auditor identifies and states a specific departure or concern"}, {"front": "IAS 7 cash flow categories", "back": "Operating activities, Investing activities, Financing activities — the total explains the change in cash and cash equivalents between two SOFP dates"}, {"front": "Profit vs cash — key driver of divergence", "back": "Profit is based on revenues earned/expenses incurred (accruals basis); cash reflects actual receipts/payments — e.g. credit sales, or a machine expensed gradually via depreciation but paid for in full in cash upfront"}, {"front": "Current vs non-current assets/liabilities", "back": "Current = due to be realised/settled within 12 months; Non-current = longer-term"}, {"front": "Depreciation vs amortisation", "back": "Depreciation applies to tangible non-current assets (e.g. plant, machinery); amortisation applies to intangible non-current assets (e.g. patents, licences); both are non-cash expenses charged to the income statement"}, {"front": "Limitations of ratio analysis", "back": "Relies on historic data (not predictive); differing accounting policies/standards hinder comparability; significant judgement is required, leading to divergence between analysts"}, {"front": "Share price and financial statements", "back": "Share prices reflect investor expectations; a share price only reacts significantly to results when they diverge from what the market had already anticipated"}, {"front": "Diluted EPS securities to consider", "back": "Convertible loan stock/convertible preference shares, and issued share options or warrants — both can increase shares in issue and dilute EPS"}, {"front": "Dividend cover below 1", "back": "An 'uncovered' dividend — the year's profits were insufficient to cover the dividends actually paid out"}, {"front": "Purpose of the strategic/CFO review in the annual report", "back": "Sets out company strategy, key risks, and performance metrics (including many accounting ratios), providing context beyond the raw financial statements"}]}, {"id": "sec-m11", "number": 11, "title": "Investment Management & Portfolio Theory", "examWeight": "Core module", "summaryHtml": "<p>Module 11 draws together risk, return and asset-class knowledge into a framework for constructing and managing diversified portfolios. Investment decisions balance expected return (ER) against risk, where risk is quantified using variance and standard deviation of returns across probability-weighted economic scenarios (ER = &Sigma;(probability &times; scenario return); standard deviation = &radic;variance). Candidates must recognise the main risk types: market/systematic risk (affects the whole market, cannot be diversified away), specific/unsystematic risk (company-specific, can be diversified away), inflation risk (erodes fixed-income returns more than equity returns), interest rate risk (greatest for long-maturity, low-coupon fixed-rate bonds; largely removed by floating-rate coupons), reinvestment risk (maturing proceeds reinvested at a different prevailing rate), exchange rate risk (overseas holdings), political/legal risk, regulatory risk, default/credit risk (rated by S&amp;P, Moody's and Fitch; investment grade is at least BBB/Baa), and liquidity risk.</p><p>Equities offer capital appreciation and dividend income with limited liability, but demand an equity risk premium over the risk-free government rate to compensate for higher risk. Money market instruments (eg, T-bills) are low-risk, liquid and short-term, offering income but little capital growth; in a crisis, a 'flight to safety' into short-dated government paper can occur. Debt instruments pay fixed or floating coupons; floating-rate coupons (referencing SONIA in the UK, SOFR in the US, replacing LIBOR) largely remove interest rate risk, while index-linked bonds remove inflation risk. Overseas equities and bonds add currency (exchange rate) risk and country (political/legal) risk on top of normal domestic risks.</p><p>Correlation and diversification sit at the heart of portfolio theory. The correlation coefficient ranges from +1 (perfectly positively correlated, no diversification benefit) to -1 (perfectly negatively correlated, maximum diversification benefit); any correlation below +1 provides some diversification benefit. Diversification reduces unsystematic (specific) risk but cannot remove systematic (market) risk. It is achieved across asset classes (cash, bonds, equities, collective investments, derivatives, property) and by maturity (laddering fixed-income holdings to reduce reinvestment risk).</p><p>Hedging reduces risk, at a cost, typically using derivatives: futures (eg, selling stock index futures, sized using the portfolio's beta relative to the index) remove both upside and downside; options (buying put options) cap losses at the premium paid while preserving upside, with break-even = strike price minus premium and maximum profit if the underlying falls to zero; CFDs are margin-traded derivatives that track an underlying asset's price without conferring ownership, useful for cost-effective hedging via initial and variation/maintenance margin.</p><p>Finally, company liquidation ranking is a classic exact-recall topic: the basic rule is that all debt holders rank above all shareholders. Within debt, seniority runs senior debt, then subordinated debt, then mezzanine debt (which may include PIK notes). Among shareholders, preference shareholders (entitled to par value, or a liquidation preference multiple for venture-backed start-ups) rank above ordinary shareholders, who receive any residual proceeds last.</p>", "sections": [{"heading": "11.1 Financial Planning: Risk, Reward and Quantifying Risk", "html": "<p>Investment management involves a trade-off between expected reward and the risk taken to achieve it, then considering the impact of that risk on the overall portfolio. A fund manager can build an <strong>expected return (ER)</strong> by weighting possible economic scenarios by their probability and summing: ER = &Sigma;(probability &times; scenario return).</p><table><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Probability</th><th>Return</th></tr><tr><td>Contraction (&lt;0% growth)</td><td>20%</td><td>-10.0%</td></tr><tr><td>Growth 0%-3%</td><td>50%</td><td>8.0%</td></tr><tr><td>Growth &gt;3%</td><td>30%</td><td>15.0%</td></tr></table><p>This gives ER = 6.5%. <strong>Variance (&sigma;&sup2;)</strong> measures how far returns vary from the ER (here 0.77%), and <strong>standard deviation (&sigma;)</strong>, the square root of variance (here 8.79%), is the standard statistical measure of risk/dispersion. Risk and reward are generally positively correlated: higher potential return generally means higher risk of loss.</p><p>Forward-looking analysis uses forecasts and probabilities to assess likely future outcomes; backward-looking analysis studies historical returns and frequencies, assuming the past is representative of the future.</p>"}, {"heading": "11.2 Types of Investment Risk", "html": "<p>Investors face a number of largely unavoidable risk categories:</p><ul><li><strong>Market/systematic risk</strong> - the whole market (or a broad sector) rises or falls with economic conditions; cannot be diversified away.</li><li><strong>Specific/unsystematic risk</strong> - an adverse event affects one company only (eg, a defective product); can be diversified away by holding many investments.</li><li><strong>Inflation risk</strong> - erodes purchasing power; greater risk for fixed-income investors (fixed coupons) than equity investors (companies can often pass on costs).</li><li><strong>Interest rate risk</strong> - price changes from rate movements; greatest for bonds with longer maturity and lower coupons; largely removed for floating-rate bonds.</li><li><strong>Reinvestment risk</strong> - maturing proceeds may only be reinvested at a lower (or higher) prevailing rate.</li><li><strong>Exchange rate risk</strong> - movements between home and foreign currency affect returns on overseas assets; a strengthening home currency lowers returns from foreign assets.</li><li><strong>Political and legal risk</strong> - government action (eg, capital controls, withholding tax changes) affecting investments, especially overseas.</li><li><strong>Regulatory risk</strong> - changes in regulation/legislation affecting a security, firm or industry (eg, Credit Suisse's forced UBS takeover, 2023).</li><li><strong>Default risk</strong> - risk of issuer insolvency; monitored via credit ratings from S&amp;P, Moody's and Fitch. Investment grade = at least BBB (S&amp;P/Fitch) or Baa (Moody's); below this is sub-investment grade/junk.</li><li><strong>Liquidity risk</strong> - inability to trade at the desired price due to limited market activity; worse in stressed or smaller/illiquid markets.</li></ul>"}, {"heading": "11.3 Equities, Money Market Instruments and Debt in Portfolio Construction", "html": "<p><strong>Equities</strong> give an ownership stake with limited liability and offer capital appreciation plus dividend income. They are generally the riskiest mainstream asset class (medium risk for large established companies, high risk for smaller/start-up companies) but offer the best long-term return potential. The <strong>equity risk premium</strong> is the extra return demanded over the risk-free (government bond/T-bill) rate to compensate for equity risk (eg, stock return 10% minus risk-free rate 4% = 6% equity risk premium).</p><p><strong>Money market instruments</strong> (eg, Treasury bills) are low-risk, short-term, liquid, income-generating but offer little capital growth; ideal for short investment horizons. In crises (eg, 2008), a 'flight to safety' into the shortest-dated, most secure paper can even push yields to near-zero or negative.</p><p><strong>Debt instruments</strong> (bonds) pay a contractually fixed or floating coupon and are generally less risky than equities but offer less upside. Floating coupons reference benchmarks such as SONIA (UK sterling) or SOFR (US, replacing LIBOR) and reset with rate movements, largely removing interest rate risk. Index-linked bonds (eg, linked to CPI) protect against inflation risk since coupon and principal are uplifted. Holding a bond to maturity gives near-certain yield for highly rated issuers; selling before maturity risks a capital loss if market value is below nominal value.</p>"}, {"heading": "11.4 Overseas Equities and Bonds", "html": "<p>Investing overseas diversifies away domestic-economy-specific problems and can add income and capital appreciation opportunities, but introduces additional risks:</p><ul><li><strong>Currency (exchange rate) risk</strong> - the investor may gain or lose more than the local price movement due to currency moves; can also affect home companies with substantial overseas operations.</li><li><strong>Country risk (political/legal)</strong> - changes in the political or legal landscape of the overseas market (eg, capital controls, tax changes, sanctions - as seen with Russia in 2022) can affect returns, liquidity and safety of capital.</li></ul><p>Investors may also be less familiar with overseas issuers, and local market idiosyncrasies (eg, restricted access to corporate actions via local custodians) can add complexity.</p>"}, {"heading": "11.5 Correlation and Diversification", "html": "<p>Diversification reduces risk without needing to give up all high-risk assets, by combining investments that are not perfectly positively correlated. The <strong>correlation coefficient</strong> measures the degree of co-movement between two assets' returns and ranges from -1 to +1:</p><table><tr><th>Correlation coefficient</th><th>Interpretation</th></tr><tr><td>+1</td><td>Perfectly positively correlated - assets move together, same direction and magnitude - <strong>no diversification benefit</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Between 0 and +1</td><td>Positively correlated but not perfect - some diversification benefit</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>Uncorrelated - returns unrelated - significant diversification benefit</td></tr><tr><td>Between -1 and 0</td><td>Negatively correlated but not perfect - strong diversification benefit</td></tr><tr><td>-1</td><td>Perfectly negatively correlated - assets move exactly opposite - <strong>maximum diversification benefit</strong></td></tr></table><p>Only investments that are <strong>perfectly positively correlated</strong> (correlation = +1) offer no diversification benefit; any lower correlation, even if still positive, provides some benefit. Diversification removes <strong>specific/unsystematic risk</strong> but not <strong>market/systematic risk</strong>.</p><p>Diversification can be achieved <strong>by asset class</strong> (cash, fixed income, equities, collective investment schemes, derivatives, real estate/property, other assets) and <strong>by maturity</strong> - spreading fixed-income maturities over time ('laddering') to reduce reinvestment risk.</p>"}, {"heading": "11.6 Hedging with Futures and Options", "html": "<p><strong>Hedging</strong> reduces risk (at a cost) by taking a derivative position opposite to the underlying portfolio exposure.</p><p><strong>Futures</strong> (eg, stock index futures such as the FTSE 100 contract): selling futures against an equity portfolio removes downside risk but also removes upside potential. Advantages: lower cost, efficiency, less portfolio disruption. Limitations: the portfolio's exact risk/beta characteristics may not be matched by an available index future; not all markets have futures contracts; timing risk (entering/exiting the hedge); operational/regulatory considerations (separate accounts, rolling contracts before expiry). The number of contracts sold is adjusted by the portfolio's <strong>beta</strong> relative to the index (beta of the index = 1): a portfolio with beta 1.20 requires selling 1.20 &times; the nominal cash-equivalent number of contracts.</p><p><strong>Options</strong>: buying <strong>put options</strong> on held equities (or a matching index) gives the right, not obligation, to sell at a predetermined strike price, capping downside while preserving upside participation - the seller/writer is 'short' the put. <em>Example</em>: 1,000 shares at $110, buy put strike $110, premium $6. Break-even = strike - premium = $110 - $6 = $104. Maximum loss is limited to the premium paid ($6/share); maximum profit on the put itself occurs if the price falls to zero. The premium is a 'wasting asset' that decays towards expiry; rolling over options to maintain 'portfolio insurance' adds ongoing cost.</p>"}, {"heading": "11.7 Hedging with Contracts for Difference (CFDs)", "html": "<p><strong>CFDs</strong> are derivatives (developed early 1990s) whose price tracks an underlying asset (equity, bond, currency, commodity, index) without conferring ownership of it. They are <strong>margin-traded</strong> - the investor deposits only a fraction of the full notional value (typically 1%-30%, eg, 5% for shares), giving a geared position. CFDs allow going short or long, making them useful for cost-effective hedging (equivalent to short-selling without actually shorting the stock) or for speculation.</p><p>Costs: commission per trade (a % of position size), the bid/offer spread, and a daily funding/financing charge (linked to a benchmark such as SOFR) on long positions. Two types of margin apply: <strong>initial margin</strong> (the deposit required to open the position) and <strong>variation/maintenance margin</strong> (additional margin required if the position moves adversely, ie, marked-to-market losses).</p><p><em>Example</em>: hedge 10,000 shares at 99p using CFDs quoted 98.5p/99.5p; 5% margin needs only £492.50 initial deposit (5% &times; £9,850). If the share price falls to 84p, the £1,500 loss on the shares is largely offset by the CFD gain (~£1,400 before commissions of ~£27.45), demonstrating an effective but not perfect hedge. Note: as with any short-equivalent hedge, a rising share price would generate a loss on the CFD position, offsetting portfolio gains.</p>"}, {"heading": "11.8 Company Liquidations: Ranking of Shares and Debt (Priority Order)", "html": "<p>On winding-up/liquidation, proceeds are distributed according to the <strong>liquidation ranking</strong>. The basic rule tested repeatedly in the exam: <strong>all shareholders (equity) rank below all debt holders</strong>.</p><table><tr><th>Priority (highest first)</th><th>Claimant</th><th>Notes</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Senior debt holders</td><td>Claim above more junior debt and all equity; may be secured by fixed or floating charge, or by other covenants</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Subordinated debt holders</td><td>Rank below senior debt; compensated with a higher interest rate for the extra risk accepted</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Mezzanine debt holders</td><td>Most junior debt tranche (if it exists); ranks below other debt but above equity; highest debt interest rate; may include payment-in-kind (PIK) notes, where coupons roll up and are paid at maturity rather than in cash</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Preference shareholders</td><td>Receive par value of shares before ordinary shareholders; venture-backed start-ups may have a 'liquidation preference' - a multiple (eg, 2x) of the original purchase price payable before ordinary shareholders on liquidation, sale, merger or change of control</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Ordinary shareholders</td><td>Residual proceeds only, if any remain, after all debt and preference claims are met</td></tr></table><p>Seniority within debt can be contractual (terms of issue) or based on the issuer's corporate structure, and each broad category (senior/subordinated/mezzanine) may itself be subdivided (eg, senior secured, senior unsecured, senior subordinated, junior subordinated).</p>"}], "mcqs": [{"question": "In the CISI expected return/variance framework, if an economy has a 20% probability of -10% return, a 50% probability of 8% return, and a 30% probability of 15% return, what is the expected return?", "options": ["4.5%", "6.5%", "8.0%", "13.0%"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "ER = (0.20 x -10%) + (0.50 x 8%) + (0.30 x 15%) = -2.0% + 4.0% + 4.5% = 6.5%."}, {"question": "Standard deviation, as used to quantify investment risk, is calculated as:", "options": ["The mean of all scenario returns", "The square root of the variance", "The variance squared", "The difference between the highest and lowest scenario returns"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Standard deviation (sigma) is the square root of variance (sigma-squared) and is the standard statistical measure of the dispersion of returns around the expected return."}, {"question": "Which type of risk arises from the possibility that the overall market, or a broad sector, will rise or fall due to economic conditions, and is very difficult to remove through diversification?", "options": ["Specific/unsystematic risk", "Reinvestment risk", "Market/systematic risk", "Liquidity risk"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Market or systematic risk affects the whole market (or broad sector) and, because most securities belong to some broad asset class, this type of risk is difficult to avoid even with diversification."}, {"question": "Why does inflation risk tend to be greater for fixed-income investors than for equity investors?", "options": ["Bonds are always sub-investment grade", "Bonds usually pay a fixed coupon, whereas companies can often pass on higher costs to customers", "Equities are never affected by inflation", "Bond coupons automatically rise with inflation"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Bonds typically pay a fixed coupon that loses real value as inflation rises, while equity-issuing companies can often pass on cost increases to customers, partially offsetting inflation's impact."}, {"question": "Which bonds are most susceptible to interest rate risk?", "options": ["Short-dated, high-coupon bonds", "Floating-rate bonds", "Long-dated bonds with low coupon rates", "Index-linked bonds"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The amount of price change from interest rate movements is related to term to maturity and coupon rate; bonds with longer maturities and lower coupons are the most exposed to interest rate risk."}, {"question": "Reinvestment risk refers to:", "options": ["The risk that a bond issuer defaults on interest payments", "The risk that maturing investment proceeds cannot be reinvested at the same rate as originally obtained", "The risk that exchange rates move against an investor", "The risk that a security cannot be sold at the desired price"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Reinvestment risk is the risk that, when an investment matures, the proceeds must be reinvested at a different (potentially lower) prevailing interest rate than originally obtained."}, {"question": "If a UK investor's home currency (sterling) strengthens against the US dollar, what is the likely effect on the sterling value of their US dollar-denominated equity returns?", "options": ["Returns are unaffected", "Returns from the foreign asset are increased", "Returns from the foreign asset are lowered", "The investment automatically becomes risk-free"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "If the investor's base currency strengthens against the currency of the overseas investment, this lowers the returns when converted back into the home currency."}, {"question": "Which UK sterling interest rate benchmark replaced LIBOR and is based on actual overnight borrowing transactions between banks?", "options": ["SOFR", "SONIA", "CPI", "GEPF"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The Sterling Overnight Index Average (SONIA) is the primary sterling interest rate benchmark, based on actual transactions, replacing LIBOR. SOFR is the equivalent US dollar benchmark."}, {"question": "According to the ratings framework described in the syllabus, what is the minimum rating from S&P or Fitch Ratings for a bond issue to be classified as 'investment grade'?", "options": ["AAA", "AA", "BBB", "CCC"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "An investment grade rating is at least BBB from S&P or Fitch Ratings, or at least Baa from Moody's. Ratings below this level are classed as sub-investment grade or 'junk'."}, {"question": "If the total return on a stock over a period is 11% and the risk-free rate over the same period is 4%, what is the equity risk premium?", "options": ["4%", "7%", "11%", "15%"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Equity risk premium = total stock return - risk-free rate = 11% - 4% = 7%."}, {"question": "Which of the following best describes money market instruments such as Treasury bills as an investment?", "options": ["High risk, illiquid, and best suited to a 20-year investment horizon", "Low risk, liquid, short-term, offering income but little scope for capital growth", "Instruments that only ever offer negative yields", "Instruments exclusively available to retail investors"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Money market instruments are low-risk, relatively secure, liquid and deliver income, but offer little scope for capital growth, making them suitable for short investment horizons."}, {"question": "The correlation coefficient between the returns of two assets can range between:", "options": ["0 and 100", "-1 and +1", "-100 and 0", "0 and 1 only"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The correlation coefficient ranges from -1 (perfectly negatively correlated) to +1 (perfectly positively correlated)."}, {"question": "Which combination of assets provides NO diversification benefit when held together in a portfolio?", "options": ["Assets with a correlation coefficient of 0", "Assets with a correlation coefficient of -0.5", "Assets that are perfectly positively correlated (correlation = +1)", "Assets with a correlation coefficient of -1"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "It is only a combination of investments that are perfectly positively correlated (correlation = +1) that offers no diversification benefit; any correlation below +1, including positive-but-imperfect correlation, still provides some benefit."}, {"question": "Maximum diversification benefit between two assets is achieved when their correlation coefficient is:", "options": ["+1", "0", "-1", "+0.75"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "A correlation coefficient of -1 (perfectly negatively correlated) provides the maximum diversification benefit, as the assets move in exactly opposite directions."}, {"question": "Which type of risk can be substantially reduced through diversification of a portfolio?", "options": ["Market/systematic risk", "Specific/unsystematic risk", "Global inflation risk", "Overall economic cycle risk"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Diversification removes specific (unsystematic) risk, which relates to individual companies or sectors. Market (systematic) risk cannot be diversified away."}, {"question": "Spreading the maturity dates of fixed-income holdings across a portfolio, in order to reduce reinvestment risk, is known as:", "options": ["Hedging", "Laddering", "Netting", "Gearing"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "'Laddering' is the strategy of diversifying fixed-income holdings by maturity date, spreading reinvestment risk over time."}, {"question": "A fund manager sells stock index futures against an equity portfolio to hedge market risk. What is a key drawback of this strategy?", "options": ["It has no cost implications at all", "It removes downside risk while retaining full upside potential", "It removes both downside risk and upside potential", "It can only be used on overseas markets"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Selling futures contracts to hedge a portfolio removes exposure to both downside losses and upside gains in the underlying market."}, {"question": "A pension fund's equity portfolio has a market value-weighted average beta of 1.20 relative to the FTSE 100. To fully hedge using FTSE 100 futures, the fund should:", "options": ["Sell futures with a nominal value equal to 0.80 times the portfolio's cash value", "Sell futures with a nominal value equal to 1.20 times the portfolio's cash value", "Buy futures with a nominal value equal to the portfolio's cash value", "Take no futures position, as beta is irrelevant to hedging"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The number/nominal value of futures contracts sold is prorated by the ratio of the portfolio's beta to the index's beta (which is 1). A beta of 1.20 means selling futures worth 1.20 times the cash portfolio value."}, {"question": "An investor holds shares in XYZ at $110 and buys a put option with a strike price of $110 and a premium of $6. What is the break-even price for this hedge?", "options": ["$104", "$110", "$116", "$6"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Break-even = exercise (strike) price minus premium paid = $110 - $6 = $104. The premium must be recovered before any net profit or full hedge protection is achieved."}, {"question": "For the holder of a purchased put option used to hedge a long equity position, the maximum possible loss is:", "options": ["Unlimited", "Limited to the premium paid for the option", "Limited to the strike price", "Always zero"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The risk to a put option purchaser is limited to the premium paid; the option's value cannot fall below zero, so losses cannot exceed the premium."}, {"question": "What is the primary motivation for a fund manager to buy put options on shares already held in the portfolio, rather than sell the shares outright?", "options": ["To generate additional trading commissions", "To cap downside loss while retaining upside participation in the shares", "To eliminate all costs associated with holding the portfolio", "To convert the equity holding into a fixed-income instrument"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Holding the shares and buying puts allows continued participation in any share price upside while providing the right to sell at the strike price if the price falls, capping downside loss at the premium paid."}, {"question": "Which statement about Contracts for Difference (CFDs) is correct?", "options": ["CFD holders take full ownership of the underlying asset", "CFDs require the investor to deposit the full notional value of the position", "CFDs are margin-traded derivatives whose price tracks an underlying asset without conferring ownership of it", "CFDs cannot be used for hedging purposes"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "CFDs are derivatives that track the price of an underlying asset; the holder benefits or loses from price movements but does not own the underlying asset, and only a margin (not full notional value) is deposited."}, {"question": "In CFD trading, the additional margin that becomes payable when a position moves adversely and is marked-to-market is known as:", "options": ["Initial margin", "Variation (or maintenance) margin", "Liquidation preference", "Break-even margin"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Initial margin is the deposit required to open the CFD position; variation (or maintenance) margin is the additional margin required if the marked-to-market position has moved adversely."}, {"question": "In the event of a company's liquidation, what is the basic rule governing the priority of claims?", "options": ["Ordinary shareholders always rank above debt holders", "All shareholders (equity) rank below all debt holders", "Preference shareholders rank above all debt holders", "All claimants rank equally regardless of instrument type"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The basic liquidation rule is that all shareholders/equity participants are subordinate to (rank below) debt holders."}, {"question": "Within a company's debt structure, which of the following correctly reflects the ranking of debt seniority from highest to lowest priority in a liquidation?", "options": ["Mezzanine debt, subordinated debt, senior debt", "Senior debt, subordinated debt, mezzanine debt", "Subordinated debt, senior debt, mezzanine debt", "Mezzanine debt, senior debt, subordinated debt"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Senior debt has the highest claim, ranking above subordinated debt, which in turn ranks above mezzanine debt (the most junior debt tranche, if it exists)."}, {"question": "Once all debt obligations have been discharged in a liquidation, which class of shareholder is paid next, ahead of ordinary shareholders?", "options": ["Employees", "Preference shareholders", "Government/tax authorities", "Regulators"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "After debt holders are satisfied, preference shareholders take priority over ordinary shareholders and receive the par value of their shares before any distribution to ordinary shareholders."}, {"question": "A venture capital investor bought preference shares in a start-up at $2 per share with a liquidation preference of 2x. On liquidation, how much per share will the investor receive before proceeds are distributed to ordinary shareholders (assuming sufficient proceeds)?", "options": ["$1 per share", "$2 per share", "$4 per share", "$6 per share"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "A liquidation preference of 2x on a $2 purchase price means the preference shareholder receives 2 x $2 = $4 per share before any distribution to ordinary shareholders."}, {"question": "Payment-in-kind (PIK) notes are typically associated with which level of a company's debt structure?", "options": ["Senior secured debt", "Government debt", "Mezzanine debt", "Money market instruments"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "PIK notes are an example of mezzanine-level finance; their coupons are not paid in cash but are effectively rolled up until the bond matures."}, {"question": "Subordinated bondholders typically receive a higher rate of interest than senior debt holders because:", "options": ["They rank above senior debt in a liquidation", "They have accepted a lower-ranking claim on the issuer's assets, taking on greater risk", "Subordinated debt is always government-guaranteed", "They are paid before any debt holders in a liquidation"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Subordinated debt ranks below senior debt in a liquidation; as compensation for accepting this greater risk, subordinated bondholders are entitled to a higher rate of interest."}, {"question": "An investor is considering an overseas equity in a country with the possibility of newly imposed capital controls restricting the flow of foreign currency out of the country. This is best described as an example of:", "options": ["Reinvestment risk", "Liquidity risk", "Country/political risk", "Equity risk premium"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Capital controls restricting currency flows are a form of political risk specific to investing in another country, which can overlap with foreign exchange risk."}], "flashcards": [{"front": "How is expected return (ER) calculated across probability-weighted scenarios?", "back": "ER = sum of (probability of each scenario x scenario return)."}, {"front": "What does standard deviation measure in portfolio theory?", "back": "The square root of variance; a statistical measure of risk showing the likely dispersion of returns from the expected return."}, {"front": "Market/systematic risk", "back": "Risk that the overall market or relevant sector rises or falls with economic conditions; cannot be diversified away."}, {"front": "Specific/unsystematic risk", "back": "Risk that something adverse affects one particular investment/company only, not the wider market; can be diversified away."}, {"front": "Why is inflation risk generally greater for bond investors than equity investors?", "back": "Bonds usually pay a fixed coupon that loses real value with inflation; companies issuing equity can often pass on higher costs to customers."}, {"front": "Which bonds are most exposed to interest rate risk?", "back": "Bonds with longer maturities and lower coupon rates; floating-rate bonds are largely protected as coupons reset with market rates."}, {"front": "Reinvestment risk", "back": "The risk that an investor cannot reinvest maturing assets at the same rate originally obtained."}, {"front": "Exchange rate risk effect if home currency strengthens", "back": "Lowers the returns from foreign assets when converted back to the home currency."}, {"front": "SONIA", "back": "Sterling Overnight Index Average - UK's primary sterling interest rate benchmark, based on actual overnight transactions, replacing LIBOR."}, {"front": "SOFR", "back": "Secured Overnight Financing Rate - US benchmark based on the cost of overnight borrowing collateralised by US Treasury securities, replacing USD LIBOR."}, {"front": "Minimum rating for 'investment grade' bonds", "back": "At least BBB from S&P or Fitch Ratings, or at least Baa from Moody's; below this is sub-investment grade/junk."}, {"front": "Equity risk premium", "back": "The extra return required to compensate investors for the additional risk of holding equities over the risk-free rate (eg, government bonds)."}, {"front": "Money market instruments - key characteristics", "back": "Low risk, short-term, liquid, deliver income, but little scope for capital growth (eg, Treasury bills)."}, {"front": "'Flight to safety'", "back": "The tendency of investors to move funds into the shortest-duration, most secure instruments (eg, T-bills) during periods of financial crisis."}, {"front": "Debt instruments - relative risk vs equities", "back": "Generally less risky than equities, offering less potential for substantial returns but greater certainty of yield if held to maturity."}, {"front": "Country/political risk (overseas investment)", "back": "Risk from changes in the political or legal landscape of a country (eg, capital controls, withholding tax changes, sanctions) affecting overseas investments."}, {"front": "Correlation coefficient range", "back": "Ranges from -1 (perfectly negatively correlated) to +1 (perfectly positively correlated)."}, {"front": "Correlation coefficient of +1", "back": "Perfectly positively correlated assets - move in the same direction and magnitude - offers NO diversification benefit."}, {"front": "Correlation coefficient of -1", "back": "Perfectly negatively correlated assets - move in exactly opposite directions - offers MAXIMUM diversification benefit."}, {"front": "Do positively correlated (but not perfectly correlated) assets provide diversification benefit?", "back": "Yes - any correlation below +1 provides some diversification benefit; it is not necessary for assets to be negatively correlated."}, {"front": "Which risk does diversification reduce, and which does it not?", "back": "Reduces specific/unsystematic risk; does NOT reduce market/systematic risk."}, {"front": "Diversification by asset class - examples", "back": "Cash, fixed-income securities, equities, collective investment schemes, derivatives, real estate/property, and other assets."}, {"front": "Laddering", "back": "Diversifying fixed-income holdings by spreading maturity dates over time, to reduce reinvestment risk."}, {"front": "Hedging - definition", "back": "The attempt to reduce risk, usually via derivatives (options, futures, forwards/CFDs), by taking an opposite position to the portfolio exposure; it has a cost."}, {"front": "Effect of hedging an equity portfolio with stock index futures (sold)", "back": "Removes both downside risk AND upside potential in the hedged exposure."}, {"front": "How is the number of futures contracts to sell for hedging adjusted for beta?", "back": "Multiply the nominal value/number of contracts needed by the portfolio's beta relative to the benchmark index (index beta = 1)."}, {"front": "Long put option - purpose in hedging", "back": "Gives the right (not obligation) to sell the underlying at a fixed strike price; caps downside loss while allowing continued upside participation in the held asset."}, {"front": "Put option break-even formula", "back": "Break-even price = strike (exercise) price - premium paid."}, {"front": "Maximum loss for a put option purchaser", "back": "Limited to the premium paid for the option."}, {"front": "When does maximum profit occur for a purchased put option?", "back": "If the underlying asset price falls to zero."}, {"front": "Why is an option premium called a 'wasting asset'?", "back": "Its value decays over time as the option approaches its expiration date."}, {"front": "CFD (Contract for Difference) - key feature", "back": "A margin-traded derivative that tracks an underlying asset's price without conferring ownership of that asset."}, {"front": "CFD initial margin vs variation margin", "back": "Initial margin is the deposit required to open the position; variation (maintenance) margin is additional margin required if the position moves adversely (mark-to-market)."}, {"front": "Basic rule of company liquidation ranking", "back": "All shareholders (equity) are subordinate to (rank below) all debt holders."}, {"front": "Debt seniority order in a liquidation (highest to lowest)", "back": "Senior debt, then subordinated debt, then mezzanine debt."}, {"front": "Shareholder ranking order in a liquidation", "back": "Preference shareholders rank above ordinary shareholders; preference shareholders receive par value (or liquidation preference) before ordinary shareholders receive any residual proceeds."}, {"front": "Liquidation preference (venture funding)", "back": "The amount, usually a multiple (eg, 2x) of the original purchase price, that must be paid to preference shareholders before ordinary shareholders on liquidation, sale, merger or change of control."}, {"front": "PIK (payment-in-kind) notes", "back": "Mezzanine-level bonds with a quoted coupon rate that is not paid in cash but rolled up and paid at maturity."}]}, {"id": "sec-m12", "number": 12, "title": "Institutional Clients, Investment Management & ESG", "examWeight": "Core module", "summaryHtml": "<p>This module covers three linked syllabus areas: the profiles of institutional investors, the methodologies of active versus passive investment management, and ESG (environmental, social and governance) investing.</p><p><b>Institutional clients</b> differ in time horizon, risk tolerance, liquidity needs and tax treatment, which drives their asset allocation. <b>Pension funds</b> (defined benefit/DB, where the employer promises a set benefit, versus defined contribution/DC, where the payout depends on contributions and investment performance) and <b>life assurance funds</b> (term assurance, whole of life, endowment policies) share a long time horizon, high risk tolerance and heavy equity weighting, and accumulate largely tax-free (pensions) or tax-paying (life funds) returns. <b>General insurance funds</b> and <b>banks</b> have short-term liabilities, so invest in low-risk, liquid money market instruments (Treasury bills, CP, interbank deposits) and are taxed on income and gains. <b>Regulated (authorised) mutual funds</b> can be freely marketed to the public; <b>unregulated (unauthorised) funds</b> face marketing restrictions and are typically sold only to sophisticated/HNW or institutional investors. <b>Hedge funds</b> are unauthorised, often highly geared, pursue absolute-return and long/short strategies, and are illiquid/opaque. <b>ESG funds</b> screen investments against environmental, social and governance criteria. <b>Sovereign wealth funds (SWFs)</b> are state-owned funds (eg, Norway's Government Pension Fund Global, China's SAFE and CIC, ADIA, Saudi PIF, KIA, GIC, QIA) funded from FX/commodity surpluses, generally with a high risk tolerance and long horizon.</p><p><b>Active management</b> seeks returns above a benchmark through security selection, sector rotation, and (for bonds) switching strategies (anomaly switching, policy switching/riding the anticipated rate move, inter-market spread switching) and riding the yield curve. It offers flexibility and the chance of outperformance but carries manager risk, higher fees and higher transaction/tax costs; evidence (eg, SPIVA scorecards) shows most active funds underperform over time. <b>Passive management</b> tracks a benchmark (index funds, ETFs) at low cost, consistent with the efficient markets hypothesis (EMH), but cannot outperform and offers no defensive flexibility. <b>Smart beta</b> uses alternative (non-market-cap) weighting to blend active-style outperformance potential with passive-style low cost. Passive bond strategies use <b>immunisation</b> (cash flow matching or duration matching) via bullet, barbell or ladder portfolios to meet known liabilities regardless of rate moves. The EMH has weak and strong forms, underpinning the active/passive debate.</p><p><b>ESG investing</b> considers Environmental (energy use, pollution, carbon footprint, climate impact), Social (community support, charitable giving, supply chain standards, health & safety) and Governance (board diversity, executive/non-executive balance, transparency, ethics) factors alongside risk and return. Key frameworks are the UN-backed <b>Principles for Responsible Investment (PRI)</b>, six voluntary principles launched in 2006, and the FSB's <b>Task Force on Climate-related Financial Disclosures (TCFD)</b>, covering governance, strategy, risk management, and metrics/targets. The <b>spectrum of investing</b> runs from fully delegated (reliance on the manager's ESG policy) through to philanthropic/impact investing. Advantages of ESG investing include potentially better returns, lower downside risk and rising investor demand; disadvantages include a narrower opportunity set, greater research burden and the risk of 'greenwashing'.</p>", "sections": [{"heading": "12.1 Institutional Client Profiles: Pension Funds and Insurance Companies", "html": "<p><b>Pension funds</b> exist to provide retirement income for beneficiaries, funded by employer and/or employee contributions. They are typically approved by tax authorities and accumulate income and capital gains largely tax-free. Because liabilities are long-dated, pension funds have traditionally taken a relatively high risk tolerance and invested heavily in equities. Pension funds split into two broad classes:</p><ul><li><b>Defined benefit (DB) / final salary schemes</b> – the employer promises a set level of benefit at retirement.</li><li><b>Defined contribution (DC) schemes</b> – the eventual benefit depends on contributions made and the investment performance achieved; investment risk sits with the member.</li></ul><p>African examples include South Africa's GEPF, Kenya's NSSF, Namibia's GIPF and Botswana's BPOPF; pension assets in some emerging African markets (eg, Nigeria, Ghana, Tanzania) are growing rapidly (sometimes 30%+ a year) due to new contribution legislation.</p><p><b>Life assurance</b> arises from insurance contracts on an individual's life, mainly comprising: <b>term assurance</b> (pays out only if death occurs within the policy term, in exchange for regular premiums); <b>whole of life assurance</b> (pays out on death whenever it occurs, for regular premiums); policies with a significant investment element linked to the insurer's fund performance; and <b>single-premium endowments</b>, which also have a substantial investment component. Like pension funds, life funds have a long time horizon and a heavy equity weighting, but unlike pension funds, their income and gains are typically taxable.</p><p><b>General insurance</b> covers short-term personal/commercial risks (eg, motor, household). Because liabilities are short-term, general insurance funds invest premiums in low-risk, liquid, short-term assets such as money market instruments, and (like life funds) are taxed on income and gains.</p>"}, {"heading": "12.2 Institutional Client Profiles: Banks, Regulated Mutual Funds and Hedge Funds", "html": "<p><b>Banks</b> place surplus end-of-day cash in the interbank market and in eligible money market instruments (eg, Treasury bills, commercial paper) — low-risk, short-term investments matching the short-term nature of their liabilities to depositors. In the UK, the Bank of England will accept certain 'eligible' money market instruments as collateral for loans. Banks are taxed on investment income and gains.</p><table><tr><th>Institutional investor</th><th>Time horizon</th><th>Risk tolerance</th><th>Liquidity requirement</th><th>Typical equity/growth weighting</th></tr><tr><td>Pension fund</td><td>Long-term*</td><td>High</td><td>Low</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><td>Life assurance fund</td><td>Long-term</td><td>High</td><td>Low</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><td>General insurance fund</td><td>Short-term</td><td>Low</td><td>High</td><td>Low</td></tr><tr><td>Bank</td><td>Short-term</td><td>Low</td><td>High</td><td>Low</td></tr></table><p>*Depends on the maturity profile of the scheme — a fund with most members near retirement will take a shorter-term view.</p><p><b>Regulated (authorised) mutual funds/collective investment schemes</b> are authorised by a regulator (eg, the SEC in the US) and can be freely marketed to the public. <b>Unregulated (unauthorised) funds</b> can still be sold, but only with restrictions — eg, to sophisticated investors, high net worth individuals (HNWIs), or those already holding such investments. ETFs have become a popular fund vehicle due to their transparency, liquidity and low cost versus traditional open-ended funds.</p><p><b>Hedge funds</b> are unauthorised vehicles not subject to the same regulatory oversight as conventional pooled funds. This allows them to pursue high-risk strategies — gearing via derivatives, arbitrage, and long/short positions (unlike most conventional funds, which are long-only). Hedge funds aim for 'absolute return' — positive returns in all market conditions — and are often highly geared. Because they are unauthorised, they cannot be freely marketed, need not report as frequently as authorised funds, and typically require large minimum investments, restricting access mainly to institutions and HNWIs.</p>"}, {"heading": "12.3 Sovereign Wealth Funds and ESG Funds", "html": "<p><b>Sovereign wealth funds (SWFs)</b> are state-owned investment funds, commonly established from balance of payments surpluses, privatisation proceeds, fiscal surpluses and/or resource export receipts. They tend to prefer returns over liquidity and so have a relatively high risk tolerance. Common objectives include: stabilising the budget/economy against export revenue volatility; diversifying away from non-renewable commodity exports; earning greater returns than on FX reserves; absorbing unwanted liquidity; saving for future generations; funding social/economic development; and promoting long-term sustainable capital growth. Global SWF AuM exceeded $11 trillion in 2025.</p><table><tr><th>Fund</th><th>Country/sponsor</th><th>Approx. AuM (2025)</th></tr><tr><td>Government Pension Fund Global (Norges Bank Investment Management)</td><td>Norway (oil/gas revenue)</td><td>$1.945 trillion</td></tr><tr><td>SAFE (State Administration of Foreign Exchange)</td><td>China (PBOC subsidiary, FX reserves)</td><td>$1.582 trillion</td></tr><tr><td>China Investment Corporation (CIC)</td><td>China</td><td>$1.332 trillion</td></tr><tr><td>Abu Dhabi Investment Authority (ADIA)</td><td>UAE (Abu Dhabi, oil surpluses)</td><td>$993 billion</td></tr><tr><td>Public Investment Fund (PIF)</td><td>Saudi Arabia</td><td>$925 billion</td></tr><tr><td>Kuwait Investment Authority (KIA)</td><td>Kuwait</td><td>$923 billion</td></tr><tr><td>GIC</td><td>Singapore</td><td>$770 billion</td></tr><tr><td>Qatar Investment Authority (QIA)</td><td>Qatar</td><td>$526 billion</td></tr></table><p><b>ESG funds</b> capitalise on demand for sustainable investing, giving investors easy access to investments meeting environmental, social and governance standards. For equities, this typically assesses whether a company minimises carbon emissions, protects society (eg, equal opportunities) and is soundly governed (eg, remuneration linked to performance).</p>"}, {"heading": "12.4 Active Investment Management: Approaches, Advantages and Disadvantages", "html": "<p><b>Active management</b> is a portfolio management method designed to outperform a benchmark index, using fundamental and/or technical analysis to exploit perceived mispricing. Strategies include security selection (eg, based on P/E and PEG ratios), sector rotation anticipating macro trends, buying undervalued/out-of-favour stocks, and specialised approaches such as merger arbitrage, option writing and statistical arbitrage. Note that a simple index futures contract only captures index price changes, not dividend income, so does not replicate total index returns — more complex derivatives can emulate total return without buying all constituents.</p><p>Evidence (eg, SPIVA scorecards comparing active US mutual funds against the S&P) shows only a minority of active funds beat their benchmark, and the proportion beating the benchmark falls as the time horizon lengthens.</p><table><tr><th></th><th>Active management</th><th>Passive management</th></tr><tr><td>Objective</td><td>Outperform the benchmark</td><td>Match the benchmark</td></tr><tr><td>Costs/fees</td><td>Higher (research, trading, manager skill)</td><td>Lower (minimal management fee)</td></tr><tr><td>Trading/turnover</td><td>Frequent — higher transaction costs & tax drag</td><td>Buy-and-hold — lower transaction costs & tax drag</td></tr><tr><td>Risk exposure</td><td>Systematic + unsystematic (selection) risk</td><td>Systematic (market) risk only</td></tr><tr><td>Underlying belief</td><td>Markets are inefficient/mispriced</td><td>Markets are efficient (EMH)</td></tr><tr><td>Flexibility</td><td>Can defend against falling markets</td><td>No defensive flexibility — must track down as well as up</td></tr></table><p><b>Advantages</b> of active management: choice of investments rather than the whole market; scepticism about EMH can be acted on; ability to reduce volatility by favouring quality companies; ability to seek higher-than-market returns; ability to avoid/underweight certain sectors (eg, an employee avoiding overexposure to their own employer's sector).</p><p><b>Disadvantages</b>: manager may make poor choices; higher fees than passive; frequent trading raises transaction costs and unfavourable short-term capital gains tax; very large active funds can become 'closet trackers' — charging active fees for effectively passive, index-like performance.</p><p>Some active managers have 'skin in the game' — personal equity stakes aligning their interests with investors (common in private equity and hedge funds), unlike most conventional funds where managers have no equity stake.</p>"}, {"heading": "12.5 Active Bond Management Strategies", "html": "<p>Active bond strategies assume the bond market is not perfectly efficient and therefore subject to mispricing.</p><p><b>Bond switching (bond swapping)</b> — exchanging bonds believed overpriced for those believed underpriced — takes three forms:</p><ul><li><b>Anomaly switching</b> — moving between two bonds similar in all respects except yield/price, switching from the more highly priced to the less highly priced bond.</li><li><b>Policy switching</b> — when a rate cut is expected but not priced into the yield curve, selling shorter-dated, low-duration bonds in favour of longer-dated, high-duration bonds to benefit from greater price volatility when the cut occurs.</li><li><b>Inter-market spread switching</b> — switching between markets (eg, gilts to corporate bonds) when the yield spread is judged excessive relative to the perceived risk differential; a 'flight to quality' works the other way (into government bonds) when risk appetite is expected to fall.</li></ul><p><b>Market timing</b> refers to positioning based on a view that the market's implied future rate path (from the yield curve) is wrong.</p><p><b>Riding the yield curve</b> exploits an upward-sloping curve without needing a pricing anomaly: rather than buying and holding a bond matching the investment horizon exactly (eg, 2 years), a longer bond (eg, 5 years) is bought and sold before maturity (eg, after 2 years, with 3 years remaining) to capture higher yield/price appreciation as it 'rolls down' the curve — profitable only if the curve stays static.</p>"}, {"heading": "12.6 Passive Investment Management: Approaches, Advantages and Disadvantages", "html": "<p><b>Passive management</b> does not aim to beat a benchmark; it invests in an index fund or derivative that replicates the index's weighting and returns as closely as possible (eg, FTSE 100, S&P 500, or emerging market indices across Africa, Asia and Latin America).</p><p><b>Advantages</b>: performance does not depend on manager skill (or lack of it); consistent with EMH — exposure is limited to systematic risk only; lower fees and lower transaction costs (buy-and-hold); less exposure to short-term capital gains tax; index-based ETFs/sector trackers now offer wide, low-cost access, including 'smart beta' variants; some active funds effectively become 'closet trackers' anyway, so paying for genuine tracking can be more cost-efficient.</p><p><b>Disadvantages</b>: performance is capped at the index return — no potential for outperformance (alpha); no ability to take defensive action ahead of a downturn; investors accept losses whenever the index falls (acceptable relatively, but not always acceptable in absolute terms); tracking isn't possible in some frontier/pre-emerging markets (eg, BRVM in Francophone West Africa, Rwanda, Myanmar, Bangladesh) where illiquidity or a lack of developed indices forces active/direct investment instead (eg, private equity).</p>"}, {"heading": "12.7 Smart Beta and Passive Bond Strategies (Immunisation)", "html": "<p><b>Beta</b> measures an investment's sensitivity to overall market movements (market beta = 1). Standard market-cap-weighted index tracking is inherently overweight overvalued stocks and underweight undervalued stocks. <b>Smart beta</b> addresses this by using alternative weighting schemes (equal weighting, dividends, sales revenue, cash flow) instead of market capitalisation — combining the outperformance potential of active management with the diversification and lower cost of passive management. Related concepts include hedge fund replication strategies and factor investing (eg, value, small-cap), both aiming to mimic active-style returns systematically and transparently at lower cost.</p><p><b>Passive bond strategies</b> are used when the market is believed efficient (buy-and-hold) or to meet a known future liability fixed in nominal terms, via <b>immunisation</b> — insulating a bond portfolio from future interest rate changes:</p><ul><li><b>Cash flow matching</b> — constructing a portfolio whose coupon and redemption cash flows are synchronised with the liability's cash flows.</li><li><b>Duration matching</b> — constructing a portfolio with the same initial value (present value) and the same duration as the liability. A portfolio closely aligned this way is a <b>bullet portfolio</b>.</li></ul><p>A <b>barbell strategy</b> holds bonds with durations either side of the liability duration (eg, 5-year and 15-year bonds for a 10-year liability) — this requires more frequent rebalancing than a bullet portfolio. A <b>ladder portfolio</b> holds equal amounts across a spread of durations (eg, 1-year through to 20-year, for a 10-year liability).</p>"}, {"heading": "12.8 Management Strategy and the Efficient Markets Hypothesis (EMH)", "html": "<p>The <b>efficient markets hypothesis (EMH)</b> holds that share prices always fully reflect all relevant available information, so it is impossible to consistently beat the market through stock selection, technical analysis or market timing; the only route to higher returns is taking on more risk.</p><p>Passive management is consistent with EMH — if markets are efficient, active trading only adds cost without benefit. Active managers, by contrast, aim to exploit inefficiencies by buying undervalued and/or short-selling overvalued securities (and may also aim to reduce volatility relative to the benchmark, as an alternative or addition to seeking outperformance).</p><p>There are different forms of EMH:</p><ul><li><b>Strong form EMH</b> — no one can beat the broad market over the long term (all information, public and private, is reflected in prices).</li><li><b>Weak form EMH</b> — acknowledged by many active managers; recognises that differences in information flow (eg, proprietary research) and trading/structural cost differences can make some strategies profitable for some participants but not others.</li></ul>"}, {"heading": "12.9 ESG Investing: E, S, G Factors and Global Frameworks (PRI, TCFD)", "html": "<p>ESG investing considers Environmental, Social and Governance factors alongside risk and return — also known as responsible investing, socially responsible investing (SRI), sustainable investing or impact investing. Traditionally this meant simply avoiding 'sin' stocks (tobacco, alcohol, weapons); modern ESG investing takes a much broader range of factors into account, sometimes limiting rather than excluding exposure, or pursuing activist engagement (buying a stake to influence company behaviour) rather than divestment.</p><table><tr><th>Pillar</th><th>Example factors considered</th></tr><tr><td>Environmental</td><td>Energy use and conservation; waste/pollution/carbon footprint; conservation efforts; climate change strategy</td></tr><tr><td>Social</td><td>Support for local community; charitable contributions; supply chain standards; workforce health & safety</td></tr><tr><td>Governance</td><td>Diversity of management/board; balance of executive vs non-executive directors; transparency beyond statutory reporting; ethical standards for staff and senior management</td></tr></table><p><b>UN Principles for Responsible Investment (PRI)</b>: initiated in 2005 by UN Secretary-General Kofi Annan; launched April 2006 at the NYSE; grown from 100 to over 5,000 signatories (asset owners, investment managers, financial service providers) by September 2025. The six principles commit signatories to: (1) incorporate ESG issues into investment analysis and decision-making; (2) be active owners and incorporate ESG into ownership policies/practices; (3) seek appropriate ESG disclosure from investee entities; (4) promote acceptance and implementation of the Principles across the industry; (5) work together to enhance effectiveness in implementing the Principles; (6) report on activities and progress.</p><p><b>Task Force on Climate-related Financial Disclosures (TCFD)</b>: established by the Financial Stability Board (FSB) at the request of the G20, publishing recommendations in 2017 across four pillars: <b>Governance</b> (climate-related governance), <b>Strategy</b> (actual/potential impacts on business, strategy, financial planning), <b>Risk Management</b> (identifying, assessing, managing climate risk), and <b>Metrics and Targets</b> (used to assess/manage climate-related risks and opportunities).</p>"}, {"heading": "12.10 Spectrum of ESG Investing and Advantages/Disadvantages", "html": "<p>ESG integration can be viewed as a <b>spectrum of investing</b>, running from minimal to maximal ESG emphasis:</p><table><tr><th>Approach</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td>Fully delegated</td><td>Investor relies on the appointed manager's own ESG policy (may not align fully with investor's objectives)</td></tr><tr><td>Screened/negative screening</td><td>Excludes or limits exposure to certain sectors/companies (eg, tobacco, weapons)</td></tr><tr><td>Integrated sustainable investing</td><td>ESG factors systematically integrated into mainstream investment analysis alongside financial factors</td></tr><tr><td>Investing for impact</td><td>Actively targets measurable positive environmental/social outcomes alongside financial return</td></tr><tr><td>Philanthropic investment</td><td>ESG/impact objectives dominate; financial return is a secondary consideration</td></tr></table><p>Most investors sit in the fully delegated category by default, but objectives are better met via integrated sustainable investing or impact investing.</p><p><b>Advantages</b> of ESG investing: some studies show ESG-focused companies deliver better returns; sustainable/ESG funds have shown lower downside risk, particularly in turbulent periods; growing investor demand (especially younger investors) makes ESG credentials commercially valuable for attracting/retaining capital.</p><p><b>Disadvantages</b>: values-based screening can cloud judgement and exclude profitable opportunities; ESG investing requires more intensive research (companies must be profitable and align with ESG values); <b>greenwashing</b> — some companies/funds falsely claim to be environmentally/socially responsible, potentially misleading investors. Regulators are increasingly responding to calls for climate-related legislation, which is expected to create significant winners and losers at industry and company level before 2050.</p>"}], "mcqs": [{"question": "A defined contribution (DC) pension scheme differs from a defined benefit (DB) scheme in that:", "options": ["The employer guarantees a fixed retirement benefit under DC", "The eventual benefit under DC depends on contributions made and investment performance achieved", "DC schemes cannot invest in equities", "DC schemes are always unfunded"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Under a DC scheme the benefit paid out is driven by the contributions made and the investment returns achieved; investment risk sits with the member. Under a DB (final salary) scheme, the employer promises a defined level of benefit."}, {"question": "Why do pension funds and life assurance funds typically have a heavy weighting in equities?", "options": ["They are required by regulation to hold only equities", "Their long-term liabilities allow them to take on higher risk tolerance in pursuit of higher returns", "Equities are the only tax-free asset class", "Their liabilities are short-term and unpredictable"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Because pension and life assurance liabilities are long-dated, these funds can tolerate more risk and have traditionally invested heavily in equities in pursuit of higher long-term returns."}, {"question": "Which of the following best describes why general insurance funds hold predominantly money market instruments?", "options": ["General insurance liabilities are short-term in nature, requiring low-risk, liquid, short-term investments", "General insurance funds are tax-exempt only if invested in money market instruments", "Regulators prohibit general insurers from holding equities", "Money market instruments provide the highest possible returns"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "General insurance covers short-term risks (eg, motor, household), so the funds backing these liabilities are invested in low-risk, liquid, short-term assets such as Treasury bills and CP."}, {"question": "Which institutional investor is subject to tax on income and gains, unlike a pension fund?", "options": ["Only sovereign wealth funds", "Life assurance funds, general insurance funds and banks", "Only hedge funds", "No institutional investors pay tax"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Pension funds typically accumulate income and gains tax-free. Life assurance funds, general insurance funds and banks are all typically taxed on income and gains generated from their investments."}, {"question": "An unregulated (unauthorised) collective investment scheme:", "options": ["Cannot legally be marketed under any circumstances", "Can be marketed, but only with restrictions, eg, to sophisticated or high net worth investors", "Must always be more expensive than a regulated fund", "Is illegal in all jurisdictions"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Unregulated funds are legal and can be marketed, but with restrictions — typically only to sophisticated investors, HNWIs, or those already holding such investments."}, {"question": "Which feature distinguishes hedge funds from most conventional regulated collective investment schemes?", "options": ["Hedge funds are restricted to a long-only strategy", "Hedge funds can take substantial short positions and use gearing via derivatives", "Hedge funds must report holdings daily to all investors", "Hedge funds cannot charge performance fees"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Unlike most conventional funds, which are long-only, hedge funds can be highly geared and take substantial short positions, employing strategies such as long/short equity and arbitrage."}, {"question": "Hedge funds describe themselves as 'absolute return' vehicles because they aim to:", "options": ["Match the return of a specified benchmark index exactly", "Generate positive returns irrespective of general market conditions", "Guarantee a fixed coupon regardless of performance", "Only invest in government bonds"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Hedge funds aim to produce positive returns in all market conditions, reducing overall portfolio risk when combined with conventional strategies — hence the term 'absolute return'."}, {"question": "Sovereign wealth funds are typically funded from all of the following EXCEPT:", "options": ["Balance of payments surpluses", "Privatisation proceeds", "Resource export receipts", "Retail investor subscriptions"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "SWFs are state-owned, typically funded from balance of payments surpluses, privatisation proceeds, fiscal surpluses and/or resource export receipts — not retail subscriptions."}, {"question": "Which sovereign wealth fund is the asset management arm of Norges Bank and manages Norway's oil and gas revenue?", "options": ["China Investment Corporation (CIC)", "Abu Dhabi Investment Authority (ADIA)", "Government Pension Fund Global (Norges Bank Investment Management)", "Qatar Investment Authority (QIA)"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The Government Pension Fund Global, run by Norges Bank Investment Management, is Norway's sovereign wealth fund, designed to manage oil and gas revenue for current and future generations."}, {"question": "Active investment management is best described as:", "options": ["An approach that invests in all constituents of a benchmark index in proportion to their weighting", "An approach where the manager's strategy is designed to outperform a benchmark index", "A strategy only available to sovereign wealth funds", "A strategy that guarantees returns above inflation"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Active management refers to a portfolio management style where the manager seeks to outperform a chosen benchmark index, typically via security selection, sector calls, or other strategies."}, {"question": "Why does simply buying a stock index futures contract fail to replicate the total return of the underlying index?", "options": ["Futures contracts are illegal in most jurisdictions", "The futures contract only captures changes in the index price level, not dividend income", "Futures contracts always underperform due to high margin costs", "Index futures track a different index to the cash index"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A simple index futures contract reflects only price-level changes; it does not capture dividend income, so it will not replicate the index's total return."}, {"question": "According to SPIVA-type research referenced in the CISI syllabus, what tends to happen to the proportion of active funds beating their benchmark as the comparison period lengthens?", "options": ["It rises steadily over time", "It stays constant regardless of time horizon", "It declines further as the time period increases", "It becomes 100% after ten years"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Research shows that as the comparison time horizon lengthens, the percentage of actively managed funds whose returns exceed the benchmark declines further."}, {"question": "A key disadvantage of active management, compared with passive management, is that:", "options": ["Active funds cannot legally use derivatives", "Fees and transaction costs are generally higher, reducing net returns", "Active funds are always more diversified", "Active management is prohibited from equity investment"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Active management typically involves higher management fees and, where trading is frequent, higher transaction costs and potentially unfavourable short-term capital gains tax treatment."}, {"question": "When an actively managed fund becomes very large and its holdings begin to resemble the whole market, it is described as a:", "options": ["Smart beta fund", "Closet index tracker", "Sovereign wealth fund", "Immunised portfolio"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "As an actively managed fund's assets grow very large, it must hold an increasingly broad range of securities, causing it to perform like the market while still charging active fees — a 'closet tracker'."}, {"question": "The term 'skin in the game' in the context of active fund management refers to:", "options": ["A fund manager who has invested their own money alongside investors in the fund they manage", "A regulatory requirement for all fund managers", "The practice of index tracking", "A form of hedging using derivatives"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "'Skin in the game' describes a manager (common in hedge funds and private equity) who has a personal financial stake in the fund, aligning their interests with those of investors."}, {"question": "An anomaly switch in active bond management involves:", "options": ["Switching from corporate bonds to government bonds ahead of a flight to quality", "Switching between two similar bonds to exploit a pricing/yield discrepancy between them", "Selling short-duration bonds in anticipation of a rate cut", "Buying a bond and holding it to maturity"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Anomaly switching exploits a pricing anomaly between two bonds that are similar in all respects except yield and price, switching from the more highly priced (lower yield) to the less highly priced (higher yield) bond."}, {"question": "A policy switch is undertaken when a manager:", "options": ["Expects an interest rate cut not yet implied by the yield curve, and sells short-duration bonds in favour of long-duration bonds", "Wants to move from gilts into corporate bonds due to an excessive credit spread", "Wants to reduce portfolio duration ahead of a rate rise", "Believes markets are perfectly efficient"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "A policy switch anticipates a rate cut not implied by the yield curve; the manager sells low-duration bonds for high-duration bonds to benefit from the greater price appreciation the latter will experience."}, {"question": "Inter-market spread switching is triggered by:", "options": ["A change in a company's credit rating", "A view that the yield spread between two bond markets (eg, gilts and corporates) is excessive relative to the perceived risk differential", "A change in a bond's coupon frequency", "The maturity of a bond"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Inter-market spread switching moves between bond markets (eg, from gilts to corporates, or vice versa in a flight to quality) when the yield spread between them appears too wide or narrow given the perceived relative risk."}, {"question": "Riding the yield curve as an active bond strategy involves:", "options": ["Buying a bond that exactly matches the investment horizon and holding it to maturity", "Buying a longer-maturity bond than the investment horizon and selling it before maturity to benefit from price appreciation as it 'rolls down' an upward-sloping curve", "Immunising a bond portfolio against interest rate risk", "Investing solely in zero-coupon government bonds"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Riding the yield curve exploits an upward-sloping curve by buying a bond with a longer maturity than the investment horizon and selling it before maturity, generating a better return than simply matching maturity to horizon, provided the curve stays static."}, {"question": "Passive investment management is best described as an approach that:", "options": ["Seeks to outperform a chosen benchmark using fundamental analysis", "Aims to replicate the returns of a chosen benchmark index as closely as possible", "Only invests in government bonds", "Guarantees a minimum absolute return"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Passive management does not aim to beat a benchmark; instead it seeks to track/replicate the index's returns as closely as possible, typically via an index fund or derivative."}, {"question": "Which of the following is a genuine advantage of passive management over active management?", "options": ["Guaranteed outperformance of the market", "Lower fees and lower transaction costs due to a buy-and-hold approach", "Full flexibility to sell out of the market ahead of a downturn", "No exposure to systematic risk"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Because passive funds typically buy and hold, they incur lower transaction costs and management fees than active funds, and are less exposed to unfavourable short-term capital gains tax."}, {"question": "A key disadvantage of passive management is that:", "options": ["It always incurs higher fees than active management", "The investor forgoes any chance of returns above the benchmark index, and cannot take defensive action ahead of a downturn", "It cannot be used in developed markets", "It requires daily manager intervention"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Passive management caps returns at the benchmark level (no alpha potential) and lacks the flexibility to take defensive measures if the manager anticipates a market downturn."}, {"question": "In some frontier or pre-emerging markets (eg, the BRVM in Francophone West Africa or Rwanda), why is passive investment management often not a viable option?", "options": ["These markets prohibit foreign investment entirely", "These markets may lack developed indices, or be too illiquid to attract index tracking, favouring active/direct investment instead", "Passive management is banned by local regulators", "These markets only permit bond investment"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Where a securities exchange is recently established or too illiquid to attract index providers, tracking is not feasible, so active management (including direct/private investment) tends to dominate."}, {"question": "Smart beta investing differs from traditional market-capitalisation-weighted index tracking because it:", "options": ["Uses alternative weighting methods, such as equal weighting or weighting by dividends, sales or cash flow", "Only invests in bonds", "Requires the fund to be actively traded intraday", "Guarantees higher returns than the market in all conditions"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "Smart beta uses non-market-cap weighting schemes (eg, equal weighting, dividends, revenue, cash flow) to avoid being systematically overweight overvalued stocks and underweight undervalued stocks, as occurs under pure cap weighting."}, {"question": "A bond portfolio manager wants to construct a portfolio whose coupon and redemption cash flows exactly match a known future liability. This is an example of:", "options": ["Duration matching", "Cash flow matching", "Riding the yield curve", "Anomaly switching"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Cash flow matching is an immunisation technique that synchronises a bond portfolio's coupon and redemption payments with the timing and amount of the liabilities to be met."}, {"question": "A bullet portfolio, in the context of bond portfolio immunisation, is one that:", "options": ["Holds bonds with durations either side of the liability's duration", "Has the same initial value and duration as the liability it is designed to meet, via duration matching", "Holds equal amounts across a wide spread of maturities", "Consists solely of zero-coupon bonds"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A bullet portfolio is created via duration matching — it has an initial value equal to the present value of the liability and the same duration as that liability."}, {"question": "A barbell strategy for bond portfolio immunisation, compared to a bullet portfolio, requires:", "options": ["No rebalancing at all", "More frequent rebalancing", "Investment in a single bond only", "Investment exclusively in equities"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A barbell strategy holds bonds with durations either side of the liability duration (eg, 5-year and 15-year bonds for a 10-year liability); this structure requires more frequent rebalancing than a bullet portfolio."}, {"question": "A ladder bond portfolio strategy involves:", "options": ["Investing solely in the shortest-duration bond available", "Holding equal amounts invested in bonds across a spread of different durations", "Switching continuously between government and corporate bonds", "Matching the portfolio duration exactly to the liability with a single bond"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A ladder portfolio spreads equal amounts of investment across bonds of different durations (eg, 1-year through to 20-year), rather than concentrating around a single duration point."}, {"question": "According to the strong form of the efficient markets hypothesis (EMH):", "options": ["Only technical analysis can generate outperformance", "No investor can consistently beat the broad market over the long term, as all information is reflected in prices", "Active managers with proprietary research can reliably outperform", "Passive management always underperforms active management"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Strong form EMH holds that all information (public and private) is already reflected in prices, meaning no one can consistently outperform the market over the long term."}, {"question": "Weak form EMH, as acknowledged by many active managers, recognises that:", "options": ["Markets are entirely unpredictable and no analysis is of any use", "Differences in the spread of information (eg, proprietary research) and trading costs can make some strategies profitable for some but not all traders", "Passive management is always superior to active management", "Only government bonds are efficiently priced"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Weak form EMH recognises that, despite competitive and fast-moving markets, differences in information access and trading/structural costs can still create profitable opportunities for some market participants."}, {"question": "Which of the following is an Environmental (E) factor typically considered under ESG criteria?", "options": ["Balance between executive and non-executive directors", "A company's carbon footprint and efforts to manage climate change", "Charitable contributions made by the company", "Board diversity"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Environmental factors include energy use, waste/pollution minimisation, carbon footprint, and the company's approach to managing climate change. Board balance and diversity are Governance factors; charitable contributions are Social factors."}, {"question": "Under the UN Principles for Responsible Investment (PRI), signatories commit to which of the following?", "options": ["Guaranteeing a minimum annual return to investors", "Incorporating ESG issues into investment analysis and decision-making, and seeking appropriate ESG disclosure from investee entities", "Only investing in government-issued securities", "Avoiding all engagement with investee companies"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Among the six PRI principles are commitments to incorporate ESG into investment analysis/decision-making and to seek appropriate ESG disclosure by the entities invested in, alongside active ownership, promoting the Principles, collaboration, and reporting."}, {"question": "The Task Force on Climate-related Financial Disclosures (TCFD) was established by which body?", "options": ["The United Nations PRI", "The Financial Stability Board (FSB), at the request of the G20", "The International Monetary Fund (IMF)", "The London Stock Exchange"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The FSB established the TCFD, following a request from G20 finance ministers to review how the financial sector could account for climate-related issues."}, {"question": "The TCFD's 2017 recommendations covered four areas. Which of the following is NOT one of them?", "options": ["Governance", "Strategy", "Risk Management", "Shareholder Voting Rights"], "correctIndex": 3, "explanation": "The four TCFD pillars are Governance, Strategy, Risk Management, and Metrics and Targets. Shareholder voting rights is not one of the four TCFD disclosure areas."}, {"question": "On the ESG 'spectrum of investing', an approach that relies significantly on the appointed investment manager's own ESG policy, which may not align fully with the investor's objectives, is known as:", "options": ["Investing for impact", "Philanthropic investment", "The fully delegated approach", "Integrated sustainable investing"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The fully delegated approach places significant reliance on the manager's own ESG policy. Investors seeking closer alignment with their own objectives would instead adopt integrated sustainable investing or investing for impact."}, {"question": "'Greenwashing' refers to:", "options": ["A legitimate tax-efficient ESG investment wrapper", "The practice of making an investment appear more ESG-compliant than it actually is", "A form of active bond switching strategy", "A method of index replication using synthetic derivatives"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Greenwashing describes companies or funds falsely presenting themselves as more environmentally or socially responsible than they genuinely are, potentially misleading investors."}, {"question": "Which of the following is a genuine disadvantage of adopting an ESG-driven investment approach?", "options": ["ESG funds cannot legally be marketed to retail investors", "ESG investing requires more intensive research, as an additional layer of non-financial criteria must be assessed alongside profitability and growth", "ESG funds are prohibited from holding equities", "ESG investing guarantees underperformance versus non-ESG funds"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A recognised disadvantage of ESG investing is the extra research burden — investments must be assessed not only on profitability and growth but also against ESG criteria, which can be resource-intensive."}], "flashcards": [{"front": "Defined benefit (DB) pension scheme", "back": "A pension scheme where the employer promises to pay a set level of retirement benefit, regardless of investment performance."}, {"front": "Defined contribution (DC) pension scheme", "back": "A pension scheme where the eventual retirement benefit depends on the contributions made and the investment performance achieved; investment risk sits with the member."}, {"front": "Why do pension funds tend to invest heavily in equities?", "back": "Because their liabilities are long-term, allowing a higher risk tolerance in pursuit of higher long-term returns."}, {"front": "Term assurance", "back": "A life assurance policy that, for regular premiums, pays out only if the insured dies before the end of a set policy term."}, {"front": "Whole of life assurance", "back": "A life assurance policy that pays out on death (whenever it occurs) in exchange for regular premiums."}, {"front": "Endowment policy (life assurance)", "back": "A single-premium life assurance policy with a significant investment element, dependent on the performance of the insurer's fund."}, {"front": "Why does general insurance invest mainly in money market instruments?", "back": "Because general insurance liabilities (eg, motor, household claims) are short-term, requiring low-risk, liquid, short-term assets."}, {"front": "Tax treatment: pension funds vs life/general insurance funds", "back": "Pension funds accumulate income and gains largely tax-free; life assurance and general insurance funds are typically taxed on income and gains."}, {"front": "Regulated (authorised) fund", "back": "A collective investment scheme authorised by a regulator (eg, the SEC); can be freely marketed to the public."}, {"front": "Unregulated (unauthorised) fund", "back": "A collective investment scheme not authorised by a regulator; can still be marketed, but only with restrictions (eg, to sophisticated/HNW investors)."}, {"front": "Hedge fund key characteristics", "back": "Unauthorised, often highly geared, can go long and short, pursue 'absolute return', minimal reporting, large minimum investments — accessible mainly to institutions and HNWIs."}, {"front": "'Absolute return' vehicle", "back": "A fund (eg, a hedge fund) that aims to generate positive returns regardless of general market direction."}, {"front": "Sovereign wealth fund (SWF) definition", "back": "A state-owned investment fund, typically funded from balance of payments surpluses, privatisation proceeds, fiscal surpluses, and/or resource export receipts."}, {"front": "Common SWF objectives", "back": "Stabilise the economy against export revenue volatility; diversify from commodity exports; earn better returns than FX reserves; save for future generations; fund social/economic development."}, {"front": "Norway's SWF", "back": "Government Pension Fund Global, managed by Norges Bank Investment Management, funded by oil and gas revenue; largest SWF globally (~$1.945 trillion, 2025)."}, {"front": "China's two major SWF entities", "back": "SAFE (State Administration of Foreign Exchange, PBOC subsidiary, FX reserves) and China Investment Corporation (CIC, flagship SWF with subsidiaries Central Huijin, CIC International, CIC Capital)."}, {"front": "ADIA", "back": "Abu Dhabi Investment Authority — SWF of Abu Dhabi, funded from oil export surpluses; large allocator to real estate, infrastructure and private equity."}, {"front": "Active management definition", "back": "A portfolio management approach where the manager aims to outperform a chosen benchmark index, using research, analysis and security selection."}, {"front": "Why doesn't an index futures contract replicate total index return?", "back": "It only captures changes in the index price level, not the dividend income component of total return."}, {"front": "Main disadvantage of active management", "back": "Higher fees and (if trading is frequent) higher transaction costs and unfavourable short-term capital gains tax, plus the risk of poor manager decisions."}, {"front": "'Closet index tracker'", "back": "A very large actively managed fund whose holdings become so broad they mimic the index, while still charging active management fees."}, {"front": "'Skin in the game'", "back": "When a fund manager has personally invested their own money in the fund they manage, aligning their interests with investors' (common in hedge funds/private equity)."}, {"front": "Bond switching / bond swapping", "back": "An active bond strategy exchanging bonds perceived as overpriced for those perceived as underpriced, to outperform a buy-and-hold approach."}, {"front": "Anomaly switching", "back": "Switching between two bonds that are similar except for yield/price, moving from the higher-priced (lower-yield) to the lower-priced (higher-yield) bond."}, {"front": "Policy switching", "back": "Selling short-duration bonds for long-duration bonds in anticipation of a rate cut not yet reflected in the yield curve, to benefit from greater price volatility."}, {"front": "Inter-market spread switching", "back": "Switching between bond markets (eg, gilts and corporates) when the yield spread is judged excessive relative to the perceived risk differential; reverses in a 'flight to quality'."}, {"front": "Riding the yield curve", "back": "Buying a longer-maturity bond than the investment horizon and selling before maturity to profit from price appreciation as it rolls down an upward-sloping (static) yield curve."}, {"front": "Passive management definition", "back": "An investment approach that does not aim to beat a benchmark, instead replicating its weighting and returns as closely as possible (eg, via an index fund/ETF)."}, {"front": "Main advantage of passive management", "back": "Lower fees and transaction costs, and performance not dependent on manager skill — consistent with the efficient markets hypothesis."}, {"front": "Main disadvantage of passive management", "back": "No potential for outperformance (alpha), and no flexibility to defend the portfolio ahead of a market downturn."}, {"front": "Smart beta", "back": "An index strategy using alternative weighting methods (eg, equal weighting, dividends, sales, cash flow) instead of market-cap weighting, blending active-style outperformance potential with passive-style low cost."}, {"front": "Why is pure market-cap weighting a disadvantage?", "back": "It goes overweight in overvalued stocks and underweight in undervalued stocks, since weighting is based purely on market value."}, {"front": "Immunisation (bond portfolios)", "back": "A passive technique to insulate a bond portfolio from future interest rate changes so it can meet a known future liability."}, {"front": "Cash flow matching", "back": "Immunisation technique constructing a bond portfolio whose coupon and redemption cash flows are synchronised with the liability's cash flows."}, {"front": "Duration matching / bullet portfolio", "back": "Immunisation technique constructing a portfolio with the same initial value (PV) and same duration as the liability."}, {"front": "Barbell strategy", "back": "Holding bonds with durations either side of the liability's duration (eg, 5yr and 15yr for a 10yr liability); requires more frequent rebalancing than a bullet portfolio."}, {"front": "Ladder portfolio", "back": "Holding equal amounts invested in bonds across a spread of durations (eg, 1yr through to 20yr for a 10yr liability)."}, {"front": "Efficient Markets Hypothesis (EMH)", "back": "Theory that share prices always reflect all available relevant information, making it impossible to consistently beat the market other than by taking on more risk."}, {"front": "Strong form EMH", "back": "No one can beat the broad market over the long term, as all information (public and private) is already reflected in prices."}, {"front": "Weak form EMH", "back": "Recognises that differences in information access (eg, proprietary research) and trading costs can make some strategies profitable for some, but not all, participants."}, {"front": "ESG — Environmental factors", "back": "Energy use/conservation, waste and pollution (carbon footprint), conservation efforts, and approach to managing climate change."}, {"front": "ESG — Social factors", "back": "Support for local community, charitable contributions, supply chain standards, and workforce health and safety."}, {"front": "ESG — Governance factors", "back": "Board/management diversity, balance of executive vs non-executive directors, transparency beyond statutory reporting, and ethical standards."}, {"front": "UN Principles for Responsible Investment (PRI)", "back": "Six voluntary principles (launched 2006, NYSE) committing signatories to incorporate ESG into analysis/ownership, seek ESG disclosure, promote the Principles, collaborate, and report progress."}, {"front": "TCFD", "back": "Task Force on Climate-related Financial Disclosures, established by the FSB (at G20 request); 2017 recommendations cover Governance, Strategy, Risk Management, and Metrics and Targets."}, {"front": "Spectrum of ESG investing", "back": "Ranges from fully delegated (reliance on manager's ESG policy) through screening, integrated sustainable investing, investing for impact, to philanthropic investment."}, {"front": "Greenwashing", "back": "The practice of making an investment, company or fund appear more ESG-compliant/sustainable than it genuinely is."}, {"front": "Key advantages of ESG investing", "back": "Potentially better returns, lower downside risk in turbulent markets, and growing investor demand (especially from younger investors)."}, {"front": "Key disadvantages of ESG investing", "back": "Can cloud investment judgement/restrict opportunity set, requires more intensive research, and carries greenwashing risk."}]}], "chapterCount": 12, "mcqCount": 420, "flashcardCount": 509};
